window.MFE_SEARCH = {"pages":[{"id":"start/overview","url":"start/overview.html","title":"What the editor does","section":"Get started","level":"basic","description":"The Manim Formula Editor at one look: formulas, the timeline, transformations between glyphs, and the Python it writes for Manim.","headings":[["see-it","See it move"],["idea","The idea"],["workflow","The workflow"],["window","The window"],["transformations","How one formula becomes another"],["output","What you get"],["needs","What you need"],["path","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"The Manim Formula Editor is a browser app for animating formulas. You write them in LaTeX, lay them out in time, turn one into the next, and export the scene for Manim or as a video."],["see-it","Four formulas, one on each step of (a+b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2. The glyphs that stay glide to their new places, new ones come in, and a block at the end draws a box round the result. The letters a and b keep their colours all the way. The player is the editor's own renderer playing the project, so the strip under the scene is the timeline the scene was built on: two tracks of clips, the three transformations between them, and the block at the end. You can download this project and open it in the editor to take it apart: choose File ▸ Open…, then press Other file… in the dialog (Projects and files explains it)."],["idea","Manim can turn one formula into another, but you describe it in code, glyph by glyph: formula[0][3] is the fourth symbol, and you count them by hand. The editor makes that visual. You write the formulas, put them on a timeline, say which glyph of the first becomes which glyph of the second, and press Play. When the animation looks right, you export it. A glyph is one drawn symbol of a formula: the x, the +, the 3. A clip is one appearance of a formula on the timeline. A relation says how the glyphs of one clip become the glyphs of the next. The editor is for teachers, video makers and anyone who explains mathematics by showing it move. You can know a little LaTeX and no Manim at all."],["workflow","WriteCreatorformulas and texts, in LaTeX PlaceTimeline, Previewclips on tracks, set in the frame AnimateAnimationsintros, outros, animation blocks TransformAnimationspairs of glyphs between formulas ExportExport… buttonPython for Manim, video, sound Preview runs through all of it: play the scene in the editor after every change, with no Manim installed. The five steps of an animation, and the panel where you do each one. Write. Press + New in the Creator and write a formula in a visual math field or in raw LaTeX; the two stay in step. Texts are written there too. Writing formulas Place. Drag a formula onto a track of the timeline. Its clip says when the formula is on screen; the Preview says where. The timeline, the Preview Animate. Each clip can come in and go out with an intro and an outro (Write, FadeIn…), and take animation blocks in between, such as an emphasis. Appearing and disappearing, animation blocks Transform. Relate the glyphs of one clip to the glyphs of the next, and the formula turns into the other. Inside one formula, a Morph block writes a derivation line by line. A first transformation, relations, Morph blocks Export. Choose Export ▸ Python — ManimCE… or Python — ManimGL… for a scene Manim runs, or Video… for a file. Exporting"],["window","The editor is one window with a menu bar and four panels. Each panel is a step of the workflow. The window with the square of a sum open. Click the picture to enlarge it. The menu bar holds the file commands, the edit commands, the view and the preferences, the export, and the help. It also shows where the project is saved. The Creator keeps what you can put in the scene: your formulas and texts, a library of templates, and your audio recordings. The Preview draws the scene at the playhead. Click a formula to select it, drag it to move it, and play the scene from the bar under it. The Animations panel edits the selected clip: its relations to other formulas, its timing, and its animations. The Timeline lays the scene out in time: tracks of clips under a ruler, with the playhead you scrub. The next pages take you through them one by one: a tour of the interface. Open the editor (Getting the app running says how). Download the square of a sum. Choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… and pick the file. Click a clip on the timeline. The Animations panel shows how its glyphs are linked to the next formula's. Press Play under the Preview, or Space, and watch the scene you saw above."],["transformations","This is the idea the editor is built on. To turn x + 3 = 5 into x = 5 − 3, you do not move the whole formula: you pair its glyphs. The x goes to the x, the equals sign to the equals sign, and so on. A glyph with no partner leaves with the outro of its clip, or comes in with the intro of the next clip: in this example, a fade out and a fade in. Four pairs and two glyphs without a partner: the plus sign leaves and the minus sign arrives. The 3 keeps its amber colour on its way across. The small numbers are the glyphs' indices, which the Python below uses. Here is the same relation running, as the editor plays it: Now make the pairs yourself. Click a glyph of the formula on top and then a glyph of the formula below: a line joins them. Give each glyph its partner, the x, the equals sign, the 5 and the 3; the plus sign and the minus sign have none, and the line under the drawing says what happens to them. Then choose Preview in the switch above the drawing to watch the transformation with your pairs. Suggest links by label offers the pairs the editor would make by itself. And here is what the editor writes for Manim from that relation, an excerpt of the exported file. Each pair here is one ReplacementTransform; the glyph with no partner on the left is a FadeOut, the outro of its clip, and the one with no partner on the right is a FadeIn, the intro of its clip: # 2.2 s – 3.2 s: step1 fade_out, step1 → step2, step2 fade_in self.play( FadeOut(step1_g[1]), ReplacementTransform(step1_g[0], step2_g[0]), ReplacementTransform(step1_g[3], step2_g[1]), ReplacementTransform(step1_g[4], step2_g[2]), ReplacementTransform(step1_g[2], step2_g[4]), FadeIn(step2_g[3]), run_time=1, ) You never write those lines. You make the pairs by clicking, or let the editor suggest them, and the Python follows. How relations work says what a pair can do."],["output","You make With It is Python for Manim Export ▸ Python — ManimCE… or Python — ManimGL… A .py scene that renders the same animation in ManimCE or in ManimGL. A video Export ▸ Video… A file drawn frame by frame by the editor and encoded in the browser, as MP4 or WebM. With the companion, ffmpeg can encode it instead (MP4, WebM, ProRes or GIF), or your own Manim can render it. Sound Export ▸ Audio… The sound of the project, mixed into one WAV or WebM file (M4A where your browser can encode AAC). Your work File ▸ Save A .mfe.json project file, or a .mfe package when the project holds recordings. The video the editor draws itself needs no Manim at all. The Python is for when you want Manim's own render, or want to change the scene by hand afterwards. The generated Python explains the file."],["needs","Part Do you need it? What it gives you A browser Yes The editor itself. Chrome or Edge read and write your files in place; Firefox and Safari work on copies. Browsers Manim Only to run the exported Python ManimCE 0.22 or ManimGL 1.7 render the .py file the editor writes. Manim The companion No A small Python program you start yourself. It adds exact LaTeX glyphs, font text, fast video encoding with ffmpeg, and rendering with Manim. The companion Chroma Lab It comes inside A colour studio for designing palettes, opened from Preferences ▸ Chroma Lab…. Chroma Lab"],["path","The pages are in the order of a first project. Getting the app running comes next, then your first animation, step by step. After that, each section builds on the ones before it: the basics, transformations, animations and timing, texts and objects, layout, colour, audio and video, and Manim. The examples on these pages play in the page and download as projects you can open in the editor. Or start from what you want to do: If you want to… Read Make a first animation, step by step Your first animation, then Solving a linear equation Know your way around the window A tour of the interface, Projects and files Show an equation changing, line by line How relations work, Transformation recipes, Morph blocks Colour the parts of a formula Colouring glyphs and objects, Palettes and Chroma Lab Add braces, boxes, lines and arrows Braces, Shapes, Lines and arrows Get a video, with sound Exporting, Audio, Exporting video Use the Python in Manim The generated Python, ManimCE and ManimGL Look something up Shortcuts, Every menu, Glossary, Troubleshooting"]],"short":"Overview"},{"id":"start/install","url":"start/install.html","title":"Getting the app running","section":"Get started","level":"basic","description":"Download the editor ready-built or build it, and open it from disk; the dev server, the browsers it works in, Chroma Lab inside, the optional companion, and Manim.","headings":[["ready-built","The ready-built app"],["requirements","What you need"],["built-app","The built app, from disk"],["dev-server","The dev server"],["ways","Three ways to open it"],["check","Check that it works"],["browsers","Browsers"],["chroma-lab","Chroma Lab, inside the app"],["companion","The optional companion"],["manim","Manim, to run the Python"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"The editor is a web app. Download it ready-built or build it once, then open its index.html from your disk: no server to run, no account to make. Everything else on this page is optional."],["ready-built","The editor is published ready-built on the releases page of its repository: a ZIP of about 2.5 MB, manim-formula-editor-1.0.0.zip for this version. Unzip it anywhere and open the index.html of its folder in your browser. It is the editor a build makes (below), so you need neither Git nor Node.js for it. The ZIP holds the editor alone, with Chroma Lab inside and the licences of what it contains. The companion is a file of the repository, companion/mfe_companion.py: take it from there when you want it."],["requirements","To build the editor yourself and run it: Git, to copy the repository (or download its ZIP from the GitHub page). Node.js with npm: 22.22.2 or later in the 22 line, 24.15.0 or later in the 24 line, or any version from 26 on (the engines field of app/package.json). A browser. A recent Chrome or Edge gives the whole editor; see Browsers for what Firefox and Safari do differently. You need no Manim to write, preview and play an animation, nor to export a video from the browser. Manim is for running the Python the editor exports (below)."],["built-app","Get the code, install what it depends on, and build it: git clone https://github.com/zavden/mfe.git cd mfe/app npm install npm run build npm run build type-checks the app and writes the finished editor into app/dist/. Open app/dist/index.html in your browser: double-click it, drag it onto a browser window, or ask the system to open it: xdg-open dist/index.html # Linux open dist/index.html # macOS start dist\\index.html # Windows dist/ is the whole editor: one script, its stylesheet, its fonts, and Chroma Lab in dist/chroma-lab/. Its addresses are relative and it needs nothing from the internet, which is why it opens from disk. You can copy the folder wherever you like. The first time, the editor shows an empty project: no formulas, two empty tracks, and in the Creator two buttons, Try an example, which opens a finished project to play and change (The examples), and Sync folder…, for the folder of your own projects. The editor at its first start. The bar says Untitled · Not saved yet and Renderer: MathJax. To update, pull the repository and build again: git pull, npm install, npm run build. Your projects are your own files, so a new build does not touch them."],["dev-server","If you would rather not build, or you want to see changes to the app at once, run the development server from app/: cd mfe/app npm install # the first time npm run dev Vite prints the address to open, http://localhost:5173/ unless that port is busy. It is the same editor. The difference that matters to you: a page served from localhost is one the companion accepts without any option, which a page opened from disk is not."],["ways","The editor is the same whichever way you open it. What changes is the address the page has, and that decides whether the companion accepts it. Way You run You open The companion The built app, from disk npm run build, once app/dist/index.html Needs --allow-file-origin The dev server npm run dev http://localhost:5173/ Accepts it as it is Served by the companion mfe_companion.py serve --app app/dist http://127.0.0.1:7799/app/ Is the page's own origin: nothing to allow"],["check","Open Help ▸ About. It names the editor's own version first (Version 1.0.0), then the version of the project format, the version of MathJax that draws the formulas, and whether a companion is running. Its Third-party licenses… button, which Help ▸ Third-party licenses… also opens, lists the open-source components the editor contains, with their versions and licences. The About dialog of an editor with no companion running. Open the editor and press + New in the Creator. In the Raw LaTeX field, type x + 3 = 5. Press Apply. The line beside the button reads 5 glyphs · MathJax: the editor has drawn your formula. The formula editor at the end of the steps: the Raw LaTeX field (1), Apply (2), and the line that counts the glyphs and names what drew them (3). On a Mac the editor uses ⌘ where the other systems use Ctrl and ⌥ where they use Alt, and writes its keys as a Mac's menus do: ⌘S saves, and ⇧⌘Z redoes. A few shortcuts have keys of their own there, because the system keeps the usual ones for itself: Save As is ⇧⌘S and Sync folder ⌥⌘S, Distribute is ⌃⌥H and ⌃⌥V, and ⌘↩ adds the formula that has the focus in the list to the selection. On a trackpad, a pinch zooms the Preview and the timeline, and a scroll with two fingers moves the Preview's view. These pages show the keys for the system you read them on."],["browsers","The editor runs in the browser and draws its formulas there, so it needs nothing but the page. One thing differs between browsers: whether the page may open and write the files and folders of your disk. Chrome and Edge give it the File System Access API. Firefox and Safari do not. What you do Chrome, Edge Firefox, Safari File ▸ Open…, then Other file… Reads the file and remembers where it is. Reads a copy of the file you choose. File ▸ Save Writes the same file. The first time, a picker asks where. Downloads a new file each time; the file you opened is not written. File ▸ Sync folder… Keeps the folder and lists it again whenever the Projects dialog opens. A project opened from it saves in place, and Save As starts there. Takes the folder as it is at that moment. It is not read again until you sync it again, and nothing is written into it. Your templates Kept in the browser, and also in the synced folder. Kept in the browser only; Export takes them elsewhere. Export ▸ Video… The dialog asks where to save the file first, then writes it as it encodes. The video is put together in memory and downloaded at the end. The editor decides by what the browser can do, not by its name. When the browser cannot write files or folders, the editor tells you once per session, as it opens, in a notice that stays until you close it: The notice a browser without file access shows. Whatever the browser, the editor also keeps your work in the browser as you go, so a reload brings it back. That is a safety net, not a file: Projects and files says what it does and what it cannot do."],["chroma-lab","Chroma Lab is a colour studio for designing the palette of a scene: harmonies, roles for the parts of a formula, and contrast against the scene's background. It comes inside every build, as a copy kept in dist/chroma-lab/, and works offline. There is nothing to install. Open it with Preferences ▸ Chroma Lab…. Editor palettes…, the entry after it, is where the palettes you keep are listed and edited. Chroma Lab (1) and Editor palettes… (2) in the Preferences menu. Chroma Lab, open over the editor. A theme goes to the editor's palettes with one button. Palettes and Chroma Lab shows how to design a palette and send it to the editor."],["companion","The companion is one Python file that you start yourself. The editor works without it: Without the companion says what you have then, and the one place where it shows in what you export. With it, the editor can get: exact glyphs: formulas compiled by real LaTeX, as Manim compiles them, so glyph order and shapes match Manim's; font text, shaped by Pango; fast video encoding with ffmpeg; rendering of the exported scene with your own Manim. It needs Python 3.10 or later (and nothing to install for the file itself), and, for exact glyphs, latex and dvisvgm on your PATH. Start it from the repository's root: python3 companion/mfe_companion.py serve That gives exact glyphs, and encoding with ffmpeg where ffmpeg is installed. Font text also needs the Python of your Manim (--ce-python or --gl-python names it), and rendering with Manim needs --allow-manim-render as well. The editor finds it by itself. Once formulas use it, the bar says Renderer: LaTeX (or mixed while some still use MathJax). If something is missing, ask the companion what this machine lacks and how to get it: python3 companion/mfe_companion.py health When you open the built editor from disk, the companion refuses the page unless you start it with --allow-file-origin. Or let the companion serve the editor itself, and open http://127.0.0.1:7799/app/: python3 companion/mfe_companion.py serve --allow-file-origin python3 companion/mfe_companion.py serve --app app/dist On Windows the command is python companion/mfe_companion.py serve. Where no Python is on the PATH, Windows answers that name with the Microsoft Store's alias, which runs nothing: use the launcher (py companion/mfe_companion.py serve) or the full name of an interpreter. MiKTeX brings latex and dvisvgm. There is no sandbox there, so use the companion with pages you trust. Font text works on Linux, with Pango 1.50 or later, Cairo, Fontconfig and ManimPango in the Python you give the companion. Elsewhere a font text is drawn as placeholder boxes: it can still be placed, coloured and animated, and it exports. Which options turn on which features, and what each costs, is in The companion."],["manim","The editor exports a Python scene for ManimCE (the community edition, 0.22) or ManimGL (3Blue1Brown's, 1.7). To render that file you need the matching Manim, installed the way its own documentation says: Installing ManimCE Installing ManimGL The first lines of every exported file give the command that renders it at the project's size and frame rate, for example manim -r 1920,1080 --fps 60 FirstTransform.py FirstTransform. The generated Python and ManimCE and ManimGL tell the rest."],["next","Your first animation takes you, step by step, from two formulas and a transformation between them to the export for Manim. A tour of the interface shows the menu bar and the four panels of the editor. The companion says how to start the optional helper, check it and use it safely."]],"short":"Install and run"},{"id":"start/first-animation","url":"start/first-animation.html","title":"Your first animation","section":"Get started","level":"basic","description":"Step by step: two formulas, one transformation between them, and the export to Manim.","headings":[["empty","Start from an empty project"],["formula","Write the first formula"],["timeline","Put it on the timeline"],["intro","Make it write itself in"],["two-formulas","A second formula, a second clip"],["transformation","Make one formula turn into the other"],["play","Play it"],["export","Export it for Manim"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"In about fifteen minutes you make a five-second animation: the equation x + 3 = 5 writes itself onto the screen, then turns into x = 5 − 3. You write two formulas, put them on the timeline, relate them, play the result and export it for Manim. Keep the editor open beside this page and do each step in it. Every step shows what the editor looks like when it is done, and you can download the project as it stands after each one, to check your work or to skip ahead. You need no Manim until the last step: the editor plays your animation on its own. Manim comes in at the end, to turn the Python you export into a video. If the editor is not running yet, install it and open it first."],["empty","The editor opens with an empty project. Three places matter for this animation. The Creator (1) will hold your formulas. The Preview (2) shows the scene at the playhead. The Timeline (3) has two empty tracks: a clip on a track says when a formula is on screen. The empty project. Press + New (1) to write a formula; Try an example (4) opens a finished one. If another project is open, choose File ▸ New: the editor asks first when there is work to keep. To see a finished animation first, press Try an example (4). It opens Completing the square, the quadratic formula derived step by step, to play, take apart and change. Help ▸ Examples has it and three short scenes. An example becomes a project of your own that has no file, so Save asks where it goes (The examples)."],["formula","You write a formula in LaTeX, in a window of its own, the formula editor. Press + New in the Creator. It makes a formula (its arrow lists the other kinds of object). The formula editor opens with the cursor in its visual math field. Type x + 3 = 5. The field draws the formula as you type, and the Raw LaTeX field under it shows the LaTeX it keeps: x+3=5, because the visual field leaves the spaces out. In Size, type 2 in the first box. The formula is drawn twice as large: size 1 is Manim's 48 pt, so the second box now reads 96. Press Apply (or Ctrl+Enter). The Glyphs column draws the formula and counts its glyphs. Press Close. The formula editor: the LaTeX typed (1), the size (2) and Apply (3). The formula is called f1 until you rename it. If the LaTeX has a mistake, the area under Apply says Cannot render and shows what is wrong. Fix the source and the message goes. Closing the editor with a change you did not apply asks whether to discard it. A glyph is one symbol of the formula as the editor draws it: x, +, 3, = and 5 are five glyphs. A transformation moves glyphs, so the word comes back below. The glyph view is also where you colour single glyphs (Colouring glyphs and objects), and Writing formulas covers the rest of the editor."],["timeline","A formula in the Creator is not in the scene yet. The scene is what the timeline holds. Drag the card of f1 from the Creator onto the top track of the timeline, Track 2 (tracks are numbered from the bottom), and drop it at the left end, at 0 s. Dragging the card onto the top track. The clip is three seconds long, and it starts where you drop it. The clip is selected, and the Animations panel on the right shows its settings. Drag the playhead along the ruler: the Preview shows the formula for the three seconds of the clip. You can do it without dragging: select the formula in the Creator and press Add to the timeline in the Animations panel. The clip goes on the top track, at the playhead. Compare with the project after this step. Open it with File ▸ Open…: press Other file… in the dialog and pick the downloaded file (Projects and files explains the dialog)."],["intro","By default a clip's formula appears at once, at the start of the clip. An intro is the animation a clip starts with instead. Click the clip to select it. In the Animations panel, open the Timing tab (1). In its Intro section, choose Write in the Animation field (2). The Timing tab (1) and the Animation field of the intro (2). Its Duration is one second. The first second of the clip turns hatched and says Write: that is the intro. Move the playhead to the start and press Space: the formula writes itself in, stroke by stroke. Press Space again to stop. The same choice is in the menu of the clip: right-click it and choose Intro ▸ Write. To give every new clip an intro, tick Edit ▸ Always add intro (Write). The menu of the clip, with Intro open. Compare with the project after this step."],["two-formulas","A transformation needs two formulas. Write the second formula as you wrote the first: + New, type x = 5 - 3, a size of 2, Apply, Close. The Creator calls it f2. Drag the card of f2 onto the track below, Track 1, and drop it at 2 s, one second before the first clip ends (2). The first clip (1) and the second (2). They overlap for one second, from 2 s to 3 s. Why a second track, and why the overlap? A relation links two clips that are on screen together for a moment: the first formula is still there when the second starts. Two clips of the same track cannot overlap, so the second clip goes on another track. The timeline snaps a dropped clip to its ticks, half a second apart: a drop within a quarter of a second of 2 s lands exactly on 2 s. Play it from the start: the first formula writes itself in, and the second appears at 2 s while the first is still there. They do not connect yet. Compare with the project after this step."],["transformation","You relate the first clip to the second, and then say which glyph goes where. Hold Shift. A round link port appears on each clip. Click the port of the first clip. A hint says Click the target clip. Click the second clip. The relation editor opens: the source, f1, is on top, and the target, f2, below it. Shift shows the ports. A click on the port of the first clip, then a click on the second clip, opens the relation editor. The ports are for the pointer. Without one, select the first clip, and in the Relations tab of the Animations panel choose the other clip in Relate with and press Create relation…. Press Suggest links by label. The editor proposes one link for each pair of glyphs that are the same symbol: x to x, = to =, 5 to 5 and 3 to 3. Press Accept all. Each link gets a number, and the line above the drawing says 4 linked pairs · 1 glyph leaves · 1 glyph arrives. A pair is one of these links: a glyph of the first formula that turns into a glyph of the second. The + has no partner, so it leaves; the − has none either, so it arrives. Before you save, switch from Link to Preview over the drawing: the editor plays the transformation in a loop, with the pairs as they stand. The links accepted. Suggest links by label (1), the line that counts the pairs (2) and the Link and Preview switch (3). The last thing to set is how the glyphs without a partner come and go. Open the Animation tab of the editor. Under Unpaired glyphs choose FadeOut as the Outro of f1 (1) and FadeIn as the Intro of f2 (2). Then press Save relation (3). The glyphs no pair takes leave with the outro of the first clip (1) and enter with the intro of the second (2). Without these two choices the + would stay until its clip ends and then disappear at once, and the − would appear at once, with nothing to soften either. The editor says that the outro and the intro belong to the clips: the timeline now shows FadeOut on f1 and FadeIn on f2. The saved relation: an arrow from the first clip to the second, and its window (1), the second in which the glyphs travel. The window is the stretch where the transformation runs. By default it is one second long. It starts where the playhead is, the yellow line of the timeline, when a window fits there; with the playhead at the start of the timeline, as here, none fits, and the window starts as soon as both clips allow: at the start of the second clip. Compare with the finished project. The page Transforming one formula into another explains relations in more detail."],["play","Move the playhead to the start with Home and press Space, or press the play button of the Preview. The playhead crosses the timeline and the Preview draws the scene at each moment. Click the ruler to jump to a time. Here are two moments of your animation. The same scene, played here by the player of these pages. Press its play button, or drag its time bar. Turn on Loop in the transport bar of the Preview to see the scene again and again. To watch the transformation alone, use the Preview switch of the relation editor."],["export","The editor can export the scene as a Python file that Manim runs. The scene and its file take the name of the project, so name it first: click the name in the menu bar (it reads Untitled), type First animation and press Enter. Click the name in the menu bar (1): it becomes a field, with the name selected. Type the new name over it. Open the arrow of the Export… button, beside the menus, and choose Python — ManimCE… (1). Pressing the button itself opens the same dialog. The arrow of the Export… button lists the exports. The dialog shows the Python it generates. The Scene name is the name of the Python class and of the file. The list under the options says what the scene cannot guarantee: here, that the glyphs are found by position when the scene runs, because the editor typesets with MathJax and Manim with LaTeX. The scene still plays as the Preview does (The generated Python explains why). Press Download FirstAnimation.py, or Copy to put the code on the clipboard. Exporting shows the dialog and its options. This is the file the editor makes for your project, in the two versions. Export the one for the Manim you have installed; if you have none yet, ManimCE is the usual choice. To make the video you need Manim, installed as its own documentation says (the Manim Community edition, ManimCE, is the usual choice). Put the file in a folder and run, from that folder, the command that the first lines of the file give: manim -r 1920,1080 --fps 60 FirstAnimation.py FirstAnimation That renders the whole animation at 1920×1080 and 60 frames a second, which takes a little while. For a quick look, run manim -ql -p FirstAnimation.py FirstAnimation instead: -ql renders at 854×480 and 15 frames a second, and -p plays the video when it is done. Leave out -r and --fps: they would override -ql and bring the size and the frame rate back to 1920×1080 at 60. This is what ManimCE makes of your scene at -ql: The finished project, with the Python and this video, is here to download: Exporting covers every export, and The generated Python explains what is in the file."],["next","You wrote two formulas, put them on the timeline, gave the first an intro, related the two clips, played the result and exported it. Every part has a page of its own. Writing formulas, Working with the timeline and Appearing and disappearing go deeper into the steps you took. How relations work says what a pair can be: one glyph to many, many to one, and the styles of a transformation. Solving a linear equation is a whole animation of five formulas and four transformations, built step by step. A tour of the interface names every part of the window."]],"short":"First animation"},{"id":"start/interface","url":"start/interface.html","title":"A tour of the interface","section":"Get started","level":"basic","description":"The menu bar and the four panels of the editor, how to fold and resize them, the narrow layout, and the themes.","headings":[["window","The window at a glance"],["menu-bar","The menu bar"],["creator","Creator"],["preview","Preview"],["animations","Animations"],["timeline","Timeline"],["panels","Folding and resizing panels"],["zen","Zen mode"],["narrow","Narrow windows"],["themes","Themes"],["contrast","High contrast"],["keyboard","Using the keyboard"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"The editor is one window: a menu bar over four panels. This page goes through them from top to bottom, then shows how to fold and resize the panels, how the window behaves when it is narrow, and how to change its theme."],["window","The pictures on this page show one project: the quadratic equation solved by completing the square. Its scene plays here, with the strip of its timeline under it: Download it and open it in the editor to follow along: choose File ▸ Open… and press Other file… in the dialog (Projects and files explains it). Any other project has the same parts."],["menu-bar","The bar runs along the top of the window. Its left half holds the menus and the export button. The five menus (1) and the Export… button (2). The five menus. The table below says what each holds; Every menu lists every entry. Export… opens the Python export for the target you used last (ManimCE the first time). Its arrow lists every export: Python for ManimCE, Python for ManimGL, Video…, Image…, Audio… and HTML…. Exporting says what each gives. Menu What it holds File New, Open…, Sync folder…, Recover autosave, Save, Save As… and Project settings…. Projects and files Edit Undo and Redo, Delete formula, copying the properties of a clip, Align and Distribute, Apply palette…, Match colors, Colours in order, the defaults for the intros and outros of new clips, and Suggest morph pairs, which gives a new Morph block pairs to start from. View The theme, which panels are shown, and Glyph source: which renderer draws the formulas. Preferences Snapping and its guides, the colour of the glyphs you pick with Alt, the transform handles, how faded the formulas locked in the Preview are drawn, the grid, the standard scale of new formulas, Chroma Lab and Editor palettes. Help Keyboard shortcuts…, Examples (the example projects the editor carries, Projects and files), Engine inspector…, Third-party licenses… and About. The right half of the bar is about the project you have open. The project's name (1), its file (2) and its mark (3), the renderer (4) and the theme switch (5). The project's name. Click it to rename the project in place: type, then press Enter, or Esc to leave it as it was. A name too long for the bar is cut short; point at it to read the whole name and where it is saved. The file the project was opened from or saved to. It appears once the project has one. The mark: Not saved yet, Saved, Unsaved changes or Recovered, not saved. Projects and files says when each shows. The renderer that draws the formulas: MathJax in the browser, LaTeX when a companion supplies exact glyphs, mixed while both are in use. Point at it for the counts and for the state of the companion. The theme switch: dark or light. Themes has the rest."],["creator","The Creator, on the left, keeps what you can put in the scene. It has three tabs: Formulas (your formulas and texts), Templates (a library of ready-made formulas) and Audio (your recordings). The Creator with the Formulas tab open. The tabs: Formulas, Templates and Audio. The search (the magnifier, or Ctrl+F) and the key of the view, which switches the list between the list view, a row per formula with its rendering, and the block view, a grid of squares. It shows the icon of the view it switches to. The ∑ key: point at it, or click it, and its menu selects every formula, every LaTeX text or every font text of the project (see Select by kind). A row is greyed while the project has none of its kind. The +/− key makes the previews of the view you are in larger with a click and smaller with a right-click, up to three times: the rendering of each row in the list view, the tooltip beside a block in the block view (see Larger previews). + New writes a new formula. Its arrow lists the other kinds of object. A row: the formula's coloured tag, its name (double-click it, or press F2, to rename), a badge for its kind (∑ formula, T LaTeX text, P font text), a badge for how it was drawn (MathJax, or exact for LaTeX through the companion), how many clips use it, and the buttons to edit, duplicate and delete it. The drawing under the row shows the formula itself. Double-click a row to edit its formula, and drag it onto a track of the timeline to put it in the scene. The Creator starts as a fifth of the window, which in a small window can cut long names and the renderer badge: drag the gap beside it to widen it, as the picture above shows. The arrow of + New offers three kinds of object: The menu of the + New arrow. Braces, shapes, lines and arrows are not written in the Creator: braces and shapes are added from a clip, and lines and arrows from a clip or from a right-click on empty space in the Preview, as the pages on braces, shapes and lines and arrows show. For writing formulas and texts, see Writing formulas and LaTeX texts and font texts."],["preview","The Preview, in the middle, draws the scene at the playhead, the way the video will show it. The rectangle in the project's background colour is the frame of the scene: what the video will show. Click a formula to select its clip, drag it to move it. A hint at the bottom of the frame says what the pointer can do at that moment, for example that holding Shift shows the transform handles. On its title bar, at the right, are the tools of the view: The tools of the Preview's title bar. Align by symbol (Alt+G): click a symbol to move, then the symbol it should line up with. Align by symbol Circle selector (S): the pointer becomes a circle that picks the nearest symbol, so that thin symbols such as a minus sign can be picked. Keep the transform handles shown: the handles that scale and rotate the selected clip show only while you hold Shift, unless you press this. Snap to objects: a dragged formula snaps to the edges and centres of the others and of the frame. Hold Ctrl to suspend it. Multicamera (Alt+M): gives the scene a camera that can move and zoom while the video plays. The camera The zoom: Zoom out, the percentage, Zoom in, and Fit, which shows the whole frame again. Zoom with Ctrl and the wheel, and pan with the middle button. Under the Preview is the playback bar: The playback bar. The playback keys: Go to start (Home), Previous frame (←), Play and Pause (Space), Next frame (→) and Go to end (End). With Shift the arrows step a second. Stop, which also returns to the start, and Loop. The grid: show it, make it sparser or denser, and choose whether it holds the formula's centre or its nearest edges while you drag. Placing things in the Preview The time of the playhead, three ways: in seconds, as minutes, seconds and frames, and as the frame number out of the total."],["animations","The Animations panel, on the right, edits whatever you have selected. With nothing selected it says Select a clip on the timeline to edit its animations. Select a clip, and it shows that clip: The Relations tab. The Timing tab. The header names the clip and where it is, and has Delete clip. Relations shows how the glyphs of this clip are linked to the glyphs of other clips, and lets you add, edit and remove relations. How relations work Timing holds the clip's track, start, end and length; its intro; its initial state (position, scale, colour); its animation blocks, with Add animation; and its outro. Appearing and disappearing, animation blocks The panel follows the selection: it shows an animation block's own settings when you select a block, the clips of a formula you select in the Creator, and the properties of an audio clip when you select one."],["timeline","The Timeline, along the bottom, lays the scene out in time. Its toolbar is on top, then the ruler, then one row for each track: a header on the left and, beside it, the lane that holds the clips. The toolbar of the Timeline. + Track adds a track on top of the others, in front of them. Audio adds an audio track, for recordings. Snap makes clips snap to the playhead, to the edges of other clips and to the time grid. Hold Shift to suspend it. The zoom keys, Zoom out and Zoom in. The slider moves the view along the timeline. Its thumb fills the bar while the whole timeline is in view, and gets shorter as you zoom in. The Show the whole timeline key, the zoom in pixels per second, and dt: the time between two small ticks of the ruler, which is also the step clips snap to. The − and + on either side of it set a dt of your own, until the zoom changes. A time step of your own The key between Snap and the zoom keys turns the Slides mode on and off: the ruler then shows where the scene animates and where it rests, with its pauses and comments. Slides and the HTML page Click or drag along the ruler to move the playhead. Each track has a header: The corner of the timeline and the header of the first track, made taller with its own keys so that the numbers fit. The keys in the corner make every track lower or taller; the percentage between them puts every track back to the standard height. The track's name. Double-click it, or press Enter or F2, to rename the track. The height keys of this track alone, Lower and Taller. The eye hides the track in the Preview; a hidden track is still exported. On an audio track the key mutes it instead. The padlock locks the track: its clips cannot be edited. The arrows move the track towards the front or towards the back. The track at the top is drawn in front of the others. The cross deletes the track. The editor asks first, and lists what goes with it. A clip is a block in a lane: its intro at the left, its outro at the right, and its animation blocks inside it as small coloured labels. Working with the timeline goes through all of it."],["panels","The gaps between the panels are handles: drag one to give a panel more room or less. The clip drags the gap between the Creator and the Preview, and then the edge of the Timeline, out and back. Dragging the gap between the Creator and the Preview, and the edge of the Timeline. The Creator, the Animations panel and the Timeline also fold to their title bars, so the Preview can have the room. Press the arrow in a panel's header, or double-click the header; press it again to bring the panel back. A panel keeps what it was showing while it is folded. The Creator (1) and the Timeline (2) folded. The arrow in each bar brings its panel back. View ▸ Panels lists the three panels that fold, Creator, Animations and Timeline, ticked while they are open; choosing one folds it or brings it back. Outside a text field, the key C does it for the Creator and A for the Animations panel. The sizes of the panels, and which ones are folded, are kept in this browser: the next time you open the editor, it looks as you left it. Dragging a gap past the smallest size a panel can have folds that panel."],["zen","On a small screen the four panels leave the Preview little room. Preferences ▸ Zen mode arranges the same window another way, designed for a screen of 1366 by 768 pixels: the Timeline becomes a column down the left side, and the Preview takes everything else. Nothing is taken away: the menus, the title bars of the panels and the transport are the ones you know. Zen mode at 1366×768: the Timeline as a column (1), the strip with the two panels and the tools (2), and the Preview (3). The Timeline is the same timeline, turned: time runs down the screen, and the tracks stand side by side. It is the usual timeline turned a quarter: the ruler runs down the right edge of the column, beside the Preview, so the pointer has a short way to it, and what is under the ruler in the usual layout is beside it here. The first track is the one next to the ruler, and the others follow to the left. The track headers are at the top of the columns, and the scrollbar of time is on the other side, down the left edge. Everything you do there works along the other direction of the screen: Drag a clip down to move it later, and sideways to put it on another track. Its start and its end are its top and its bottom edges. The wheel moves along time; Shift with the wheel moves across the tracks, and Ctrl with the wheel zooms about the time under the pointer. The arrow keys follow the screen: ↑ and ↓ go along time, ← and → across the tracks, → towards the ruler; Alt with ↑ or ↓ nudges a clip by a frame. The names of clips and tracks are written across their track, and cut with “…” where they do not fit. The name of an animation, on a block or on the intro or the outro of a clip, runs down what it names under its icon, and reads from top to bottom. The tracks start wider than in the usual layout; the two keys in a track's header, or its side edges, make it wider or narrower, and each layout keeps the sizes you give it. The Creator and the Animations panel are behind the two buttons at the start of the strip. Each button opens and closes its own panel, as a half of a drawer over the Preview: the Creator on the left, the Animations panel on the right. Open both and they stand side by side; open one and you still see the Preview through the other half. A panel stays open while you work on the timeline. A press on the Preview's scene closes the drawer, and does what a press there always does; so does Esc, though in a field you are typing in Esc belongs to the field. From the keyboard, C opens and closes the Creator and A the Animations panel. The Creator and the Animations panel, side by side over the Preview (1). The tools of the strip (2), the grip that makes the drawer taller or shorter (3), and the arrow that moves the strip to the bottom of the Preview (4). Drag the grip on the free edge of a panel to make the drawer taller or shorter, and the line where the two panels meet to give one of them more room; with either focused, the arrow keys do it in steps. The arrow at the far end of the strip moves the strip to the bottom of the Preview, and the drawer then rises from there; press it again to bring the strip back to the top. The strip also keeps at hand what you would open the Creator for: the magnifier that searches the formulas, + New with the arrow that lists every kind of object, My templates and Favorites, and then Undo and Redo. In Zen mode View ▸ Panels opens and closes the half of Creator and the half of Animations, and folds the Timeline to a strip at the left. The browser remembers the mode, the side of the strip, which panels were open, how tall the drawer was and where its halves met. A window narrower than about 760 pixels keeps the stacked layout below, with Zen mode on or off. In any layout, hold T and move the mouse: the timeline's view moves along time with it, wherever the pointer is, as if you were dragging its scrollbar. Let go of the key and the view stays there. Hold Shift+T and the playhead follows the pointer instead, to the time the pointer is level with; let go and it stays there. While you hold the key, the Preview outlines and selects nothing of what the pointer passes over."],["narrow","Under about 760 pixels wide, on a phone or on a desktop zoomed to 400 %, four panels side by side would not fit. The window stacks them instead: the Creator, the Preview, the Animations panel and the Timeline, one under the other, each as tall as most of the window. The menu bar stays at the top, wrapped over as many lines as it needs, and the stack scrolls up and down under it, never sideways. The resizable layout comes back as soon as the window is wide enough. While the panels are stacked there is nothing to fold, and the three entries of View ▸ Panels are greyed out. A window 420 pixels wide: the menu bar wrapped over several lines, and the four panels one under the other. The clip scrolls down through them and back up."],["themes","The editor has a dark theme and a light one, both in the same Mint style. It starts in the dark one. The half-filled circle at the right end of the menu bar, beside the renderer, flips between them: the whole window changes at once. View ▸ Theme. View ▸ Theme chooses between System, Dark and Light. System follows your operating system's setting, and follows it when it changes. The switch in the bar, and View ▸ Toggle theme, flip between dark and light, and from then on the choice is yours rather than the system's. The editor remembers it in this browser. This documentation has a theme switch of its own in its header, and shows its pictures of the editor in the theme you are reading in."],["contrast","When the system asks for forced colours, as Windows high contrast does, the browser replaces the colours of the page with the system's. The editor stays readable: every state, such as selected, pressed or focused, keeps a ring, a border or an underline instead of relying on a colour, and the formulas in the Preview and in the Creator keep their own colours. The top left of the editor under forced colours."],["keyboard","The menu bar works from the keyboard. Tab moves from one menu to the next, File first; ↓ opens the focused menu on its first entry (↑, on its last), and ← and → move to the previous or next menu, opening it if one is open. Home and End go to the first and last, a letter goes to the next menu that starts with it, and Esc closes the menu. Inside an open menu, ↓ and ↑ move between the entries, → on an entry that has a submenu opens it, and Enter chooses. Reaching the File menu with Tab, opening it with ↓, then moving along the bar with →. Point at almost any button and the editor names it and gives its key. Press ? anywhere outside a text field for the full list, or choose Help ▸ Keyboard shortcuts…. Keyboard and mouse shortcuts has the table, searchable. Click the project's name in the bar, type a new one, and press Enter. The mark next to it turns to Unsaved changes. Press the arrow in the Timeline's header. The timeline folds to its title bar. Open View ▸ Panels ▸ Timeline to bring it back. Press the half-filled circle in the bar to try the other theme, and press it again."],["next","Projects and files covers new, open and save, autosave and recovery, and the project library. Writing formulas begins the basics: the formula editor, its LaTeX fields and the Creator list. Every menu lists every entry of every menu, with its keys."]],"short":"The interface"},{"id":"start/projects","url":"start/projects.html","title":"Projects and files","section":"Get started","level":"basic","description":"New, open and save; the examples the editor carries; the file name and the unsaved mark; autosave and recovery; the project library and its Sync folder; .mfe packages.","headings":[["files","What a project is saved as"],["file-menu","The File menu"],["new-open-save","New, open and save"],["new","New"],["open","Open"],["examples","The examples"],["save","Save and Save As"],["settings","Project settings"],["status","The file name and the unsaved mark"],["autosave","Autosave and recovery"],["library","The project library and the Sync folder"],["favorites","Favorites"],["folder-browsers","The folder in each browser"],["packages","Packages (.mfe)"],["not-in-the-file","What the file does not hold"],["no-file-access","Browsers without file access"],["one-tab","One tab at a time"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A project is everything you have made in the editor: the formulas, the timeline, the relations between glyphs, the recordings and the settings. It lives in the browser while you work, and in a file when you save it. This page is about the file, and about the copies the editor keeps so you do not lose work."],["files","A project is one file. There are two kinds, and the editor picks the one a project needs. File What it is When you get it name.mfe.json The project as text (JSON). It also carries the shapes of any font texts, so they draw without a companion. Almost always. name.mfe A package: the project together with the original of every recording. When the project has recordings, or more font-text shapes than a .mfe.json can carry. Both open with File ▸ Open…: from a card of the Projects dialog, or with its Other file… button (Open, below). A file saved by an older version of the editor opens too: the editor upgrades it in memory and says so, Upgraded from format 27 for example, and the next Save writes the current format. Project files and their format describes what is inside."],["file-menu","The File menu. Entry Keys What it does New Ctrl+Alt+N Starts an empty project. Open… Ctrl+O Opens the Projects dialog, which lists the projects of your folder. Sync folder… Ctrl+Shift+S Chooses the folder whose projects the dialog lists. Recover autosave Lists the work of tabs that were closed. Save Ctrl+S Writes the project to its file. Save As… Ctrl+Alt+S Writes the project to a file you choose. Project settings… Name, Manim target, size, frame rate, background, duration and LaTeX preamble."],["new-open-save",""],["new","File ▸ New replaces the project with an empty one. If the project you have open has changes that were not saved to a file, the editor asks first: The question before a project is replaced. Cancel keeps working. Don't save drops the changes. Save saves, and goes on only if the save went through: when you cancel the picker, the project stays. Open, and Recover autosave, ask the same question."],["open","File ▸ Open… does not open a file picker. It opens the Projects dialog, which lists the projects of a folder you chose once. The project library, below, says how it works. A click on a project opens it. To open a file that is not in that folder, press Other file… in the dialog: the browser's own file picker opens, and offers .mfe.json and .mfe files. The picker is a window of your operating system, so no picture of it is shown here. The editor reads and checks a file before it replaces anything. A file it cannot read is reported in a notice (Could not open …, with the reason), and the project you were working on stays as it was. A file that opens with errors or warnings says how many (Opened with 2 issues) and lists them."],["examples","The editor carries four example projects, to play, take apart and change. While a project has no formula, the Creator offers Try an example beside the way to your own projects: it opens Completing the square, the quadratic formula derived step by step. Help ▸ Examples has it too, with three short scenes, each for one thing: Hello: one-to-one (two formulas and the relation between them), Camera (the scene's camera and its moves) and Braces. An example opens as a file does: when the project you have open has unsaved changes, the editor asks first, as above. The example then becomes a project of yours that has no file yet: the bar says Not saved yet, and Save asks where it goes. The examples are part of the editor, so they open with no synced folder, no server and no companion."],["save","File ▸ Save writes the project to its file. The first time, or for a project that has no file yet, a picker asks where, and suggests a name made from the project's name: First transform becomes first-transform.mfe.json. After that, Save writes the same file without asking. File ▸ Save As… always asks, and starts in your synced folder when you have one. The new file becomes the project's file. A save writes the project as it was the moment you pressed it. If you go on working while it finishes, what you change meanwhile is not in the file, and the bar says Unsaved changes afterwards. That is what Chrome and Edge do. Firefox and Safari cannot write to a file you opened: Save downloads a new file each time. The Browsers table in the installation page says what else differs."],["settings","File ▸ Project settings… holds what describes the scene as a whole. The Project settings dialog. Field What it sets Name The project's name. The exported scene is named after it. Manim target ManimCE (Community Edition) or ManimGL (3b1b): the Manim the scene is made for. ManimCE and ManimGL says how they differ. Resolution 1920 × 1080, 1280 × 720 or 3840 × 2160, or Custom… from 16 × 16 to 7680 × 4320 pixels. Frame rate 24, 25, 30 or 60 frames a second, or Custom… from 1 to 240. Background The colour behind the scene. A new project takes the background of your active palette, when you have one; otherwise black. Duration Until the last clip ends, or Fixed: a number of seconds. LaTeX preamble LaTeX added after the packages of every formula, such as \\newcommand{\\R}{\\mathbb{R}}. MathJax understands \\newcommand and \\DeclareMathOperator; the companion compiles the whole preamble. Apply makes all the changes one undo step. Download first-transform.mfe.json, the project of the card below. Choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… and pick the file. Click the project's name in the menu bar, type Class notes and press Enter. Press Ctrl+S. In Chrome or Edge the file you opened is written (the browser may ask first whether the editor may change it); in Firefox or Safari a new file is downloaded."],["status","The right half of the menu bar always says where the project stands: its name, its file, and a mark. Not saved yet. A new project that has never been saved. It has no file name. Saved. The project is in its file and has not changed since. Unsaved changes. The project changed after it was saved. The mark is amber, and the title of the browser tab starts with a bullet, •, so you can see it from another tab. Recovered, not saved. Unsaved work that came back from the autosave. Save it to keep it. Undoing back to the saved state brings Saved back: the mark follows the project, not the number of edits. A name too long for the bar is cut short; point at it for the whole name and the file. Click the name to rename the project. Type, and press Enter to keep the new name or Esc to leave it; either key leaves the focus on the name, so the keyboard goes on from there. Renaming is one undo step, like any other change, and the name is also a field of Project settings…. While you type, the name is a field, and the mark still says Saved. Press Enter and it turns to Unsaved changes; press Ctrl+S and it goes back to Saved."],["autosave","While you work, the editor keeps a copy of the project in the browser, a moment after each change. If you reload the tab, or the tab crashes, the work comes back. It is a safety net, not a file: save to a file to keep a project, because clearing the browser's data for the editor removes the copy. Each tab has an autosave of its own, so two tabs never overwrite each other's work. When a tab is closed, its work is not lost. Open the editor in a new tab and it takes up the newest work of the tabs that were closed; the older work waits in the menu described next. If a tab crashed instead of being closed, the editor cannot tell that it is gone for a minute. A tab you open after that takes its work up; one opened sooner does not, and the work waits in the menu described next. The work of the other tabs that were closed stays available for a week, up to five of them, under File ▸ Recover autosave: File ▸ Recover autosave with the work of two closed tabs. Each entry gives the file name, or the project's name when it has no file; (unsaved changes) when it had changes not yet saved; and how long ago it was kept. Choose one to bring it back into this tab, after the usual question about the project you have open. The bar then says Recovered, not saved when the entry had unsaved changes. An entry without that mark was saved: it comes back as Saved, with its file name, or as Not saved yet when it had none. When the browser itself closed unexpectedly and restores its tabs, each tab gets its work back within a minute: by itself if you have not used the tab yet, and otherwise through a notice, Work from this tab's last session is available, with a Recover button. If the browser does not let the editor keep a copy, because its storage is blocked or full, a notice says so once: No autosave in this browser. Nothing is then kept for you, so save to a file as you go."],["library","The Projects dialog is the editor's way to open a project. It lists the projects of one folder, with a drawing of the first formulas of each. You choose the folder once with File ▸ Sync folder… (also a button of the dialog, and of the Creator while a project is empty). The browser's own folder picker opens; like the file picker, it is a window of your operating system. The Projects dialog before a folder is synced. Once a folder is synced, its projects are cards, newest first: The Projects dialog over a folder of six projects. The card of the project that is open is tinted. The folder, and Sync folder… to choose another. The search. It keeps the projects whose name or file name has every word you type, ignoring capitals and accents. The field has the focus when the dialog opens. Esc empties it; a second Esc closes the dialog. All and Favorites, each with its count. A card: the first three formulas, the project's name, its file name (.mfe.json is shown as .json), how many formulas it has, and when it was last changed. The star marks it as a favourite. Other file…, for a file that is not in the folder. Typing sq keeps one project; Esc brings them all back. A star marks a favourite, and Favorites lists the starred ones. The dialog fits as many columns of cards as it has room for. Drag its bottom-right corner to resize it, or press the square in its title bar to make it fill the window; a double-click on the corner gives it back its first size. It opens at the size you left it until you reload the page. The folder is read one level deep: projects in its sub-folders are not listed, and a .json file that is not a project is counted at the foot of the dialog (One other .json file in the folder is not a project) and left alone. A file of the folder that is a project and does not open, one saved by a newer version of the editor, one with a field out of place, a .mfe that is damaged, a .mfe.json whose text was cut short, is not counted there: it is named under the cards (One project of the folder does not open:) with its reason, the one opening that file gives."],["favorites","The star of a card keeps that project in Favorites. The stars are kept in this browser, by the names of the folder and of the file; nothing is written in your folder. A file you rename loses its star, and another browser does not see yours."],["folder-browsers","What the dialog can do with the folder depends on the browser. Chrome and Edge keep the folder and read it again every time the dialog opens, after the browser's permission question once per session. When the browser has forgotten its permission, the dialog says The browser did not allow reading the folder and offers Allow; when the folder was moved or deleted, it says so. A project opened from the folder carries its file, so Save writes it in place, and Save As… starts in that folder. Firefox and Safari keep a snapshot of the folder as it was when you synced it, and the dialog says when (Manim projects, as of Nov 12, 2026, 6:32 PM. Sync the folder again to refresh.). Nothing is written into the folder: Save downloads a file. In Chrome and Edge the folder also holds your own templates, in templates/templates.json, once you let the editor write there. Make a new folder and copy three or four files of the repository's examples/ folder into it. Work in the copies: Save writes the files of a synced folder. Choose File ▸ Sync folder… and pick the new folder. Choose File ▸ Open…. The projects of the folder are listed, each with a drawing of its first formulas. Type a word of a project's name in the search field, then press Esc to empty it. Star a project, press Favorites, then click the project to open it."],["packages","A project that has recordings cannot be one text file: the sound has to travel with it. The editor saves such a project as a package, a .mfe file that holds the project and the original of every recording, including the ones you muted or never used. The editor chooses by itself: Save suggests a .mfe name when the project has recordings, and a .mfe.json name when it has none. Opening a package checks every recording and copies it into the browser's storage. With a large package a dialog shows how far it is, and Cancel leaves the project you have open as it was. The package is also the portable backup of a project with sound: the browser's own storage can be cleared or evicted, and the file cannot. If the browser no longer holds the original of a recording, perhaps because you cleared its data, Save first looks for it in the file it is about to replace. If it is not there either, Save tells you which recordings it cannot include and asks before it writes a file without them. Audio has the rest."],["not-in-the-file","The file holds the project, and nothing about how you like to work. These do not travel with it: your preferences: snapping and its guides, the grid, the transform handles, the scale step, and the default intros and outros of new clips; your palettes (Palettes and Chroma Lab): a project keeps the colours it uses as plain colours, so it looks the same on a computer that has none of your palettes; your templates, which Chrome and Edge also keep in the synced folder; the theme, the sizes of the panels and which of them are folded; the zoom of the timeline and the height of its tracks, which are a view of the timeline and last as long as the tab. Open a project on another computer and it plays the same; the editor around it is that computer's."],["no-file-access","A browser without the File System Access API works on copies. Opening a file (File ▸ Open…, then Other file…) reads a copy of it; Save downloads a file named from the project each time, and the bar says Saved once the download has started even though the editor cannot write that file again; Sync folder… takes a snapshot. The editor tells you once per session, as it opens: The notice of a browser that cannot write your files. To carry on from a file you downloaded, open it again with File ▸ Open… and Other file…. Browsers compares the two kinds of browser side by side."],["one-tab","Use one tab of the editor at a time. Every tab keeps an autosave of its own and never overwrites another's, but two open tabs do not see each other's changes, and nothing merges them. A window opened or duplicated from another tab keeps an autosave of its own too. If you need the same project in two places, save it to a file and open the file."],["next","Writing formulas begins the basics: the formula editor, its LaTeX fields and the Creator list. Project files and their format says what a project stores, and how older files open. The template library starts a formula from one of 257 ready-made ones."]],"short":"Projects and files"},{"id":"basics/formulas","url":"basics/formulas.html","title":"Writing formulas","section":"Basics","level":"basic","description":"Type a formula in the formula editor: LaTeX fields, the on-screen keyboard, errors, packages, size, colour, name and tag, the Creator list, and why glyph order matters.","headings":[["create","Create a formula"],["formula-editor","The formula editor"],["typing","Typing LaTeX"],["keyboard","The on-screen keyboard"],["preview","The status line and the errors"],["examples","Formulas to try"],["try-plain","Plain"],["try-fractions","Fractions"],["try-roots","Roots, powers and symbols"],["try-sums","Sums and limits"],["try-integrals","Integrals"],["try-rows","Rows"],["packages","LaTeX packages"],["size","Size"],["colour","Colour"],["names-tags","Names and tags"],["creator-list","The Creator list"],["list-blocks","List and blocks"],["preview-size","Larger previews"],["rename-duplicate-delete","Rename, duplicate and delete"],["glyph-order","Why the order of glyphs matters"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A formula is LaTeX typed into the formula editor. This page follows one from its first keystroke to its card in the Creator: how to type it, what the editor says when it fails, its packages, size, colour and name, and why the order in which the app numbers its glyphs matters once you start to animate."],["create","The Creator, on the left, keeps every formula of the project. Press + New to write one. The formula editor opens with the cursor in its LaTeX field, and the formula has a name of its own already (f1, then f2…) that you can change whenever you like. A project with no formulas says what to press first, and offers an example to open (The examples). The arrow beside the button lists every kind of object the Creator makes: Formula, Text (LaTeX) and Text (font). A formula is maths; the two texts have an editor of their own, described in LaTeX texts and font texts. + New writes a formula. Its arrow lists the other kinds. A formula in the Creator is not on the screen of your scene yet. It is an object that clips show: drag it onto the timeline, or choose Add to the timeline in its menu (see Working with the timeline)."],["formula-editor","Double-click a formula in the Creator, or press Enter on it, to open the editor again. Go through its parts one by one: The fields of the header act on the formula at once, each change as one undo step. The LaTeX and the packages wait for Apply, so you can type for as long as you like and still see the formula as it was. Outside a text field, Ctrl+Z undoes the header's changes and the applied LaTeX while the editor is open. Drag the bar between the two columns to give the glyphs more or less room, or the corner of the dialog to resize it."],["typing","Typing in Raw LaTeX: the visual field above it typesets the formula as you go, the line beside Apply counts its glyphs, and Apply draws them in the column on the right. There are two fields for the same formula, and they stay in step: The LaTeX field is a visual math editor (MathLive). It typesets what you write, and the ⌨ Keyboard key beside its title shows keys that write in it (see The on-screen keyboard). The Raw LaTeX field is the text itself. What you type there is kept exactly as you typed it: the visual field may write the same formula another way, and never rewrites yours. A formula with several rows uses & to line the rows up and \\\\ to end a row, as Manim's align* reads them. The visual field shows such a formula inside an aligned block, and the raw field keeps what you typed. When you are ready, press Apply or Ctrl+Enter. The LaTeX and the packages go into the project as one undo step, and the glyph column draws the formula. Until then the footer says Unapplied changes, Revert brings the applied formula back, and closing the dialog asks before it throws your changes away. A new formula that is still empty says Empty: nothing is drawn. beside Apply. Nothing breaks if you close it: the formula stays in the Creator, empty, until you write something."],["keyboard","If you would rather press a fraction than type \\frac, press ⌨ Keyboard, beside the LaTeX title (1). A keypad opens in the editor, under the visual field. Its four tabs (2) are Algebra, Trigonometry, Calculus and Alphabet, which has the digits, the letters and two rows of Greek ones. A key writes a digit, an operator, a function, or a structure with its holes: a fraction, a root, a power, an absolute value, a limit, an integral, a sum. A hole you have not filled yet is an empty group in the raw LaTeX (\\frac{}{}), and the formula still renders. The last row (3) moves the caret, deletes backward, clears the field (AC) and applies (Apply, the same as the editor's button). ⌨ Keyboard (1) shows the keypad: its tabs (2) and its last row (3). The fraction key, 1, → and 2 wrote the formula in the visual field, and the raw LaTeX followed. A press leaves the cursor in the visual field, so what you type next goes on in the formula. A keypad left shown is there again the next time the editor opens, until its key hides it."],["preview","About a fifth of a second after you stop typing, the editor renders your draft, before you apply it, and reports the result beside Apply: The line says It means Rendering… The draft is being typeset. 16 glyphs · MathJax It rendered: the number of glyphs, and which renderer drew them. See Why the order of glyphs matters. 16 glyphs · exact (LaTeX) The same, typeset by real LaTeX through the companion. Cannot render The LaTeX has a problem. A panel under the line says what. The panel names the problem in plain words, and, when it can tell, what to do about it. A brace left open: A brace that is never closed. A command from a package you have not added names the package: The panel names the package that has the command, and the Common: row (1) offers it. One click on cancel adds the chip (1), and the formula renders. With the companion running, the panel adds LaTeX's own log under the message. Warnings, such as a package MathJax cannot load, are listed under the line in the same place."],["examples","Six formulas, from a plain equation to two aligned rows. Type each one into the Raw LaTeX field of a new formula, and look at what the status line says. The project with all six is at the end of this section if you would rather open it."],["try-plain","x + 3 = 5"],["try-fractions","\\frac{numerator}{denominator}: each braced group is one piece. Spaces in the source are for you; LaTeX sets the spacing of a formula itself. \\frac{a}{b} + \\frac{c}{d} = \\frac{ad + bc}{bd}"],["try-roots","\\sqrt{…} draws a root, ^ raises, and \\pm is one of many named symbols (\\pi, \\alpha, \\infty…). x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}"],["try-sums","_ lowers and ^ raises. Braces make a longer index one piece: _{k=1}. \\sum_{k=1}^{n} k = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}"],["try-integrals","An integral, with a thin space before the dx. \\, is a thin space. It reads better before the dx. \\int_0^1 x^2 \\, dx = \\frac{1}{3}"],["try-rows","One & before the sign each row is lined up on, and \\\\ between rows. (a + b)^2 &= a^2 + 2ab + b^2 \\\\ (a - b)^2 &= a^2 - 2ab + b^2 Press + New and click Raw LaTeX. Type the fractions: \\frac{a}{b} + \\frac{c}{d}. Watch the line beside Apply count the glyphs. Break it on purpose: delete the last }, and read what the editor says. Put it back. Press Apply, then name the formula sum_of_fractions and give it a tag icon. Drag it onto the timeline, and press play."],["packages","Some commands are not plain LaTeX: \\cancel belongs to the cancel package, \\dv to physics, \\bm to bm. A formula lists the packages it needs under Packages, one chip each. Chips with a ×, the search, its results, the Common: row and, under them, notes about what the preview cannot do. Press a name in the Common: row to add it. Type in Search packages, or type any name… to search a catalogue of more than eighty packages by name or by what they do. The results that carry a preview tag are ones the visual field also understands. A package that is not in the catalogue works too: type its name (letters, digits, - and _) and press Add or Enter. The × on a chip removes it. Manim's own template already loads amsmath and amssymb, so you never add those. Packages count when you press Apply, like the LaTeX. In the browser the preview is MathJax, which knows some packages (physics, mathtools, cancel, braket, mhchem and a few more) and approximates a few others (bm, siunitx, esvect). For any other the chip turns amber, and the note under it says that the preview ignores the package. The exported scene still asks for it, and the companion compiles it with real LaTeX. What every formula of the project needs, such as a \\newcommand, goes in the project's LaTeX preamble instead: File ▸ Project settings…, field LaTeX preamble. It is added after the packages of each formula. cancel: crossed-out terms. \\frac{\\cancel{x}\\,y}{\\cancel{x}\\,z} = \\frac{y}{z} physics: \\dv{f}{x} is the derivative d f / d x. \\dv{f}{x} = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{f(x + h) - f(x)}{h} bm: bold symbols. \\bm{F} = m\\,\\bm{a} When you export Python, each formula's packages become a TexTemplate (ManimCE) or an additional_preamble string (ManimGL) in the scene, and the formula is built with it. These are the lines the editor exports for the first formula above, in ManimCE: template = TexTemplate() template.add_to_preamble(r\"\\usepackage{cancel}\") … cancelled = MathTex(r\"…\", font_size=72, tex_template=template) and in ManimGL: preamble = r\"\\usepackage{cancel}\" … cancelled = Tex(r\"…\", font_size=74.2416, additional_preamble=preamble) The whole file, for the three formulas, is under the tabs. The generated Python explains the rest of it."],["size","The header of the editor: Name (1), Tag (2), Size (3), Colour (4) and Appears with (5). Size (3) is one value written twice. Scale is a factor of Manim's default font size, 48, and the second field is the font size it makes: 1.5 × 48 = 72. Change either and the other follows. The scale takes a number or a fraction (3/2) from 0.1 to 10, and the font size a number from 4 to 512. Changing the size resizes every clip of the formula where it stands. A clip also has a scale of its own, which the handles of the Preview change; the two multiply. New formulas start at the standard scale, 1 unless you say otherwise, set in Preferences ▸ Standard scale…. That dialog can also give the same scale to every formula of the project (see Standard scale)."],["colour","Colour (4) colours the whole formula, every glyph at once. Click the swatch to pick a colour in your system's picker, or type a hex value, #RRGGBB, and press Enter. The glyph column and the Preview use it straight away. To colour only some glyphs, select them in the glyph column and choose a colour for them. A glyph with a colour of its own keeps it when you change the colour of the formula. The same formula, three ways: Colouring glyphs and objects covers colours of single glyphs, and Palettes and Chroma Lab the named colours a project can share."],["names-tags","The Name (1) identifies the formula in the Creator, the timeline and your exported code, where it becomes a Python variable. For that reason a name has rules, and the field tells you which one it breaks as you type: A space is not allowed in a name. The reason shows under the field as you type; the name is kept when it is valid and you press Enter or leave the field. Letters, digits and _, not starting with a digit. Not a Python keyword (for, class, None…). Not the name of another formula. The Tag (2) is a small mark for finding the formula in the lists: one of eight colours or a hex value of your own, and optionally an icon of up to eight characters (a letter, an emoji). A tag never colours the glyphs of the formula. It identifies it: the dot in the Creator, and the clips of the formula on the timeline. Appears with (5) chooses the intro that every new clip of the formula starts with. It is explained in Appearing and disappearing."],["creator-list","Every formula of the project is a card in the Creator. In the list view a card has a row of information and a drawing of the formula on the project's background. You can drag the bar between the Creator and the Preview to give the cards room. The list view, with the Creator widened by dragging the bar beside it. Each card says: The parts of a card. Its tag: the dot, with its icon if it has one. Its name. Double-click it, or press F2 on the card, to rename it. Its kind: ∑ for a formula, T for a LaTeX text, P for a font text. Its status: MathJax or exact once it has rendered (see below), rendering… while it is being typeset, error when its LaTeX does not compile, and empty before you write anything. Point at the badge for the tooltip. How many clips use it on the timeline. Three buttons: edit (the pencil), duplicate and delete. A click selects a card; Ctrl+click and Shift+click select several (see Finding and organizing, which also lists the keys). A double-click or Enter opens the editor. The arrow keys walk the list, and a card can be dragged onto the timeline."],["list-blocks","The key beside the magnifier switches how the Creator draws its cards, between the list view and the block view: it shows the icon of the view a press brings. In the block view each formula is a small square with its tag, name, clip count, kind and status. Point at a block and a tooltip beside it shows the formula's drawing. Its edit, duplicate and delete buttons appear while the pointer or the focus is on it. The choice is a preference of the editor, and it is remembered. The block view, with the tooltip of the block the pointer is on. A block narrower than 112 px has no room for all of that beside its name. It shows a compact face instead: the whole block is a pane of glass tinted with the colour of its tag, with the name alone on it. The rest comes at once while the pointer is on the block, in its corners: the edit, duplicate and delete buttons at the top, the kind and a letter for the status at the bottom left, the clip count at the bottom right. The blocks are that narrow whenever the Creator is wide enough for four columns or more, and often when it is narrower. Letter The formula is M drawn by MathJax e drawn with the exact glyphs, from the companion ? a font text that needs the companion … not drawn yet ! in error. This one shows without the pointer, and so does the warning mark ⚠ Compact blocks: (1) the name on glass tinted with the tag’s colour; (2) the buttons, (3) the kind and the status letter and (4) the clip count of the block the pointer is on."],["preview-size","The +/− key of the toolbar, after the ∑ key, makes the previews of the view you see larger with a click and smaller with a right-click (or Shift+click): 1×, 1.5×, 2× or 3×. With the focus on it, ↑ and ↓ do the same. In the list view it sizes the drawing of every card, 52 pixels tall at 1× and 156 at 3×. In the block view it sizes the tooltip beside a block, its drawing as many times wider and taller. The formula is always drawn as large as its box allows. The list at 2×, with +/− ringed. Each view keeps its own size: the cards of the list can stay small while the tooltips of the blocks are large. Both are preferences of the editor, and they are remembered. The tooltip of the key says the size now, and which end is reached: at 1× a right-click does nothing, at 3× a click."],["rename-duplicate-delete","To rename a formula from the list, double-click its name (or press F2 on the card): the name becomes a field, and Enter keeps the new one. The rules of a name are the editor's own. Duplicate makes a copy right after the original, with everything the original has (its LaTeX, size, colours, hidden glyphs, packages, tag and intro) under the next free name: plain becomes plain2, and f007 becomes f008. The copy is a new formula with no clips. The duplicate button of a card puts a copy after it. Deleting a formula removes its clips, and the relations those clips have, with it, so the editor asks first and lists what will go. The button has to be held down: it fills while you hold it. Del on the selected formula, or Edit ▸ Delete formula, does the same. One undo step brings everything back. What deleting a formula asks, and lists."],["glyph-order","The editor does not animate a formula as a picture. It animates its glyphs, the individual characters and rules it is made of, and everything that points at a glyph does so by its number: its colour, whether it is hidden at the start, and above all the pairs that make one formula turn into another. Hover over the glyph column in the editor, or press its list button, and each glyph shows its number. Which number a glyph gets depends on who typeset the formula, and that is where the editor's two renderers differ. MathJax runs in the browser and needs nothing else. It typesets well, but it numbers the glyphs in its own order. Real LaTeX, through the companion, is how Manim itself builds a formula. The editor then uses Manim's glyphs, in Manim's order: exact mode. The two orders often agree and often do not. A fraction shows it. MathJax numbers its numerator, its denominator and then its bar; LaTeX numbers the numerator, the bar and then the denominator. MathJax numbers the glyphs of a fraction: a (0), b (1), then the bar (2). Formula MathJax order LaTeX order (as Manim builds it) \\frac{a}{b} a, b, bar a, bar, b \\sum_{i=0}^{n} i^2 ∑, i, =, 0, n, i, 2 n, ∑, i, =, 0, i, 2 You can work in the browser alone. A project that relates the glyphs of two formulas, or colours single glyphs, stores them by the numbers the editor sees. When it exports Python and those numbers are MathJax's, the exported scene finds each glyph by where it sits in the formula, which is reliable whenever the two typesettings make the same glyphs. Where they do not (tall brackets, stretchy arrows, ligatures), the scene prints a warning when you run it, and the glyphs without a counterpart are not animated. With the companion running, the badge in the glyph column says exact, the status line says exact (LaTeX), and the exported Python uses Manim's numbers straight, with no matching. The menu bar says Renderer: MathJax, Renderer: LaTeX or Renderer: mixed, as the formulas of the project stand. A formula you made without the companion keeps working when it comes up: the editor re-numbers its glyphs and carries your colours and pairs over to the new numbers. The View menu lets you ask for one renderer: Glyph source: Automatic (LaTeX when the companion is online), Glyph source: MathJax or Glyph source: LaTeX (companion). If you plan to relate glyphs and render with Manim, start the companion before you write the formulas. Nothing is lost if you do not, but you will see the real order from the start."],["next","Working with the timeline: put a formula in the scene. Transforming one formula into another: where the glyph numbers pay off. The template library: start from a ready-made formula. Finding and organizing: search, groups, several at once. Colouring glyphs and objects."]],"short":"Formulas"},{"id":"basics/preview","url":"basics/preview.html","title":"Placing things in the Preview","section":"Basics","level":"basic","description":"Select, drag and snap; the grid, axis lock and nudging; the handles; and working on several things at once.","headings":[["preview","The Preview at a glance"],["select","Selecting"],["drag-snap","Dragging and snapping"],["axis-lock","Holding a drag to one axis, or setting it free"],["nudge","Nudging with the arrow keys"],["grid","The grid"],["handles","Scale and rotate: the handles"],["several","Several at once"],["stays","When a clip does not move"],["view","Zooming and panning"],["lock","Locking a formula in the preview"],["menus","Right-click and double-click"],["try-it","Try it"],["keys","Keys and gestures of this page"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"The Preview is where you put things on the scene. Click to select, drag to move, with guides that line things up; nudge with the arrow keys; scale and turn with handles; and work on several things at once."],["preview","The Preview shows the scene at the time of the playhead (the line you move along the ruler of the timeline), as Manim will draw it. What you do in it edits the clip of the formula you touch — one appearance of that formula on the timeline (see the glossary): its position, its scale and its rotation. Scene positions are in Manim's units: the frame is 8 units tall and, at 16:9, 14.22 units wide, with the origin at its centre and y going up. The view toolbar sits in the title bar of the panel, at the right. Its buttons, from the left: (1) Align by symbol, (2) Circle selector, (3) Keep the transform handles shown, (4) Snap to objects, (5) Multicamera, which gives the scene a camera, (6) Zoom out, the zoom and Zoom in, (7) Fit. A clip that has a Transform block is locked after the first one. Before it, a drag in the Preview edits the clip's starting state; after it, the Preview does not move the clip, and a notice says where to edit instead: the end icon of the block on the timeline, or the padlock of the clip. See Animation blocks."],["select","Point at a formula and the glyph under the pointer is outlined. Click, and the formula's clip is selected: it gets a teal box. A click on a clip of the timeline selects the same thing, so the Preview and the timeline always agree. While the scene plays, the Preview leaves the pointer alone: nothing is outlined, selected or dragged, and the transform handles are hidden. What is selected stays selected. Stop the scene to work on it. You can still pan and zoom the view while it plays. A click selects a clip; Ctrl+click adds a second one; a rectangle drawn from empty space selects the clips it touches, in place of the ones selected before. Ctrl+click or Shift+click adds a clip to the selection, or takes it out when it is already there. Such a click only changes the selection: nothing is dragged. A drag that starts on empty space draws a rectangle. It selects every clip whose formula it touches, as it grows. With Ctrl or Shift held when you press, it adds to the selection; Esc brings the selection from before back. A plain click on empty space clears the selection. Ctrl+A selects every clip that is on screen at the playhead. With several clips selected, a plain click on one of them narrows the selection to it. The whole formula counts, not only its ink: the gap between two glyphs belongs to the formula. To pick single glyphs of a formula, hold Alt: that is its own page in the Layout section."],["drag-snap","Press on a formula and drag: its clip follows the pointer. If it is one of several selected clips, they all move together. The box around what you move snaps to the edges and the centres of the other formulas and of the frame, as soon as it is within 8 pixels of a match. Yellow guides show what it lines up with. The third formula goes up to the row of the other two: the yellow lines say what its box is lined up with, first the middle of the frame, then the other formulas. Between the two it stops where the gaps are equal. Ctrl+Z undoes the whole drag. On an axis where no edge or centre matches, a dragged formula can still snap to the spot that is as far from two formulas as they are from it, or to the gap two neighbours keep. Those equal gaps are drawn as bars with a tick at each end. The yellow line says the tops are level; the two bars under the gaps say the gaps are equal. A corner drag of a handle (below) sticks the same way to the width or the height of another formula, and shows the two equal sizes as bars. Snapping has a switch: the magnet button of the view toolbar (Snap to objects, also in Preferences ▸ Snap to objects). The colour and the width of the guides are in the same menu, as Guide colour and Guide width. One drag is one undo step, and Esc during the drag puts everything back."],["axis-lock","Press the button first, then the key: with a key already down, the press means something else (Ctrl+click adds to the selection, and Alt+click picks glyphs or pans the view). On a Mac the app takes ⌘ where other systems take Ctrl (a Ctrl+click is the secondary click there) and ⌥ is Alt. The keys of this page are written for your system. Held during the drag What the drag does nothing Follows the pointer, snapping to the other formulas and to the grid. Alt Moves only along one axis: horizontally or vertically, whichever the pointer has gone further along since the drag began. It still snaps along that axis. Ctrl Follows the pointer with no snapping at all: no guides, no grid. Ctrl+Alt Along one axis, with no snapping. The axis is chosen again as the pointer moves, and a note at the bottom of the Preview says which one holds. Let go of Alt and the drag follows the pointer again. A key pressed or let go changes the drag at once, without a pointer move. Alt pressed in the middle of a drag holds the formula on one horizontal line, and it still snaps along it; let go of Alt and the formula goes to the pointer."],["nudge","Click a formula, and the arrow keys move the selected clips: ← → ↑ ↓ by 0.01 scene units, with Shift by 0.1. A hint at the bottom of the Preview says so once it has the keys. There is no snapping: a press moves by its step exactly. The X and Y of the clip's Initial state, in the Timing tab of the Animations panel (ringed), follow the keys. Four presses of → make X 3.74; two of Shift+↑ make Y 2.75; one of Shift+← makes X 3.64. One Ctrl+Z undoes the whole run. The keys edit the clip at the playhead, as a drag would. A clip that a drag would leave where it is stays, and a notice names it. A run of presses is one undo step, a key held down included. Any other edit in between, a new click on the scene, or another time of the playhead starts a new step. With nothing selected on screen, ← and → step the playhead, as everywhere else. With Ctrl or Alt held the arrows are not taken. For an exact place, type it: the X, Y, Scale and Rotation fields of Initial state take numbers, and Flip X and Flip Y mirror the formula."],["grid","The Show grid button, in the middle of the transport bar, draws a grid under the formulas and makes a dragged formula land on its nearest node. The grid exists only in the editor: it is never in an export. (1) Show grid; (2) Sparser grid, the spacing in Manim units and Denser grid; (3) what the grid holds. The spacing goes through 2, 1, 0.5, 0.25 and 0.1 units. The shortcuts are Ctrl+' to show or hide the grid, and Alt+↑ and Alt+↓ for denser and sparser. The last button chooses what lands on a node: the formula's centre (a dot in a box) or its nearest edges (four corners). Ctrl held during the drag frees it. A guide to another formula wins on its axis, because a line-up with another formula is more specific. The same choices are in Preferences ▸ Show grid, Grid spacing and Grid snaps. The grid is shown (1 unit). With the magnet off, so that only the grid holds the formula, a drag across it jumps from node to node by the formula's centre."],["handles","Hold Shift over a selected clip and its transform handles appear, with a hint in the corner. The button that keeps them on without Shift is the third one of the view toolbar (Preferences ▸ Keep the transform handles shown). Four square handles scale; the circle above the box turns. A corner scales the formula uniformly about its centre. The scale goes in steps, 0.05 by default: change it with Preferences ▸ Scale step (0.05, 0.1, 0.15, 0.2, or Custom…, which takes a number or a fraction such as 1/3). The circle above the box turns the formula, in steps of 15°. Hold Ctrl while dragging for free values, with no steps. A readout next to the pointer says the scale (× 1.45) or the angle (30°). A corner drag also sticks to the width or the height of another formula, showing the two equal sizes as bars; Ctrl frees that too. A corner scales the formula and the circle turns it; the ringed Scale and Rotation fields of its Initial state follow."],["several","With two or more clips selected, a bar appears in the top-left corner of the Preview: the number of clips, the buttons to align and distribute them (Align and distribute), and the menus Paste properties and Match colors (Match colors). Dragging any of the selected clips moves them all, and the box around the whole group is what snaps. Hold Shift and the handles frame the whole group: a corner scales every clip about the middle of the group, and the circle turns the group, each clip about that same point. The scale snaps in steps, as for one clip. Ctrl+A selects the four formulas; with Shift held, the handles frame the group. A corner scales them all toward its middle; the circle turns them all. Not every selected clip can move. A clip on a locked track, a clip locked in the preview, a clip locked after its transformations and a clip that is not on screen stay where they are. A drag leaves them without a word; Align, Distribute and Paste properties name them in a notice, and so do the arrow keys and the group's handles for the ones that are on screen. Braces, shapes and labels attached to a formula follow the formula they hang on."],["stays","Some clips cannot be moved from the Preview. For the first three kinds below, a notice says why: Locked after its first transformation. The clip has a Transform block and the playhead is past its start. A notice says so and says where to edit: the end icon of the block on the timeline, or the padlock of the clip. Attached. A brace, a shape, a label or a line with an end on glyphs is placed by the glyphs it hangs on. The notice reads “Attached to …”, and Detach, in the Animations panel, frees it. Carried by a Transform. A shape that a Transform took to other glyphs is placed by them: the notice reads “Carried to … by its Transform”. On a locked track (the padlock of the track's header), or locked in the preview: nothing is dragged. Unlock it first. Not on screen at the playhead: there is nothing to press. Move the playhead into the clip. When several clips are selected and only some of them can move, the others stay. A drag leaves them without a word; Align, Distribute, Paste properties, the arrow keys and the group's handles name them in a notice (“2 clips stayed”)."],["view","Zoom with Ctrl and the mouse wheel, about the pointer, or with the Zoom out and Zoom in buttons of the toolbar (about the middle of the view). Pan with the middle mouse button held, or with Alt held while you drag on empty space. Fit shows the whole frame again. On a trackpad, a pinch zooms about the pointer as far as your fingers go: twice as far apart, twice as large. A scroll with two fingers moves the view, and the scene follows your fingers. With a mouse the wheel alone moves the view too, and ⌘ and the wheel zoom. Ctrl and the wheel zoom about the formula under the pointer; Alt and a drag on empty space pan; Fit brings the whole frame back. The view is for looking: zooming and panning change nothing in the project, and they do not move the formulas or the export."],["lock","A formula you have placed can be locked, so that you cannot move it by accident while you place the others around it. Right-click its clip on the timeline and choose Lock in the preview. The entry is at the end of the clip's menu on the timeline. The Preview draws the formula faded (Preferences ▸ Locked formulas: 20 % to 100 %), and the pointer goes through it: a click selects what is under it, and a rectangle or Ctrl+A leaves it out. It has no handles, and Align, Distribute, Align by symbol and Paste properties leave it alone. The other formulas still snap to it as you drag them, which is what it is for. The timeline and the Animations panel edit it as any other clip. Its clip on the timeline wears a mark; click it, or choose Unlock in the preview, to unlock. The formula at the upper right is locked: it is drawn at 40 %. The mark on a locked clip. Locking is for the editor only: the exported Python and the video are the same with or without it."],["menus","A right-click on a formula selects its clip (unless it is already among the selected ones) and opens its menu. The menu of a formula in the Preview. Copy properties and Paste properties ▸ copy the look of a clip — scale, rotation, flips, opacity, colours — and, if you choose Position, its place, onto other clips; Align ▸ and Distribute ▸ are described in Align and distribute; Add brace ▸, Add shape ▸ and Add line ▸ add those objects on the formula (Braces, Shapes, Lines and arrows); the rest are the clip's own entries, as on the timeline. A right-click on empty space opens a small menu of scene actions, Add line. A double-click on a text in the Preview opens that text in the composition editor, at that clip (LaTeX texts and font texts); a formula opens from the Creator, or by a double-click on its clip on the timeline."],["try-it","Open preview-placing and put the four formulas where you want them. Open the file: choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… in the dialog and pick it (see Open). Click the ruler of the timeline at 4 s, so that all four formulas are on screen. Drag circle (the area of a circle, at the lower left) up toward pythagoras. Watch the yellow guides when their left edges line up. Drag circle again; with the mouse button down, hold Alt and move the pointer sideways: the formula slides along one line. Click euler, press → three times, then read its X in the Animations panel (Timing tab). Hold Shift and scale quadratic with a corner: watch the readout, and try the same with Ctrl held. Press Ctrl+A, hold Shift, and drag a corner of the group toward its centre. Then undo with Ctrl+Z. Right-click the clip of euler on the timeline and choose Lock in the preview. Drag pythagoras up until it is level with it: it still snaps to the locked formula, which you cannot move by accident. The scene itself writes its four formulas one after the other. Here it is, as you will find it once you have tidied the layout: The snapping example, preview-guides, is the scene of the clips about guides:"],["keys","Gesture What it does Click; Ctrl or Shift+click Select; add to the selection or take out Drag on empty space Rectangle that selects the clips it touches (Esc cancels) Ctrl+A Select every clip on screen at the playhead Drag; then Alt Move; hold to one axis Drag; then Ctrl Move without snapping ← → ↑ ↓; with Shift Move the selected clips 0.01; 0.1 Shift held Show the transform handles (a group's when several are selected) Ctrl held while scaling or turning Free values, no steps and no size guides Ctrl+'; Alt+↑ Alt+↓ Show or hide the grid; denser, sparser Pinch or Ctrl + wheel; scroll, middle button or Alt + drag on empty space Zoom about the pointer; pan Esc Cancel the drag in progress The whole list is in the keyboard and mouse reference. To pick single glyphs, see Picking glyphs with Alt; to line formulas up exactly, Align and distribute and Align by symbol."],["next","Working with the timeline covers tracks and clips, the playhead, zoom and snapping. Animation blocks covers every animation block a clip can hold, from Write to FocusOn."]],"short":"The Preview"},{"id":"basics/timeline","url":"basics/timeline.html","title":"Working with the timeline","section":"Basics","level":"basic","description":"Tracks and clips, splitting a clip at the playhead, zoom and the time step dt, snapping, selecting several clips, and the keyboard: how to work on the timeline.","headings":[["parts","The parts of the timeline"],["tracks","Tracks"],["track-buttons","The buttons of a track"],["track-heights","Track heights"],["which-track","What goes on which track"],["clips","Clips"],["putting-clips","Putting a formula on the timeline"],["what-a-clip-shows","What a clip shows"],["moving-clips","Moving a clip"],["resizing-clips","Resizing a clip"],["splitting","Splitting a clip"],["relations-on-timeline","Relations between clips"],["copies-of-clips","Copies and deletion"],["playhead","The playhead"],["zoom-step","Zoom and the time step"],["dt-of-your-own","A time step of your own"],["zooming","Zooming"],["moving-the-view","Moving along the timeline"],["snapping","Snapping"],["region","The region: where the next thing goes"],["region-marking","Marking it"],["region-use","What takes it"],["selecting","Selecting several clips"],["keyboard","From the keyboard"],["try-it","Try it"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"The timeline says when each formula is on screen. Here you learn its parts, how to put clips on it, move them and cut them in two, how to zoom and snap, and how to get around it with the mouse and the keyboard. The timeline is a set of tracks, one under another. On a track you put clips: a clip is the time during which an object (a formula, a text, a brace, a shape or a line) is on screen. A ruler counts the seconds along the top, and the playhead marks the instant the Preview shows."],["parts","The rest of the page takes the parts one by one. You can follow it on the project above, timeline-basics, which the last section offers for download."],["tracks","A track is a row of the timeline. The clips of a track never overlap, so two things that are on screen together are on two tracks. Tracks are layers: the one on top is drawn in front of the ones below it. A new project starts with two, Track 2 over Track 1. Press + Track, on the left of the toolbar, to add a track. It goes on top, in front of the others, and takes the next free number as its name. The new track is on top and empty. To rename it, double-click its name, select the text with Ctrl+A, type a new name and press Enter. Here it is called Notes. A name is changed by double-clicking it, or by pressing Enter or F2 on it once Tab has brought the focus there. Enter keeps the new name and Esc keeps the old one."],["track-buttons","Hide (the eye) hides the track in the Preview, so that you see what is behind it. It is a view only: a hidden track is still exported, in the Python and in the video; the Python export lists it among its issues. Lock (the padlock) protects the track. Its clips can be selected, but nothing changes them: they cannot be moved, trimmed or deleted, the Animations panel shows them read-only, and nothing can be dropped on the track. Unlock it to work on them again. Up and down (the arrows) move the track towards the front or towards the back. The up arrow of the track on top and the down arrow of the one at the bottom are off. Delete (the cross) deletes the track with its clips. A dialog lists what goes with it, clips, the objects attached to them and their relations, and its red button has to be held down until it fills, so that nobody deletes a track by accident. One undo step brings everything back. The cross asks first. The dialog lists what goes with the track; its red button only deletes once it has been held down."],["track-heights","A track grows by itself when its clips have animation blocks that run at the same time: each one gets a lane. You can also change heights to read better. The − and + beside a track's name make that track alone lower or taller, down to the standard height and up to four times it. Dragging the top or bottom edge of its header does the same: the track an edge resizes lights up as the pointer reaches it, a double-click gives the track the height of the others again, and Esc during the drag puts it back. The keys in the corner over the headers, − 100 % +, change every track at once, from the standard height to three times it. The percentage in the middle puts them all back. Heights are a view of the timeline, like its zoom: the project does not store them."],["which-track","Formulas, texts, braces, shapes and lines go on the ordinary tracks, the ones + Track adds. Recordings go on audio tracks, which the Audio button next to it adds (see Audio). A clip never moves onto a track of the other kind."],["clips","A clip is the time an object is on screen: it starts at one time, ends at another and lives on one track. A formula can have many clips. It is still one object, so editing it changes all of them (to give one clip a formula of its own, see Unlinking a formula)."],["putting-clips","Drag the card of a formula from the Creator onto a track. A dashed ghost shows where the clip would go: it is 3 seconds long, its start snaps to the ticks, and if that place is taken it moves to the nearest free time. Drop it and the clip is made and selected. The card of check dragged onto Track 1. The ghost fills the free time before step2; dropped, it is a clip of 3 s. Without dragging, choose Add to the timeline in the formula's menu (right-click its card), or press the same button in the Animations panel when the formula is not on the timeline yet. The clip goes on the first track that is not locked, at the playhead. What a new clip starts with is the subject of Appearing and disappearing."],["what-a-clip-shows","A clip wears the tag colour of its formula as a stripe on its left edge, and its name, with the tag's icon when it has one. In its lower corners it names its intro, on the left, and its outro, on the right; both are hatched. Rest the pointer on a clip for a moment and a tooltip shows its formula and its times. Double-click a clip to edit its formula; right-click it for its menu."],["moving-clips","Drag a clip. Sideways changes its time; up or down puts it on another track. Clips never overlap on a track, so a clip stops at the nearest free place instead of going over a neighbour. It never starts before 0, and it never goes onto a locked track or an audio track. Nor does it leave out the window of a relation whose other clip is on a locked track (below). Several selected clips move together, and Esc during the drag puts everything back. Dragging step3 to the left, with the playhead at 5.75 s: it moves in jumps of dt, sticks to the playhead and follows the pointer freely while Shift is held."],["resizing-clips","Drag the left or the right edge of a clip to change when it starts or when it ends. The clip stops at the next clip of its track, and it is never shorter than its intro and its outro together, nor than a tenth of a second, and it stops where the window of a relation with a clip on a locked track would no longer fit in it. Trimming the start does not move what the clip shows: its animation blocks stay at their times, and one that no longer fits inside the clip is shortened or removed. The end of step1 dragged to the right: the clip grows until it meets step3. Several clips at once. With several clips selected (Ctrl-click them, or drag a box over them), drag an edge of one of them: the same edge of every selected clip moves with it, by the same time, each from where it is. Sounds are trimmed along with the clips of the scene. The clips go as far as all of them allow: when one of them meets the next clip of its track, its shortest length, the end of its recording or the window of a relation with a clip on a locked track, they all stop. The edge you hold is the one that snaps. An edge of a clip that is not selected still trims that clip alone, and the edges of an intro or an outro are always one clip's. The window of a relation lies over part of the lower half of a clip, and an animation block over part of its upper half. A press there is theirs, not the edge's: the end of step1 above is trimmed from the upper half of its edge. Press where nothing lies over the edge, or hold Alt: with Alt held only the edges answer the pointer, whatever lies over them (more about it)."],["splitting","To cut a clip in two, put the playhead where the cut goes, click the clip to select it and press S. The clip's menu has Split at playhead (S) too, on the timeline and in the Preview, and so has the menu of several selected clips, which splits those the playhead is inside. The clip becomes two clips that touch at the playhead. step2 split at 5.5 s. The second half is a clip of step2_s, and the arrow to step3 now leaves from it. Ctrl+Z undoes the split in one step. The first half is still a clip of its formula, and keeps the intro. The second half is a clip of a copy of the formula: a new formula in the Creator, named after it with a suffix, step2_s here, or step2_s1, step2_s2… when that name is taken. From then on the two are separate formulas: editing one no longer changes the other. Preferences ▸ Split suffix… changes the suffix (see Preferences). The second half has no intro: it starts exactly as the object is at that instant, with its place, size, turn, colours and opacity, and with the glyphs that are on screen then. The copy hides the other glyphs; a block or a relation after the cut still brings them in. The outro goes with the second half. The glyphs that have not come on screen yet at the cut, and that an Appear block, a relation or a Morph block brings in after it, stay out of the first half too. What brings them goes with the second half, so the first half leaves them out of its intro itself, and up to the cut it shows what the whole clip showed. Its Timing tab says how many, under Starts empty: 2 glyphs stay out of the intro. Bring them back (Bring it back for one glyph) gives them to its intro again, in one undo step (Appearing and disappearing). If nothing of the clip was on screen before the cut, the first half shows nothing, and its panel says so under Warnings: … this clip never shows. Delete that half, or trim the clip. A clip of a + b = c whose = c an Appear block brings at 2 s, split at 1.8 s: the Intro section of its first half. What lies whole on one side of the cut goes with its half: the animation blocks, the relations (one whose window comes after the cut now leaves from, or arrives at, the second half) and the objects attached to the clip. A brace, a shape or a line is split the same way: its second half is another object, equal to it. The split is one undo step, and both halves are left selected. A sound selected with the clips is cut at the playhead too, in the same step (Audio). When the clip stays whole. The split is refused, and a notice names the reason, when one of the following holds. With several clips selected, the others are split, and one notice lists each clip that stayed whole with its reason. something crosses the playhead: an intro or an outro still running, an animation block, the window of a relation, or the clip of an object attached to this one. Move the playhead off it. For an attached object, you can also select its clip: S then splits both. the second half could not start as the object is then: glyphs that a Transform block moved or made fainter (a colour it gave them is kept), a shape that a Transform carried to other glyphs, a line whose ends a Transform moved, or an attached object whose target has moved since it was placed, or that a Transform of its own moved (detach it first, and it is split where it is). A formula the Preview has not drawn yet waits for its render. a brace, a shape or a line only comes on screen after the playhead, through an Appear block, say. It is one piece, so its first half cannot leave it out of its intro, as a formula leaves out some glyphs (above): split after it has appeared. a relation whose other clip is on a locked track has its window after the playhead: it would go to the second half, and a relation of a locked track is not changed. Unlock the track first. Each half must also last a tenth of a second at least. The menu entry is off while the playhead is outside the clip, or the clip is on a locked track; its tooltip says which. S is also the key of the Preview's circle selector. It splits only while a selected clip has the playhead inside it and the keyboard is not in the Preview. After a click on the scene the keyboard is there, and S switches the circle selector: choose Split at playhead (S) in the menu instead, or click the clip on the timeline first. With a sound selected, S always splits."],["relations-on-timeline","When one clip turns into another, an arrow joins them on the timeline and a band, the window, marks on both clips when the transformation runs. The relations of the selected clips are drawn in full, the others faintly. Click an arrow or a band to select the relation, and double-click either to open the relation editor. Drag a band to move its window, or its edges to resize it; a readout gives its start, its end and its length. Right-click a band for the menu of the relation. How relations are made and what they do is the subject of How relations work. A relation with a clip on a locked track cannot be changed, so it plays where it does whatever you do to its other clip. A move or a trim of that clip, Alt and the arrow keys, its Start and End fields and the length of its intro or outro stop where the window would no longer fit in it. The clip is not deleted, and neither is its track nor its formula in the Creator; it is not split so that the relation would go to its second half either. A notice says why and names the locked track."],["copies-of-clips","Ctrl+D copies the selected clips, each into the nearest free time after it on its own track. Delete deletes them, as one undo step. A clip that has objects attached to it, such as a brace, takes them along, and a dialog you confirm by holding a button says which. A clip with a relation to a clip on a locked track stays (above)."],["playhead","The playhead is the instant the Preview shows: a small cap in the ruler and a yellow line over the tracks. Press anywhere on the ruler and the playhead jumps there. Drag, and it follows the pointer while the Preview shows the scene at each instant. It lands on whole frames and stays between 0 and the end of the scene, which is where the last clip ends unless you gave the project a fixed length in File ▸ Project settings…. With Ctrl held it moves in larger, even steps: see the time step below. The playhead (1) at 5.5 s. The Preview (2) shows the scene at that instant, and the readouts under it say which one it is. Other ways to move it: Space plays and pauses from where it is, Home and End go to the start and the end, and ← and → step one frame, or one second with Shift. Under the Preview the time is written in seconds, as minutes:seconds:frames and as a frame number. The playhead also says where things happen: a clip added with Add to the timeline starts there, and so does a block added from the Animations panel, a brace or a shape. To say where something ends as well, mark a region first."],["zoom-step","The scale of the ruler is the zoom of the timeline, in pixels per second. It starts at 80 px/s. The toolbar shows it, and next to it dt: the time between two small ticks of the ruler at that zoom. That is also the step clips snap to (see Snapping), so dt tells you how finely you can place things. The ruler chooses its ticks again at every zoom. dt is 0.5 s at 80 px/s, and shrinks as you zoom in until there is room for a tick on every frame: then it is one frame, written as a fraction (1/60 s in a project at 60 frames a second). Zooming out makes it grow: dt 5 s at the lowest zoom of an ordinary project. dt can be the step of the playhead too. Hold Ctrl while you drag on the ruler, and the playhead goes from one small tick to the next instead of frame by frame: at dt 0.5 s it stops at 0, 0.5, 1 and so on, up to the last tick before the end of the scene. Let go of the key and the drag is free again. Whether Snap is on makes no difference."],["dt-of-your-own","The − and + on either side of dt set a time step of your own, without changing the zoom: each press makes it a tenth of a second shorter or longer. The first press starts from the dt the zoom gave: at dt 0.5 s, − gives 0.4 s and + 0.6 s; at dt 1/60 s, + gives 0.1 s. It never goes under 0.1 s, so − is off at 0.1 s and below, nor over an hour. Your own dt is written in the accent colour, and everything that goes by dt goes by it: the small ticks of the ruler (left out where they would be closer together than 4 pixels; the labelled ticks stay the zoom's), the step clips, blocks and windows snap to, and the playhead dragged with Ctrl held. Zoom, with the zoom keys, Ctrl and the wheel or Show the whole timeline, and dt is the zoom's own again. When the timeline raises its lowest zoom by itself, because the window got wider or the scene shorter, your dt stays. It lasts while the editor is open; no project keeps it. − beside dt, twice: 0.3 s of your own, and the small ticks follow. Zooming gives back the zoom's own."],["zooming","Ctrl and the wheel zoom about the pointer: the time under it stays under it. A pinch on the trackpad does the same, as far as your fingers go. The − and + before the slider zoom about the middle of the view. The key after the slider, Show the whole timeline, zooms out, or in, to the zoom at which nothing is left to scroll. It is off while the whole timeline is already in view."],["moving-the-view","The slider in the middle of the toolbar moves the view. Its thumb is to the bar what the stretch in view is to the whole timeline: it fills the bar while everything is in view and gets shorter as you zoom in. Drag it, or press beside it to bring it under the pointer; Esc puts the view back. The wheel scrolls the timeline, on a trackpad both ways at once. The middle button, held down, drags the view in both directions, and Esc puts it back. Hold T and move the mouse: the view moves along the time with it, wherever the pointer is, as far as dragging a scrollbar would take it. Let go of the key and the view stays there. It works outside text fields, menus and dialogs. Hold Shift+T and the playhead follows the pointer instead: it jumps to the time the pointer is level with, wherever the pointer is, and moves with it. Let go and the playhead stays there. With T held you can press and release Shift to go from moving the view to moving the playhead and back. While you hold T, with or without Shift, the mouse is the timeline's: the Preview outlines and selects nothing of what the pointer passes over. Ctrl and the wheel zoom in about the pointer, and out again. The slider's thumb, the readout and dt follow. The slider (1) moves the view; dt (2) is the time between two small ticks of the ruler below, and its own − and + change it."],["snapping","Snapping makes what you drag stick to useful times. It is on when you start; the Snap button, with the magnet, turns it off and on. While you drag a clip, or one of its edges, the edges that move stick to whichever of these is nearest, within 8 pixels: the playhead; time 0, and the end of the scene when you gave the project a fixed length in File ▸ Project settings…; the edges of the other clips, on any track. Where none of them is near enough, they stick to the nearest small tick of the ruler, which is dt apart. A moving clip snaps by both its edges, so it can meet something with either. A formula dropped from the Creator snaps its start in the same way. Animation blocks, relation windows and the inner edges of intros and outros snap too, to the playhead, the ticks and the edges of what is in their own clip: its other blocks, its windows, its intro and its outro, and the clip's own edges. To drag a clip, a block, a window or an edge without snapping, hold Shift during the drag: let it go and snapping is back. A formula dragged from the Creator is freed the same way: hold Shift once it is over the track. With Snap off, nothing snaps until you turn it on. The timeline draws no guide where something snaps: you see the clip jump to a tick, to the playhead or to the edge of a neighbour. Something you drag that seems to resist is snapping, and Shift frees it. Zoom in to place something between two ticks: 8 pixels are then a shorter time. Holding Shift also shows a small round link port on every clip. The ports are for relating clips to each other (How relations work) and have nothing to do with the drag. For an exact time, type it. The Timing tab of the Animations panel has the Start and End of the selected clip, and Timing ▸ in its menu copies times from another clip (Timing tools)."],["region","A region is a stretch of time marked on the ruler, as in an audio editor: a green bracket where it starts, a red one where it ends, and a tint between them, on the ruler and over the tracks. It says when the next thing you create starts and when it ends."],["region-marking","Hold Shift and drag on the ruler. A plain press on the ruler still moves the playhead, inside the region too. Or use the playhead: I puts the start of the region where the playhead is, and O its end. Pressed alone, I marks from the playhead to the end of the scene, and O from the start of the scene to the playhead. Drag a bracket by its upper part to move that end; the other end stays. The ends land on whole frames and snap as the edge of a clip does. Hold Shift while dragging a bracket to place it freely, or Ctrl to go tick by tick. Esc removes the region. When the key has something else to do first, such as closing a menu or letting picked glyphs go, it does that, and the next press removes the region. The lower part of the ruler under a bracket is still the ruler: right after I or O the playhead sits on a bracket, and you can drag it away by its head."],["region-use","While a region is marked, three things take it whole, wherever the playhead is: An animation block added from the menu of a clip or with Add animation in the Animations panel starts where the region starts and lasts until it ends (Animation blocks). The window of a new relation is the region (Relations). The clip of a formula brought to the timeline is the region. Dropped on a track, it goes on that track, and its dashed outline shows it there while you drag. Added with Add to the timeline, it goes on the first unlocked track that has the region free, or on a new track on top when none has. What does not fit in the region is not made, and a notice says why: the clip does not hold the region, other animations of its glyphs are in it, the two clips do not overlap all along it, or the track already has a clip there. Nothing is moved to a free time nearby. Move the region, or remove it to work at the playhead again. The region stays after it is used, ready for the next thing. It is a view of the timeline, like the zoom: it is not saved with the project, Undo does not touch it, and opening another project removes it. When the scene gets shorter and the timeline ends before the region does, the region is shown cut at that end, or not at all; it comes back whole as soon as the timeline grows again, a trim given up with Esc or undone included. Braces, shapes and lines added from the menus do not take it; they go by the playhead as before, and so do the keyframes that an edit in the Preview makes."],["selecting","Click a clip to select it. Shift or Ctrl and a click adds a clip to the selection, or takes it out. To select many at once, drag a rectangle on an empty part of a lane: every clip it touches is selected. With Shift or Ctrl held, they are added to the selection; Esc cancels the rectangle. A press on the background lets everything go, and Ctrl+A selects every clip on screen at the playhead. A rectangle dragged from the free time of Track 2 touches step1 and step2: both are selected, and their relations light up. Selected clips move together, and the Preview treats them as a group. Right-click one of them, or press Shift+F10, for the menu of the selected clips. Its first line says how many clips and how many formulas there are. The menu of two selected clips, title and step1, with the playhead at 2 s. Unlink formula is off: each of them is the only clip of its formula. It offers what the Creator offers their formulas (Edit together…, Apply match colors), a brace or a shape over all of them, and the commands of the clips themselves: Timing, Unlink formula (make a copy), Split at playhead (S) (above), Lock in the preview, Duplicate and Delete. Each entry is explained in Timing tools or on the page of its feature."],["keyboard","The clips and blocks of the timeline are one grid with a single Tab stop: Tab brings the focus to a clip (the first selected one, else the first of the first track), and the arrows take it from there. Selecting follows the focus, and what happens is announced to screen readers. Keys What they do ← → the previous or the next clip of the track ↑ ↓ the track above or below, at the clip nearest in time; from a clip, ↑ climbs into the lanes of its blocks Home End the first or the last clip of the track Shift+← Shift+→ add the next clip along the track to the selection Enter open the formula of the clip (the menu of a block) Shift+F10 the menu of the clip, or of the selected clips when there are several Alt+← Alt+→ move the clip one frame (Alt+Shift+→: five frames) Alt+↑ Alt+↓ move the clip to the track above or below Delete delete the selected clips, as the mouse does Ctrl+D copy the selected clips S split the selected clips at the playhead (Splitting a clip) Space play and pause, with the focus on a clip too When no clip has the focus, Home, End, ← and → move the playhead instead, as under The playhead. The whole list of keys is on Keyboard and mouse shortcuts."],["try-it","Download the project below and open it: choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… in the dialog and pick the file (Projects and files). Drag check from the Creator onto Track 1, at about 0.5 s. The ghost shows 3 seconds ending exactly where step2 starts. Let go: the clip is made, and selected. Drag the clip of check sideways and watch it jump from tick to tick. Now hold Shift once you have started: it follows the pointer exactly. Let go of Shift and it jumps again; Esc puts the clip back. Press + Track, rename the new track Notes and drag the clip of check onto it. Zoom in until dt says 1/60 s, then use Alt+→ to move the clip one frame at a time. Select step1 and step2 with a rectangle, right-click one of them and look at the menu. Put the playhead at about 5.5 s, click step2 alone and press S. The clip is now two, and the Creator lists step2_s. Press Ctrl+Z to make it one clip again."],["next","Clips come and go with animations: Appearing and disappearing covers intros and outros. To turn one formula into another, go on to Transforming one formula into another, and to copy times between clips, to Timing tools."]],"short":"The timeline"},{"id":"basics/intro-outro","url":"basics/intro-outro.html","title":"Appearing and disappearing","section":"Basics","level":"basic","description":"How a clip comes in and how it goes away: the kinds of intro and outro, how to give one and set its length, what happens when it runs into something, and the defaults.","headings":[["intro","The intro"],["outro","The outro"],["kinds","What each kind of object takes"],["giving","Giving a clip an intro or an outro"],["menu","From the clip's menu"],["panel","In the Animations panel"],["drag","By dragging its edge"],["alt","When something lies over an edge"],["conflicts","When an animation runs into something"],["blocks","Appear and Disappear blocks"],["appear-waits","An Appear block makes its glyphs wait"],["defaults","What a new clip starts with"],["braces-shapes","Braces, shapes and lines"],["try-it","Try it"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A clip comes in with an intro and goes away with an outro. This page shows every kind, how to give a clip one and set its length, what happens when one runs into something else on the clip, and what new clips start with. Without an intro, an object is simply there when its clip starts, and without an outro it is simply gone when its clip ends. An intro and an outro are Manim animations (Write, FadeOut…), named by their Manim names in the app, and they take place inside the clip: the intro in its first seconds, the outro in its last ones. On the timeline they are the hatched ends of a clip."],["intro","The intro is the animation a clip starts with. It runs from the first instant of the clip for as long as you set, and the clip is at least that long. Intro What it does Lasts None (appear) Nothing: the object is on screen from the first instant. — Write Draws the glyphs stroke by stroke, as if written by hand, each glyph starting a little after the one before. 1 s FadeIn Fades the object in. It can start from a shifted place and a different scale. 1 s GrowFromCenter Grows the object from a point at its centre to its size. 1 s DrawBorderThenFill Draws the outline of each glyph, then fills it. 2 s Create Draws the outline progressively. It is the one that suits lines and shapes. 1 s The lengths are what a new intro starts with; you change them (below). An arrow has one more, GrowArrow, which grows it from its start: see Lines and arrows."],["outro","The outro is the animation a clip ends with: it finishes at the last instant of the clip. Each outro undoes an intro, more or less. Outro What it does Lasts None (disappear) Nothing: the object is on screen until the clip ends, then gone. — Unwrite Erases the glyphs stroke by stroke, the opposite of Write. 1 s FadeOut Fades the object out, with the same shift and scale as FadeIn. 1 s ShrinkToCenter Shrinks the object to a point at its centre. 1 s Uncreate Draws the outline backwards until nothing is left. 1 s"],["kinds","Not every object can do every animation. The app offers each one only what it can do: Object Intros Outros Formula, text (LaTeX), text (font), brace, shape none, Write, FadeIn, GrowFromCenter, DrawBorderThenFill, Create none, Unwrite, FadeOut, ShrinkToCenter, Uncreate Line, dashed line none, Create, Write, FadeIn none, Uncreate, Unwrite, FadeOut Arrow, double arrow those of a line, and GrowArrow those of a line A line is drawn or faded; it has no centre to grow from or shrink to."],["giving","There are three ways, and each works on its own."],["menu","Right-click the clip, on the timeline or in the Preview, and open Intro or Outro. The list holds what the object takes; the current choice is ticked. None (appear), in the Intro list, and None (disappear), in the Outro list, take the animation off; any other choice gives the clip that animation, with the length that kind starts with. The menu of a clip, with Intro open and the current intro ticked. Remove animations, in the same menu, takes off the intro, the outro and every animation block of the clip but the Morph ones, in one step."],["panel","Select the clip and open the Timing tab of the Animations panel. Its Intro and Outro sections have the same list, in Animation, and the numbers behind the animation: The Intro section of a clip that fades in. Duration: how long it lasts, in seconds. It cannot be longer than what the clip leaves beside its other animation. Easing: the rate function, the way speed changes along the animation (smooth, linear, rush_into…, Manim's own names). Lag ratio: how much the animation of each glyph waits for the one before it. Default is Manim's own value for that animation, shown in brackets; Custom lets you type one: 0 animates every glyph at once, 1 one after the other. Shift X, Shift Y and Scale, for FadeIn and FadeOut only: where the object starts from (or goes to) and how big it is there. The Intro section of a formula or a text begins with a box, Starts empty. Ticked, no glyph of the clip comes in with the intro: each one appears only when a relation, a Morph block or an Appear block brings it, and a glyph nothing brings never shows. It is for a clip that is the target of a transformation and should show only what was transformed: How relations work has an example. The intro keeps its animation and its time, with nothing to animate; the section says so, and None (appear) frees that time. If an edit later takes away what filled the clip (its relation, say), a notice tells you that the clip will not show. A brace, a shape or a line is one piece, and has no such box. A clip can also leave only some of its glyphs out of its intro: the first half of a split clip leaves out the glyphs that came on screen after the cut. Its Intro section says how many, under the box: 2 glyphs stay out of the intro. Bring them back gives them to the intro again, as the other glyphs, in one undo step. While Starts empty is ticked every glyph stays out, and the line waits until the box is unticked. The easings are Manim's rate functions. Each curve below shows how an animation advances over its time, and a dot and a glyph move at that pace: The select applies the animation at once, and trims the clip's keyframes to make room for it. The menu above is more careful: see When an animation runs into something."],["drag","The inner edge of an intro or an outro, the dashed line where it ends, is a handle. Its grab zone is the lower part of the clip, a few pixels either side of the line; the cursor turns into a double arrow over it. Drag it to make the animation longer or shorter. A clip, closely: the intro (1) and the outro (3) are hatched and carry the icon and the name of their animation, or the icon alone when they are too short for the name; the name of the clip (2) is between them; the inner edge of the intro (4) is the handle. The edge of the intro of step1 dragged to the right, then to the left. A readout over the clip gives the animation, where it runs and how long it lasts. The edge snaps to the playhead, the ticks of the ruler, and the edges of the clip's blocks and windows. Hold Shift to move it freely. It stops where the clip says it must. An animation lasts at least 0.05 s, an intro and an outro together cannot be longer than the clip, and neither can run over a Transform of the whole formula (a keyframe), which cannot share its time with them. The whole drag is one undo step, and Esc during it cancels it. A clip on a locked track is selected by the press and is not changed. The same for an outro: the edge of the title's outro dragged to the left, then back to the right with Shift held."],["alt","A relation window lies over where an outro starts when a transformation takes glyphs out of the clip at the same moment, and an animation block can lie over an edge too. Pressed there, the window or the block answers, not the handle of the outro. Hold Alt. While it is down, the edges of the clips and the inner edges of their intros and outros light up, and nothing else in the lanes answers the pointer: not the blocks, not the windows, not the body of a clip. The edge is the clip's again. The window of the relation from step1 to step2 lies over the start of the outro of step1. Pressed without Alt, the window moves (Esc puts it back); with Alt held, the same press lengthens the outro. Alt also frees the edges of the clips themselves, where a block or a window ends with the clip. A dialog or a text field keeps the key for itself, and a locked track shows no lit edges."],["conflicts","An intro or an outro animates the whole object, so it cannot share time with some things on the clip. When you choose one from the clip's menu, the app checks first, and if the new animation, at its own length, would run into any of these it asks: a Transform of the whole formula (a keyframe) that it would cut or remove; the window of a transformation that takes glyphs from the clip (for an intro) or brings glyphs to it (for an outro); a block, or an attached object such as a brace, that needs the same glyphs while the animation runs. A keyframe at 1.2 s, and a two-second DrawBorderThenFill chosen as the intro: the dialog names what is in the way. The dialog says what the animation would meet, and you decide: Add it anyway gives it the whole length, and the clip is adjusted as far as it can be (a keyframe is cut or removed). Shorten it to… is offered when a shorter length avoids everything: the animation lasts only as long as fits. Cancel leaves the clip as it was. When there is no room at all (the clip is all outro, say), a notice says so and nothing changes. If you add one anyway and something is wrong, the Problems section of the Animations panel lists it."],["blocks","An intro always starts with the clip and an outro always ends with it. To bring glyphs in or take them out at another time, or only some of the glyphs, use an Appear or a Disappear block. Put the playhead at the time you want, right-click the clip or use Add animation in the Animations panel, and choose from Appear or Disappear: the block starts at the playhead. Add animation ▸ Appear lists the same animations as an intro, with Write first. Appear: Write, Create, FadeIn, GrowFromCenter and DrawBorderThenFill (and GrowArrow for an arrow). It brings glyphs that are off screen onto it. Disappear: FadeOut, Unwrite, Uncreate and ShrinkToCenter. It takes glyphs that are on screen off it, and a glyph it took out comes back with a later Appear. A line offers only what it can do. A new block lasts as long as that animation does by default, and you drag its edges to change it. It names the glyphs it animates, which you pick in its dialog or in the Animations panel (Animation blocks); a block of an arrow takes all of it (Lines and arrows)."],["appear-waits","An Appear block brings its glyphs in when it runs, wherever in the clip. Its rule keeps it from fighting with the intro: the glyphs an Appear block names do not take part in the intro. They wait for the block, unless a Disappear block takes them out earlier. The intro animates what no block names, and each block brings in its own glyphs when it runs. The Python the app exports for that scene shows it. The intro writes the first three glyphs of the lower formula, a + b, together with the whole upper one; the block fades in the other two, = c: # 0.4 s – 1.4 s: alone write, waiting write self.play(Write(VGroup(*alone_g[:])), Write(VGroup(*waiting_g[:3])), run_time=1) # ... # 2 s – 2.8 s: waiting fade_in self.play(FadeIn(VGroup(waiting_g[3], waiting_g[4])), run_time=0.8) The same applies to glyphs hidden at the start, to every glyph of a clip that starts empty and to glyphs that arrive through a transformation: the intro leaves them out too. If a block names every glyph, the intro has nothing left to animate, and the Animations panel says so, under Warnings: The Write intro of \"x\" animates no glyph: its glyphs wait for Appear blocks. The scene still plays; the intro is just a leftover you can take off. Blocks are the subject of Animation blocks."],["defaults","By default a new clip has no intro and no outro: it is there from its first instant to its last. Three settings decide what a new clip gets: The formula's own intro. The field Appears with in the formula editor holds the intro every new clip of that formula starts with, and its length. An intro you give a clip from its menu becomes what the formula says from then on. Appears with has its own section in Timing tools. The editor's preference. Edit ▸ Always add intro and Edit ▸ Always add outro, both off when you start, give every new clip an intro, an outro or both; the entries name the animation they will use, such as Always add intro (Write). The intro goes to the formulas whose Appears with says Default; the outro goes to every new clip. Which animations those are. Edit ▸ Default intro & outro… opens a dialog where you pick the intro and the outro, and set their length, easing, lag ratio and, for fades, shift and scale. The three entries in the Edit menu. Default intro & outro…. It is used for clips you add from now on; existing clips keep theirs. These are preferences of the editor, kept in the browser. They are not part of the project file, and they are not undoable."],["braces-shapes","A brace, a shape or a line you add from a clip's menu comes with no animation: its clip starts, and it is there. Give it one from its own menu, Intro, like any other clip: Create is the one Manim uses for a box, GrowFromCenter or FadeIn suit a brace. A box added around step3 shows at once, and its clip has no hatched ends. Intro ▸ Create, in the menu of its clip, gives it one. Braces, shapes and lines are the subject of Braces, Shapes and Lines and arrows."],["try-it","Open timeline-basics.mfe.json (from Working with the timeline): choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… in the dialog and pick the file (Projects and files). Right-click the clip title, on the top track, and choose Intro ▸ DrawBorderThenFill. Its hatched left end now says Border and is longer. Drag the dashed line where that intro ends, in the lower part of the clip, to the right: the intro lasts longer, and a readout says how long. Press Ctrl+Z once to take the whole drag back. Right-click it again and choose Outro ▸ Unwrite. Then hold Alt over the timeline and look at which edges light up. Open Edit and tick Always add intro (Write). Drag check from the Creator onto the timeline: its clip comes with a Write intro. (A new clip of title would come with DrawBorderThenFill: step 2 made that what the formula's Appears with says.)"],["next","For a formula that turns into another one, see Transforming one formula into another. Animations that happen during a clip are animation blocks, and Timing tools has the commands that copy a clip's intro and outro lengths."]],"short":"Intro and outro"},{"id":"basics/first-transform","url":"basics/first-transform.html","title":"Transforming one formula into another","section":"Basics","level":"basic","description":"The basic move of the editor: one formula on the timeline turns into the next.","headings":[["how","How a transformation works"],["clips","What a relation needs"],["making","Making one"],["timing","Timing: the window"],["playing","Playing it back"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A transformation is the basic move of the editor: one formula turns into the next, and each glyph travels to its new place. You make one by relating two clips on the timeline."],["how","A formula is a set of glyphs, the symbols the editor draws: x + 3 = 5 has five. A transformation sends the glyphs of one formula to the glyphs of the next. You say which goes where, and the editor animates the journey: the 3 of x + 3 = 5 slides across to become the 3 of x = 5 − 3. On the timeline a transformation is a relation between two clips. The source is the clip of the formula that turns into another one. The target is the clip of the formula it becomes. A relation holds pairs. Each pair links glyphs of the source to glyphs of the target: x to x, 3 to 3. The window is the stretch of time in which the glyphs travel. A glyph that no pair takes does not travel. A glyph of the source that is in no pair leaves with the outro of the source clip. A glyph of the target that is in no pair enters with the intro of the target clip. In the example above the + fades out and the − fades in. Here is the same scene without the relations, and with them. Without them the formulas cross-fade. With them the glyphs travel. Here are the pairs of the first relation. Click a glyph of the top formula and then a glyph of the bottom one to link them yourself, and press Preview to see your transformation."],["clips","A relation needs two clips that are on different tracks, and overlap in time: for a stretch, the source is still on screen (after its intro) while the target already is (before its outro). The scene of this page has three clips of three formulas, step1, step2 and step3. They alternate between two tracks, and each overlaps the next by one second. You relate step1 to step2, and then step2 to step3. step1 and step3 are on the same track, so they cannot be related to each other. There are two ways to start a relation. With the pointer. Hold Shift: a round link port appears at the lower right of each clip. Click the port of the source clip, then click the target clip (or drag from the port onto it). With Shift held, every clip shows its link port, ringed here: the round mark at its lower right. From the Animations panel. Select the source clip. In the Relations tab, choose the other clip in Relate with (1), pick the direction (2) and press Create relation…. This route works from the keyboard too. When the two clips cannot be related, the panel says why (3). Choosing step3 for step1: Clips on the same track cannot be related. The button stays off. When the clips do not overlap, the notice reads The clips must overlap in time: the source must still be on screen (after its intro) while the target is (before its outro)."],["making","Open the project before the relations with File ▸ Open…: press Other file… in the dialog and pick the downloaded file (Projects and files explains the dialog). Then relate its three clips. Hold Shift, click the port of step1, then click the step2 clip. The relation editor opens. step1 is the source, on top, and step2 the target, below. Press Suggest links by label and then Accept all. The editor links the glyphs that are the same symbol: x, =, 5 and 3. The + and the − are left over. Press Save relation. The whole session is one step of the undo history. Relate step2 to step3 the same way: press Suggest links by label, then Accept all. This time only x and = are linked: the line above the drawing says 2 linked pairs · 3 glyphs leave · 1 glyph arrives. Nothing in 5 − 3 has the same symbol as the 2. Link the rest by hand. Click the 5 of the source, then Shift-click the − and the 3. Then click the 2 of the target: the three source glyphs are linked to it in one pair. In the list of pairs, set this pair's style to Fade transform, so that the three glyphs fade into the 2 instead of twisting into its shape. Press Save relation. One pair of three glyphs and one: click 5, Shift-click − and 3, then click 2. A pair has one of four styles. The relation has a default, and each pair can have its own. Style What the glyphs do Transform The source glyphs turn into the target glyphs and are gone from where they were. Copy (source stays) A copy of the source glyphs travels to the target glyphs. The source glyphs stay where they are. Fade transform The source glyphs fade out as they move and resize onto the target glyphs, which fade in from the place of the source. Fade copy (source stays) A copy of the source glyphs fades into the target glyphs. The source glyphs stay where they are. Here is the second relation played with each style, side by side. Watch what the source glyphs do and what happens to the target. Try the pair that no label can suggest, in the widget below. It starts with no pairs: press Suggest links by label and accept them, which links the x and the =. Then link the 5, the − and the 3 to the 2, and press Preview. Your two relations make the scene of First transform, the example at the top of this page."],["timing","A relation's window is, by default, one second long, and it sits where the two clips overlap: it starts where the playhead is when a window fits there, and otherwise as soon as both clips allow. On the timeline a relation is drawn as an arrow from the source clip to the target clip (1), a band on the target clip, the window (2), and a dashed outline on the source clip (3), where the glyphs are taken from. The arrow (1), the window on the target (2) and its source (3). Click the band to select the relation. The Animations panel then shows its pairs, and its timing: Offset is where the window starts, counted from the start of the target clip, and Duration is how long it lasts. You can also drag the band to move the window, or its edges to make it longer or shorter. The panel of a selected relation: the pairs (1), the window (2) and the default style (3). The window cannot leave the stretch where both clips are on screen: it starts after the intro of the source, and it ends before the source ends and before the outro of the target begins. Drag the window in the widget to see it. How the glyphs without a partner leave and enter is set in the Animation tab of the relation editor, under Unpaired glyphs: the outro of the source and the intro of the target, each with its duration. The two boxes under them make the outro start with the window and the intro end with it; Starts empty, between the two, keeps the unpaired glyphs of the target from arriving at all (How relations work)."],["playing","You can see a relation in three places. In the relation editor, switch from Link to Preview. The two formulas alone play the transformation in a loop, from a little before the window to a little after it, with the pairs as they stand now, before you save anything. In the Preview of the editor: put the playhead before the window and press Space. With Preview raw, the last key of the editor's toolbar: the glyphs go from one formula to the other in straight lines, as the pairs and styles say. The Preview view (1): a bar to choose the moment (2), then the time and how far through the transformation it is (3). To change a relation later, double-click its band, or right-click it and choose Edit pairs…. Delete relation is in the same menu. After a relation is saved, Ctrl+Z takes back the whole editing session at once."],["next","How relations work goes through every kind of pair, the styles and what unpaired glyphs do. The relation editor covers linking, the pair list and the Animation tab. Previewing a transformation has Preview and Preview raw in detail. Transformation recipes shows the moves of algebra: moving a term, cancelling, expanding. Your first animation builds a transformation from an empty project, step by step."]],"short":"First transformation"},{"id":"basics/export","url":"basics/export.html","title":"Exporting","section":"Basics","level":"basic","description":"Export the scene as ManimCE or ManimGL Python, as a video, as a picture of one frame, as its audio alone, or as a web page that plays it.","headings":[["overview","What you can export"],["menu","The Export… button"],["python","Python for ManimCE and ManimGL"],["running","Running the Python"],["video","Video"],["image","An image"],["audio","Audio"],["html","A web page"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"Export the scene as ManimCE or ManimGL Python, as a video, as a picture of one frame, as its audio alone, or as a web page that plays it. The Python is the way to Manim: you run it there and get the animation in Manim's own renderer."],["overview","Everything is in the arrow of the Export… button, next to the menus. Each entry opens a dialog. Entry What you get What it needs Python — ManimCE… A .py scene for ManimCE Nothing, to export. Manim, to run it. Python — ManimGL… A .py scene for ManimGL Nothing, to export. Manim, to run it. Video… with Browser An MP4 or a WebM, encoded in the page Only the browser. Video… with Companion · ffmpeg An MP4, WebM, MOV (ProRes) or GIF The companion, with ffmpeg. Video… with Render with Manim The video Manim renders from the exported scene The companion, started to allow it, and your Manim. Image… The frame at the playhead, as a PNG picture Only the browser. Audio… The sound of the project: a WAV, a WebM or an M4A Only the browser. HTML… A web page that plays the scene live and stops at its pauses: a folder, a zip or one file Only the browser. Which one do you need? You want the scene as Manim code, to render with Manim or to change by hand: Python. You want a video now, and have no Manim: Video with Browser. You want a GIF, a ProRes file, or an H.264 file without the specks the browser's encoder can leave: Video with Companion · ffmpeg. You want the video Manim itself draws, without leaving the editor: Video with Render with Manim. You want one moment of the scene as a still picture: Image. You want the sound alone: Audio. You want to present the scene in a talk that stops where you say, put it in a web page of your own, or go on with it in code: HTML (Slides and the HTML page). An export is not a way to save your work. The Python and the video cannot be opened in the editor again. Keep the project itself with File ▸ Save, which writes its file. File ▸ Open… opens it again, from the list of your project folder or with Other file… (Projects and files)."],["menu","The Export… button is the mint button to the right of the menus: the bar has no Export menu. Pressing it opens the dialog of the last Python target you used, ManimCE at first. Its arrow lists the six exports, and this documentation writes an entry of that list as Export ▸ Video…. The Export… button (1) and its arrow (2), with the six exports."],["python","Choose Export ▸ Python — ManimCE… or Export ▸ Python — ManimGL…. The dialog writes the scene as you watch, and changes it as you change its options. Nothing is saved until you press Download <scene name>.py or Copy. The Python dialog, for the scene of First transform. Scene name: the name of the Python class and of the file. It starts as the name of the project, written as a Python name (First transform becomes FirstTransform). Type another name in the field, or rename the project in the menu bar. Always use the positional helper: finds every glyph by its position when the scene runs, also for formulas the companion rendered exactly. Without it the file uses the exact index of each glyph where it has one. Comments (formulas, timing): the comments that name each formula and each step with its times. Untick it for a bare file. The notes. They list what the scene cannot guarantee. For a formula the browser typeset with MathJax they say that its glyphs are found by position when the scene runs, because MathJax and LaTeX can order glyphs differently; the scene still plays as the Preview does. With nothing to say, the dialog reads The scene reproduces the preview: exact glyph indices, whole frames., or, with the positional helper ticked, The scene reproduces the preview: glyphs matched by position when it runs, whole frames. Copy puts the code on the clipboard. Download <scene name>.py saves the file under that name: FirstTransform.py here. The code is one MathTex (ManimCE) or Tex (ManimGL) per clip, and then plain self.play(…) and self.wait(…) calls, one for each step of the timeline, each in a whole number of frames. This is the file for First transform, in both versions: The first lines of the file are comments that tell you how to render it. Export the version for the Manim you have installed. The two differ in small ways, which ManimCE and ManimGL lists. The editor writes for ManimCE 0.22 and ManimGL 1.7."],["running","You need Manim installed, as its own documentation says: ManimCE (the Community Edition) or ManimGL (the version of 3Blue1Brown). Put the downloaded file in a folder, open a terminal there, and run the command written in the first lines of the file. For a project named First transform they are these. ManimCE: manim -r 1920,1080 --fps 60 FirstTransform.py FirstTransform ManimGL: manimgl FirstTransform.py FirstTransform -w -r 1920x1080 --fps 60 The command gives Manim the file, the name of the scene to render, and the size and frame rate of your project (set in File ▸ Project settings…). The video lasts exactly as long as the timeline, because every step is a whole number of frames. ManimGL needs -w to write a movie file; without it, it opens its window instead. ManimCE writes the video under media/videos/, in a folder named after the file and one named after the size and frame rate (1080p60 here). Add -p to play it when it is done. For a quick render, leave out -r and --fps, which would override the quality flag, and use -ql: manim -ql -p FirstTransform.py FirstTransform renders at 854×480 and 15 frames a second. ManimGL writes FirstTransform.mp4 to its output folder, videos by default. Add -o to open the file when it is done. For a quick render, leave out -r and --fps and use -l: manimgl FirstTransform.py FirstTransform -w -l -o renders at 480p. If the shell does not find manim, python -m manim, with the Python that has Manim installed, does the same. This is what ManimCE makes of the file above, rendered with -ql: Why the exported scene has a helper at its top, how close it is to the Preview, and how to change it by hand are on The generated Python."],["video","Export ▸ Video… makes a video file of the scene, without Manim. You choose how it is encoded, and its size, its frame rate and the part of the scene it covers. The video dialog without the companion: only Browser is on. Encoder. Each choice says what it does. Browser: Encoded here with WebCodecs. No installation needed. Companion · ffmpeg: The companion encodes the frames with ffmpeg: MP4, WebM, ProRes and GIF. Render with Manim: The companion renders the exported Python scene with your Manim: the official look. The last two need the companion, a small program you run on your computer; without it they are greyed out, and each says what is missing (The companion). Format. For the browser: MP4 · H.264, MP4 · AV1 or WebM · VP9, those of them that the browser can encode. With ffmpeg: MP4 · H.264, WebM · VP9, MOV · ProRes 422 HQ or GIF, and a Quality from Low (small file) to High (large file) for the first two. With Manim there is no format: Manim chooses ManimCE (Community Edition) or ManimGL (3b1b), and the scene is converted when the project targets the other one. For the browser, the dialog estimates how large the file will be. Size, Frame rate and Range: the project's own, or others. Render with Manim always renders the whole scene, and the dialog says so under Range. The line under them says how many frames the video has, how long it is and at what size. Export starts it. A bar shows how far it is, and Cancel export stops it. The same dialog with the companion online: the three encoders are on (1). With Render with Manim chosen, a Manim field (2) asks which Manim. The file is named after the project (First transform.mp4), and the video lasts exactly the frames of the range divided by the frame rate. In Chrome and Edge the dialog asks where to save the file and writes it as it encodes; in other browsers it assembles the file in memory and downloads it at the end. Exporting video goes through the three encoders, their formats and their limits. Chrome on Linux has no AAC encoder. A project with sound exported to MP4 in the browser carries Opus sound, which some players and video editors do not play, and the dialog says so before it starts. The companion's ffmpeg can encode AAC."],["image","Export ▸ Image… writes the frame the playhead is at as a PNG picture. It is the frame a video shows at that instant: through the scene's camera when it has one, and with the tracks you hid in the Preview drawn too, since only the Preview hides them (the buttons of a track). The image dialog, for the frame at 1.5 s of First transform. Frame: the instant it draws, in seconds and as minutes, seconds and frames. Opening the dialog stops the playback, so that this instant is the one of the picture: put the playhead where you want it first. Size: 1× is the project's size; 2× is twice it each way. The scene is curves, so the larger picture is sharper, not stretched. Transparent leaves the scene's background out: what is drawn, over nothing, to lay over another picture. Export draws it. It waits while a formula is rendering. As the video dialog does, the dialog tells of the formulas on screen at that instant that it cannot draw as they are written: one whose LaTeX fails is drawn as its last good render, or left out when it never had one; a font text nothing has shaped is drawn as its placeholder boxes; one with nothing to draw it holds the picture back. The file is named after the project and the instant (First transform 1.50s.png), so the pictures of several frames have each their name. In Chrome and Edge the dialog asks where to save it; in other browsers it is downloaded, and Download again downloads it once more. The dialog then says what it saved, with the size of the picture in pixels and of the file. The picture is the one of the click on Export, whatever changes while the browser asks where it goes. The dialog opens with the size and the background you left it at, while the page is open: for several transparent frames you tick Transparent once. A page loaded again starts from 1×, with the background."],["audio","Export ▸ Audio… writes the sound of the project alone: the recordings of the timeline with the volume curves and the muted tracks applied, mixed to stereo at 48 kHz. It does not draw any formula. The audio dialog: format (1), the part to export (2) and the button (3). Choose WAV · PCM 16-bit or WebM · Opus, and a start and an end in seconds. M4A · AAC is offered where the browser can encode AAC. The file is named after the project (First transform.wav). A project without recordings still exports: the file is silent. To put the sound in a video, tick Include audio · volume curves and track mute in the video dialog, which appears when the project has recordings (Audio). The exported Python has no sound: when the project has recordings, the Python dialog says Audio is omitted from Manim exports."],["html","Export ▸ HTML… writes the scene as a web page that is the animation itself: the editor's own engine draws it as it plays, sharp at any size, and it opens from disk without a server. It stops at each pause of the Slides mode until its reader goes on, so that you can present the scene in a talk; it can be embedded in a page of your own, and its folder is written for a coding agent to extend. Slides and the HTML page covers it, from the pauses to the agent."],["next","The generated Python and ManimCE and ManimGL explain the scene file. Exporting video covers the encoders and the companion. Slides and the HTML page covers the web page: pauses, presenting, embedding it, and handing it to a coding agent. Your first animation ends with an export, step by step."]],"short":"Exporting"},{"id":"basics/slides","url":"basics/slides.html","title":"Slides and the HTML page","section":"Basics","level":"intermediate","description":"Put pauses and comments on the scene’s time, rehearse the talk, and export the scene as a web page that plays it live and stops at each pause.","headings":[["on","Turning the mode on"],["ruler","The ruler by zone"],["add","Adding a pause or a comment"],["all-pauses","A long scene: all the pauses at once"],["delete-pauses","Deleting every pause"],["marks","Moving and editing a mark"],["keys","From the keyboard"],["covered","When an edit covers a mark"],["rehearsal","Rehearsing the talk"],["skip-wait","A pause that skips its wait"],["skip-ruler","What the ruler shows"],["skip-all","At every pause at once"],["skip-rehearsal","In the rehearsal and on the page"],["python","The marks in the exported Python"],["export-html","Export ▸ HTML"],["present","Presenting with the page"],["embed","Embedding the scene in a page of your own"],["agents","Handing the folder to a coding agent"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A scene made in the editor is meant to be shown, and a talk stops where the speaker says. The Slides mode puts pauses and comments on the scene’s time; Export ▸ HTML… turns the scene into a web page that plays it live and stops at each pause until you go on. Here is one, embedded in this page with its own tags: what Export ▸ HTML… makes of First transform, as a talk, the scene of Your first transform with a pause after each of its first two steps, each with a note, and a comment where the answer arrives. The pictures of this page show the same project. Press Play: the scene stops at each pause. Then press Next, or click the scene and use → and ←; S shows the notes. The first pause skips its wait: after the first stop, going on starts the first transformation at once, with no rest before it. Not a video: the editor’s engine draws the scene as it plays, and stops at the pauses at 1.00 s and 3.20 s. Going on from the first, it jumps over the rest to 2.20 s. The mode is for the end of the work, when the animation is done and you prepare to show it. It changes nothing of the scene: the Preview, the video and the animations of the exported Python are the same with it and without it. What it adds is read by the exports that can stop or carry words. A pause is an instant where the page stops until its reader goes on. It can carry a note: words for whoever presents. A comment is words for whoever works on the scene after the export, you or someone else, by hand or with a coding agent. The page shows it in its notes, the Python carries it as a comment, and the guide of the exported folder lists it as a task."],["on","Choose View ▸ Slides, or press the button with a screen after Snap in the timeline’s toolbar (1 in the picture below). The button is drawn on, as Snap is, and the ruler is tinted. The mode has no key of its own. The mode belongs to the project: it is saved with it, and turning it on or off is one undo step. It stays on while the project has a pause or a comment. Choosing it again then changes nothing, and a notice, Slides stays on, says how many marks are left: delete them first to turn it off."],["ruler","With the mode on, the ruler shows where a mark may stand. The toggle (1), a pause (2) and a comment (3) on the ruler of the example. The violet pause skips its wait (below). Blue-green is where the scene rests: nothing animates there, and a pause or a comment may stand. The edges count: the instant an animation ends, or the one before the next starts, is where a pause is wanted most. Hatched red-orange is where something animates: an intro or an outro, an animation block, a relation, a camera move. Animations that overlap make one stretch. A thin notch of blue-green is an instant of rest with no length: between two animations that touch, or at the start or the end of a scene that begins or ends with an animation. A red-orange zone takes no mark because a pause shows the scene at rest. A page stopped halfway through a transformation would show glyphs in flight, and the exported Python can only wait between two of its animations, not inside one. The zones are, in fact, the waits of the exported Python. The zones follow every edit as it is made: drag a clip and they move before you let it go."],["add","Right-click the ruler where the scene rests. The menu offers Add pause here, with the time it will have, and Add comment here…. The time is the one clicked, put on a frame; with Snap on it is pulled onto the playhead or the edge of the zone when they are near. Where something animates, the two entries are greyed out and a line says what runs there. Under a line, wherever you open it, the menu holds four commands over every pause: adding one after every animation, skipping or keeping the wait at each, and deleting them all. Where the scene rests. Where something animates. From the keyboard, put the playhead where the scene rests and press P for a pause or Shift+P for a comment; View ▸ Add pause at the playhead and View ▸ Add comment at the playhead… do the same. The keys work only while the mode is on and never in a text field. Where something animates, nothing is added and a notice says what runs then. A pause is a small tag with «‖» in the upper part of the ruler, with a thin dashed line down the tracks; a comment is a speech bubble. The new mark has the keyboard’s focus. A comment needs words, so Add comment here… asks for them first, in a dialog of several lines: Enter starts a new line and Ctrl+Enter gives the text. A comment without words cannot be added; Cancel adds nothing. A comment of two lines, a task for whoever goes on with the scene. The scene stops at an instant once: a second pause on a frame that has one is not added, and the pause already there takes the focus. A pause and a comment may share an instant; the comment is read first."],["all-pauses","In a scene of many steps, choose Add a pause after every animation: the first entry under the line of the ruler’s right-click menu, wherever you open it, and an entry of the View menu. It adds a pause where each animation ends and another follows, at the start of each rest between two animations. The start of the scene, where nothing has happened yet, and the time after its last animation get none. A rest that holds a pause already gets no other, so you can run it again after more work on the scene. A scene of four transformations: one choice adds four pauses and says so; one undo takes them all back. All of them are one undo step, and a notice says how many were added (Added 4 pauses), or why none was (No pause to add). Then delete the ones you do not want: the first pause added has the focus, Delete removes it and the focus goes on to the next mark, and Tab skips one you keep."],["delete-pauses","Delete every pause… is the way back: the last entry of the ruler’s right-click menu, in red, and an entry of the View menu, greyed out while the project has no pause. Every pause goes at once, with its note; the comments stay. It asks first. The question says how many pauses go and how many comments stay, and its red button names them (Hold to delete 4 pauses): hold it down for a moment, as for every deletion that asks; Cancel or Esc leaves everything as it was. Then it is one undo step: Ctrl+Z brings every pause back, and the notice says so (Deleted 4 pauses, and how many comments stay). The keyboard’s focus goes to the first comment left, or to the mode’s button in the toolbar when there is none."],["marks","Point at a mark and its words show at once under the ruler: what it is, when, and its text. The tip of a pause, with its note. A click puts the playhead on the mark. A drag moves it, on frames, inside the zone it stands in. It snaps as a new mark does; hold Shift to move it freely, and press Esc to cancel. A drag is one undo step. A double click writes its words, in the same dialog: a comment’s, which it cannot be without, or a pause’s note. Emptied, a note goes; a comment refuses to be emptied. A right-click opens its menu: Edit comment… for a comment, Add a note… or Edit note… for a pause, for a pause Skip the wait after it (below), and Delete."],["keys","Keys What they do P; Shift+P Add a pause at the playhead; add a comment there, its words asked for first. Tab Reaches the marks of the ruler one by one, in the order of their times. ← → Move the focused mark a frame, inside its zone (↑ ↓ in Zen mode, where the timeline is vertical). Delete, Backspace Delete the focused mark; the focus goes on to the next one. Enter Write the focused mark’s words. Shift+F10 Open the focused mark’s menu (the Menu key does too)."],["covered","A mark keeps its instant when clips move: it belongs to no clip. So an edit can put an animation over it — a clip dragged, an intro made longer — or leave it past the end of a scene made shorter. The editor asks at once, when you let go of the drag or the change is made: The clip of step1 dragged half a second later: its intro now runs over the first pause, its outro over the second. Delete them and keep the change deletes the marks the change covered. The change and the deletion are one undo step: one Ctrl+Z brings back the clip where it was and the marks with it. Take the change back, or Esc, puts the project as it was before the change, with nothing left to redo. An undo or a redo asks nothing. Nor does opening a file edited by hand with a mark under an animation or past the end: such a mark is drawn in amber, its tip says why, and you can drag it to where one may stand. Until then every export leaves it out and says so."],["rehearsal","With the mode on, the transport under the Preview has Stop at pauses. Turn it on, and Play stops the Preview at each pause exactly as the exported page will; Play again goes on to the next. Stop at pauses on: Play writes x + 3 = 5 and stops at 1.00 s; Play again skips the rest that follows, plays the first transformation at once and stops at 3.20 s. It is a way of playing, not part of the project: it is not saved, and the video is the whole scene whatever it says. From a pause that skips its wait, Play goes on where the next animation starts, as the page does (next section)."],["skip-wait","A scene made for a video has rests between its animations: a breath between two steps, time to read the last one. A talk that stopped at a pause has no use for the rest that follows it: you have just waited there, as long as you wanted. So a pause can skip its wait: going on from it, the exported page, and the rehearsal, jump to where the next animation starts. Right-click the pause and tick Skip the wait after it. The pause turns violet, its tag reads «‖▸», and a violet line with an arrowhead runs along the ruler from the pause to where the scene will go on. Untick it to keep the wait again. Either is one undo step. The first pause of the example, set to skip its wait. Where there is nothing to skip, the entry is greyed out and a line under it says why: An animation starts at this instant: there is no wait after it to skip. The pause stands where the next animation starts: at the end of a rest, or between two animations that touch. No animation follows this pause: the scene only ends after it. The rest after the last animation is kept, and it may hold the end of the scene’s sound. The exports leave this pause out: it is not where the scene rests. The pause is under an animation or past the end (above). Moving the pause, dragging it or writing its note keeps the setting. If an edit leaves it with nothing to skip, it stays violet without its line, its tip says it skips nothing there and why, and its entry is still there to untick."],["skip-ruler","Point at a pause that skips its wait, or reach it with Tab, and its tip says how long the wait is and where the scene goes on. Its dashed line down the tracks is violet too. The first pause skips the 1.20 s rest before the first transformation; the second keeps its wait."],["skip-all","View ▸ Skip the wait at every pause sets it on every pause that has a wait to skip, and leaves the others as they are: its notice counts them. View ▸ Keep the wait at every pause takes it off every pause. Both are in the ruler’s right-click menu too, wherever you open it, each is one undo step, and neither has a key. Both are greyed out while the project has no pause."],["skip-rehearsal","With Stop at pauses on, Play from a pause that skips its wait jumps over the rest: the playhead and the time leap to where the next animation starts, and the Preview plays from there, its sound too. When another pause stands in the same rest before that, the playhead goes to it and stays there. Stop at pauses on, the playhead at the first pause: Play jumps from 1.00 s to 2.20 s, plays the first transformation and stops at the second pause. The exported page does the same where it really stopped. Standing at such a pause, every way of going on (Play, Next, a key, a click on the scene) first jumps over the wait, the sound with it, then plays. Try it in the scene at the top of this page: after its first stop, Next starts the first transformation at once. On the bar, the notch of such a pause is violet and the wait it skips is dashed: The notch of the first pause is violet and the wait it skips dashed; the second pause, amber, keeps its wait. Played through its pauses (its Play through the pauses button, or data-pauses=\"off\" when it is embedded), the page stops nowhere and skips nothing: the rest plays, as in the Preview without Stop at pauses. The wait is still part of the scene: a press on the timeline inside the dashes shows that instant. Nothing else changes. The scene’s time and length are the same with the setting and without it, every video plays the rest, and the exported Python keeps its self.wait: only its # PAUSE line says that the page skips the wait (next section)."],["python","Export ▸ Python — ManimCE… and — ManimGL… write each mark as a comment at its instant in construct, for whoever goes on with the scene: a pause as # PAUSE n of N with its time and its note, a comment as # NOTE with its time and its words. This is the example’s ManimCE scene: # 0 s – 1 s: step1 write self.play(Write(VGroup(*step1_g[:])), run_time=1) # PAUSE 1 of 2 (1.00 s), the exported page skips the 1.20 s wait after it: Ask: what takes the 3 away from the x? # 1 s – 2.2 s self.wait(1.2) … # PAUSE 2 of 2 (3.20 s): Work out 5 − 3 together before going on. … # NOTE (5.40 s): Make the 2 of the answer glow once it is in place. # 5.4 s – 6.6 s self.wait(1.2) They are written with Comments (formulas, timing) unticked too: they are your words, not the editor’s. Each line of a comment’s text is a line of the Python comment. A mark in the middle of a wait splits the self.wait(…) in two that add up to the same frames, so the video keeps its length. A mark under an animation or past the end of the scene is left out, and the dialog lists it. A pause that skips its wait, the first one here, says so before its note, and the self.wait after it stays. Manim itself does not stop at them: they are comments. The page of the next section is what stops."],["export-html","Export ▸ HTML… writes the scene as a web page that is the animation: the editor’s own engine draws it as it plays, sharp at any size, rather than a video of it. It opens from disk with a double click: no server, no internet connection, nothing to install. It stops at each pause of the Slides mode until its reader goes on; a scene without the mode plays through. The dialog, with nothing to choose about size or frame rate: the page is drawn as it plays. Export as: a folder, to work on the scene and to embed it in another page, or one file, to send to someone or to present from: the same page with everything inside it, a single .html. Slides: how many pauses and comments the page carries, how many of the pauses skip their wait (1 of them skips the wait after it), and, in amber, each mark it leaves out because it stands under an animation or past the end. The buttons. In Chrome and Edge, Choose where… asks for a folder and writes the scene inside it, as a folder of the scene’s name; Download <scene>.zip downloads the same folder as a zip, which is the way in browsers that cannot write a folder. With One file the button is Download <scene>.html. A project with recordings gets one more line, The scene’s sound: the whole sound of the scene, mixed as the Preview plays it, beside the page as audio.m4a (or inside it, for one file). It plays with the scene and stops at its pauses. The scene’s name is the project’s name in small letters and hyphens: First transform, as a talk is first-transform-as-a-talk. Chrome on Linux has no AAC encoder: the sound is written uncompressed, as audio.wav, and the dialog says so and how large it will be. Exported again over a folder written before, the scene keeps custom.js, player.css and index.html as they are: what you or an agent added to the scene is in them, and survives the scene going back to the editor. A notice names the files it left alone. The sound an earlier export wrote there is removed when the new one does not use it: the scene exported without its sound, or from a browser that writes the other kind of file. An audio.m4a or audio.wav that is not the file the app wrote may be yours: it stays, and the notice, in amber, says so."],["present","Open index.html (or the single file) in a browser. The scene takes the window, with a bar under it. The page at its first pause, the end of slide 1 of 3. Previous, Play and Next. Next plays to the next pause, or jumps there at once while the scene plays; Previous goes back to the pause before, or to the start. From a pause that skips its wait, Play and Next first jump over that wait. The timeline, with a notch at each pause, violet for a pause that skips its wait, the wait it skips dashed: a press there goes to that time, on a notch to the pause exactly, and a drag scrubs. The slide and the time. A scene with two pauses has three slides. Play through the pauses, the notes and full screen. A scene with sound also has a mute button. A click on the scene plays or pauses it. The keys are the ones of a presentation program, so that a presenter’s remote works: Keys What they do → ↓ Space PageDown N Enter Go on to the next pause, over the wait of a pause that skips it ← ↑ PageUp P Backspace Shift+Space Back to the pause before Home, End The start, the end K Play or pause F Full screen, and back S Show or hide the notes B or . Black the stage out, and back M Sound off and on, when the scene has sound The notes are for whoever presents: the note of the last pause and the comments of the slide you are on, under the slide’s number. The stage grows smaller to leave them room. The notes of the last slide: the note of the second pause and the example’s comment. In full screen the bar and the pointer hide after a moment of rest, and come back when the pointer moves. The notes then float over the stage. While the stage is black, the first key or click brings the scene back where it was and does nothing else. A presenter’s remote such as the Logitech R400, R700 or R800 sends PageDown, PageUp and .: next, back and black. Its start button sends F5, which reloads the page: press F for full screen instead. A browser plays sound only after a key or a click on the page: the first key you press is enough. The address of the page takes the options of the next section: index.html?controls=minimal&autoplay shows only Play and the timeline and starts at once, and index.html?controls=none gives the stage the whole window."],["embed","Copy the folder next to your page and add its stylesheet, an element that names the scene and its scripts. The element takes the width of what holds it, and keeps the scene’s shape: <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"first-transform-as-a-talk/player.css\"> … <div class=\"mfe-deck\" data-scene=\"first-transform-as-a-talk\"></div> … <script src=\"first-transform-as-a-talk/mfe-runtime.js\"></script> <script src=\"first-transform-as-a-talk/glyphs.js\"></script> <script src=\"first-transform-as-a-talk/scene.js\"></script> <script src=\"first-transform-as-a-talk/custom.js\"></script> <!-- when the scene has code of its own --> The engine first, then the outlines and the scene: the engine mounts every .mfe-deck once the page has loaded. A page can hold several scenes, each with its own two files and one engine for all. The scene at the top of this page is embedded so, its controls following the site’s theme through data-theme. A site that takes no scripts of yours can show the scene’s own page in a frame, its options in the address: <iframe src=\"first-transform-as-a-talk/index.html?controls=minimal\" title=\"First transform, as a talk\" allow=\"fullscreen\" style=\"width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; border: 0\"></iframe> The folder’s embed-example.html is a page that does both, with what to copy marked. An embedded scene hears its keys only while the focus is inside it, so that the arrows and Space stay your page’s; a click on it gives it the focus. Its options are data- attributes of its element: Attribute What it does data-controls full (the default), minimal (Play and the timeline) or none (no bar, and no keys) data-notes=\"off\" No notes: neither their button nor the S key. The notes show the scene’s comments, which are tasks for whoever works on it, and the notes of its pauses: turn them off in a page for readers (minimal keeps the keys and the notes) data-autoplay, data-loop Plays when it comes on screen (never for a reader who asked for less motion); starts again at the end data-start The second it shows before it plays data-pauses=\"off\" Plays through the pauses, and skips no wait data-background=\"transparent\" No background of its own: your page shows behind the scene data-theme light or dark controls; without it, the reader’s system decides data-speed How fast it plays: 0.5, 2… data-keys focus (the default), page (keys heard anywhere on the page) or none data-renderer=\"canvas\" Draws on a canvas instead of SVG, for a scene too heavy for the browser’s SVG Its look is player.css, which is yours to change. Every rule of it stays under .mfe-deck, so nothing in it reaches the rest of your page, and a rule of yours with the same classes, written after it, wins. The simplest change is a custom property, set from your own stylesheet: <style> .my-article .mfe-deck { --mfe-accent: #ff8a3d; /* the played part */ --mfe-pause-mark: #ffd166; /* a pause's notch */ --mfe-bar-background: #1b1b2f; /* the bar */ --mfe-radius: 0; /* square buttons */ } </style> The head of player.css lists every custom property and every class, which are the contract a page’s rules and scripts may rely on. The folder’s guide (AGENTS.md, «Changing the look») has more: how to give the controls your page’s font, what to do with a light page, and the CSS for controls that only appear under the pointer."],["agents","The folder is written to be extended, by you or by a coding agent that has never seen the editor: Claude Code, Codex, or any other. Give it the folder and say what you want. What it finds there: AGENTS.md, its guide, which opens with your comments as its tasks: each with its instant, what ended before it, what starts after it and what is on screen. The notes of the pauses follow, as context. Then the files, how to look at the scene and check it, the two ways to extend it, the stage and its coordinates, embedding, restyling and the pitfalls. CLAUDE.md imports it for Claude Code; README.md is one screen for a person. scene.js, the animation as data you can read: its head comment names the clips (step1, step2…), the glyphs of each formula by index, and every event of the timeline with its time and where it is in the data. Edited and reloaded, the page plays the edit. custom.js, for code of your own: it runs after the scene is loaded, and reaches the scene through window.MFE (an animation of its own on the scene’s time, the place of a clip on the stage, the stage’s free layers). The stage is SVG: every clip is a group named by data-clip, every glyph a path. node check.mjs, which says what is wrong with an edited scene without a browser, by line of scene.js, and OK when it plays as written; node check.mjs <folder> checks a copy of the scene’s files in another folder. types/, the types of the page’s API and of the scene, made from the editor’s sources. The guide of the scene at the top of this page begins with its comment, as a task: ## 1. What the author wants done next The author left these comments at instants of the scene for whoever works on it next. Treat each as a task. Times are seconds of the scene; names are the clips’ (section 4). 1. **5.40 s** (`slides.marks[2]`) — after `step2 → step3 (transform, 3 pairs)`, nothing after; on screen: `step3`. > Make the 2 of the answer glow once it is in place. and its scene.js with the timeline in words, each line with where it is in the data below it: * The clips, by the names the page gives them (data-clip=\"…\", deck.clip('…')): * step1 clips[0] 0.00–3.20 s formulas[0] math “x + 3 = 5” * step2 clips[1] 2.20–5.40 s formulas[1] math “x = 5 - 3” * step3 clips[2] 4.40–6.60 s formulas[2] math “x = 2” … * What happens, in order (seconds of the scene; each line ends with where it is here): * 0.00–1.00 s step1 write clips[0].intro * 1.00 s PAUSE 1 (skips its wait to 2.20 s) · “Ask: what takes the 3 away from the x?” slides.marks[0] * 2.20–3.20 s step1 fade_out clips[0].outro * 2.20–3.20 s step2 fade_in clips[1].intro * 2.20–3.20 s step1 → step2 (transform, 4 pairs) relations[0] … * 5.40 s NOTE · “Make the 2 of the answer glow once it is in place.” slides.marks[2] When you export the scene again from the editor, custom.js, player.css and index.html are kept (above): the agent’s code goes on working on the new scene. Write the comments as tasks while the scene is in front of you: “Make the 2 of the answer glow once it is in place” at the instant it arrives tells an agent what, where and when, and the guide hands it all three."],["next","Exporting covers the other exports: Python, video, the frame as an image, and audio. The generated Python explains the scene file the marks go into. Keyboard shortcuts and Menus list every key and entry of the mode."]],"short":"Slides and HTML"},{"id":"transforms/relations","url":"transforms/relations.html","title":"How relations work","section":"Transformations","level":"intermediate","description":"Pairs of glyphs, their styles, one-to-many and many-to-one, the glyphs left unpaired, and timing.","headings":[["idea","The idea"],["pairs","Pairs"],["one-to-one","One-to-one"],["one-to-many","One-to-many"],["many-to-one","Many-to-one"],["extras","Several sources, one target: how they arrive"],["pair-rules","What a pair cannot do"],["try-linking","Try it: link the glyphs yourself"],["styles","Styles"],["unpaired","Glyphs that belong to no pair"],["starts-empty","Only what the pairs bring: a target that starts empty"],["timing","Timing"],["timing-timeline","On the timeline"],["twice","More than once between the same two clips"],["path-arc","Path arc"],["managing","Finding, editing and removing relations"],["python","In the exported Python"],["examples","The examples of this page"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A relation turns one formula into the next. It links two clips and says, glyph by glyph, where each glyph goes, how it travels and when. Every animation on this page plays in your browser, and four small tools let you try the ideas yourself."],["idea","On the timeline a formula is a clip. A relation links two clips: the source, which is on screen first, and the target, which takes over. While the relation runs, the glyphs of the source move to their places in the target. A relation is made of three things. Pairs. Each pair says which glyphs of the source become which glyphs of the target. The swap above has three, one for each glyph. A style for each pair: how its glyphs get there. They move, they move as copies, they fade into each other, or a copy of them fades. A window. The stretch of the timeline during which the transformation runs. The two clips must be on different tracks, and they must overlap in time: the source has to be on screen (after its intro) while the target is already there (before its outro). The window lies inside that overlap. A clip can be in several relations. The target of one and the source of the next make a chain, the way a derivation goes from line to line (the quadratic formula tutorial chains six). A clip can also be the source of several relations, to feed several formulas."],["pairs","A pair names some glyphs of the source and some glyphs of the target, and says that the first become the second. The editor numbers the glyphs of every formula (0, 1, 2… in the order the renderer gives them) and keeps the pairs by those numbers. You make pairs in the relation editor, by clicking glyphs. Here you only look at what they mean."],["one-to-one","The simplest pair joins one glyph to one glyph. The glyph goes to its place, and its shape and its colour change into those of its partner. The swap above has three of them. The swap in the relation editor. The source is on top, the target below. Each pair is a link with a colour of its own and a badge: its number, and its style (I:T means Transform, inherited from the relation). The red dashes round the sources say that their pairs consume them (below)."],["one-to-many","A glyph of the source can go to several glyphs of the target in one pair. It splits. The same relation in the editor. The a of the source has two links, one to each a of the target; the parentheses have none. Manim makes it work by padding the shorter side with invisible copies of its glyphs until both sides have the same number. The second a is such a copy: it starts invisible, on top of the first, and shows as it moves away. The exported Python shows the pair as one animation of two groups: ReplacementTransform(VGroup(before_g[0]), VGroup(after_g[0], after_g[3])),"],["many-to-one","Several glyphs of the source can go to one glyph of the target. They merge. The two x of xx link to the x of x², through one badge. The 2 belongs to no pair. A glyph of the target arrives through one pair only, so the sources of this pair share it. How they reach it is a choice, and the next section shows it."],["extras","A pair with several sources and one target has an extra choice in the pair list, Sources. It decides how the sources beyond the first reach the target. The three players below run the same relation, xx into x², with each choice. Play the three together, on the same pair, and compare how the second x reaches the target: Choice What happens In the exported Python Separate Each source makes its own transform onto the target. All of them arrive. ReplacementTransform(source_g[0], target_g[0]), Transform(source_g[1], target_g[0], remover=True), Merge (Manim) Manim aligns the group of sources with the group of targets: the first source becomes the target, and the others fade into it. ReplacementTransform(VGroup(source_2_g[0], source_2_g[1]), VGroup(target_2_g[0])), Copies The first source, the principal, transforms. Each other source sends a copy and stays. ReplacementTransform(source_3_g[0], target_3_g[0]), Transform(source_3_g[1].copy(), target_3_g[0], remover=True), The editor shows which source is the principal (Principal: x (0)), and keeps it when the glyphs are renumbered. In a Transform or Fade pair, Separate and Merge (Manim) consume every source; Copies consumes only the principal. With Copies the principal plays the style of the pair, and the other sources send a plain copy, whatever that style."],["pair-rules","A glyph of the target arrives through one pair only. A glyph of the source is consumed by one pair only: once it has turned into other glyphs it is gone, and another pair can only copy it. The relation editor outlines such a source in red dashes. To send one glyph to two places, put both places in the same pair (a split), or make its pairs Copy or Fade copy: a copied glyph stays, and can be copied again. A glyph that was consumed cannot come back as the target of a later relation, and a source has to have arrived: a glyph that another relation is still bringing in cannot be a source yet. These rules count over the whole project, not inside one relation: two relations into the same clip cannot bring the same glyph, whichever clips they come from. The relation editor refuses the links it can tell are wrong, dims the glyphs another relation has taken, and lists the other problems before you save. The relation editor shows them."],["try-linking","This is a light version of the editor, built on the example of the one-to-many section. Click a glyph of the source and a glyph of the target to link them, then press Play to see what your pairs do. Link the a of the source to the first a of the target, and play: only one a travels, and the other one shows up when its clip begins, because it has no partner. Link the a to both a of the target, and play again: it splits. Leave the parentheses unlinked, as the example does, and watch them fade out."],["styles","The style of a pair says how its glyphs get from the source to the target. The relation has a Default style, and each pair either follows it (Inherit) or has a style of its own. Style What the glyphs do The source glyphs In Manim Transform Move and change shape into the target glyphs. Consumed: gone afterwards. ReplacementTransform(source_g[0], target_g[0]) Copy (source stays) A copy of each glyph moves and changes shape into the target glyph. Stay where they are, until the outro of the source clip. ManimCE: ReplacementTransform(source_2_g[0].copy(), target_2_g[0]) ManimGL: TransformFromCopy(source_2_g[0], target_2_g[0]) Fade transform The source fades out while it stretches onto the box of the target, and the target fades in from the box of the source. The shapes are not morphed into each other. Consumed. FadeTransform(source_3_g[0], target_3_g[0]) Fade copy (source stays) A copy of each glyph fades out while it stretches onto the box of the target, and the target fades in. Stay where they are, until the outro of the source clip. FadeTransform(source_4_g[0].copy(), target_4_g[0]) Here is the same pair of formulas, a + b above and α + β below, with each style in turn, and then with three of them in a single relation. The last relation in the editor. The badges give the number and the style of each pair: I:T is Transform inherited from the relation, C is Copy and F is Fade transform; a Fade copy reads FC. The a and the b, which their pairs consume, are outlined in red dashes; the plus sign, copied, is not. Use Copy to repeat a glyph or a term somewhere else without losing the original. Use Fade transform when two glyphs have nothing in common: a morph between unrelated shapes looks strange, a fade does not. Use Fade copy when the source must stay and the two glyphs have nothing in common to morph. Try the four on a relation of your own:"],["unpaired","Not every glyph has a partner. The glyphs of the source that no pair consumes leave, and the glyphs of the target that no pair brings arrive. They do it with the animations of their clips: the glyphs of the source that no pair takes leave with the outro of the source clip; the glyphs of the target that no pair brings arrive with the intro of the target clip. A glyph that a block of its clip takes care of is the block's: one that a Disappear or a Morph block of the source takes away before the outro, or one that an Appear or a Morph block of the target brings in. The outro and the intro leave it alone, and the relation editor does not count it with them. In the example below, a + b = c becomes a = c − b. The plus sign belongs to no pair and fades out; the minus sign belongs to none and fades in. The relation editor counts them in the line over the drawing, for example 4 linked pairs · 1 glyph leaves · 1 glyph arrives. The settings in the relation editor. (1) The outro of the source clip: what the unpaired glyphs of the source do. (2) Whether it runs during the relation window. (3) Whether the target starts empty. (4) The intro of the target clip. Choose another animation in the lists (Unwrite, ShrinkToCenter, Write, GrowFromCenter…), or None (disappear) and None (appear) so that the glyphs vanish or show up at once. The outro and the intro belong to the clips: changing them here changes those clips. The outro of a clip sits at its end. If the source clip goes on after the window, its unpaired glyphs stay until the outro begins. Below, the clip goes on for almost three seconds after the window: the plus sign hangs over the equals sign until it fades. Two things fix it. Shorten the source clip so that it ends with the window, or tick Run it during the relation window (ends with the source clip): the outro then starts with the window and ends with the clip. Run it up to the end of the relation window (starts with the target clip) does the same for the intro of the target."],["starts-empty","Sometimes the glyphs of the target that no pair brings should not arrive at all, or not yet. Below, a = b becomes a² = b², and no pair brings the two exponents. With an intro of None (appear) they show up at once, when the target clip starts, before anything has moved: Tick Starts empty for the target, and no glyph of that clip comes in with its intro. Each glyph appears only when something brings it: a pair of a relation, a Morph block of the clip, or an Appear block. A glyph nothing brings never shows. This is what a Morph block does inside one formula, between two clips. The box is in two places, and both set the same thing on the target clip: in the relation editor, on the Animation tab, under Unpaired glyphs, for the target of the relation; in the Animations panel, on the Timing tab of the clip, at the top of its Intro section. With the box ticked, the line over the drawing counts those glyphs as staying out, for example 3 linked pairs · 0 glyphs leave · 2 glyphs stay out. The intro of the clip keeps its animation and its time but has nothing to animate, so its fields in the editor wait. To bring a glyph in later, add an Appear block to the target clip and choose the glyph: Hidden at start, in the block’s glyph picker, picks every glyph of a clip that starts empty. Starts empty belongs to the clip, not to one relation: every relation into that clip finds it empty. A clip that starts empty and that nothing ever fills never shows: its panel says so, under Warnings, and a notice tells you when an edit leaves it that way (its relation removed, for one). Only a formula or a text can start empty: a brace, a shape or a line is one piece."],["timing","The window of a relation is the stretch of time in which its glyphs move. It is counted from the start of the target clip: the Offset is how long after the target starts the window opens, and the Duration is how long it lasts. The window has to lie inside the overlap of the clips: after the intro of the source, and before the end of the source; after the start of the target, and before its outro. The Animation tab of the editor says it in words, for example Window 3.50 → 5.00 s (allowed 2.00 → 7.00 s). A new relation is made where the playhead is. When you ask for one, its window starts at the yellow line of the timeline, one second long or as long as the clips leave after it. When no window fits there, because the playhead is outside the overlap or too close to its end, the window starts as soon as both clips allow, and the Animation tab says so under the window: No window fits at the playhead (0.00 s): the two clips allow one within 2.00–7.00 s. It starts at 2.00 s instead. With a region marked on the timeline’s ruler, the window of a new relation is the region instead, wherever the playhead is. When the two clips do not allow a window all along the region, no relation is made, and the editor says why. Drag the handles below to move the window. The scene plays with the timing you choose."],["timing-timeline","A relation is drawn over the two clips: a band on the source clip and a band on the target clip mark the window, and an arrow joins the two clips. The relations of the selected clips are drawn in full; the others are faint. Click an arrow or a band to select the relation. A relation with an error is drawn in red, the edges of its bands and its arrow, and one with only warnings in amber; select it to read them at the end of the Animations panel, under Problems or Warnings. The selected relation on the timeline. (1) The window on the source clip. (2) The arrow that leaves the source. (3) The window on the target clip. Drag the band to move the window, and its edges to change its length. A readout above the band gives the start, the end and the length while you drag. With Snap on, the window sticks to the ticks of the ruler, to the playhead, and to the edges of the blocks, the intros, the outros and the relation windows of the two clips; hold Shift to move freely. The window stops where it would leave the overlap, and where it would clash with an animation block or another relation on the same glyphs. The whole window moves, then its right edge shortens it. Each drag is one undo step, and the clip undoes both. The window follows the clips. The offset counts from the start of the target, so the window moves with the target clip when you move it. When you move or trim a clip so that the overlap shrinks, the window is shifted and then shortened to stay inside it. A relation with a clip on a locked track is the exception: it is not changed, so its window stays where it plays, and a move or a trim of its other clip stops where the window would no longer fit (The timeline). The same numbers are in two panels. Select the relation and the Animations panel shows them, with the pairs; the Animation tab of the relation editor has them too. Easing is the rate function of the transformation: how its speed changes between the start and the end of the window. It is smooth unless you change it. The Animations panel for the selected relation: its pairs, then its timing and style."],["twice","A formula does not have to turn into another all at once. Two clips can have several relations: the same source and the same target, each relation with its own pairs, its own window and its own style. Below, a first relation takes the c and the equals sign; a moment later a second one, between the same two clips, takes the rest. The target starts empty, so it is made of what the two relations bring. The rules of the pairs are what keeps such relations apart. Each glyph of the target arrives through one pair, and each glyph of the source is consumed by one, whichever relation the pair belongs to. So their windows can follow each other, as above, or even overlap, as long as they move different glyphs. The Default style of a relation decides what its pairs without a style of their own consume, so changing it can break those rules: a pair that copied a source, made a Transform, would consume a glyph another relation consumes already. A default style that would give the project such an error is not set, and the notice Default style not changed says why. Give the pairs that clash a style of their own in Edit pairs…, or keep the default style. To make the second one, ask for a relation between the two clips again, from the Relations tab or with a link port. Because they are related already, a menu asks what you mean: Edit relation 1…, or Add another relation…. Its window starts at the playhead, or where the last one of theirs ends when no window fits there. The relation editor shows it. The relations of two clips are numbered by when they play, the first on the timeline being 1, and the number tells them apart where the names of the formulas cannot: Relation 2 of 2: source → target in the title of the editor, 2 of 2 in the list of the Relations tab. Each relation has its own band and its own arrow on the timeline. Bands of a clip whose windows overlap share its lane in stripes, one under the other, so that two relations with the same window can both be seen, dragged and opened."],["path-arc","Glyphs move in straight lines unless the relation says otherwise. Path arc bends the lines into arcs. It is a number of degrees, Manim’s path_arc: 0 is a straight line, and the bigger the number, the rounder the arc, up to a full turn, 360°, either way. The sign decides the side. A glyph that moves to the right dips below its straight line with a positive arc, and goes over the top with a negative one. Below, the same swap three times: with an arc of 0°, of 90° and of −90°. The arc belongs to the relation, so every pair follows it. To shape the path of one pair, draw it by hand: custom paths explains how."],["managing","The Relations tab of the Animations panel lists the relations of the selected clip, with a diagram of the two formulas and their links. Edit… opens the relation editor, Edit path… goes to the path of a pair, and × deletes the relation. All related… shows every relation connected to the clip, as one chain. The form under the list makes a new relation, or, between two clips that are related already, asks whether to edit one of theirs or add another: the relation editor tells how. A relation is between formulas and texts: a brace, a shape or a line is one piece, and its clip has no Relations tab and no link port. A project made with an earlier version may still hold a relation with a brace, a shape or a line on one side. It plays and exports as it is, but it cannot be edited: its band is drawn in amber, its warning says what the object is, Edit pairs… and Edit path… are greyed out with the reason in their tooltip, and a drag of its band leaves it where it is. Delete relation, in the menu of its band, removes it. The Relations tab of a clip. Right-click the band of a relation on the timeline for its menu: Edit pairs…; under Edit path, each of its pairs (with more than five, one entry, Choose a pair…, opens a list of them); and Delete relation. The menu of a relation’s band. You can remove a relation in four ways: select it and press Delete; press Delete in its panel, or × in the list of the clip; choose Delete relation in the menu of its band; right-click a clip and choose Remove relations: it takes off every relation of the clip, as source or as target, and its Morph blocks. Remove relations (1) in the menu of a clip. Removing a relation never touches the two formulas. The glyphs of the target arrive with its intro again, and the glyphs of the source leave with its outro. A relation on a locked track can be selected but not changed: unlock the track first."],["python","The exported scene plays a relation with a self.play(…) over its window, one animation for each pair. The glyphs that belong to no pair leave or arrive in that call when their outro or intro runs during the window, as in the scene below; anything that starts or ends inside the window cuts the play into slices (_Part). This is the scene of the one-to-many example, as the editor exports it: The preview and the export agree: what you see in the player is what Manim renders. The page The generated Python explains the rest of the scene."],["examples","Download a project and open it with File ▸ Open…, then Other file… in the dialog that opens (Projects explains it), to take it apart: select a relation, open its editor, and change a pair."],["next","The relation editor: how to link glyphs, set the styles and the timing, and save. Previewing a transformation: watch it before you save it. Custom paths: the path of one pair, drawn by hand. Transformation recipes: common moves of algebra, ready to copy. Transforming one formula into another: your first relation, step by step."]],"short":"Relations"},{"id":"transforms/relation-editor","url":"transforms/relation-editor.html","title":"The relation editor","section":"Transformations","level":"intermediate","description":"Linking glyphs, the pair list, the styles of pairs and the Animation tab.","headings":[["opening","Opening the editor"],["from-the-timeline","From the timeline"],["from-the-panel","From the Animations panel"],["from-a-relation","From a relation that exists"],["second-relation","Between two clips that are related already"],["tour","A tour of the editor"],["linking","Linking glyphs"],["link-one","A glyph to a glyph"],["link-several","One glyph to several"],["link-box","With a box"],["joining","Linking a target that is linked already"],["separate-pairs","Separate pairs onto one target"],["link-as","Choosing the style while linking"],["suggest","Suggest links by label"],["keyboard","From the keyboard"],["consumed","A source a transform consumes"],["refused","What the editor refuses"],["taken","Glyphs another relation has taken"],["drawing","The drawing: aim, zoom and layout"],["pair-list","The pair list"],["pair-menu","The menu of a pair"],["animation-tab","The Animation tab"],["problems","Problems and saving"],["preview","Watching it"],["try","Try it"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"The relation editor is where you tell the app which glyph of one formula becomes which glyph of the next. You link glyphs, choose how each pair moves, set the timing and watch the result, all in one dialog that changes the project only when you press Save relation."],["opening","The editor opens on two clips: to make a new relation between them, or to change the one they already have. How relations work explains what a relation is; this page is about the tool. A new relation is made where the playhead is: its window starts at the yellow line of the timeline, when a window fits there. Put the playhead where the transformation should begin, then ask for the relation. How relations work tells what happens when none fits."],["from-the-timeline","Hold Shift. A link port, a small round dot, shows in the lower half of every clip. Click the port of the source clip, then click the target clip. A line follows the pointer between the two clicks; Esc cancels it. You can also drag from the port and release over the target. With Shift held, the ports appear. A click on the port of second and a click on third open the editor on a new relation from second to third. If the two clips cannot be related, a notice says why: they have to be on different tracks, they have to overlap in time, and neither track can be locked. If they are related already, a menu opens where the pointer is and asks which of their relations to edit, or to add another: see below."],["from-the-panel","Select the source clip. On its Relations tab, choose the other clip in Relate with (1), the direction (2) and press Create relation… (3). When the two clips are related already, the button reads Edit or add a relation… and opens a menu: see below. The form that makes a relation, in the Relations tab of a clip."],["from-a-relation","Double-click its band or its arrow on the timeline. Right-click its band and choose Edit pairs…. Press Edit… beside it in the Relations tab of a clip, or Edit pairs… in the panel of the selected relation."],["second-relation","Two clips can have several relations. So when you ask for a relation between two clips that have one already, with the button of the Relations tab or with a link port, a menu asks what you mean: The menu of two clips that have two relations. Edit relation 1…, Edit relation 2…: the relations the two clips have in that direction, in the order of their windows, each with its window in seconds and its number of pairs. One opens that relation in the editor. Add another relation… opens the editor on a new relation between the same two clips. Its window starts at the playhead, or, when no window fits there, where the last one of theirs ends; with a region marked on the timeline, its window is the region. If it cannot be made (the clips no longer overlap, for one), the entry is off and a line under it says why. The title of the editor then names the relation by its number, Relation 2 of 2: source → target, and so does the list of the Relations tab. The number is the order on the timeline: the relation that plays first is 1, and it follows the window when you move it. The other direction counts apart: a relation from the second clip back to the first is the first of its own."],["tour","This is the editor on the relation of a(b + c) into ab + ac. Step through it, or let it go on by itself. The editor works on a copy of the relation. Nothing changes in the project, neither the timeline nor the preview, until you press Save relation. Then the whole session is one undo step."],["linking","The source is on top and the target below, or at the left of it when that draws the glyphs of a tall formula larger (the drawing). The glyphs are the buttons: click them to select, and the toolbar under the drawing tells what is selected. You need not hit the ink of a glyph: a dashed circle follows the pointer, and a click takes the glyph under the pointer or, on none, the nearest one inside the circle. The glyph it would take is marked as you move, and its number appears at its lower left, the number the pair is stored by. A glyph that has a link takes the colour of its pair. The drawing also zooms and moves."],["link-one","Click a glyph of the source, then a glyph of the target. The link appears, with the colour and the number of the new pair. You can start from the target as well. A plain click on a side that has nothing selected, while the other side has a selection, links at once."],["link-several","Hold Shift (or Ctrl) while you click to add glyphs to the selection of a side, or to take one out. With glyphs selected on both sides, press Link: they become one pair. Selecting several sources and then clicking one target links them at once, many-to-one. The plus signs are linked already, by a click on each. Then the a of the source is selected, the two a of the target are added with Shift, and Link makes one pair of one glyph and two."],["link-box","Drag a rectangle over the glyphs: every glyph it touches is selected, on either formula. A side the box does not touch keeps its selection, so you can pick one side and then the other. Hold Shift or Ctrl when you press, and the box adds to the selection instead of replacing it. A box over the two x of the source, and one click on the x of the target: a pair of two glyphs and one."],["joining","When you link a source to a target glyph that has a pair, the source joins that pair instead of making a second one: two glyphs become one. A message says so (Glyph b (2) joined pair 1: a (0), b (2) → a (0).). The new source has the style of the pair it joined, and it joins the pair’s choice of how the extra sources arrive (below)."],["separate-pairs","A pair has one style, so the sources that join cannot do different things: they all transform, or they all send a copy. To give each source its own style, and its own path, take the pair apart: right-click a glyph of the pair and choose Split into separate pairs. Each source becomes a pair of its own onto the same target, with the style it played (a source that only sent a copy becomes a Copy) and the path it followed. The animation is the same until you change one of them, unless the pair merged its sources (Merge): apart, every source arrives. The menu of a pair of two sources onto one target. Join into one pair, in the menu of any pair whose target other pairs bring too, puts them back into one. Their paths are kept, as one path when they all followed the same one and as paths of their routes otherwise. One pair has one style, so the pairs can be joined when it can play what they play: the same style for all of them, or one that transforms with copies around it. Otherwise the entry is greyed out, with the reason under it: give the pairs one style first. A pair that merges its sources (Merge (Manim)) is not joined with another either, since each of its sources would then arrive on its own: set it to Separate first. Pairs can share a target only inside one relation, and only when they bring exactly the same glyphs. Linking still joins by itself; the menu of the next section makes a separate pair while linking. Once two pairs bring a target, a new link onto it is one more pair of its own, and a message says so."],["link-as","A link made by a click has the style of the relation: Transform, unless you changed it in the Animation tab. To give a pair a style of its own from the start, select the glyphs of one side and right-click a glyph of the other side, where you would have clicked it. The glyph joins the selection and a menu opens with Link as Transform, Link as Copy, Link as Fade transform and Link as Fade copy. With glyphs selected on both sides already, a right-click on any of them opens the same menu. The a of the source already turns into the first a of the target, and is outlined in red. To the second one it can only send a copy: the two styles that would consume it again are greyed out, over the reason. The entries of the pair it has follow. The menu tries each style before it offers it. A style those glyphs cannot take is greyed out, with the reason under it: a source that another pair consumes, or that another relation consumes later, can only be copied. When no style can be, as for a target that arrives through another relation, the reason is said once. A style that would bring a warning stays available, and the warning is written under it. Onto a target that has a pair the menu offers Join pair N, since by default the source joins that pair and takes its style, and under it Separate pair as Transform, Copy, Fade transform and Fade copy, which make a pair of its own onto that target. When the selected glyphs have links already, the entries of their pairs come after a line."],["suggest","Suggest links by label proposes one-to-one links between glyphs that have the same label: the first x of the source with the first x of the target, the second with the second, in reading order, left to right and then top to bottom. A glyph that has a pair is left out. The suggestions are drawn dashed, with a bar that lists them. Accept all makes them pairs; Dismiss drops them. When no glyphs are left to match, the editor says so. The suggestions, before they are accepted. The bar (1) lists them. A suggestion is a start, not an answer: it knows labels, not mathematics. Accept it and fix what is wrong. Edit, in the pair list, takes a pair apart and selects its glyphs again: add the second a with Shift and press Link (the pair list, below)."],["keyboard","The key before the eye in the toolbar lists the glyphs of both formulas under the drawing, a key each, with its number and its label. A press does what a click on the glyph does, Shift and Ctrl included. The lists are left out until you ask for them, because they grow with the formulas; the browser remembers your choice. After a click on a glyph, Enter does what Link does. The menu key on one of these keys (or Shift+F10, or a right-click) opens the menu of the drawing: Link as, and the entries of the pairs. The key that lists the glyphs (1), and the lists (2). The eye hides the links and their badges, so that you can see the glyphs alone."],["consumed","A pair with the style Transform or Fade transform consumes its sources: once it has played they are gone, so another pair can only copy them. The drawing outlines such a source in red dashes, and its tooltip names the pair that consumes it, for example pair 1 consumes it (Transform): another pair can only copy it. Of several sources that arrive as Copies, only the principal is outlined. A pair that copies, Copy or Fade copy, leaves its sources on screen and outlines nothing: give the pair one of those styles and the outline goes. The pairs of the a and the c transform: their sources are outlined. The b fades as a copy (FC) and the plus sign is copied (C): they stay, and have no outline. A source that another relation or a Morph block consumes, when this window starts or later, is outlined in the same way, and its tooltip names what consumes it."],["refused","Some links would be errors, and the editor says so instead of making them. The message sits under the toolbar. The a of the source has gone to the two a of the target already (its red outline says so), so it cannot go to the plus sign as well (1). The message says What to do A source glyph is already consumed by another pair. A glyph can leave once. Make one of the two pairs a Copy, or put both targets in one pair. Target glyphs are linked in different pairs. Link onto the targets of one pair, to add a source to it. A target glyph is not linked, while another is in pair N. Link onto the targets of that pair alone, to add a source to it, or take the pair apart first with Edit."],["taken","A glyph does one thing in the whole scene: it arrives through one pair and is consumed by one. In a second relation between two clips, or in a relation into a clip that another one feeds, some glyphs are spoken for. The drawing shows them dimmed, and their tooltip says why: The second relation of the example: the first one took the c and the equals sign, on both sides. The tooltip says What it means A target is brought in by another relation, or by a Morph block. It arrives through that pair already. A second pair onto it would be an error. A glyph was consumed earlier by another relation, or by a Morph block. It turned into other glyphs before this window starts: it is gone. A source is still arriving through another relation. It is not whole yet when this window starts. Start the window once it has arrived. Another relation takes a target before it would have arrived. Something leaves from that glyph while this window would still be bringing it. End the window before that. Another relation consumes it, or copies it later: here it can only be copied (not dimmed; outlined in red dashes when the other consumes it). The other pair needs the glyph at the same time or later. A Copy or Fade copy pair can still start from it; a pair that consumes it cannot. Another relation brings it in later: it is not on screen yet (not dimmed). The source is not there when this window starts. Copied, it would appear out of nowhere; start the window once it has arrived. The other relation is named by its two formulas, and by its number when the two clips have several: relation 1 of 2 (source → target). The reasons follow the window: move it, in the Animation tab, and a glyph that was only noted can become dimmed. A dimmed glyph can still be clicked; the link it makes is listed among the problems. Suggest links by label leaves these glyphs out."],["drawing","The drawing is the same in this editor and in the pair editor of a Morph block. It is spaced by the size of its text, the mark of a glyph is the glyph's own box, and the badges of the pairs keep their size on screen: a formula of several lines is drawn as large as the dialog allows. The circle is the Preview's circle selector, always on here: a minus sign, a dot or the bar of a fraction is easy to take. Shift and the wheel make it larger or smaller; it is one size for the Preview and for the two editors, and the browser remembers it. Ctrl and the wheel zoom about the pointer. The middle button, or Alt and a drag, moves the view; on a Mac a scroll moves it and a pinch zooms. The keys over the upper right corner zoom in steps and say how near the view is, and Fit shows the whole drawing. The drawing opens framed on what takes part: the glyphs that can be linked and those of the pairs made. When that is about all of it, it opens whole. There are two layouts. Stacked, the source is over the target and the links go through the gap between them. Side by side, the source is at the left, each line of a tall formula is beside its own, and the links are arcs over the two. The drawing opens in the layout that draws the glyphs that can take part larger: stacked for most formulas of one line, side by side for tall ones, whose glyphs would be small stacked. A short formula, as xx into x² above, is drawn large either way, and opens stacked. The first key of the corner changes it while the editor is open. The raw preview follows the layout. Over the corner of the drawing: the layout (1), the zoom (2) and Fit (3). The same relation side by side: each link is an arc over the two formulas."],["pair-list","The Pairs tab lists every pair of the relation, in the order you made them, with the number and the colour of its link. Each row has, from left to right: the glyphs of the pair, a → a a, and its shape, 1→2; its style, in a list: Inherit: Transform (the relation’s Default style, whatever it is), Transform, Copy (source stays), Fade transform or Fade copy (source stays); Edit, which takes the pair apart: it leaves the list and its glyphs are selected again, so that you can change them and press Link; Edit path…, which saves the relation and opens the preview’s Edit-path mode on this pair; ×, which deletes the pair. Point at a row and its links light up in the drawing; point at a glyph and its pairs light up in the list, which scrolls to them. The list of a relation with paths of its own. The dot (1) marks a pair whose path was shaped by hand; a pair can have a style of its own (2); Edit path… (3) goes to the preview to shape one. A pair of several sources and one target has one more row under it, Sources: how the sources beyond the first arrive (Separate, Merge (Manim) or Copies). How relations work shows the three. With Copies the row also names the principal source. The choice of how the extra sources arrive (1), and the principal (2)."],["pair-menu","Right-click a glyph that has links. The menu is for the pairs that hold the selected glyphs: their style, all at once; Edit path… when it is one pair; Straight path to drop a path drawn by hand; Split into separate pairs or Join into one pair (above); and Delete link…, which asks first. A press on the badge of a link opens the same menu, when the pair holds a selected glyph. The menu of a pair."],["animation-tab","The second tab of the side column has two groups of settings, shown in the last steps of the tour above. Timing and style: Offset and Duration of the window, with a line that gives the window in seconds and the range it is allowed in (and, for a new relation whose window could not start at the playhead, a line that says so); Easing; the Default style of the pairs; and the Path arc in degrees. Unpaired glyphs: what the glyphs of the source that no pair takes do when they leave (the Outro of the source clip), and what the glyphs of the target that no pair brings do when they arrive (the Intro of the target clip), each with its Duration and a checkbox that runs it during the window. A line over each names its glyphs, those that no relation and no block of the clip takes care of: 1 glyph of source is not taken away by a relation or a block (+), … is not brought in by a relation or a block (−). Between the two, Starts empty makes the target show only what is brought into it: its unpaired glyphs then stay out, and the fields of its intro wait. The outro, the intro and Starts empty belong to the clips: changing them here changes those clips when you save. How relations work explains the window, the unpaired glyphs and a target that starts empty. A press on the drawing, or on a key of the glyph lists, brings the Pairs tab back, with the row of the pair you pressed in view."],["problems","The editor checks the relation as you work. What it finds is listed under both tabs, one line each, led by error or warning. In the list of pairs, a pair with an error is outlined in red, and a pair with only warnings in amber. A problem that is an error keeps Save relation off, and the button says why. A warning only tells you. A relation whose window opens while the source glyphs are still arriving through another relation. The problem is listed (1), and Save relation (2) is off. This one is common when you chain relations: the second formula is still receiving its glyphs when the next relation starts. Move the window of the new relation later, in the Animation tab, until it starts after the first one has ended. Save relation is also off when there is no pair yet, when nothing has changed in a relation that exists, and when a formula changed under the editor: close it and open it again. Close closes the editor; once you have changed something it reads Cancel and asks Discard the changes to this relation? Esc does the same, after it has cleared the selection. Delete relation, on a relation that exists, asks before it deletes the relation and all its pairs. Saving is one undo step: Ctrl+Z takes back the whole session, pairs, window and the changes to the intro and the outro."],["preview","The switch Link | Preview over the drawing puts the transformation in its place, and Preview raw, the last key of the toolbar, animates the drawing itself. Neither saves anything. Previewing a transformation shows both."],["try","The widget below is a light version of the editor, on the relation of the page about relations. Link a few glyphs, then press Preview. Download the chain example, three formulas on three tracks, and open it with File ▸ Open…, then Other file… in the dialog that opens (Projects explains it). Hold Shift, click the port of second and then third. Link the b of second to the b of third, and read the problem the editor lists. Open the Animation tab and raise the Offset until the problem goes. Then press Save relation."],["next","Previewing a transformation: the Preview tab and Preview raw. Custom paths: what Edit path… opens. How relations work: pairs, styles, unpaired glyphs and timing. Morph blocks: the same pair editor, inside one formula."]],"short":"Relation editor"},{"id":"transforms/previewing","url":"transforms/previewing.html","title":"Previewing a transformation","section":"Transformations","level":"intermediate","description":"Watch a transformation before you export it: the Preview tab and Preview raw.","headings":[["why","Why preview in the editor"],["preview-tab","The Preview tab"],["instant","Choosing the instant"],["live","Changing things while you look"],["blocked","When there is nothing to show"],["preview-raw","Preview raw"],["ending","Ending the preview"],["compare","Preview or Preview raw?"],["after-saving","After you save"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"You do not have to save a relation to see what it does. The relation editor can play the transformation as it is now, in two ways: Preview shows it as the scene will, and Preview raw animates the drawing itself, so that you can check the pairs."],["why","Without a preview you would save the relation, close the editor and play the timeline to see a pair you got wrong, then open the editor again. The previews of the editor play the draft: the pairs, the styles, the window and what the unpaired glyphs do, as they would be saved now. Nothing is saved for them, and the side column stays beside them, so that a change shows at once. Both use the scene engine of the main Preview, and the export agrees with it. What loops in the editor is what the scene will show."],["preview-tab","Over the drawing that links glyphs there is a switch, Link | Preview. Press Preview and the transformation takes the place of the drawing. The picture shows only the two formulas of the relation, as the scene draws them, in a loop. The loop starts half a second before the window and ends half a second after it, so that you see the source standing and the target settled. It does not reach before the first of the two clips starts, nor after the last one ends. The play button pauses the loop and plays it again from where it stopped. The bar chooses the instant. The band under it marks the window of the transformation. The time is in seconds of the scene, and the percentage says how far through the transformation that instant is: 0% before the window, 100% after it. The camera frames the formulas for the whole loop, so a glyph that follows a wide arc stays in the picture. A clip on a hidden track is drawn too: hiding a track is a choice of the preview, and the export draws it. Switch to Link to add a pair, and back to Preview: the picture is at the instant you left it, and the new pair already moves."],["instant","Press the bar, or drag its thumb, and the loop stops where you put it. The picture follows the thumb in both directions, so you can go back and forth through the transformation as slowly as you like. Press play to let the loop go on from there. The loop runs by itself. A press on the bar stops it, and dragging the thumb through the band goes through the transformation, forwards and back."],["live","The side column works while the preview plays. Change the style of a pair, the window, the easing, the path arc or what the unpaired glyphs do, and the picture shows it at once. This is the quickest way to choose a style: try each on the instant that matters."],["blocked","If the draft has an error, the editor does not draw it: the picture is replaced by Fix the problems listed to see the transformation. Fix the problems under the side column and the preview comes back. If a formula has no glyphs to show, the editor says why: it is empty, it does not render (with the reason), it draws no glyphs, or its glyphs are still rendering."],["preview-raw","The last key of the toolbar of the Link view is Preview raw. It animates the drawing itself: the source and the target stay where the drawing has them, one over the other or side by side, and the glyphs go from one to the other as the pairs and their styles say. It is the quickest check that the right glyph goes to the right place. Four things set it apart from Preview. The glyphs go in straight lines. The path arc and the paths drawn by hand belong to the scene, and show in Preview. The target is there all along, as a ghost at half its opacity: you see where every glyph is going to land. A glyph that is not on screen while the transformation runs is outlined, dashed. A glyph hidden at the start of its clip, or written by a block after the transformation, is not there to move, and the dashed outline shows where it is. The two clips are bare. Each is at its formula’s own size, upright and attached to nothing, and keeps only its Appear and Disappear blocks; the other relations of the two clips are left out. So a source that starts empty, or that leaves glyphs out of its intro, is whole here: in the scene, what fills it may be one of those relations. The pairs are the scene’s. A style, a copy, a fade and what the unpaired glyphs do are what the scene would do, so a Copy pair leaves its source where it is, and the glyphs of the source that belong to no pair leave with the outro of their clip. The bar of the loop takes the place of the toolbar: play and pause, the bar with the window marked, the time and the percentage, and End preview. The side column stays, and a change there shows at once. Preview raw playing in a loop; Esc ends it, and the editor stays open."],["ending","End preview brings the drawing back. So does Esc: the editor stays open, and the focus returns to Preview raw. (A field of the side column with an edit to take back keeps its first Esc, which takes the edit back.) The next Esc clears the selected glyphs, if there are any; then Esc closes the editor, or asks first when you have changed something. Switching to Preview ends the raw preview as well. Preview raw is off while the draft has an error, with a note that says why, and while a formula is not rendered."],["compare","They answer different questions. Here is one swap with a path arc of 90°, at the same instant: the scene bends the paths, the raw preview does not. Preview: the letters follow the arc, one above the plus sign and one below. Preview raw: the letters go straight, and meet in the middle. Preview Preview raw Shows The transformation as the scene plays it, the formulas where they are in the scene. The drawing that links glyphs, animated: the source and the target where the drawing has them. Paths The path arc, and the paths drawn by hand. Always straight. Glyphs that are not on screen Not drawn. Outlined, dashed. Use it to Judge the result: the look, the timing, the easing, the arc. Check the pairs: which glyph goes where, what a copy or a fade does, what is left unpaired."],["after-saving","The previews of the editor never change the project. When the transformation is right, press Save relation: the relation is in the timeline, and the main Preview plays it with everything else. This is the relation of the raw tour above, as the player plays it: the glyphs follow the arc of −60°, and the minus sign and the b are written after the transformation."],["next","The relation editor: linking glyphs, styles and timing. How relations work: pairs, styles, unpaired glyphs and the window. Custom paths: shape the path of a pair in the main Preview."]],"short":"Previewing"},{"id":"transforms/custom-paths","url":"transforms/custom-paths.html","title":"Custom paths","section":"Transformations","level":"advanced","description":"Make glyphs travel along an arc, or along a path you draw yourself in the Preview.","headings":[["how-a-glyph-travels","How a glyph travels"],["path-arc","The path arc"],["by-hand","Paths edited by hand"],["open-the-mode","Opening the mode"],["in-the-mode","In the mode"],["what-is-kept","What is kept"],["routes","A path for one glyph of a pair"],["picker","The path picker"],["marks","The marks of an edited path"],["removing","Taking a path away"],["export","How paths export"],["limits","Good to know"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A glyph travels from where it is to where it is going in a straight line, unless you say otherwise. Bend every line of a relation with one number, or draw the route of a single pair in the Preview, as you would a curve in a vector editor."],["how-a-glyph-travels","A relation moves the glyphs of its pairs during its window. Each glyph starts where its source formula has it when the window opens and ends where its target formula has it when the window closes. Between the two it follows a path. There are three kinds: Straight, the default: the shortest way. The relation's Path arc: one angle that bends the way of every pair that has no path of its own. A custom path: a curve of your own for one pair, with anchors and handles. A pair with a custom path ignores the Path arc. The same two tools exist on Morph blocks, which move glyphs inside one formula. You want Use A direct move, the least to look at Straight: leave the Path arc at 0°. Glyphs that pass each other, or a gentle swing for everything that moves The relation's Path arc. One glyph to hop, loop or weave, or two glyphs to take different routes A custom path for each of those pairs."],["path-arc","Select a relation: click its band on the timeline, or its row in the Relations list of one of its clips. In the Animations panel, the section Timing and style has the field Path arc, in degrees (the Animation tab of the relation editor has it too). 0° keeps the glyphs on straight lines; any value from −360° to 360° bends them. The Path arc (1) sits with the relation's timing and its default style. The sign says which way the glyph swings. A positive arc swings to the right of the glyph's own direction, a negative one to the left. A glyph that travels to the right has its right-hand side below it, so it passes under the straight line; one that travels to the left passes over it. Because every glyph bends to the same side of its own way, glyphs that go different ways swing past each other instead of through each other: the swap of the two sides of an equation is the classic case. The arc is one angle for the whole relation. A pair whose path you drew keeps its own curve and does not follow it."],["by-hand","When one glyph must hop over another, or weave round a third, a single angle is not enough. Give that pair a path of its own. Here two letters trade places. Along straight paths they run through each other; with a path for each, the a hops over and the b slides under."],["open-the-mode","The curve is edited in the Preview, on the real positions, in a mode of its own called Edit path. There are several ways in; all of them open it on one pair. Where What Edit path… does there A clip's Relations list, on the row of the relation Opens the mode on its only pair, or the path picker when the relation has several. The panel of a selected relation, in the list of Pairs Opens the mode on that pair. A right-click on the relation's band on the timeline Lists the pairs under Edit path, or offers Choose a pair…. The relation editor: the list of pairs, or the menu of a pair Saves the relation, closes the editor and opens the mode on that pair. A Morph block: its panel, its dialog, its pair editor Opens the mode on that pair (the dialog and the editor close first). The row of a relation in a clip's Relations list: the dot and “4 paths edited” count the pairs with a path of their own, and Edit path… sits between Edit… and the delete key."],["in-the-mode","The playhead jumps to the middle of the pair's window, so the glyph is on screen halfway along its way. The curve is drawn over the Preview from where the glyph starts to where it ends; those two places are marked by a faint copy of the glyph, and a dashed line joins them (the straight chord). A pair that has no path of its own starts from the arc it follows, turned into anchors and handles: the plain chord at 0°. The mode: an anchor (1) with its handles (2), the hint line (3) and Done (4). The glyph is drawn at the middle of the way, where the curve puts it. Four words are enough to read the curve: Chord the straight line from where the glyph starts to where it ends (dashed); Anchor a point the curve passes through; the first and the last sit on the chord's ends; Handle the control point that sets how the curve leaves or reaches an anchor: the farther it is, the longer the curve keeps its direction; Smooth or corner on a smooth anchor the two handles stay in a line, so the curve has no kink there; on a corner they point anywhere. Every gesture is one step of the undo history, and the glyph follows the curve as you change it. Do this What happens Press on the curve and drag Adds an anchor there and moves it with the pointer. Drag an anchor Moves it with its handles. The first and the last anchor sit on the glyph's two places and stay put. Drag a handle Reshapes the curve beside its anchor. On a smooth anchor the other handle mirrors it: it turns to stay opposite and takes the same length. Drag a handle with Shift held On a smooth anchor the other handle turns to stay opposite, but keeps its own length. Drag a handle with Ctrl held Moves only that handle: the anchor becomes a corner. Double-click an anchor Makes a corner smooth, or a smooth anchor a corner. Delete Removes the selected anchor, or takes the selected handle back onto its anchor. ← → ↑ ↓ Nudge the selected anchor or handle by one pixel of the Preview; with Shift, by ten. A nudged handle mirrors the other one, as a drag does. Enter, Esc or Done Leaves the mode. Esc first cancels a drag in progress. An entry chosen in the Preview's right-click menu leaves the mode too, keeping the path; closing that menu with Esc does not. On a button of the mode's bar (Path per glyph, the arrows of the routes…), Enter presses that button instead. The keys count at every move of a drag: press Shift or Ctrl, or let go of it, in the middle of a drag, and the handle follows the keys you hold from then on. Shaping a path with the mode open: press on the curve and drag up, double-click the new anchor to make it smooth, draw one of its handles out (the other mirrors it), and press Enter to leave."],["what-is-kept","The curve is stored in the frame of the pair's own chord, as fractions of its length, so it follows the formulas when you move or scale them. The glyph travels the curve at a steady pace; the relation's Easing still decides how that pace speeds up and slows down. A pair of several glyphs applies the curve to each glyph along its own chord. A path that you leave as the plain chord, or as the arc it started from, is dropped: the pair goes back to following the relation's Path arc."],["routes","A pair of several glyphs has several routes: each is one source glyph on its way to one target glyph. Two glyphs that become one have two routes, one glyph that becomes two has two, and two glyphs that become three have three, as Manim pairs them up. The path of the pair is common to all its routes, each on its own chord. A route can also have a path of its own. In the mode, such a pair is edited as one path to begin with. The curve is drawn once, along the first route, and a bar at the top left says that it is the path of all the glyphs: each glyph takes the same shape on its own way. A pair of two x that become one, as the mode opens on it: Path per glyph is off (1), and the curve is the path of all the glyphs (2). It is drawn along the first x; the second x, halfway along its own way, bends the same way. To shape one route alone, press Path per glyph. Every route is drawn now, dimmed: dashed while it follows the common path, solid with a dot once it has its own. The bar says what you are editing, and starts on All together, the common path. Click a route's curve, or the faint glyph at its start or at its end, or step through the routes with the arrows of the bar: the curve with the anchors is then that route's, on its own chord, and the first change gives the route a path of its own. When a route of the pair has a path of its own already, the mode opens with Path per glyph on. The first of the two routes of a pair, with a path of its own. The padlock (1) locks the other routes, the bar names the route (2), and Remove own path (3) gives it back to the common path, which the other route, dashed, still follows. Editing the common path moves every route that has no path of its own. The padlock beside Path per glyph locks the other routes: they stay drawn, but a click on them or on their glyphs chooses nothing, so a curve can be shaped right next to them. The arrows of the bar still choose the route. Remove own path takes the path of the chosen route away; All together goes back to the common path. Turning Path per glyph off goes back to the common path and deletes nothing: the routes keep their own paths and play them, and the bar says how many have one (“1 route with its own path”). A path of a route that you leave as what the route would follow anyway is dropped, as a plain path of the pair is. A Fade transform or a Fade copy of a group has no routes: the group cross-fades whole, so that pair has its one path. Several sources onto one target have a route each when they arrive Separate or as Copies (the pair list). To give each source its own style too, take the pair apart: separate pairs onto one target."],["picker","With several pairs, Edit path… on a relation's row opens the picker instead of choosing for you. It shows the relation as a diagram, with one row for each pair: a small picture of the curve that pair follows, what it goes from and to, its style, and whether it follows a custom path, the arc or a straight line. Point at a row and its link lights up in the diagram, and the other way round; a click on either opens the mode on that pair. The picker of a relation of seven pairs. Four rows wear the dot of an edited path; the others follow the relation's arc of 30°. The relation's band on the timeline has a menu of its own (right-click it). With up to five pairs it lists them under Edit path; with more it offers Choose a pair…, which opens the picker. Up to five pairs are in the menu. With more, Choose a pair…."],["marks","A pair whose path you drew wears a small dot wherever pairs are listed beside an Edit path…; point at it and it says Edited path. A relation's row in the Relations list counts them (“4 paths edited”). The lists of pairs in the panel of a relation and of a Morph block also say what each pair follows: custom path, arc 30° or straight. A pair whose only edited paths are those of its routes wears the dot too, and reads custom path on a route. The pairs of a relation: a dot on the four with a path of their own, and what each one follows."],["removing","To give a pair the relation's arc back, open the relation editor, right-click one of its glyphs (or select it and click the badge of its link), and choose Straight path, which takes the paths of its routes away as well. With several edited links selected the entry says how many (Straight paths (3)). Ctrl+Z undoes any change of a path, one gesture at a time. The menu of a pair in the relation editor: the style of the pair, Edit path… and Straight path."],["export","The Path arc becomes Manim's own path_arc, in degrees, on each transform of the relation. A custom path is played through a small helper, _spline_path, which the export writes at the top of the file. It takes the curve as a list of anchors and handles, in the frame of the chord, and moves each glyph along it at a steady pace. The helper is the same in ManimCE and in ManimGL, and it does the same arithmetic as the Preview, so the video shows what you drew. When the glyphs of one transform follow different paths (a pair whose routes have paths of their own), a second helper, _route_paths, wraps that transform and gives each glyph the path of its route, also when two glyphs are drawn on the same spot. In the project of the three rows at the top of this page, the transform of the second row carries the arc and the transform of the third row carries its own path: ReplacementTransform(arc_g[0], arc_end_g[0], path_arc=-45 * DEGREES), ReplacementTransform(edited_g[0], edited_end_g[0], path_func=_spline_path([…])), The swap of the two letters exports the same way, one path_func=_spline_path([…]) for each of its two pairs:"],["limits","A glyph that does not move (its start and its end are the same place) has no chord, so it has no path: the hint says so and the glyph plays straight. On a locked track Edit path… is greyed out, and says why. In the relation editor, Preview raw shows every glyph on a straight line. Use Preview (or the main Preview) to see arcs and custom paths. Download the project below, choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… and pick the file (Projects explains the dialog). Press Space: the two letters run through each other. Click the clip ab on the timeline. In the Relations tab press Edit path…, and choose a → a in the picker. Press on the middle of the curve and drag it up: an anchor appears. Double-click it to make it smooth, then draw one of its handles out. Press Enter. The panel now shows the relation and its pairs: press Edit path… on the row b → b and do the same, but drag the curve down: this letter slides under the a. Play it. Press Ctrl+Z to take the changes back one by one."],["next","Morph blocks make a derivation inside one formula, each row grown out of the one above it. How relations work covers pairs of glyphs, their styles and their timing. Transformation recipes gives eight common moves of algebra, each a small project to play and open."]],"short":"Custom paths"},{"id":"transforms/morph","url":"transforms/morph.html","title":"Morph blocks","section":"Transformations","level":"advanced","description":"A derivation inside one formula: Morph blocks grow each row out of the one above it.","headings":[["what","What a Morph block does"],["morph-or-relation","Morph, or a relation between clips?"],["formula","Write the derivation"],["making","Add a Morph block"],["pairs","Choose the pairs"],["linking","Linking glyphs"],["suggest","Suggest pairs by label"],["dimmed","Which glyphs can take part"],["arrivals","Glyphs that no pair brings"],["settings","The block's settings"],["editing","Moving, copying and deleting"],["export","How it exports"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A Morph block turns glyphs of a formula into other glyphs of the same formula, the way a derivation is written: each row grows out of the one above it, and the rows stay."],["what","You write the whole derivation as one formula, one row per line, and put it on the timeline. Only its first row appears with the clip. A Morph block placed later in the clip names pairs of glyphs: a source, which is on screen when the block starts, and a target, a glyph of a later row. During the block the targets arrive, moving out of their sources. By default the sources stay where they are, so the rows pile up like a derivation on a blackboard. A Morph block is a relation inside one clip. Its pairs, their styles, the arc of their paths and their timing work as they do between two clips: see How relations work and the relation editor. What is new is where the glyphs live. A Morph block works on an object that has glyphs, such as a formula or a text. A brace, a shape or a line is one piece and has no Morph."],["morph-or-relation","Both solve the same equation in the same three steps. Here is the equation of the first transform twice: on the left three formulas linked by two relations, each replacing the one before it; on the right one formula with two Morph blocks, whose rows stay. Relation between clips Morph block Formulas Two, each with its own clip on its own track. One, in one clip. Layout Each clip is placed, scaled and coloured on its own. LaTeX lays the rows out: an & lines the equals signs up. The source Consumed by Transform and Fade transform; stays with Copy (source stays) and Fade copy (source stays). Stays: Copy is the default style. Timing A window inside the target clip. A block anywhere in the clip, with the same easing and duration. The glyphs come from Another formula, which may be anywhere in the scene. Other glyphs of the same formula. Choose Morph for a derivation that stays on screen, aligned on an equals sign, where each line comes from the one above. Choose a relation when one formula becomes another in place, when the second one must be placed, scaled or coloured on its own, or when the first one leaves. The two mix: a glyph of a row can also arrive from another formula through a relation."],["formula","Type the rows in the Raw LaTeX field of the formula editor, one per line. Manim sets every formula in an aligned environment, so & marks the column the rows line up on and \\\\ ends a row: x + 3 &= 5 \\\\ x &= 5 - 3 \\\\ x &= 2 Place the formula on the timeline as it is: nothing needs hiding. A glyph that a Morph block brings in skips the clip's intro and arrives through the block. A dropped clip lasts three seconds; drag its right edge, or set its End in the Timing tab, so that there is room for the blocks. Keep a {{ }} group inside one cell of an aligned line. ManimGL cannot compile a group that spans &, and the formula gets a warning."],["making","Put the playhead at the time the block should start, right-click the clip and choose Morph within the formula: the block starts at the playhead. (The Add animation button of the Timing tab offers the same entry, at the playhead too.) The block is one second long and has no pairs yet: the timeline writes it Morph · 0, the number being its pairs. With the playhead at 2 s, a right-click on the clip opens the Add animation menu; Morph within the formula starts a block there. The clip with its three blocks. The first Morph block has five pairs, the second two."],["pairs","Open the pair editor with Edit pairs…: in the Animations panel when the block is selected, or in the block's right-click menu. The editor draws the formula twice: the glyphs that are on screen when the block starts, the sources, and the glyphs the block can bring in, the targets. It lays the two copies out the way that draws the glyphs that can take part larger. For these three rows that is one over the other: the sources above, the targets below. In a taller derivation a block may open side by side, the sources at the left, each line beside its own and the links as arcs over them, as the second block of the derivative tutorial does. The first key over the corner of the drawing changes the layout, and the drawing takes glyphs with a circle, zooms and moves as the relation editor's does."],["linking","Click a source, then a target (or the other way round): they become a pair. Several at once: Shift-click glyphs of one side, or drag a box over them (with Shift or Ctrl held it adds to what is selected), do the same on the other side, and press Link. The pair has many sources, many targets, or both. A target that is already linked takes the new source into its pair, so two glyphs become one. The style from the start: a pair made by a click has the style of the block. Right-click the target instead of clicking it and choose Link as Transform, Link as Copy, Link as Fade transform or Link as Fade copy; a style those glyphs cannot take is greyed out, with the reason under it, as in the relation editor. A source that a pair consumes, with the style Transform or Fade transform, is outlined in red dashes, as in the relation editor: it is gone once the pair has played, so another pair can only copy it. Copy, the block's default, and Fade copy outline nothing. Each row of the list of pairs has a menu for the style of its pair, and an Edit path… button. Right-click a glyph of a pair (or select it and click the badge of its link) for the same choices and for Delete link…."],["suggest","Suggest pairs by label draws dashed links for you: each glyph that can arrive takes the nearest glyph before it, in reading order, that has the same label and can be a source. Accept all turns them into pairs; Dismiss throws them away. It is a quick start for the glyphs that stay the same from one row to the next. It cannot know that a + becomes a −: link those by hand, as the clip shows. Suggest looks at labels only. When a symbol repeats inside a row, as the x and h of a derivative do, check each dashed link before you accept, or link by hand. The derivative tutorial does the latter. Suggest pairs by label, Accept all, and the plus linked to the minus by two clicks. The five pairs are then ready to be saved with Save pairs."],["dimmed","Glyphs that cannot take part as they are drawn dimmed; point at one and its tooltip says why. A source must be fully on screen when the block starts. A target must not be on screen yet, or be there only because the intro brought it, and nothing else may bring it in. Neither may be busy with another animation while the block runs. The second block. Below, where the targets are, rows 1 and 2 are dimmed: they are on screen before the block starts, so nothing can bring them in. Above, where the sources are, the 2 of row 3 is dimmed: it is not on screen yet. The tooltip says It means not on screen yet · still arriving · off screen by then As a source: it is not fully on screen when the block starts. consumed earlier An earlier pair of the clip took the glyph out with Transform or Fade transform: it is gone, as a source and as a target. a source of an earlier transfer As a target: an earlier pair copies from it, so it must be on screen already. brought in by a relation · brought in by another Morph block As a target: something else brings it in. A glyph has one owner. on screen already · on screen earlier As a target: it is there before the block. busy during the block (…) Another animation, the intro or the outro runs on it. A pair is never made of one glyph on both sides of the block. When the pairs would break a rule, the side column of the editor lists the problems, each line starting with error or warning, and Save pairs waits until no error is left. The whole session is one step of the undo history; Cancel asks before it throws changes away."],["arrivals","The 2 of the last row comes from nothing: no glyph turns into it. Give it its own block. Add an Appear animation, such as Write, at the time it should be drawn, and pick the glyph in the panel of the block. A glyph that is the target of a pair, or the glyph of an Appear block, waits off screen by itself. A glyph that nothing brings in appears with the clip's intro, like the first row. If a row shows up too early, look for a glyph that no pair and no Appear block names. The Write block of the 2. Its glyph picker shows the formula; the glyphs it names are marked."],["settings","Select the block to see its panel. Its Default style is the style of every pair that has none of its own (a pair can choose in the editor): Copy (source stays), the default, sends a copy of the glyph and leaves the original in its row, as Manim's TransformFromCopy does. Transform moves the glyph itself: its row loses it (Manim's ReplacementTransform). Fade transform fades the source into the target (FadeTransform). Fade copy (source stays) fades a copy of the source into the target and leaves the original in its row (FadeTransform of a copy). A default style that would give the project an error, a source that another pair consumes already for instance, is not set, and the notice Default style not changed says why: give the pairs that clash a style of their own, or keep the default. Below the style come the Path arc, in degrees (a full turn at most, either way), that bends the glyphs' paths (Custom paths explains it), the pairs, read-only, each with what its glyphs follow and its own Edit path…, and the usual timing: start, duration and easing. The panel of the first block: its default style, its arc, its pairs and its timing."],["editing","Drag a block on the timeline to move it and pull its edges to resize it, like any block. A Morph block stops before it would run into another animation of one of its glyphs. A block's right-click menu has Edit pairs…, Duplicate without pairs (a glyph arrives through one pair only, so a copy starts empty) and Delete…. The menu of a Morph block. Edit ▸ Suggest morph pairs. With Edit ▸ Suggest morph pairs on (it is off by default), a new Morph block starts with the pairs Suggest finds, as one undo step."],["export","A Morph block is a self.play(…) over its window, with one animation for each pair; when another animation starts or ends inside the window, the export cuts the play into slices (the blocks page shows how). A copy is ReplacementTransform of a copy of the source in ManimCE and TransformFromCopy in ManimGL; the block's duration becomes run_time, and an easing other than the default smooth becomes rate_func. The first block of the project below, in ManimCE and in ManimGL: self.play( ReplacementTransform(rows_g[0].copy(), rows_g[5]), ReplacementTransform(rows_g[3].copy(), rows_g[6]), … run_time=1, ) self.play( TransformFromCopy(rows_g[0], rows_g[5]), TransformFromCopy(rows_g[3], rows_g[6]), … run_time=1, ) Press + New in the Creator and type the three rows above in the Raw LaTeX field. Press Apply. Add the formula to the timeline and drag the right edge of its clip to about 9 seconds. Put the playhead at 2 s, right-click the clip and choose Morph within the formula, then Edit pairs…. Press Suggest pairs by label, Accept all, then link the + of row 1 to the − of row 2 and press Save pairs. Add a second block at 4.4 s the same way. Suggest pairs row 2 with row 3 this time. Add Appear ▸ Write at 5.6 s and pick the 2 of the last row. Press Space."],["next","Transformation recipes gives eight common moves of algebra, each a small project to play and open. A derivative from first principles builds a five-row derivation with Morph blocks, from start to finish. Custom paths makes glyphs travel along an arc, or along a path you draw in the Preview."]],"short":"Morph blocks"},{"id":"transforms/recipes","url":"transforms/recipes.html","title":"Transformation recipes","section":"Transformations","level":"intermediate","description":"Eight common moves of algebra, each a small project to play and open: which pairs to link, which styles to choose and how long to take.","headings":[["reading","How to read a recipe"],["overview","The recipes at a glance"],["moving-a-term","Moving a term to the other side"],["cancelling","Cancelling a factor"],["expanding","Expanding a square"],["combining","Combining like terms"],["simplifying","Simplifying a fraction"],["substituting","Substituting a value"],["swapping-the-sides","Swapping the two sides"],["factoring","Factoring out a common factor"],["make-your-own","Making your own"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"Eight common moves of algebra, each one a small project you can play, open and change. A recipe says which pairs to link, which of them the app finds by itself, which you add by hand, and what to choose for the style and the timing."],["reading","Every recipe is a relation between two formulas on two tracks: the first formula writes itself in during a second, holds for 1.2 s, then turns into the second one during a window of 1.5 s, and the second holds for 1.6 s. Download a project, choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… and pick the file (Projects explains the dialog). Then select the first clip and press Edit… on the relation in the Relations tab: the pairs are the ones in the table. The recipes follow the way you would make them in the relation editor. You press Suggest links by label, which links glyphs with the same label, in reading order, then fix what it cannot know: glyphs that change their label, such as a − that becomes a +; a label that appears twice, where the first match is not the one you mean; glyphs that split in two or merge into one. The tables say which is which: Suggest for a link the app proposes, and by hand for one you add or change. A glyph that no pair names leaves with the outro of the first clip, or arrives with the intro of the second. You link glyphs by clicking them, so you rarely need their numbers. When a recipe says “the first x”, it means the leftmost one."],["overview","Recipe What it shows Moving a term A pair added by hand so that a sign changes on the way; a Path arc so that glyphs pass each other. Cancelling a factor Unpaired glyphs, which leave with the outro of the first clip. Expanding a square One-to-many pairs: a glyph that splits in two. Combining like terms Many-to-one pairs and their arrival modes. Simplifying a fraction A style for one pair: Fade transform. Substituting a value The Copy style: the source stays on screen. Swapping the two sides Every pair found by Suggest; the arc does the rest. Factoring out a common factor Splitting a glyph in two, a label that changes, and glyphs that enter with the intro."],["moving-a-term","2x − 4 = 6 becomes 2x = 6 + 4. The 4 crosses the equals sign and its minus turns into a plus on the way. Pair Made by 2 → 2, x → x, = → =, 6 → 6, 4 → 4 Suggest: five one-to-one links. − → + By hand. The labels differ, so Suggest leaves both glyphs out. Linking them makes the sign travel with its 4 and change shape. Make these pairs yourself. Press Suggest links by label and Accept all, then click the − of the top formula and the + of the bottom one and press Link. Preview plays the transformation with your pairs. Style and timing. Every pair has the relation's default style, Transform: the glyphs move and the old ones are gone. The relation has a Path arc of −45°: the 4 and its sign, which go right, swing over the top, while the = and the 6, which go left, pass under them. With no arc the 4 would run through the 6. Custom paths says which way each sign swings. Project and Python"],["cancelling","(x + 1)(x − 2) over x + 1 becomes x − 2. The factor that is on top and underneath goes; what is left settles into the middle. Pair Made by x → x, − → −, 2 → 2, from the factor that stays Suggest finds the three labels, but it links the first x of the source, which is inside the factor that cancels. Delete that link and link the x of the second factor. The cancelled factor, the brackets of the other one, the denominator and the bar No pair. They leave with the outro of the first clip. Style and timing. The survivors use Transform. What is not paired fades with the outro of the first clip, a FadeOut as long as the window: in the relation editor, the Animation tab lists those glyphs and lets you choose the animation and its length (Outro). The cancelled glyphs are coloured red in the formula, a colour that stays with each glyph, so the viewer sees what is leaving before it leaves. Project and Python"],["expanding","(a + b)² becomes a² + 2ab + b². Each letter splits in two, the exponent is shared between the squares, and the brackets go. Pair Made by a → a, a · b → b, b · + → +, + · 2 → 2, 2 By hand: one-to-many pairs. Suggest proposes one-to-one links to the first match of each label. Select the source glyph and both targets, then press Link. The two brackets No pair: they leave with the outro of the first clip. The 2 of 2ab No pair: it arrives with the intro of the second clip, a FadeIn as long as the window. Style and timing. With Transform a source glyph that has two targets splits: Manim pads the smaller group with copies of its glyph, so one glyph becomes two. Choose Copy (source stays) for the relation and the original (a + b)² stays on screen while the terms grow from it. The letters are coloured so you can follow each one. Project and Python"],["combining","3x + 2x + 4 becomes 5x + 4. The two numbers merge into one, and the two x land on a single one. Pair Made by 3, 2 → 5 By hand: many-to-one. Select both numbers above and the 5 below, then press Link. x, x → x Suggest links the first x; link the second one onto the same target to add it to the pair: two glyphs become one. second + → +, 4 → 4 Suggest links the first plus to the one below, but the plus between the like terms is the one that goes. Delete that link and link the second plus instead. The first + No pair: it leaves with the outro of the first clip. The relation editor of this recipe. Under a pair of several sources and one target, the Sources menu says how they arrive. Style and timing. A pair of several sources and one target has an arrival mode, chosen in the Sources menu under it: Separate sends every source to the target, Merge (Manim) lets the extra sources fade into it as Manim does, and Copies lets one source transform while the others send copies and stay. The two pairs here merge. How relations work compares the three. Project and Python"],["simplifying","4x over 6 becomes 2x over 3. The numbers are halved in place. Pair Made by x → x, bar → bar Suggest. 4 → 2, 6 → 3 By hand: different labels. In the list of pairs, choose Fade transform in the style menu of each of the two rows. The relation editor of this recipe. The links of the two digits are set to Fade transform in the list on the right; the x and the bar keep the relation's style. Style and timing. A pair can have a style of its own. Transform bends the outline of the 4 into the outline of the 2, which looks like a blur halfway for two digits that stand still. Fade transform fades one numeral into the other, which reads better. The glyphs that do not change keep the relation's Transform. Project and Python"],["substituting","x² + 3x with x = 2 becomes 2² + 3 · 2. The original stays; a copy of each x flies down and turns into a 2. Pair Made by x → 2 (the base), x → 2 (the last glyph) By hand: the labels differ. exponent 2 → 2 Suggest pairs the source's 2 with the first 2 of the target, which is the base. Delete that link and link the exponent to the exponent. + → +, 3 → 3 Suggest. The · No pair: it arrives with the intro of the second clip. Style and timing. The relation's default style is Copy (source stays): every pair sends a copy and the original remains. So the first clip does not leave: it is placed above the second and lasts as long as the scene, with no outro. Each x and the 2 it becomes are amber (a colour belongs to a glyph of its formula), so the colour holds all the way. Project and Python"],["swapping-the-sides","y = x + 2 becomes x + 2 = y. The two sides trade places. Pair Made by all five: y → y, = → =, x → x, + → +, 2 → 2 Suggest finds every one. Style and timing. The pairs are the five that Suggest finds; the movement is the point. On straight paths the y and the other side would run through each other. A Path arc of 60° makes every glyph swing to the right of its own direction: the y and the =, which go right, pass below, and x + 2, which goes left, passes above. If you want one glyph to take a different route, draw its path. Project and Python"],["factoring","6x + 9 becomes 3(2x + 3). The 6 splits into a 3 and a 2, the 9 turns into a 3, and brackets appear. Pair Made by x → x, + → + Suggest. 6 → 3, 2 By hand: one-to-many. Select the 6 and the two targets, then press Link. 9 → 3 By hand: the labels differ. The two brackets No pair: they arrive with the intro of the second clip. Style and timing. Transform again; the 6 splits like the letters of the square. The intro of the second clip brings the brackets in at the same time as the window, so they appear as the numbers land. Project and Python Download the project of Substituting a value (its Project and Python box has the download) and open it as described above. Press Space: the first expression stays and the second grows below it. Click the relation's band on the timeline. In the Animations panel, in Timing and style, change Default style to Transform, and play again: now the glyphs themselves fly down, and the first expression loses them. Set Path arc to 40 and play: the x sweeps down along a curve. Press Ctrl+Z to undo each change."],["make-your-own","Start from the recipe that is closest to your equation. Open its project, change the LaTeX of its two formulas in the Creator, then open the relation with Edit… and check its pairs: use Suggest links by label for what stays the same, and fix the rest by hand as above. Link the labels that differ, point a glyph that repeats at the right match, and let glyphs that split or merge share a pair. When one formula should grow into several rows instead, use Morph blocks."],["next","Animation blocks covers every animation block a clip can hold, from Write to FocusOn. The relation editor covers linking glyphs, the pair list and the styles of pairs. Custom paths makes glyphs travel along an arc, or along a path you draw in the Preview."]],"short":"Recipes"},{"id":"animations/blocks","url":"animations/blocks.html","title":"Animation blocks","section":"Animations and timing","level":"intermediate","description":"Every animation block a clip can hold, from Write to FocusOn, running side by side: how to add, time and edit one, run several at once, and what the export makes of it.","headings":[["what","What a block is"],["adding","Adding a block"],["adding-timeline","From the timeline"],["adding-panel","From the Animations panel"],["adding-region","In a region of the timeline"],["adding-glyphs","Which glyphs a new block takes"],["gallery","The blocks, one by one"],["appear","Appear: bringing glyphs on screen"],["disappear","Disappear: taking glyphs off screen"],["transform","Transform: moving glyphs"],["morph","Morph: one formula becomes the next line"],["emphasis","Emphasis: drawing the eye"],["glyphs","Choosing the glyphs"],["timing","Timing a block"],["timing-fields","The fields"],["timing-defaults","What a new block starts with"],["easing","Easing"],["timing-drag","Moving and resizing on the timeline"],["parallel","Blocks at the same time"],["dialog","The block dialog"],["menu","The menu of a block"],["export","What the export does with blocks"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A clip appears with an intro and leaves with an outro. Between the two, blocks animate it: a term arrives, another lights up, a line turns into the next. Each block is one animation, on some glyphs, for some seconds. One clip with four blocks. A Write, an Indicate and a Wiggle run at the same time on different glyphs, each in a lane of its own; a Transform of the Wiggle’s glyphs comes later."],["what","A block is a coloured bar in the upper part of a clip on the timeline. It carries the icon of its animation, then its name and the number of glyphs it acts on: Wiggle · 2. A block too short for its name shows the icon alone; point at it and its tooltip gives the name. It has a start, a length in seconds, an easing, and, for most blocks, the glyphs it animates. Two other kinds of animation are not blocks. A clip’s intro and outro are the hatched ends of the clip (see Appearing and disappearing), and a relation carries glyphs from one clip into another (see How relations work). Blocks come in five families, each with its own colour on the timeline. Family What it does, and what it acts on Appear Brings glyphs on screen: Write, Create, FadeIn, GrowFromCenter, DrawBorderThenFill. It acts on the glyphs you pick. Disappear Takes glyphs off screen: FadeOut, Unwrite, Uncreate, ShrinkToCenter. It acts on the glyphs you pick. Transform Moves, scales, turns or recolours the whole formula, or some of its glyphs, the ones you pick. Morph Turns glyphs of the formula into other glyphs of the same formula, in pairs that you choose. Emphasis Draws attention to glyphs for a moment and leaves them as they were: Indicate, Wiggle, Circumscribe, Flash, FocusOn. It acts on the glyphs you pick. A brace, a shape and a line are one piece each, with no glyphs to animate apart. Their Transform moves the whole object, and they take no Morph. A line is more limited still. Their own pages say what each one takes: Braces, Shapes, Lines and arrows."],["adding",""],["adding-timeline","Put the playhead at the moment the animation should start (click the ruler there), then right-click the clip, in a place where no block lies. The Add animation menu opens. Its header says which clip it is for and where the block will start: at the playhead, with its time. When the playhead is not on the clip, the block starts where you clicked instead, and the header says so: the playhead is outside this clip. The block lasts as long as its animation does by default: one second for most, two for DrawBorderThenFill, Wiggle and FocusOn. If other animations of its glyphs are in the way, it goes to the nearest free time, shortened when it must be. When it was to start at the playhead, a notice, No room at the playhead, says where it starts and what was in the way: the intro, another block, or the end of the clip. If there is no room at all, a notice says why and nothing is added."],["adding-panel","Select the clip, open the Timing tab and press Add animation in the Animations section: the same menu opens, for the time the playhead is at, wherever the playhead is. The section lists the clip’s blocks below the button. Add animation (1) adds a block at the playhead. From the keyboard, Tab reaches a clip on the timeline, and Shift+F10 (or the Menu key) opens the same menu: at the playhead when it is on the clip, else at the clip’s start."],["adding-region","To say when a block starts and when it ends before making it, mark a region on the timeline’s ruler first. While a region is marked, a block added from either menu takes it whole, wherever the playhead is: it starts where the region starts and lasts until it ends. The header of the menu says so: region 1.00–3.00 s. A block that does not fit in the region is not added, and a notice says why: the clip does not hold the region, or other animations of its glyphs are in it. Nothing moves the block to a free time nearby. Move the region, or remove it with Esc to add at the playhead again."],["adding-glyphs","A new Appear or Disappear block takes every glyph it can: all the glyphs that wait to arrive, or all those on screen. Added where other blocks already run, it names only the glyphs they leave, so moving either block later changes nothing for the other."],["gallery","Every card plays a few seconds of a small example while it is on screen. Press a card to pause it, and drag its time bar to look at a moment closely. Each family ends with the project behind its cards, which opens in the app."],["appear","An Appear block brings on screen the glyphs it names. They wait off screen until it runs, whether the clip starts with them hidden or not: the intro leaves them alone. A glyph a Disappear took away comes back the same way. In these cards the right side of y = ax^2 + bx + c waits for the block. Write Draws each glyph stroke by stroke and fills it, one glyph after another. One second, linear. Create Draws each glyph along its outline, one glyph after another. ManimGL calls it ShowCreation. FadeIn Fades all the glyphs in together. Shift X, Shift Y and Scale make them fade in from an offset or at another size. GrowFromCenter Grows the glyphs from the centre of the group, all together. DrawBorderThenFill Draws the outlines of all the glyphs at once, then fills them. Two seconds, double_smooth."],["disappear","A Disappear block takes off the glyphs it names. A glyph that is not on screen when the block starts is left alone. Here the right side of the same formula goes, and y = stays. FadeOut Fades the glyphs out together. Shift X, Shift Y and Scale set where they drift to and how large they are at the end. Unwrite Writes backwards: the last glyph is erased first, stroke by stroke. Uncreate Traces the outlines away, one glyph after another, and removes them. ShrinkToCenter Shrinks the glyphs to the centre of the group and removes them."],["transform","A Transform block has two kinds, and the Animations panel switches a block between them with Whole formula and Part of the formula. Whole formula (keyframe) Takes the whole formula to a new state: a position, a scale, a rotation, a flip, an opacity, a colour. Only the fields you tick change. Part of the formula Moves chosen glyphs from where they are: a shift, a scale and a rotation about their centre, and a colour and an opacity if you tick them. A keyframe stays between the end of the intro and the start of the outro. A part of the formula follows the formula if the whole formula moves later. The Animations section of a clip’s Timing tab has a second button, Add keyframe at playhead. It adds a whole-formula Transform that starts at the playhead and goes to the state the clip has there; you then edit what it goes to. The panel calls each of these a tween, and heads its editor Tween 1, Tween 2 and so on. You can set where a Transform ends in the Preview, by hand. Select its block: two round icons appear on its top corners. The left one moves the playhead to where the block starts and shows the state it starts from. The right one moves the playhead to where the block ends and lets you move, scale and turn the formula there, or only the glyphs of a part, with the state it starts from drawn faintly beneath. Esc, pressing the icon again, moving the playhead or selecting something else ends the edit. The icon at the end of the block (1) takes the Preview (2) to the end state of the Transform, where the formula is edited."],["morph","A Morph block turns glyphs of the formula into other glyphs of the same formula, the way a derivation grows line by line. You choose the pairs; the glyphs that arrive through the block skip the intro. Morph within the formula The second row grows out of copies of the glyphs of the first. The − has nothing to come from, so a Write brings it in. Morph blocks have their own page, with the pair editor: Morph blocks."],["emphasis","An Emphasis block acts on the glyphs that are on screen when it starts, and leaves them as they were. Indicate and Wiggle animate the glyphs themselves. Circumscribe, Flash and FocusOn draw something of their own where the glyphs are when the block starts. Two of these cards have their settings turned up, to make the effect visible in a small card: the Wiggle swells to 1.2 and rocks by 10°, where a new one starts with 1.1 and 3.6°, and the FocusOn disc is gold at 0.35 opacity, where a new one is grey at 0.2. Indicate The glyphs swell and take a colour, then come back. Scale and Colour are its settings. Wiggle The glyphs swell and rock about their centre, then come back. Scale, Angle and Wiggles set how much and how often. Turned up here to be seen. Circumscribe A rectangle is traced around the glyphs and untraced again. Colour, Gap, Stroke and Time width shape it. Flash Twelve lines shoot out of the centre of the glyphs. Its only setting is the Colour. FocusOn A disc shrinks from the whole frame onto the glyphs, over everything else. Colour and Opacity are its settings. Turned up here to be seen. The defaults of every block, the ones a new block starts with, are in the table of what a new block starts with."],["glyphs","Appear, Disappear, Emphasis and a part Transform name the glyphs they act on. The Glyphs section of the Animations panel draws the formula: click a glyph, Shift+click for a range, Ctrl+click to add or remove one, or drag a box over several. A block that names All becomes an explicit list the first time you pick by hand. Glyphs that are not on screen when the block starts are drawn faint, so you see what the block will find. Four buttons above the formula choose in one press: All: every glyph of the formula. Not on screen: the glyphs that are not on screen when the block starts, which is what an Appear brings. On screen: the glyphs that are on screen then, which is what a Disappear or an Emphasis takes. Hidden at start: the glyphs the formula hides at the start and those the clip leaves out of its intro, or every glyph of a clip that starts empty. Picking glyphs with the pointer in the Preview works for this too: see Picking glyphs."],["timing",""],["timing-fields","Select a block on the timeline: the Animations panel shows it. The Timing section has: Start: the second of the scene the block begins at. It cannot be earlier than its clip. Duration: how long it lasts, at least 0.05 s. Easing: how its speed changes (below). Lag ratio, for an Appear or a Disappear: how much later each glyph starts than the one before. Default follows Manim’s own value for the animation; Custom lets you type one in Value. 0 plays all the glyphs together; 1 plays them one after another. Shift X, Shift Y and Scale, for FadeIn and FadeOut: where the glyphs fade in from, or out to, and at what size. The settings of the animation itself are in the Animation section above. An Appear or Disappear has just its Animation menu, so you can change from Write to Create without adding a new block. A part Transform has Shift X, Shift Y, Scale, Rotation, Set opacity and Set colour. An Emphasis has its Effect and the settings of that effect. The panel for a block: what it animates (1), when (2) and on which glyphs (3)."],["timing-defaults","Block Duration Easing Settings Write 1 s linear Lag ratio Create 1 s smooth Lag ratio FadeIn 1 s smooth Lag ratio, shift, scale GrowFromCenter 1 s smooth Lag ratio DrawBorderThenFill 2 s double_smooth Lag ratio FadeOut 1 s smooth Lag ratio, shift, scale Unwrite 1 s linear Lag ratio Uncreate 1 s smooth Lag ratio ShrinkToCenter 1 s smooth Lag ratio Transform, whole formula or part 1 s smooth Position, scale, rotation, flips, opacity, colour Morph 1 s smooth Default style, path arc, pairs Indicate 1 s there_and_back Colour (yellow), scale 1.2 Wiggle 2 s smooth Scale 1.1, angle 3.6°, 6 wiggles Circumscribe 1 s smooth Colour (yellow), gap 0.1, stroke 4, time width 0.3 Flash 1 s none: each library’s own tempo Colour (yellow) FocusOn 2 s smooth Colour (grey), opacity 0.2"],["easing","An easing is a rate function: it reshapes the speed of a block without changing its length. The curve plots progress against time, so a steep stretch is fast and a flat one is slow. The eight the editor offers are the ones ManimCE and ManimGL share, named as Manim names them. Click a curve to stop on a moment and see how far along each easing is. ManimCE and ManimGL mean other curves by smooth, and by the functions made of it, so the switch above the cards shows either library’s. Indicate offers only the two that come back to where they started, marked comes back: there_and_back and there_and_back_with_pause, since it is a pulse. Flash has no easing field at all."],["timing-drag","Drag a block by its body to move it, and by an edge to change its length. It snaps to the ruler’s small ticks, the playhead, the clip’s edges, the end of the intro, the start of the outro, the edges of the clip’s other blocks and the windows of its relations. Hold Shift to move without snapping. A readout under the block gives its start, its end and its length while you drag, and names the blocks it runs alongside. Where the pointer asks for more than the clip or the other animations allow, the block stops and the readout says why. The Wiggle dragged to the left runs in parallel with the Write and the Indicate, and the readout says so. Dragged to the right, it stops at 5 s, where a Transform of the same two glyphs starts. Esc puts it back. With a block selected, Alt+← and Alt+→ move it by one frame, and with Shift by five. A block that is in a group takes the whole group with it."],["parallel","Blocks on different glyphs can run at the same time. Where they overlap, the timeline gives each its own lane, and the track grows taller to hold them. The block you drag lies on top of the others. The Write (1) and the Indicate (2) start together; the Wiggle (3) starts in the middle of both. What blocks cannot do is animate the same glyph twice at once. A glyph is busy during the intro, the outro, a relation’s window and any block that animates it; another block cannot take it at the same time. When you drag one into such a clash, it stops short of it. There is one exception. A Circumscribe, a Flash or a FocusOn only looks at its glyphs when it starts, so it may run while another block moves them. It cannot start while something is still drawing them. Three commands help to make blocks that run together. Split into runs turns one block into one block for each run of consecutive glyphs, all at the same time. Split… lets you choose the glyphs of each new block. Duplicate for the other glyphs puts a copy beside the block for the glyphs it does not animate. Blocks made this way start together and last the same, so the timeline draws them as a single block: that is a group of animation blocks."],["dialog","Double-click a block to open its dialog: the fields of the Animations panel with more room, and beside them the formula in large to choose the glyphs. It starts wide, and you can maximise it or drag its corner to resize it; the size is kept for the session. The panel keeps showing the same block, and every edit made in either place is one undo step. A keyframe and a Morph name no glyphs, so their dialog is narrower, with the fields alone and the same Preview button. A button in the dialog that starts something in the Preview, such as Edit path… of a Morph pair, closes the dialog first, because the dialog would cover what you are about to click. The dialog of a keyframe. Download blocks-parallel.mfe.json. Choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… in the Projects dialog and pick the file. Double-click the Wiggle · 2 block. In the formula on the right, Ctrl+click the 6 to add it to the block’s glyphs, and watch the label change to Wiggle · 3. Press Preview and close it with Esc. Change the Easing in the fields and press Preview again: the curve is the new one."],["menu","Right-click a block for its own menu. An Appear or a Disappear lists the other animations of both families under Change animation; an Emphasis lists its five effects under Change effect. Both keep the block’s time and glyphs. The menu of a block that names glyphs. Duplicate puts a copy right after the block, in the nearest free time. A Morph’s copy has no pairs, since a glyph arrives through one pair only. Duplicate for the other glyphs, Split into runs and Split… make blocks that run together, as above. Delete… asks before it deletes. Ctrl+D duplicates the selected block, and Delete deletes it after a confirmation."],["export","The Python the app writes follows the timeline. Each block becomes a self.play(…) of the Manim animation it names, and every stretch where nothing happens becomes a self.wait(…). Glyphs that wait for an Appear are not added to the scene until it plays. This is the first clip of the Appear example: self.add(eq_g[0], eq_g[1]) # 0 s – 0.5 s self.wait(0.5) # 0.5 s – 1.5 s: eq write self.play(Write(VGroup(*eq_g[2:])), run_time=1) # 1.5 s – 4 s self.wait(2.5) eq_g holds the glyphs of the formula in the editor’s order, so eq_g[2:] is the right side of the equation. Most blocks use Manim’s own classes, with the names you see in the menus. ManimGL calls Create ShowCreation. Wiggle and Circumscribe are written as small helper classes at the top of the file, so that both libraries do the same thing. A whole-formula Transform is a .animate call on the formula, and a part Transform one on the group of its glyphs. Manim plays one self.play at a time. Where blocks overlap without starting and ending together, the export cuts the time into slices, one self.play each, and a small helper called _Part plays the right stretch of every block that runs in a slice. The three blocks that start the clip at the top of this page come out like this: eq_indicate = Indicate(VGroup(eq_g[0], eq_g[1])) eq_appear = Write(VGroup(eq_g[7], eq_g[8])) # 1.6 s – 2.2 s: eq indicate, eq write self.play(_Part(eq_indicate, 0, 0.6), _Part(eq_appear, 0, 0.6), run_time=0.6) eq_wiggle = _Wiggle(VGroup(eq_g[3], eq_g[4])) # 2.2 s – 2.6 s: eq indicate, eq write, eq wiggle self.play( _Part(eq_indicate, 0.6, 1), _Part(eq_appear, 0.6, 1), _Part(eq_wiggle, 0, 0.2), run_time=0.4, ) The preview and the exported scene agree on what plays and when. The generated Python says how to run it and how close it is to the editor’s preview."],["next","Groups of animation blocks makes blocks that start together and last the same one group. Timing tools covers the menus of a clip, copying and pasting its timing, and splitting a block. Morph blocks make a derivation inside one formula, each row grown out of the one above it."]],"short":"Animation blocks"},{"id":"animations/groups","url":"animations/groups.html","title":"Groups of animation blocks","section":"Animations and timing","level":"intermediate","description":"Blocks that start together and last the same can be one group: how to make one, how the timeline folds it, how to move and edit it, and the rules a group keeps.","headings":[["what","What a group is"],["making","Making a group"],["split","By splitting a block"],["by-hand","By hand"],["folding","Folding and unfolding"],["moving","Moving and stretching"],["panel","In the Animations panel"],["menus","The menus of a group"],["rules","The rules of a group"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"Some blocks belong together: the two sides of an equation written at once, three terms that light up in the same second. A group ties such blocks into one thing on the timeline, so that you move, stretch, copy and delete them as one. Both sides of 2x + 4 = 10 are written at once. Two Write blocks do it, one for each side, and the timeline draws them as one block."],["what","A group is a set of blocks of one clip that start together and last the same. Each keeps its own animation, its own glyphs and its own settings, as the animations of Manim’s AnimationGroup do. What they share is their time: a group has one start and one length. The timeline draws a folded group as one block on one lane, with a double line along its foot to say there are blocks under it. Its label names the animation and says how many blocks run it: Write ×2. When the members run different animations it reads Group ×3 instead, and when they are of different families, such as an Emphasis and an Appear, the block takes a colour of its own. Only blocks that name glyphs can be in a group: Appear, Disappear, a part of the formula Transform and Emphasis. A keyframe takes the whole formula, so no block that animates glyphs runs beside it; a Morph names pairs, not glyphs, and is not grouped either. A group is only a mark on its blocks. It changes nothing of what plays and nothing of the Python the app exports: blocks that run together are one self.play already. A group is there for you, so that you can work with its blocks as one."],["making",""],["split","The usual way is to split a block. Right-click a block that names glyphs and choose Split into runs: the block becomes one block for each run of consecutive glyphs, all at its time. Here a Write over 2x + 4 and 10, which leaves the equal sign out, becomes two blocks, one for each side. The other commands that make blocks run together make groups too. Split… moves glyphs you choose to new blocks, one or several at once (Timing tools shows its dialog), and Duplicate for the other glyphs puts a copy of the block beside it for the glyphs it does not animate. Download groups-split.mfe.json. Choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… in the Projects dialog and pick the file. Right-click the Write · 6 block and choose Split into runs. Press Play: both sides write at the same time, as in the player above."],["by-hand","Any blocks of a clip that start together and last the same can be grouped. Right-click one of them: if others run with it, the menu has Group with the block that runs with it, or with the number of them, for instance Group with the 2 blocks that run with it. If their starts or their lengths differ, even a little, the entry is not there. An Indicate, a Wiggle and a Write start together and last one second: each one’s menu offers to group the other two. Grouped, they read Group ×3 on the timeline, since they run three animations, and the block takes a colour of its own, since they are of two families: Emphasis and Appear. A folded group of different animations (1). The same three blocks, played: the left side lights up, the Wiggle rocks, and the right side is written, all in the same second."],["folding","A folded group is one block. To work on its members apart, unfold it: press the arrow at the left of the group, or double-click it. The members take a lane each, with dashed borders to show that they are grouped. The arrow on the first of them folds the group again. Which groups are unfolded is only how you view the timeline. The project file does not keep it, so a project opens with its groups folded, even when you open the same project again. The menus have the same commands for the keyboard: Unfold in a group’s menu, and Fold the group in the menu of one of its unfolded members. Rest the pointer on a folded group to see its tooltip: how many animations it holds, what each one is, and when the group runs."],["moving","Drag a folded group by its body to move all its blocks, or by one of its edges to stretch all of them. The group has the limits all its members share: it stops where any of them would run into an animation of the same glyphs, or leave its clip, and the readout under it says which. Esc puts it back. The group dragged by its body: both blocks move, and both sides of the equation will write a second later. Unfolded, dragging any member moves all of them as well. With a folded group selected, Ctrl+D copies the whole group (another group, right after it) and Delete asks Delete the group? and deletes all of it. With a member of an unfolded group selected, Ctrl+D and Delete act on that member alone. Alt+← and Alt+→ move the whole group by a frame (by five with Shift), folded or not, as a drag does."],["panel","Select a group and the Animations panel shows Group ×2 at the top: a key for each member, and Take out and Ungroup beside the title. Press a key to see that member’s animation, its glyphs and its settings; change them without touching the others. The members of the group (1), the buttons that take a block out or undo the group (2), and the timing, which is the group’s (3). The Timing section is shared. Change Start or Duration of any member and the whole group changes with it. Take out makes the member you are looking at a block on its own again. The others stay grouped; if only one is left, it stops being a group too. Ungroup undoes the group: its blocks are blocks on their own again, where they are."],["menus","Right-click a folded group for its menu. Its header says what it is and the line under it lists its blocks. The menu of a folded group. Unfold shows its blocks on lanes of their own, and Ungroup undoes the group. Group with the 2 blocks that run with it takes in blocks that start with the group and last as long, if there are any. Duplicate copies the whole group, and Delete… deletes all of it after you confirm. What one animation of the group can do on its own, such as changing its animation or splitting it, is in the menu of that block, once the group is unfolded, and in the Animations panel. That menu starts with the group’s commands: Fold the group, Take out of the group and Ungroup."],["rules","A group has at least two blocks, and they are all blocks of one clip. They start together and last the same. They name glyphs: no keyframe and no Morph. Inside a group the rule of any two blocks holds: two of them cannot animate the same glyph at the same time. If an edit leaves a block that no longer runs with the others of its group, that block leaves the group by itself, and a group left with one block stops being one. Groups are saved with the project, and a project made before groups existed opens as it was. In the exported Python a group is one self.play with all its animations, the same text the blocks give without the group: # 1 s – 2 s: eq write, eq write self.play(Write(VGroup(*eq_g[:4])), Write(VGroup(eq_g[5], eq_g[6])), run_time=1) More on the blocks themselves, and on blocks that overlap without being grouped, is in Animation blocks."],["next","Timing tools covers the menus of a clip, copying and pasting its timing, and splitting a block. The generated Python reads an exported scene line by line."]],"short":"Block groups"},{"id":"animations/timing","url":"animations/timing.html","title":"Timing tools","section":"Animations and timing","level":"intermediate","description":"The menus of a clip and of several clips, copying and pasting a clip's timing, splitting an animation block, Appears with, unlinking a formula, and copying properties.","headings":[["menus","The menus of a clip and of several clips"],["several-clips","Several clips"],["timing","Copying and pasting the timing"],["timing-rules","A paste does what it says, or nothing"],["split","Split"],["split-dialog","The Split dialog"],["split-result","What a split leaves"],["appears-with","Appears with"],["appears-with-clips","What it changes"],["unlink","Unlinking a formula"],["properties","Copying and pasting properties"],["try-it","Try it"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"Small commands that save you from typing numbers and from doing work twice: copy the times of one clip onto another, split an animation block, say how new clips come in, give a clip a formula of its own, and copy how a clip looks onto others. Most of these live in the menu of a clip, so that is where this page starts."],["menus","Right-click a clip on the timeline, or press Shift+F10 with the focus on it, and its menu opens. Its first entries add animations at the playhead, or where you clicked when the playhead is not on the clip; the rest are about the clip itself. The menu of a clip of a formula, on the timeline. Entry What it does Appear, Disappear, Transform, Morph within the formula, Emphasis Add an animation block to the clip at the playhead, or where you right-clicked when the playhead is not on the clip (the Animations panel adds one at the playhead). See Animation blocks. Add brace, Add shape, Add line Hang a new object on the clip. See Braces, Shapes and Lines and arrows. Intro, Outro Give the clip the animation it starts or ends with. See Appearing and disappearing. Timing Copy the clip's times, or paste times copied from another clip. See below. Remove animations Take off the intro, the outro and every block but the Morph ones, in one step. Remove relations Formulas and texts only: take off the clip's transformations of glyphs, that is its relations with other clips and its Morph blocks. A relation whose other clip is on a locked track stays, and a notice says so. Unlink formula (make a copy) Give the clip a copy of its formula of its own. See below. Lock in the preview Make the Preview leave that formula alone, so that something under it can be reached. The timeline and the panels still edit it, and the lock is saved with the project (Placing things in the Preview). A brace, a shape or a line has fewer entries and some of its own (a colour, a direction, Detach, Become): they are on the pages of those objects. The Preview has a menu of its own for a clip. It shares Intro, Outro, Remove animations, Remove relations and Unlink formula (make a copy) with the timeline's, and adds the entries for copying properties (below), aligning, match colors and colours in order."],["several-clips","With several clips selected, right-click one of them: the menu is of the whole selection. Its first line says how many clips and how many formulas they are. The menu of two selected clips, with Timing open after copying the times of energy: the pastes, and no copy. Entry What it does Edit… or Edit together… Open the formula, when the clips are of one; or open the formulas together, with one scale and one match list for all of them (Finding and organizing). Add brace, Add shape One brace over them all, or one shape around them all. They are greyed out, with the reason as a tooltip, when the clips are never on screen together from the playhead on. Apply match colors Colour their formulas by one of the project's match lists (Match colors). Timing Paste a part of the copied timing onto all of them. There is no copy here: copying is of one clip. Unlink formula (make a copy) Give each formula among them a copy of its own, shared by its selected clips. Lock in the preview Lock them all, or unlock them all when they are all locked. Duplicate, Delete Copy or delete the clips (not their formulas), as Ctrl+D and Delete do. Clips on locked tracks are left out, and the entry says so."],["timing","To make two clips start together, last the same, or have intros of the same length, copy the timing of one and paste it onto the other. It is all in the Timing entry of the clip's menu, which opens a small menu beside it. Right-click the clip to copy from and choose Timing ▸ Copy timing. The entry shows its times, such as 1 s–5 s. One copy takes the start, the end and the lengths of the intro and of the outro. A clip on a locked track can be copied from too: a copy changes nothing. Right-click the clip to change, or select several, and choose what to paste. After copying the times of energy, each paste entry says what it would paste. A line names the clip they come from. Entry What the clip gets Paste start It starts when the copied clip does, and keeps its length: the two clips are aligned on their left edge. Paste end It ends when the copied clip does, and keeps its length: aligned on their right edge. Paste duration It lasts as long as the copied clip, from where it starts. Paste intro time Its intro lasts as long as the copied clip's intro. Off, saying it has no intro, when the copied clip has none. Paste outro time Its outro lasts as long as the copied clip's outro. Each paste is one undo step: Ctrl+Z puts the clip back as it was."],["timing-rules","Where a drag would stop, a paste stops too: the clip stays as it is, and a notice says why. That happens when another clip of the track is in the way (the notice names it and its times); the track is locked; the new length would cut an animation block or a relation of the clip, or the new intro would run into a keyframe; the window of a relation whose other clip is on a locked track would no longer fit in the clip: such a relation stays where it plays; a clip would start before 0, or be shorter than its intro and its outro, or longer than the room it has. The second clip of note is on the track of energy: it cannot start where energy does. A paste never cuts anything: a clip made shorter than its blocks or its relations reach stays as it was. Onto several clips a paste applies to each in turn, in the order of the timeline, each planned where the ones before it left things; the ones that fit change, in one undo step, and the notice names the ones that stay. The menu of a sound has Timing too, without the intro and the outro (a sound has neither). Copy the timing of a formula and paste its start onto a recording, or the other way round, to line a formula up with a narration (Audio). A clip with a relation moves with Paste start and Paste end as it does when dragged: the window of the relation goes with its target clip."],["split","An animation block animates glyphs, and Manim cannot run two animations on one glyph at the same time. But two blocks may run at once on different glyphs: the two sides of an equation, written at the same time. Splitting a block makes that from the block you have: one block per part, all at the same time and of the same length, each with its own animation and its own glyphs. Splitting is offered on the blocks that name glyphs: Appear, Disappear, a Transform of part of the formula and Emphasis. A block of an arrow is not split: it takes the whole arrow. Right-click the block: The menu of a block that names glyphs. Here Duplicate for the other glyphs is off: the only glyph the block leaves out, the =, is on screen already, so an Appear has nothing to bring in for it. Split into runs makes one block per run of consecutive glyphs the block animates: with the glyphs 0, 1, 2 and 4, 5, 6 it makes two. It is the quickest way to animate the two sides of an equation together, since the = between them is a gap. It is off when the block's glyphs make a single run. Split… opens a dialog where you choose which glyphs go to new blocks. Duplicate for the other glyphs puts a copy of the block beside it for the glyphs it does not animate. It is off when there are none."],["split-dialog","Choose the glyphs that go to one new block and press Split; the others stay in the block you split. For several new blocks, choose the glyphs of the first, press Another block (it is listed under the formula, tinted like its glyphs, and a × gives its glyphs back), and choose those of the next. A glyph is in one block only, so what you choose again is taken from the block that had it. The dialog can be resized from its corner or maximized, and it keeps its size for the session: the room it gains goes to the formula. The clip of the equation after Split…, the glyphs of the right side and Split: its two blocks are folded into one group (1), Write ×2."],["split-result","The new blocks are a group, like Manim's AnimationGroup: they start together and last the same, each keeping its own animation, glyphs and options, and the timeline draws them as one block on one lane (Write ×2, or Group ×2 when their animations differ), so a block split in ten does not take ten lanes. A group moves and stretches as one block; its arrow, or a double-click, unfolds it into its blocks, and the arrow on the first of them folds it back. A split is one undo step, and what plays does not change: the two players above are the same scene. In the export, one animation over all the glyphs becomes one animation for each part, side by side in the same self.play: self.play(Write(VGroup(*equation_g[:3], *equation_g[4:])), run_time=1.2) self.play(Write(VGroup(*equation_g[:3])), Write(VGroup(*equation_g[4:])), run_time=1.2) To give the right side another animation, unfold the group and change that block's animation from its menu. Groups have their own page: Groups of animation blocks."],["appears-with","Appears with is a field of the formula editor (double-click a formula in the Creator, or press Enter on its card). It is the intro every new clip of that formula starts with, and its length. The top of the formula editor. Appears with is the last field. The list holds Default (written Default (none) or Default (Write), following the Always add intro preference of the Edit menu, and Default (none) on an object that does not take the intro of that preference: a line is never grown, for one), None (appear) and every intro the object takes, GrowArrow among them for an arrow. A length field appears next to a real animation."],["appears-with-clips","New clips of the formula, from a drop on the timeline or from Add to the timeline, start with it. Clips already there that still have the previous intro (the same kind and the same length) follow the change, each as far as its blocks, its outgoing relations and its outro leave room. A clip you retouched in the Animations panel, or from its menu, keeps its own, and so does a clip on a locked track, which a notice counts. It also goes the other way: an intro you give a clip from the menu entry Intro becomes what Appears with says from then on. The formula's other clips stay as they are. After setting Appears with to Write for note: its clip over 6–8 s, which had no intro, now writes in; the clip over 2–4.5 s, which had its own FadeIn, kept it. The composition editor of a LaTeX text has the same field. What intros there are, and the preference behind Default, are on Appearing and disappearing."],["unlink","A formula on the timeline more than once is one object: change its LaTeX, its size or its colour and every clip shows it. That is what you want until you do not. Unlink formula (make a copy) gives one clip a copy of the formula of its own, so that editing either no longer changes the clips of the other. note has two clips. Unlinking the one on Notes gave it note2, right after the original in the Creator; the clip changes name, and the other keeps note. The copy is made as Duplicate makes it in the Creator: under the next free name (note becomes note2), right after the original in the list and in its group. The clip keeps everything it had: its blocks, its colours, its relations as source or as target, the objects hung on it or on its glyphs, and the glyphs picked on it. The copy has the same glyphs, so the Preview does not change. One undo step brings the clip back to the original and removes the copy. With several clips selected, the selected clips of each formula share one copy; among clips of several formulas the entry reads Unlink formulas (make 3 copies), or however many. The entry is off, and its tooltip says why, for a formula's only clip (there is nothing to unlink it from), when all the clips of a formula are selected together, and on a locked track: a selected clip on a locked track keeps its formula, and a notice says so. Braces, shapes and lines have no such entry: they live with their clips."],["properties","To make one formula look and sit like another, copy its properties and paste them. This is done in the Preview, on the clips of the scene at the playhead. Copy properties takes the state of one clip as it is shown at the playhead: its position, scale, rotation, flips, opacity, colour and the colours of its glyphs. A paste is one undo step. Right-click the clip in the Preview and choose Copy properties. The clip under the pointer is the one copied. Select the clips to change (Ctrl or Shift and a click, or a rectangle), right-click one of them and open Paste properties. Edit ▸ Copy properties, which wants exactly one clip selected, and Edit ▸ Paste properties do the same. With two or more clips selected, a bar in the top-left corner of the Preview has Paste properties as a small menu next to the align keys. After copying energy, scaled ×1.25, turned 8°, at 80 % opacity and yellow: each entry shows what it would paste. Entry What it pastes All but position The transformation, the appearance and the glyph colours below. Transformation Scale, rotation and flips. Appearance Opacity and colour. Glyph colours The colours of single glyphs, onto clips of the same formula only, since they go by glyph. Position The place on the frame. Pasted onto several clips it stacks them on one point, on purpose. The bar that appears over the Preview when two clips are selected. A paste changes what a drag in the Preview would change at the playhead: the clip's starting state, or the keyframe in effect there. A clip it cannot change now stays as it is and a notice names it: one that is not on screen at the playhead, one on a locked track, or one locked after its transformations. A line has no scale, turn or flip of its own: it takes the rest of what is pasted."],["try-it","Download timing-clips below and open it: choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… in the dialog and pick the file (Projects and files). Right-click the clip of energy and choose Timing ▸ Copy timing. Right-click the first clip of note (on the Notes track) and choose Timing ▸ Paste start, then Paste duration. Undo twice. Try Paste start on the second clip of note: it is on the track of energy, so a notice says what is in its way and nothing moves. Right-click either clip of note and choose Unlink formula (make a copy). Open note2 in the Creator, change its LaTeX, and see only that clip change. Open note in the Creator, set Appears with to Write, and watch which clips follow. Download timing-split-start below and open it. Right-click its Appear block, choose Split…, click the glyphs 4, 5 and 6, which are the right side, and press Split. Play the scene: it is the one it was."],["next","The animations a clip plays are on Animation blocks, and groups of blocks on Groups of animation blocks. The clip's own times in numbers are in the Timing tab of the Animations panel; moving and trimming clips with the mouse is in Working with the timeline."]],"short":"Timing tools"},{"id":"animations/camera","url":"animations/camera.html","title":"The camera","section":"Animations and timing","level":"intermediate","description":"Move and zoom the frame over the scene as the video plays: the multicamera, camera moves and target cameras.","headings":[["on","Turning the multicamera on"],["move","A camera move"],["formulas","Moving a formula in the world view"],["isolate","Frames one over another"],["block","The block of a move"],["locked","Nothing else animates while the camera moves"],["targets","Target cameras"],["view","The camera view"],["keys","From the keyboard"],["off","Turning it off"],["python","In the exported Python and the video"],["try-it","Try it"]],"chunks":[[null,"A scene is filmed through a frame. With the multicamera on, that frame is a camera you can move and zoom while the video plays: it closes in on a term, travels to a formula written far away, pulls back to show everything. The camera closes in on c², which is indicated; travels to the square root, written outside the first frame; pulls back to show both; and returns to where it started."],["on","Choose View ▸ Multicamera, press Alt+M, or press the camera button of the Preview’s toolbar. The scene gets a camera, in Manim’s own frame to begin with. It is part of the project: it is saved with it, and turning it on is one undo step. Two things change on screen: the timeline gets a Camera row under the ruler, which holds the camera’s moves as blocks; the Preview shows the world: its canvas takes the whole panel, nothing is cut at the frame, and the camera is drawn over the scene as a rectangle. In the world view: the camera at the playhead is the sky-blue rectangle with the corners of a viewfinder, a cross at its centre and its name. What it leaves out is dimmed; a target camera is a pink rectangle with a name: a frame set aside for moves to go to; a pink dashed rectangle with a number is the frame a move goes to on its own; the number is the place of that move in the order they run; Start, dashed, is the frame of 0 s, shown while the camera is somewhere else; the dotted line is Manim’s own frame, where the picture is without a camera; a line with arrowheads joins the frames in the order the moves run. Fit shows everything of the camera at once. Preferences ▸ Show the camera’s route and Preferences ▸ Dim what the camera leaves out hide the line and the dimming."],["move","A move takes the camera from the frame it is in to another, over a time. To make one: Put the playhead where the move should start. Press + in the header of the Camera row (Add camera move). A block of one second appears, and the playhead jumps to its end. In the Preview, drag the camera to where the move should arrive. Take it by its edge, its name or the cross of its centre; drag a corner to zoom. Add camera move at 1.5 s; then the camera is dragged to the right and made smaller by a corner: the move now ends there. In the world view the pointer takes the camera’s rectangles only. The formulas are drawn and left alone: pointing at one outlines nothing, and a click or a drag on one neither selects nor moves it, so a press meant for the camera never takes the formula under it. A line under the scene says so. To select and move the formulas, hold F (below), look through the camera (below) or select their clips on the timeline. A click beside the rectangles clears the selection, and the view is panned and zoomed as in any Preview. While you drag, the rectangle sticks to the formulas, to the other rectangles and to Manim’s frame, and to the grid when it is shown; hold Ctrl to suspend that, and Shift to move along one axis only. The arrow keys nudge the selected rectangle by 0.01 (with Shift, 0.1). What you drag is the frame the camera rests in at the playhead: the frame of 0 s before the first move, the destination of the last move after it. With the playhead inside a move the camera is on its way and cannot be dragged: go to the end of the move."],["formulas","Sometimes a formula has to be put where the camera will be, and the camera’s rectangles are only drawn in the world view. Hold F: while the key is down the pointer takes the formulas there, as it does in any Preview. A click selects one, a drag moves it, Shift shows its handles, Alt picks its glyphs and a right-click opens its menu. The camera’s rectangles are the ones left alone meanwhile. Let the key go and the pointer is the camera’s again; a drag you had started goes on until you release the button. The key does nothing while the scene plays, in a text field or with a menu or a dialog open."],["isolate","When the camera goes to the same place more than once, each move to a frame of its own, the rectangles of those frames lie one over another, and a press takes the one on top. To move one that is underneath: Right-click its move on the Camera row and choose Isolate in the Preview. Only the rectangle of the frame that move goes to answers the pointer now. It is drawn over the others, which are faint, and the block of the move wears a dashed ring. Drag the rectangle, scale it by a corner or nudge it with the arrow keys. Press Stop isolating, in the corner of the Preview, or Esc. Every rectangle answers again. The same entry ends the Preview’s right-click menus and the menu of the move. The first move isolated: (1) the rectangle of its frame, the only one that answers; (2) the camera and the other rectangles, faint; (3) Stop isolating; (4) the block of the isolated move. A move that goes to a target camera isolates that target camera’s rectangle, and one that goes to the start, the frame of 0 s. Isolating is a way of looking: it changes nothing of the project, and it ends when its move is deleted. Moves that should arrive at the very same frame can share a target camera: one rectangle, which you edit once for all of them."],["block","On the Camera row a move is a block, and it behaves like an animation block: drag it along the time, stretch it by its edges, right-click it to Isolate it in the Preview (above), to Duplicate it or to Delete it. Right-click the row to add a move where you clicked. Its label says where it goes: Label The move goes to → 200 % a frame of its own, named by its zoom (100 % is Manim’s frame) → Result the target camera with that name → Start the frame of 0 s The Camera row: (1) its name, its padlock and +; (2) a move; (3) the locked region under a move. With a move selected, the Animations panel shows its Start, Duration and Easing, and where it Goes to: A frame of its own, with its X, Y and Zoom; a target camera; or Start (the frame at 0 s)."],["locked","A camera move has its time to itself. Under each move a hatched band crosses every track: the locked region. A move asked for where an animation runs starts after it, and one with less room than a second is as long as the room. A notice says so. A move dragged or stretched stops against the animation before it and after it; the readout under it says which. An animation block, the window of a relation and the edge of an intro or an outro dragged towards a move stop at its edge. What still ends up under a move, such as a whole clip dragged there, is an error of the project: the band turns red, and the Python is not exported until one of the two is moved. An object may appear or disappear without an animation during a move: that is no animation."],["targets","A target camera is a frame with a name that moves can go to. Several moves can share one, and moving it changes them all. Right-click the empty scene and choose Add target camera here: it is centred where you clicked, as large as the camera is then. Right-click a target camera for Move camera here (a move to it, at the playhead), Rename… and Delete. Right-click the camera itself, or a move’s own frame, and Add target camera here makes a target camera of that frame. The menu of the target camera Result. Deleting a target camera that moves go to asks first. Those moves stay: each keeps going where the target camera was, to a frame of its own. A click on the name of the Camera row shows the camera in the Animations panel: the frame it rests in at the playhead, and the list of target cameras."],["view","The world view shows the camera from outside. Press V, or the viewfinder button beside the camera’s, or choose View ▸ Camera view, to look through it: the Preview shows what the video will show at the playhead, and playing shows the moves. The rectangles are left out, and the pointer is the formulas’ again: select them, move them and edit them as ever. V again goes back to the world. V looks through the camera; the scene plays as the video will; V returns to the world. Each view keeps a zoom and a pan of its own. With the multicamera on, the frame is the camera’s at the playhead, in both views: Align to the frame and the guides of a dragged formula refer to what is seen then (Aligning to the frame). The grid stays the world’s."],["keys","Keys What they do Alt+M Multicamera on or off V Camera view on or off F, held In the world view, the pointer takes the formulas instead of the camera’s rectangles Esc Stop isolating a move Delete Delete the selected move, or the selected target camera Ctrl+D Duplicate the selected move into the first place it fits after it Alt+←, Alt+→ Nudge the selected move a frame (with Shift, five) Arrow keys, in the Preview Nudge the selected rectangle 0.01 (with Shift, 0.1) On the Camera row, Tab reaches the moves after the row’s buttons, the arrow keys go from move to move, and Enter opens the menu of the focused one."],["off","The multicamera stays on while the camera has target cameras or moves: delete them first, and a notice says so if you try. The padlock of the Camera row locks the camera instead: its moves and rectangles can be selected, not changed."],["python","The video is filmed through the camera, and so is the exported scene. A ManimCE scene becomes a MovingCameraScene. In both libraries each move is a self.play(…) of its own on the camera’s frame (cam = self.camera.frame), and a move back to the start is Restore(cam). A ManimGL scene whose camera zooms starts with a few lines that make strokes scale with the zoom as ManimCE draws them (ManimGL 1.7.2 or later). See The generated Python."],["try-it","Open the file: choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… in the dialog and pick it. Click the block → Result on the Camera row, then drag the pink rectangle Result in the Preview: the move arrives where you put it. Press V and play the scene from the start."]],"short":"The camera"},{"id":"objects/texts","url":"objects/texts.html","title":"LaTeX texts and font texts","section":"Texts and objects","level":"intermediate","description":"Write a text in LaTeX or in any font, place it on the stage of the composition editor, colour its glyphs, choose where its new clips start, and see what a font text looks like without the companion.","headings":[["kinds","Two kinds of text"],["new","Making a text"],["composition","The composition editor"],["writing","Writing a LaTeX text"],["size","Size and colour"],["layout","Layout"],["object","The text as an object"],["stage","The stage and the position"],["start-position","New clips start here"],["status","The status line, the dot and the strip"],["glyph-colours","Glyph colours"],["apply","Apply, Cancel and Add to the timeline"],["editing","Editing a text again"],["font-text","Font text"],["export","What the export writes"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A formula is mathematics. A text is words: a title, a caption, a note, with some mathematics inside it if you want. The editor has two kinds of text, and one place to write both: the composition editor. A title written in LaTeX, with its maths coloured glyph by glyph, writes in above a formula."],["kinds","LaTeX text Font text You write LaTeX in text mode: $x^2$ for maths, \\\\ for a line break, a blank line for a paragraph. Plain text in any font. Enter adds a line. Marked in the Creator by T P Drawn by LaTeX, when the companion runs; MathJax otherwise. Pango, when the companion runs. Without it, as placeholder boxes. Its form has Size, colour, width, alignment, line spacing, name, tag and Appears with. Font family, size, bold, italic and colour. Glyph colours in the editor In the Glyphs view: each glyph coloured or hidden at the start. One colour for the whole text. Use a LaTeX text when the words have mathematics in them, or when you want the look of LaTeX. Use a font text for a typeface that LaTeX does not have."],["new","The + New button of the Creator makes a formula. Its arrow lists every kind of object: Formula, Text (LaTeX) and Text (font). Choose one of the texts and the composition editor opens. The arrow of + New lists the kinds of object; the ring (1) is on Text (LaTeX). Nothing is added to the Creator until you press Apply. Cancel leaves nothing behind."],["composition","The editor is a dialog with a form on the left and a stage on the right: the video frame, in which the text is drawn and placed. It is the same for both kinds, with the fields of each kind in the form. Maximise the dialog, or drag its corner, and it gives its room to the stage, which stays whole at the frame’s aspect ratio, and to the text field."],["writing","Type in the Text field. The text is in LaTeX’s text mode: ordinary words, with $…$ around a piece of mathematics, \\\\ to break a line, and a blank line to start a paragraph. Ctrl+Enter applies from the keyboard. The stage draws the text a moment after you stop typing. The line over the stage says how many glyphs it has and who drew them: 41 glyphs · MathJax in the browser alone, 41 glyphs · exact (LaTeX) when the companion typesets it. A LaTeX text typed, with a piece of maths in it, and then dragged on the stage. Without the companion, MathJax breaks a paragraph into lines by measuring words, which is approximate: a line may break a little differently from how LaTeX would. The strip under the stage says so. See The companion."],["size","Size is one number written twice: the Scale and Manim’s Font size, with the scale times 48 equal to the font size, so 1.5 × 48 = 72. Type either, or a fraction such as 3/2 for the scale. Colour is a hexadecimal colour for the whole text."],["layout","The layout decides how the lines of the text sit in a block. Width is the width of the block in scene units. Empty means “as wide as the text comes”. The frame of a 16:9 project is 14.2 units wide, and the field says the frame’s width of your project beside it. Align sets how the lines sit inside the block: Left, Centre or Right. Line spacing is a factor of the normal distance between lines, from 0.5 to 3. The same paragraph, 4 units wide, with its lines aligned left, centred and right."],["object","The Object group holds what the text is in the Creator: its Name (a name already in use is refused when you leave the field), its Tag (a colour and a short icon) and Appears with, the intro its new clips start with. A clip that still has the previous intro follows when you change it."],["stage","Drag the text on the stage to place it. Two boxes above the stage help: Grid draws a grid, whose spacing you choose from 2 down to 0.1 units, and Snap makes the text stick to the lines of the frame. The text snaps to the grid as well. Hold Ctrl while you drag to move without snapping. The arrow keys, with the stage focused, move the text by 0.1 units, and with Shift by 1. Under the stage, X and Y give the position in scene units, and Center horizontally and Center vertically bring the text to the middle of the frame along one axis. Fit frame, the zoom and the pan change what you see of the stage without changing where the text is placed. Zoom with − and + or with Ctrl and the wheel (or with a pinch on the trackpad), and pan with the middle button or with Alt held while you drag."],["start-position","A text opened from the Creator is a library object: no clip is being edited, so the stage places nothing on the timeline. It shows where the new clips of the text will start. The box New clips start here is ticked whenever the editor opens. With it ticked, Apply keeps the position in the text: every clip of it that you drop on the timeline or add from a menu afterwards starts there instead of at the centre of the frame. Clips already on the timeline stay where they are. Untick the box and the position is only somewhere to look at the text: it is not saved, and the text keeps the start position it already had. To start at the centre again, set X and Y to 0, or press both centre buttons, and apply. The position of a clip itself belongs to that clip. Moving one clip does not move the others, and it does not change where the text’s next clip starts."],["status","The dot beside the line over the stage tells what the render of your draft is doing: amber while the text is being rendered again (the last appearance stays on the stage meanwhile); green when what is drawn is current; red when the text was refused; hollow when there is nothing to draw. While you type, the words beside the dot stay as they are and the stage does not move: only the dot changes. What the renderer says goes into a strip under the stage, of a fixed height, scrolled when it is long: the error LaTeX gave with its log, warnings such as approximate line breaks, or the fontenc line that accented letters need, and the reason Apply waits, if it does. A text that does not compile: a red dot, and the error under the stage. The stage keeps the last appearance that rendered, and the centre buttons wait for a text that renders."],["glyph-colours","For a LaTeX text, the switch over the stage shows Glyphs in place of Placement: the glyphs of the draft, to colour or to hide at the start, as in the formula editor. Click a glyph, Shift+click for a range, Ctrl+click to add or remove one, or drag a box over several; then press a colour. Clear colours takes them away, and Hide, Show and Show all set which glyphs are hidden at the start. What you colour stays with its glyphs while you go on typing or change the layout. Apply writes the colours with the text, and carries along the pairs, blocks and attachments that name glyphs of the text. Colours need a text that renders: while it does not, Apply waits, and a line says why. Where a change cannot be followed glyph by glyph, the view says so, and the text keeps the colours it had. See Colouring glyphs and objects for the other ways to colour."],["apply","Apply makes the text an object of the Creator, or updates the one you opened, as a single undo step: its text, its layout, its colours and its position go back with one Ctrl+Z. Cancel throws the draft away. A click outside the dialog does neither: it stays open. Everything you type or place stays in a draft until Apply. While the editor is open, playback pauses and does not start again by itself, and Save, Open and Export wait: apply or cancel first, then run the command again. If the tab closes with a draft unapplied, the editor offers Recover unapplied draft the next time you open that text. Apply puts nothing on the timeline: the text is in the Creator, ready to use. The line at the left of the footer says so for a new text: New library object · no clip will be added. Putting a clip on the timeline is a separate step, with the text selected in the Creator. The Creator after Apply: the LaTeX text (T), a formula, and a font text (P), drawn as placeholder boxes, with the badge that says it needs the companion. Drag its card onto the timeline. Right-click its card and choose Add to the timeline. Select the card and press Add to the timeline in the Animations panel, which adds a clip at the playhead. The menu of a card in the Creator. The Animations panel, with a text that no clip uses selected. It calls every object of the Creator a “formula”."],["editing","Open a text that exists in any of four ways: double-click its card in the Creator, double-click its clip on the timeline, double-click the text itself in the Preview, or select two or more clips of it, right-click and choose Edit…. What the stage places depends on where you came from. From the Creator, or from several selected clips, the text is a library object, with New clips start here. From one clip or from the Preview, the stage places that clip, and the box is not there. The line under the stage says Placement: clip initial state. Changes apply only to this occurrence. Opened from its clip, the editor places that clip: there is no New clips start here box. The text itself, with its layout and its colours, is shared by every clip that uses it. Change it and every clip changes, the position of each clip aside. When a text has more than one use and you opened it from a clip, Make independent on Apply gives that clip a copy of the text of its own, and Apply changes only the copy."],["font-text","A Text (font) is plain text in a typeface you name. Its form has the Font family, the Size (the font size, from 4 to 512), Bold, Italic and the Colour of the whole text. The stage and its controls are the same as for a LaTeX text. A font text needs the companion to be drawn with its real glyphs. The companion shapes the text with Pango, in the fonts of your machine, and proposes the families it has as you type the name. In version 1.0 it does this on Linux only: on macOS and Windows a font text stays as boxes. Without it, a font text is drawn as placeholder boxes: one box for each character, laid out with the font the browser has under that name. The badge needs the companion says so, in the editor and in the Creator. A font text without the companion: a placeholder box for each character, the badge, and the note under the stage. The same kind of text in a scene, as the editor draws it without the companion: a placeholder box for each character. A font text drawn as boxes still works like any other object. You can compose it, place it, colour it, give it an intro and animate it. The project saves, opens and exports. Export ▸ Python — ManimCE… (or Python — ManimGL…) writes the text and counts its glyphs one for each character, and says so. A video exported in the browser shows the boxes, as the Preview does. When the companion shapes the text, its real glyphs take the place of the boxes and keep what you did to them. The shape it found is saved in the project, so a project with font text opens with its real appearance even on a machine without the fonts or the companion. Refresh appearance accepts a new shape on purpose, after you change fonts or versions: reconnecting the companion never replaces a saved shape by itself."],["export","A LaTeX text is exported as a Tex object. Its width becomes a \\parbox, and its alignment \\centering, \\raggedright or \\raggedleft: title = Tex(r\"\\parbox{120.45pt}{\\centering Completing the square starts from $x^2 + bx + c$\\par}\", font_size=72) The glyphs are matched to the editor’s by position, as for every formula. The generated Python explains how. A font text is exported as a Manim Text, with the family, the size, the weight and the slant you chose. Right after it the file checks the text: if Manim draws it with a different number of glyphs than the editor counted, because fonts or versions differ, it prints a warning on stderr and renders the scene all the same. caption = Text(r\"Any font on your machine\", font=r\"DejaVu Sans\", font_size=96, weight=r\"BOLD\", slant=r\"NORMAL\", disable_ligatures=False) _mfe_verify_text(caption, r\"caption\", \"ce\", 20) A font text is shaped for the Manim library the project targets. An export for the other library writes the same text at a converted size, and says what may differ: the size by a few percent, the spacing of lines and tabs. The export never waits for the companion over a text. It lists the texts the editor cannot vouch for, because there is no companion to compare the fonts of this machine with, and goes on. Press the arrow of + New and choose Text (LaTeX). Type Pythagoras said $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$ about right triangles. Drag the text to the upper left of the stage. New clips start here is ticked: leave it. Press Apply, then drag the new card of the Creator onto the timeline. The clip starts where you left the text."],["next","The template library starts a formula from one of 257 ready-made ones, by filling in its placeholders. Colouring glyphs and objects covers the other ways to colour glyphs. The companion draws exact LaTeX glyphs and the real glyphs of a font text."]],"short":"Texts"},{"id":"objects/templates","url":"objects/templates.html","title":"The template library","section":"Texts and objects","level":"basic","description":"Start from one of 257 ready-made formulas by filling in its placeholders, write templates of your own, keep favourites, and learn where they are kept.","headings":[["library","The template library"],["default-templates","Default templates"],["use","Using a template"],["how-values-go-in","How a value goes into the formula"],["what-you-get","What you get"],["your-own","Your own templates"],["my-templates","My templates"],["favorites","Favorites"],["storage","Where your templates are kept"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A template is a formula with blanks. The editor comes with 257 of them, from high-school algebra to university physics: you fill in the blanks, and the formula is in your project. You can write templates of your own, star the ones you reach for most, and take them to another machine."],["library","The Templates tab is the second tab of the Creator. It has four entries, and each one opens a pop-up. The four entries of the Templates tab. Entry What it opens Default templates The 257 formulas that come with the editor. My templates Your own templates, with the buttons to change them. Create template The form that writes a new template of yours. Favorites Every template you gave a star, yours and the default ones."],["default-templates","The default library has 257 formulas in 15 categories: Algebra, Functions, Trigonometry, Analytic geometry, Sequences and series, Limits, Derivatives, Integrals, Multivariable calculus, Linear algebra, Differential equations, Complex numbers, Probability and statistics, Sets and logic, and Physics. Each one is marked High school or University. The default library, searched for derivative. Each card shows the formula with the template's own values. Along the top of the pop-up are a search field, a category list and a level list, and a count that says N of 257 while they filter the cards. Search takes any number of words, and every word has to appear in the name, the description, the LaTeX or the category of a template. Both lists start at All: type, and the cards narrow at once while the count follows. The cards are grouped under the name of their category. On each card: the name, its level, and a star that keeps it in Favorites; the drawing of the formula, with the values the template comes with; Use, which is the same as a double-click on the card, and Save a copy, which puts a copy among your own templates (see Your own templates). The pop-up is resizable, and the search and the filters of each list are still there the next time you open it. In the search field, Esc empties the search first; with nothing to empty, and anywhere else in the pop-up, it closes it."],["use","Press Use on a card. What happens next depends on whether the template has blanks, which the editor calls placeholders. A template without placeholders, such as Euler's identity, is added to the project at once: no dialog. A template with placeholders opens a dialog that asks for their values. The dialog of Product of powers: the drawing (1), its LaTeX (2), a field for each placeholder (3), the name of the formula (4) and Create formula (5). The drawing shows the formula the values make, and redraws as you type. Under it, the LaTeX those values give. A field for each placeholder, labelled #1, #2… and, when the template says what each one is, with its name (base, first exponent). It starts with the template's own value. Type any LaTeX there. The name the new formula will have. It starts as the template's name made into a valid one (product_of_powers), and the rules of names apply. Create formula adds the formula to the project. Reset values puts back the values the template came with, and Cancel leaves without making anything. Type a value in a field and the drawing follows: x for the base, then 2k for the first exponent. A value that LaTeX cannot draw shows the error under the LaTeX, and Create formula stays off until the value is right. Enter in any field creates the formula, too."],["how-values-go-in","The template's LaTeX names its blanks #1, #2… and each is replaced by the value you typed, the way a LaTeX macro takes its arguments. The braces are the template's, not yours, so a value that is longer than one character stays together: Template Values Formula #1^{#2}\\cdot #1^{#3}=#1^{#2+#3} x, 2k, 3 x^{2k}\\cdot x^{3}=x^{2k+3} Where two pieces would run together (a command followed by a letter, or two braces side by side), the editor puts a space between them, as LaTeX ignores it in maths."],["what-you-get","After Create formula the Creator goes to its Formulas tab with the new formula selected. The formula is an ordinary one: its LaTeX is final, with the values already in it, and nothing remembers that it came from a template. Edit it, rename it or delete it as you would any other (see Writing formulas). Because the LaTeX was final from the start, its glyphs are made once, of what it is, so nothing has to be matched again afterwards. A template without placeholders goes straight into the project, named after itself. The dialog, and the form that writes a template, are drawn 30 % larger than the rest of the editor, and can be resized by their corner or maximized. What you gain goes to the drawing of the formula. Open the Templates tab and press Default templates. Type quadratic in the search, and press Use on Quadratic formula. The dialog has four fields: the unknown, and the three coefficients. Change the unknown to t, and watch the drawing. Press Create formula, then double-click the new formula in the Creator to read its LaTeX. Back in the library, give the template a star, and open Favorites."],["your-own","When you write the same shape of formula again and again, make it a template. Press Create template in the Templates tab, or New template in My templates, and fill in the form. The form with {#1}+{#2}={#2}+{#1} written: a row for each number, a default value in each, and the drawing the values make. Name, Category and Description. The category is any name; the list offers the library's and the ones you have used. LaTeX: the formula, with #1, #2… where a value goes. The braces around a number are yours, as in a LaTeX macro: x^{#2} takes m+1 whole. \\# prints a hash. Placeholders: a row for every number in the LaTeX, with its Default value and, optionally, What it is (base, exponent, angle…). A default value is required. The drawing uses a box \\square for a number that has none yet. LaTeX packages, folded away until you open it, for a template that needs some. The line beside the buttons tells you what is missing: Give the template a name, Write the LaTeX of the template, Give #1 a default value or the reason the LaTeX cannot be drawn. Save template is off until the form is complete. Take a number out of the LaTeX and write it again, and it comes back with the value and the name it had. Closing a form with something written asks before it throws it away."],["my-templates","My templates: the buttons of a card, the file buttons above, and under the list where the templates are kept. On each card of yours: Use, Edit (the same form, with your template in it), Duplicate and Delete. Deleting asks first, and the button has to be held down. A formula you already made from the template stays. Tick the boxes to pick which templates Export takes. The default templates cannot be changed. To change one, press Save a copy on its card: a notice says it is now among yours, with a button, Open My templates, and the copy is yours to edit. The notice after Save a copy."],["favorites","The star on any card, default or yours, keeps the template in Favorites. That pop-up lists them together, with Mine on the ones you wrote, and press the star again to take one out. Your stars are kept like your templates. Two templates with a star."],["storage","Your templates and your stars belong to the editor, not to a project: they are kept in this browser, like the editor's preferences, so they are there in every project you open in it, and they are not in a project file. In the browser. Always. A browser that does not let the editor keep anything says so in the line under My templates; then the templates last until the tab is closed, and Export is how you keep them. In a synced folder (Chrome and Edge). After File ▸ Sync folder… the templates are also written to templates/templates.json in that folder, so they travel with it. The browser asks once for leave to write the folder. The file is read again whenever the folder is listed: of a template changed in two places the one changed last stays, and one deleted in one place and still in the other comes back. A library holds 500 templates. When the file and the browser have more than that together, or the file has entries that are not templates, the editor never writes the file again with less than it holds: it leaves it as it is, and the line under the list says what to do, such as deleting some templates here or in the file and listing the folder again. As a file. Export all downloads your templates and favorites as mfe-templates.json, or Export N selected the ticked ones. Import… reads such a file. When a template of the file is one of yours with other content, a question asks: Replace mine, Keep both or Keep mine. The line under the list says which of these is in force, and offers Try again or Write to the folder when the folder could not be written. In a browser that cannot write to folders (Firefox, Safari) the templates stay in the browser, and the line says so. Export them to take them elsewhere."],["next","Writing formulas: edit what a template made. Finding and organizing: search, group and tidy your formulas. Projects and files: the Sync folder."]],"short":"Templates"},{"id":"objects/organizing","url":"objects/organizing.html","title":"Finding and organizing","section":"Texts and objects","level":"intermediate","description":"Search your formulas, select them by region or by kind, group them in the Creator, edit several together, set a standard scale, and unlink a clip from its formula.","headings":[["toolbar","The Creator's toolbar"],["search","Search"],["region","Selecting by region"],["select-in-list","Selecting in the list"],["region-in-list","By region, in the list"],["by-kind","Select by kind"],["groups","Groups in the Creator"],["edit-together","Edit together"],["standard-scale","Standard scale"],["duplicate-unlink","Duplicate, and unlink a clip from its formula"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A project grows: nine formulas, then ninety. This page is about finding one, picking several at once, keeping them in order with groups, changing many together, and giving a clip a formula of its own. The examples use a small project, Circles and triangles, with nine formulas and two LaTeX texts. Its card is at the end of the page; to follow along, choose File ▸ Open… and press Other file… in the dialog that opens (Projects and files explains it)."],["toolbar","The toolbar: the search (1), the view (2), the menu of the kinds (3), the size of the previews (4) and + New (5). Everything below starts here. The magnifier opens the search, the key beside it switches between the list and the block view, showing the icon of the other (see Writing formulas), the ∑ key opens a menu to select a kind of object, +/− makes the previews larger with a click and smaller with a right-click, and + New makes one. In a Creator too narrow for all of it on one line, as it starts in a window of 1280 pixels (in one of 1440 or 1920 it is all on one line), the ∑ key and the key of the previews take a line of their own, under the magnifier."],["search","The search covers the Preview. Typing pi narrows the three tabs at once; the key Esc gives the Preview back. Press the magnifier, or Ctrl+F from anywhere (but over a dialog, where the browser's own find takes the key), and a panel comes up in the place of the Preview. The Preview is still there underneath, as you left it, and the rest of the app stays in use beside the search: the Creator, the Animations panel and the timeline all work while it is open. The search panel: the field (1), the tabs with their counts (2), the results (3) and the close button (4). What you type is looked for in the names, in the sources of the objects (their LaTeX, or the text of a font text) and in the names of their groups, in the three tabs at once. The three tabs, Formulas, Texts (LaTeX) and Fonts, each say how many they found. With nothing typed, each lists every object of its kind. A result is a card: the drawing, the name, a line of the source, how many clips use it and the group it is in. A result: its name and clips (1), a line of its LaTeX (2) and its group (3). The panel is not a dialog, and a result is a card of the list in what it does: A click selects it; Ctrl+click and Shift+click select several, as in the list. A double-click, or Enter, opens its editor. A right-click opens the menu of the selected formulas: Edit together…, Add to the timeline, Group, Apply match colors, Duplicate and Delete N formulas…, under a line that says how many are selected. Drag it onto the timeline. The search stays open, so several can go one after another. Close it with its close button, the magnifier or Esc. The tab and the text are there the next time, selected so that typing replaces them. While the search covers the Preview, the keys that act on the scene (align, the grid, the circle selector, align by symbol, colours in order) wait. The search is for the project in front of you: open another, and it closes and forgets its text. The list in the Creator is not filtered by the search, and its groups are not folders in it: the formulas of a group are results like any other."],["region","A rectangle dragged from the empty space between the cards selects the ones it touches; a second one, with Ctrl held, takes a card out. Drag from the empty space of the results (the gaps between the cards, or the margin around them) and a rectangle selects every card it touches: with no key, the cards become the selection; with Shift, they are added to it; with Ctrl, they are taken out of it. The list may scroll under a held rectangle, with the wheel, and the rectangle stays on the cards it was drawn over. Esc gives the selection back as it was. A press on a card is still the card's: its click, and its drag onto the timeline. With no key a region replaces the selection, and a formula that the tab or the text hides leaves it, as it does on a plain click on a card. Hold Shift to keep it."],["select-in-list","The tools of this page act on what is selected, so here is how the cards of the Creator are selected, in the list and in the block view alike. The results of the search take the same clicks and keys. A click selects that card alone. Ctrl+click adds a card to the selection, or takes it out when it is in. Shift+click selects the range from the card you clicked last to this one; with Ctrl too, the range is added to what is selected. A group's row counts as one card in a range, and brings all its formulas. The keyboard does the same on the card that has the focus: click a card, or press Tab to reach the list. Keys What they do ↑ ↓, Home, End Move to the previous or next card, or to the first or the last, and select it. Shift with those keys Select the range from the card you last clicked or selected by key. Ctrl with those keys Move the focus and leave the selection as it is. Space Select the card that has the focus. Ctrl+Space Add the card to the selection, or take it out. ← → In the block view and in the results of the search, move to the previous or next block or card; ↑ ↓ then move a whole row of them. On a group's row, Enter opens and closes the group; see Groups in the Creator."],["region-in-list","A rectangle from the free space under the blocks selects the ones it touches; the next one, with Alt held, starts on a block. The list takes the rectangle of the search too, in the list view and in the block view alike. Drag from its empty space (its margins, the gaps between the cards, the room under the last one) and every card it touches is selected, with the same keys: none to replace the selection, Shift to add, Ctrl to take out. Esc gives the selection back, and the rectangle stays on its cards while the list scrolls. In the list view the cards fill the width, so there is little empty space: hold Alt and the rectangle may start on a card too. That card is then not dragged onto the timeline, and a click with Alt that does not move is still a click. A group's row is not selected by a rectangle, nor are the formulas of a closed group, which are not drawn: open the group first."],["by-kind","The pointer on the ∑ key drops its menu. The rows add up: the formulas, then the LaTeX texts, then the font text; the first row again takes the formulas out. Off the key and the menu, it closes. (Without the companion a font text is drawn as boxes; it keeps its kind.) One key of the toolbar, left of + New, wears ∑ and a small arrow. Point at it and a menu drops under it with a row per kind: ∑ Formulas, T LaTeX texts and P Font texts. A row adds every object of its kind in the project to the selection, those in a closed group too, and whatever the search shows. The menu stays open after a choice: two rows chosen one after the other select both kinds. A row is ticked while all of its kind is selected, and the ∑ key is lit; chosen then, the row takes that kind out and leaves the rest. A row is off while the project has none of its kind, and says so in its tooltip. The menu closes once the pointer has left it and the key, on Esc, and on a click elsewhere. Without hovering: click the key, tap it, or reach it with Tab and press Enter; ↑ and ↓ walk the rows, Enter or Space chooses. Braces, shapes and lines are of none of the three; they live with their clips. With a kind selected, the right-click menu of the Creator acts on all of it: Group, Edit together…, Add to the timeline or Delete N formulas…."],["groups","A group is one row of the Creator that holds several formulas. It keeps the list in order and changes nothing else: the scene, the timeline and the exported Python are the same with or without groups. Groups are saved with the project. A closed group: its name, how many formulas it holds, and the drawings of its first three. Group, in the menu of two or more selected formulas, makes one row of them where the first was. It is called grp1 until you rename it, with the drawings of its first three formulas and how many it has. Its arrow opens it onto its formulas and closes it again. So do a double-click on the row, Enter and, in the list view, the arrow keys → and ←. Rename it with F2, a double-click on its name, or Rename group in its menu. A group is selected as its formulas: click it, and the menu acts on all of them (Add to the timeline puts a clip of each at the playhead, Edit together…, Apply match colors, Duplicate, Delete N formulas…). The menu of the row of a group: its formulas' menu, with Rename group and Ungroup. To change what is in a group, without undoing it: Add to group, a submenu in the menu of selected formulas, lists the groups there are and puts them in the one you choose, while one of them is in none. Dropping a formula from the list on the row of a group does the same. Remove from group sets them free. Ungroup, on the row of a group, leaves all its formulas on their own again. None is deleted. A formula is in one group at most. The copy of a formula in a group stays in it, deleting a formula takes it out of its group, and a group left with no formulas goes. A group is not dragged onto the timeline: use Add to the timeline. In the block view a group is a block, and its formulas' blocks come after it while it is open."],["edit-together","Select several formulas or texts, right-click one and choose Edit together…. One dialog shows the glyphs of all of them, to colour or hide glyphs of every formula at once. You can also open it from the timeline, on the clips of several formulas. The dialog: the glyph tools, then Scale and Match colors for the whole formulas, then a card for each. Here the exponents of two formulas were picked with Ctrl+click and given one colour. Select glyphs as in the formula editor: click, Shift+click for a range, Ctrl+click to toggle, or drag a box over the glyphs of every formula it touches (Ctrl adds). The bar says how many are selected, in how many formulas. Choose a colour for all of them, or Clear colour, or Hide at the start and Show at the start. Each action is one undo step, and Ctrl+Z works while the dialog is open. Grid and Cards change the layout: all the formulas at once, or one big card each that the wheel moves through. Two tools act on the whole formulas, whatever glyphs are selected, each as one undo step: Scale gives all of them one scale, as the formula editor's Size does. It is empty, saying mixed, while theirs differ. Opened from the timeline on braces, shapes or lines too, the dialog leaves those out and says how many. Match colors lists the project's match lists, each with its colours, and applies the one you choose to every formula. It is off when one of them takes no match colours (a font text, a brace); with no list yet, Create match colors… closes the dialog and opens the lists. See Match colors."],["standard-scale","New formulas and new LaTeX texts are born with a size: 1 is Manim's font size 48. The standard scale is the size they start with, one for formulas and one for LaTeX texts. Open Preferences ▸ Standard scale…. The standard scale: a field for the formulas and one for the texts, each with its own Apply to all. Type a number or a fraction: 1.5 or 3/2, from 0.1 to 10. The dialog shows the value and the font size it means. Save keeps it for the objects you create from now on. The ones that exist stay as they are. Apply to all formulas (or Apply to all texts) gives that scale to every formula, or every LaTeX text, the project has now, whatever its own. First it asks, and the question says how many change, how many have that scale already, and lists each with the scale it has now. It is one undo step, whether you then save the dialog or cancel it. The clips keep their own scale and their animations. A font text has no standard scale and is left out. To change one formula's size, use its Size in the formula editor."],["duplicate-unlink","Duplicate, in the menu of selected formulas and on the button of a card, makes a copy of each formula right after it in the Creator, and in its group, under the next free name. It copies the formula and not its clips. A clip is another matter. A formula on the timeline more than once is one object that all its clips show: edit it, and every clip changes. That is usually what you want. When it is not, Unlink formula (make a copy), in the menu of a clip, gives that clip a copy of the formula of its own. The entry is in the menu of a clip on the timeline and in the Preview, and in the menu of selected clips. It reads Unlink formula (make a copy), or Unlink formulas (make N copies) when the clips belong to several formulas. Several selected clips of one formula share one copy, and its clips outside the selection keep the original. The copy is named as Duplicate names it, comes after the original in the Creator, and has its glyphs: the clip keeps its relations, its animation blocks, its glyph colours and the objects attached to it, exactly as they were. The entry is off, with a tooltip that says why, when the clip is the formula's only one, or all of its clips are selected, or its track is locked. Braces, shapes and lines have no such entry. One undo step, Ctrl+Z, takes it back: the clip returns to the original and the copy goes. The same scene after unlinking: the first clip of the right triangle holds its copy, pythagoras2, edited to x² + y² = r²; the last clip still holds pythagoras. The project that the examples of this page use, with nine formulas and two LaTeX texts, is here to download: Download Circles and triangles from the card above, then choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… and pick the file. Press Ctrl+F, type pi, and look at the three counts. Press Esc. Open the search again, clear the text, and drag a rectangle over four cards from the space between them. Hold Shift and drag over one more. Right-click a selected card and choose Group. Rename the group. Press the ∑ key, then T: both kinds are selected. Choose Edit together… and colour a glyph in two formulas. On the timeline, right-click the first clip of pythagoras and choose Unlink formula (make a copy). Open the copy and change its LaTeX: the last clip stays as it was."],["next","Writing formulas, for the editor of one formula. The template library, for formulas that start from a template. Match colors, for colouring glyphs by pattern across formulas. Timing tools, for the other menus of a clip."]],"short":"Organizing"},{"id":"objects/braces","url":"objects/braces.html","title":"Braces","section":"Texts and objects","level":"intermediate","description":"Add braces over a formula, over some of its glyphs or over several clips, label them, change their side and animate them.","headings":[["adding","Adding a brace"],["sides","Choosing a side"],["glyphs","Over some glyphs"],["several","Over several clips"],["labels","Labels"],["panel","The Brace section"],["menu","The brace's own menu"],["animating","Animating a brace"],["follow","A brace does not follow its formula"],["python","In the Python"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A brace marks a part of a formula and names it. You add one to a clip, over the whole formula, over some of its glyphs or over several clips, hang a label at its tip, and animate it like any other object of the timeline. A brace is Manim's Brace. The editor fits it to the glyphs it hangs on, and the exported scene builds it the same way (In the Python). This page shows every way to add one, what its panel and its menu offer, and how to make it come and go."],["adding","Right-click a formula or a text, in the Preview or on its clip in the timeline. Choose Add brace ▸ Below, Above, Left or Right. Right-click the sum at 2 s: Add brace ▸ lists the four sides and the dialog for some glyphs. Below puts the brace under the whole formula, at once. A brace is an object of its own, named b1, b2… with a clip of its own. The Creator does not list it: it lives with its clip, and goes with it or with the clip it hangs on. The new clip runs from the playhead to the end of the clip it hangs on. When the playhead is inside that clip's intro, or outside the clip, it starts where the intro ends instead. It goes on the nearest free track in front of the formula's, and a new track opens right above it when none has room. The clip b1 (1) on the track above the sum's. It has no intro and no outro: a brace is there when its clip starts. The brace is fitted to the glyphs as they are when its clip starts. Move, scale or turn the formula before then and the brace follows; after that it stays where it was fitted, as it does in the Python. A brace does not follow its formula shows how to move both."],["sides","Below, Above, Left and Right are Manim's DOWN, UP, LEFT and RIGHT. Behind them a brace points in a direction in degrees, counter-clockwise from below: 0 is below the glyphs, 180 above, −90 on their left and 90 on their right. Any other number tilts the brace to that angle. The Direction field of the Brace section takes any number, not only those four. Here the same letter has braces at 45, 135, −135 and −45 degrees. Change the side later in the Brace section (The Brace section) or in the brace's menu (The brace's own menu)."],["glyphs","A brace does not have to cover the whole formula. Pick the glyphs it hangs on: Hold Alt and click a glyph in the Preview to pick it. Click it again to take it out. Alt+Shift+click picks a range, and Alt+drag draws a rectangle that picks every glyph it touches. Esc clears the pick. A bar appears in the corner of the Preview and says what is picked. Open Brace ▾ and choose a side. An Alt drag over 1+2+3 picks five glyphs; Brace ▾ ▸ Below puts the brace under them. The picked glyphs are drawn in the colour of the picks, yellow to begin with (Preferences ▸ Glyph selection colour changes it). Picking glyphs has the rest of the gestures. Two other ways lead to the same brace: Right-click the formula in the Preview while some glyphs are picked: the entry now reads Add brace · on the selected glyphs. A right-click on a picked glyph has Add brace › as well. On the timeline, the menu of the formula's clip braces the whole formula, and its On some glyphs… opens the dialog with the picked glyphs chosen. Add brace ▸ On some glyphs… opens a dialog with the glyphs of the clip. Click a glyph, Shift+click for a range, Ctrl+click to toggle one or drag a box; choose the side, then press Add brace. The dialog with the first five glyphs of the sum chosen. Its hint says where the brace will run."],["several","A brace can also span clips: the two lines of a system of equations, a formula and a text, a shape and a formula. Select the clips, then right-click one of them, in the Preview or on the timeline. Add brace now puts one brace over all of them, as Manim's Brace(VGroup(…)) does. In the Preview's menu the entry reads Add brace · on the 2 selected clips. Click one equation, Shift+click the other, right-click: Add brace puts one brace over both. The entry is greyed out, with the reason in its tooltip, when the clips are never on screen together. A brace is never braced: with a brace among the selected clips there is no Add brace. A shape takes a brace, on the whole of it. Glyphs picked in several formulas with Alt work the same way: Brace ▾ puts one brace over all of them. Delete one of the clips and the brace stays where it was, detached from all of them. A brace that hangs on one clip only goes with it. Open the starting project: choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… in the Projects dialog and pick the downloaded file (Opening a project). It holds two equations, one above the other. Click one, Shift+click the other and right-click one of them: Add brace ▸ Right puts one brace to the right of both."],["labels","A brace can carry a label: a formula or a LaTeX text at its tip. Right-click the brace in the Preview, or its clip on the timeline, and choose Add label ▸ Formula or Text (LaTeX). The Add label button of the Brace section does the same. The editor of a new label. Type 21, press Apply and close the editor: the label is in the scene. The label comes in with the brace, fading in, and sits where Manim's brace.put_at_tip(label) puts it. It follows the brace when you point it or space it. Its own section, Label, sets the gap to the tip, or Manim's gap, the one Brace.get_tex leaves: 0.25 in ManimCE, 0.1 in ManimGL. Apart from that, a label is a formula like any other. It has its intros, its colours, its relations and its Morph blocks, and it goes with its brace. A new label takes the size that Preferences ▸ Standard scale… gives to its kind. An existing label keeps its size until Apply to all formulas or Apply to all texts in that dialog gives every object its scale (Finding and organizing). A new label starts with a fade-in. With the playhead on its first instant you see nothing yet: play a little, or move the playhead a second later."],["panel","Select the brace's clip and open the Timing tab of the Animations panel: its Brace section has what the brace is made of. The Brace section of a brace under the whole sum. Direction in degrees and Gap, with the four buttons for the sides. The gap is Manim's buff, 0.2 by default. Sharpness, Colour and Width. Sharpness is ManimCE's: how long the straight runs of the brace are for its width (2 is Manim's). ManimGL draws its brace from a font and ignores it. The width is read from the glyphs: you do not set it. Labels lists the labels of the brace (none yet); Add label adds one. Detach frees the brace where it is: it keeps its shape and moves like any other clip from then on. Until then it does not drag in the Preview, because its place follows its glyphs. A brace has no size to set: its width comes from what it hangs on."],["menu","Right-click the brace in the Preview, or its clip on the timeline, for the quick changes. The menu of a brace, with Direction ▸ open. Add label ▸ a formula or a LaTeX text at the tip. Colour ▸ the Manim colours as swatches, and a field for any other colour in hexadecimal. Direction ▸ Below, Above, Left or Right; the current side is ticked. Intro ▸ and Outro ▸ the animation its clip starts and ends with. The current one is ticked; None (appear) and None (disappear) take it away. Remove animations takes off the intro, the outro and every block at once, as one undo step. Detach as in the panel."],["animating","A new brace has no animation. Give it one in Intro ▸ and Outro ▸. GrowFromCenter is Manim's for a brace, and FadeIn, Write, Create and DrawBorderThenFill work too; the outros are ShrinkToCenter, FadeOut, Unwrite and Uncreate. Drag the inner edge of the intro or the outro on the timeline to make it longer or shorter (Appearing and disappearing). With an intro, a brace is not drawn yet at the first instant of its clip. Move the playhead a little later to see it, and right-click its clip on the timeline when you need its menu there. A brace is one piece, so its Transform block moves the whole object: its position, scale, rotation, flips, opacity and colour. There is no part to transform, no Morph and no relation. The Emphasis blocks (Indicate, Wiggle, Circumscribe, Flash, FocusOn) work on it as on any object (Animation blocks)."],["follow","The formula below moves at 3.6 s with a Transform of the whole formula. The brace was fitted at 2 s, so it stays where it was. At 6 s a Transform of its own, with the same move, brings it along. To move a formula and its brace together, give both the same Transform. To make a brace that never follows the formula at all, Detach it and move it by hand. Open the starting project as above (File ▸ Open…, then Other file…): the sum 1+2+3+4+5+6 on its own. Move the playhead to 2 s. Alt+drag a rectangle over 1+2+3, open Brace ▾ in the bar and choose Below. Right-click the brace and choose Add label ▸ Formula. Type 6, press Apply and close the editor. Do the same under 4+5+6 with the label 15, then give both braces Intro ▸ GrowFromCenter and press play."],["python","The exported scene builds a brace on the glyphs it hangs on, when its clip starts, and puts its label at its tip. This is the first brace of the example above in ManimCE: b1 = Brace(VGroup(*series_g[:5]), DOWN, buff=0.2, color=\"#58C4DD\") b1.set_z_index(5) b1.put_at_tip(f2, buff=0.25) # 2 s – 3 s: b1 grow_from_center, f2 fade_in self.play(GrowFromCenter(b1), FadeIn(f2), run_time=1) b1 is the brace and f2 its label, the names the editor gives them. series_g holds the glyphs of the formula in the editor's order, which is what makes [:5] the first five. A brace over several clips is built on a VGroup of them, and a brace on a shape on the shape itself. In ManimGL the same line is Brace(VGroup(*series_g[:5]), DOWN, buff=0.2) followed by set_color, and the label's gap is its own 0.1."],["next","Shapes frame glyphs with a box, a circle, an underline, a cross or a highlight, the way braces do. Lines and arrows point at a formula or run between two of them. Picking glyphs explains the Alt pick that chose the glyphs here. Appearing and disappearing and Animation blocks cover what a brace's clip can be given."]],"short":"Braces"},{"id":"objects/shapes","url":"objects/shapes.html","title":"Shapes","section":"Texts and objects","level":"intermediate","description":"Frame, underline or cross out glyphs with a box, a circle, an underline, a cross, a cross line or a highlight; repaint a shape and carry it to other glyphs.","headings":[["adding","Adding a shape"],["kinds","The six shapes"],["glyphs","Over some glyphs, or several clips"],["panel","The Shape section"],["animation","Animating a shape"],["paint","Fill and animated paint"],["retarget","Carrying a shape to other glyphs"],["cross-line","Crossing out a term"],["python","In the Python"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A shape draws attention to glyphs: a box round a term, a circle, an underline, a cross over it or a single line through it, a highlight behind it. A shape is fitted to the glyphs it hangs on, comes with no animation until you give it one, and a Transform can repaint it or carry it to other glyphs. Shapes work like braces: you add them from the menu of a clip, they have a clip of their own, and the exported scene fits them to the glyphs when the clip starts."],["adding","Right-click a formula or a text, in the Preview or on its clip in the timeline, and choose Add shape ▸ one of Box, Circle, Underline, Cross, Cross line and Highlight. The menu of a formula: Add shape ▸ lists the six shapes, and the dialog for some glyphs. The shape is an object of its own, named s1, s2…, with a clip of its own. The Creator does not list it: it lives with its clip. The clip runs from the playhead to the end of the clip the shape hangs on (from the end of that clip's intro when the playhead is inside the intro or outside the clip). It goes on the nearest free track in front of the glyphs' track, or behind it for a highlight, and a new track opens right beside the target's when that side has no room. A new shape has no animation: it is there when its clip starts. Like a brace, it is fitted to the glyphs as they are when it appears and stays put afterwards."],["kinds","Shape What it draws In Manim Gap Starts as Box a rectangle round the glyphs SurroundingRectangle 0.1 yellow, stroke 4 Circle a circle through the corners of the glyphs' box a circle, built by a helper of the file 0.1 red, stroke 4 Underline a line under the glyphs, as wide as they are Underline 0.1 white, stroke 4 Cross two lines over the box of the glyphs Cross none red, stroke 6 Cross line one line of that cross, over a diagonal one line of a Cross, built by a helper none red, stroke 6 Highlight a box filled and with no stroke, behind the glyphs SurroundingRectangle, filled none yellow, fill 0.3 The gap is Manim's buff, in scene units. The colours and strokes are Manim's own when no palette is active; Palettes and Chroma Lab says how a palette changes what new objects get. A highlight is translucent so that the glyphs read through it."],["glyphs","A shape does not have to cover the whole formula. The ways to choose the glyphs are the ones of a brace: pick them with Alt in the Preview and open Shape ▾ in the bar, or choose Add shape ▸ On some glyphs… to pick them in a dialog. With 1+2+3 picked, Shape ▾ lists the six shapes and the dialog. Select several clips — formulas and texts — and right-click one of them: Add shape now draws one shape round all of them, as Manim fits one to a VGroup. In the Preview's menu the entry reads Add shape · on the 2 selected clips. It is greyed out, with the reason in its tooltip, when the clips are never on screen together, and it is not offered when a brace or a shape is among them. Click one equation, Shift+click the other, right-click: Add shape draws one shape round both."],["panel","Select the shape's clip and open the Timing tab of the Animations panel. Its Shape section holds everything about how the shape looks. The Shape section of a box. Shape changes which shape it is, Colour the colour of its stroke. Size is the width and the height the shape was fitted to: it is read from the glyphs while the shape is attached, and you do not set it. Gap is the space between the glyphs and the shape (a cross and a cross line have none). Corners is a box's corner radius; 0 is square. Stroke is the width of the line in Manim's units, Opacity the stroke's opacity and Fill the opacity of the inside of a box or a circle. A highlight is a box with Fill 0.3 and no stroke. Tick Fill colour to give the fill a colour of its own. Unticked, the fill has the stroke's colour, as in Manim. Detach frees the shape where it is, to move like any clip. Until then it does not drag in the Preview. A negative Gap is valid: the shape is drawn inside the box of the glyphs. When the fit would leave the shape without a size, it keeps its last valid size and place and the panel lists a problem, until you repair the gap or the target. A cross line also has a Diagonal: DL-UR, from the lower left corner to the upper right one, which is the default, or UL-DR. It is drawn from its first corner to its second."],["animation","Because a shape comes with no animation, give it one from its own menu: right-click the shape in the Preview, or its clip on the timeline. The menu of a box, with Intro ▸ open: Create is Manim's for a box. Add brace ▸ a brace under (or over, or beside) the shape. A shape takes a brace on the whole of it. Transform to another formula or glyphs… carries the shape elsewhere: the section below. Colour ▸, Intro ▸ and Outro ▸, Remove animations and Detach are the same as for a brace (The brace's own menu); a shape has no direction. Like a brace, a shape is one piece. Its Transform block moves and repaints the whole object, and there is no Morph, no relation and no part to transform."],["paint","A Transform can repaint a shape as well as move it. Four fields, each behind its own tick, go to what the block says: Stroke width, Stroke opacity, Fill opacity and Fill colour (the last two for a box or a circle). They change little by little during the block, as Manim interpolates a style. A field you leave unticked stays as it was, and the next Transform starts from what the one before left. The paint fields of a Transform block of a box."],["retarget","The Transform of a shape can also move it to surround something else, as Manim's Transform(shape, SurroundingRectangle(other)) does. The shape goes, point by point, from where it is to the new fit. The playhead at 4 s. Transform to another formula or glyphs…, then Alt+drag over 4+5+6 and Enter: the box goes there. Move the playhead to where the shape should start moving. Right-click the shape in the Preview and choose Transform to another formula or glyphs…. On the clip, in the Add animation menu or the panel's button, the entry is Transform ▸ To another formula or glyphs…. Choose the target in the Preview as you select anything there: a click takes a formula, Ctrl+click adds more, and Alt picks symbols of one formula or several. Press Enter, or Transform here, to make the block at the playhead. Esc leaves the mode without making anything. The block reads Transform → series on the timeline. Its panel says what the shape goes to and has Pick again… for another target, and the Preview outlines the target. The target is measured when the block starts and the shape is fitted to it at its own gap. From then on the shape is that fit: a later Transform can move it on, so one after another they chain, and nothing follows the shape there. A brace or a label that hangs on the shape stays where it was fitted. A block that carries a shape: where it goes (1) and Pick again… (2). After such a block the shape is placed by it, so dragging the shape in the Preview does nothing and a notice says why. Delete the block, or the clip of its target, to take it away; the shape and its other blocks stay. Open the starting project: choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… in the Projects dialog and pick the downloaded file (Opening a project). It holds the sum 1+2+3+4+5+6 with a box round 1+2+3. Move the playhead to 3.6 s, right-click the box and choose Transform to another formula or glyphs…. Alt+drag a rectangle over 4+5+6, press Enter and play: the box goes there."],["cross-line","A Cross line is one line of a cross, which makes it a pen stroke through a term. Over the same glyph, Cross draws both lines. A cross line with the intro Create is drawn from its first corner to its second."],["python","A shape is built on the glyphs it hangs on when its clip starts. A box is a SurroundingRectangle, and the Transform to other glyphs builds the new one and plays Transform: s1 = SurroundingRectangle(VGroup(*series_g[:5]), buff=0.15, color=\"#FFFF00\", corner_radius=0.12) # ... self.play(Create(s1), run_time=0.8) # ... s1_to = SurroundingRectangle(VGroup(*series_g[6:]), buff=0.15, color=\"#FFFF00\", corner_radius=0.12) # 3.6 s – 4.6 s: s1 tween self.play(Transform(s1, s1_to), run_time=1) A circle and a cross line are made by two small helpers at the top of the file, _circle_around and _cross_line, because Manim has no class for either. A repainted shape uses set_stroke and set_fill in box.animate."],["next","Braces are the other object that is fitted to glyphs, and a shape can take one. Lines and arrows run between formulas, or point at one. Transformation recipes use a cross line to cancel a factor. Appearing and disappearing covers the intros a shape can have."]],"short":"Shapes"},{"id":"objects/lines-arrows","url":"objects/lines-arrows.html","title":"Lines and arrows","section":"Texts and objects","level":"intermediate","description":"Draw lines, dashed lines, arrows and double arrows between formulas or points, choose where each end is, bend them, and move their ends over time.","headings":[["kinds","Four kinds of line"],["drawing","Drawing a line"],["anchors","Where on a formula: the compass"],["free-ends","Free ends"],["handles","Moving the ends"],["detach","Detach"],["panel","The Line section"],["curves","Bending a line"],["arc","Arcs"],["spline","Splines"],["tips","Tips"],["appearing","Drawing a line in: GrowArrow"],["ends-transform","Moving the ends with a Transform"],["reverse-become","Reverse points and Become"],["path-shape","Copying a path shape"],["menus","The menu of a line"],["python","In the Python"],["together","Everything together"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A line runs between two ends. Each end is on a formula, or on some of its glyphs, at the point of its box you choose, or free at a point of the scene. You draw one from a formula's menu, bend it into an arc or a spline, choose its tips, and move its ends over time. Lines are Manim's Line, DashedLine, Arrow and DoubleArrow. Everything on this page is the same in ManimCE and ManimGL unless a paragraph says otherwise."],["kinds","Kind In Manim Tips Stroke Gap at an end Line Line none 4 0 Dashed line DashedLine none 4 0 Arrow Arrow at the end 6 0.25 Double arrow DoubleArrow at both ends 6 0.25 A new line is white and has Manim's stroke for its kind: 4, and 6 for an arrow, with a tip 0.35 long. It has no animation: it is there when its clip starts."],["drawing","Right-click a formula or a text, in the Preview or on its clip, and choose Add line ▸ Line, Dashed line, Arrow or Double arrow. The line starts on that clip, or on its picked symbols when the menu opened on them. A small compass asks where on it. Then click where the line ends: on a formula or a text, for the whole of it; the compass asks again; with Alt held, on the symbol the Preview picks; on empty space, for a free end at that point. The formula the end would go on is marked in the colour of the picks while you choose. Esc closes an open compass, giving up that end; with no compass open, it leaves the mode. A start put on symbols with Alt follows them: edit the formula before you click the end, and the start is still on those symbols in the new render. If they are no longer there, the drawing ends, and a notice says so. Two ends at one place make no line: two clicks on one spot, or both ends on one formula at Auto, where Manim finds no direction between them. The notice No line made says why, and the mode waits for another end. A project made with an earlier version may hold such a line, or a Transform that carries one to such ends: its panel shows an error, and so does the export. While you draw, Ctrl and the wheel zoom the Preview and the middle button pans it, as always. A right-click opens the Preview's menu: an entry you choose there ends the mode first, and Esc closes the menu and leaves the mode on. To start a line at a free point, right-click the empty space of the Preview. Add line there takes two clicks: the start, then the end. The menu of the empty space lists the four kinds. Choose one, click for the start, then click on B. Open the starting project: choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… in the Projects dialog and pick the downloaded file (Opening a project). It holds the letters A and B. Right-click A and choose Add line ▸ Arrow; in the compass choose the middle of its right edge. Click B and choose the middle of its left edge in the second compass: the arrow is made. The new line is the object l1, l2…, with its own clip on a track in front of what it runs between. A line between formulas lasts while both are on screen, from the playhead on. A line with two free ends is a free object from the playhead to the end of the scene. Like a brace, it is not listed in the Creator."],["anchors","The compass has the nine points of the box of the glyphs, and Auto. A corner (↖ ↗ ↙ ↘), the middle of an edge (↑ ← → ↓) or the centre (•): these are Manim's get_corner(UL), get_top(), get_center() and the rest. Auto is the edge Manim finds towards the other end, as Arrow(a, b) does. The arrow keys move between the points, Enter or a click picks one, and Esc gives up. The compass for the start of a line on A. The mint dot says it is the start; the end's is coral. Compass Point of the glyphs' box In the Python ↖ ↗ ↙ ↘ the four corners get_corner(UL), get_corner(UR), get_corner(DL), get_corner(DR) ↑ ↓ ← → the middle of an edge get_top(), get_bottom(), get_left(), get_right() • the centre get_center() Auto where the line meets the glyphs on its way to the other end the formula itself: Arrow(a, b), by the names of the two formulas A line stops a gap short of the glyphs it is on: an arrow's 0.25, a line's 0. With Auto the two libraries find the edge differently. ManimCE takes the farthest point of the glyphs that way and ManimGL the place where the direction leaves their box. The Preview follows the library the project exports to."],["free-ends","A free end is exactly where you put it. The gap applies only to ends on glyphs, so an arrow from a free point to a formula leaves the point at the click and stops 0.25 short of the formula. An end on glyphs follows them: it is measured when its clip starts, so move the formula before then and the line follows, and it stays put afterwards. Because an end on glyphs is measured once, an arrow does not follow its formula when the formula moves later. Below, B moves down at 3.8 s and the arrow stays where it was fitted; at 6.2 s a Transform of the arrow's ends brings its end to B (Moving the ends with a Transform)."],["handles","A selected line shows a handle at each end: the start's is mint and the end's coral. What a drag does depends on the key you hold: No key: the end is left free where you drop it. It goes on nothing by itself, even when you drop it over a formula. Alt at the drop: the end goes on the symbol under the pointer, and the compass asks where on it. Alt+Shift at the drop: the end goes on the whole formula there. Ctrl held: the end stays on the line's own direction, so the line only gets longer or shorter. A drop that would put the two ends at one place, as above, leaves the end where it was, and the notice The end stays says so. Dragged with no key, the end is free where it is dropped. With Alt held at the drop, the end leaves B for C and the compass asks where on it; the lower left corner is chosen. With Ctrl held the pointer goes down and to the left, and the arrow only gets shorter. Open the starting project as above (File ▸ Open…, then Other file…): an arrow from A to B. Click the arrow to select it. Drag its end handle to an empty place: the end is free there. Undo with Ctrl+Z and drag it again with Ctrl held: the arrow only gets shorter. A line with both ends free is dragged by its body. One with an end on glyphs is moved by its handles."],["detach","Right-click a handle to Detach that end, which leaves it free where it is, or to change its point with Change its point…, which opens the compass with the current point marked. The menu of a handle says where the end is. The menu of the line's clip has Detach ▸ Detach start, Detach end (each marked with its colour) and Detach all, which frees both ends in one step. A line whose ends are both free is greyed out there. The Line section has a Detach for each end too. The menu of a line with Detach ▸ open. Deleting the clip an end is on deletes the line with it, unless the line's other end is on another clip. Then the line stays, free, where it was."],["panel","Select the line's clip and open the Timing tab of the Animations panel: its Line section. The Line section of an arrow between two formulas. Kind turns it into another of the four (Become), Colour and Reverse points. Stroke in Manim's units, Tip (an arrow's tip length), Dash (the length of a dashed line's dashes, 0.01 at least) and Gap at an end on glyphs. Tip shape, and Start tip shape for a double arrow (Tips). Curve: straight, an arc or a spline, and its Arc field (Bending a line). For each end, Start and End with their colours, where each is (on a formula, or free), its Point with the arrow the compass shows, and Detach. A line has no scale, turn or flip of its own: its two ends say where it is."],["curves","A line can be bent into an arc or a spline, and stays bent; the way back is Ctrl+Z, or a pasted straight path (below). The clip's menu has Curve ▸ Arc and Curve ▸ Spline, and the Line section has the same two buttons."],["arc","An arc is Manim's path_arc, 30 degrees to begin with. The Arc field sets the degrees: positive ones bulge to the right of the line's way, and it goes up to 300 either way. In the Preview the selected line shows a stem along the arc where it leaves its start, with a handle at its tip. Drag it to bend the line more, less or the other way; Shift goes every 15 degrees. The degrees are read beside it. Curve ▸ Arc, then the stem's handle dragged with Shift held: the degrees go in steps of 15."],["spline","A spline is a curve through points, shaped in the mode Edit curve as a custom path is. Curve ▸ Spline opens the mode on the line as it is bent, the arc of 30 degrees to begin with, with its two ends and a handle each. In the mode: a click on the curve adds a point there, smooth and with two handles of its own; drag a point to move it and a handle to bend the curve: the other handle of a smooth point mirrors it (Shift: it turns but keeps its own length; Ctrl: one handle alone, and the point becomes a corner); double-click a point to make it a corner or smooth again, and press Delete to remove the point you selected (with none selected, the key does nothing: it never deletes the line); Enter or Done keeps the curve, and Esc puts back the one the mode started with. The mode pauses the playback as it opens, and brings the playhead to the line's clip when it is elsewhere. Move the playhead off the clip and the points are not shown, but the mode stays on, and its hint says so: Edit curve: the line is not on screen at the playhead · Enter: done · Esc: as it was. Enter, Esc and Done work there too. Ctrl and the wheel, the middle button and a right-click do what they do while a line is drawn (above), and an entry of the Preview's menu ends the mode keeping the curve. So do Reverse points and Paste path shape, before they change the line. A line on a locked track, or locked in the Preview, keeps its curve: the mode does not open on it, and a notice says why. If its line is locked while the mode is on, the mode ends, keeping the curve, and the notice Edit curve ended says so. Curve ▸ Spline and a click on the curve add a point; a drag moves it, and a drag of one of its handles bends the curve. From the menus and the Line section an arc can be made a spline (the same curve, as points), not the other way round; Paste path shape (below) gives any line an arc again, or a straight path. The curve stretches and turns with the two ends of the line, which you move as always. The gap an arrow leaves at a formula is left along the arc, as Manim leaves it."],["tips","An arrow's Tip shape — and a double arrow's Start tip shape — is one of three, the same in both libraries: Manim's Triangle, a Stealth tip (ManimCE's notched arrowhead) or a Dot. Tip sets the length and width of the tips in scene units, 0.35 by default and never more than a quarter of the arrow. The tip fields of a double arrow: the length (1) and the shape of each end (2)."],["appearing","A line has no scale, so nothing grows it about its centre. It comes in with Create, Write or FadeIn, and goes with Uncreate, Unwrite or FadeOut. An arrow or a double arrow can also come in with GrowArrow, among its intros and in Appear of its menu; a line without a tip has none. A straight arrow takes Manim's own GrowArrow: it grows from its start, tips and all. In ManimCE the stroke of its shaft grows with it; in ManimGL it does not, and the Preview does as the project's library. A bent arrow — an arc or a spline — is drawn along its own way instead, as Create draws a line, with its tip on the end of what is drawn, turning with the curve. The tip grows out of the start over the first quarter of the way, and the tip at the start of a double arrow grows where it is. Manim has no such animation: the Python carries it as a small _TraceArrow helper, the same in both libraries. A Lag ratio of its own staggers the parts of a growing arrow: the shaft, then the tip at its end, then the one at its start. The Python passes it on to Manim, as lag_ratio. Unwrite takes an arrow away in Manim's order, the shaft first and then its tips, the last one first, in the Preview and in both libraries."],["ends-transform","A Transform block of a line carries its two ends. Add one from the Add animation menu of the line's clip (its entry says ends · stroke · colour). It starts with the ends the line has. With the playhead on the Transform, or after it, the handles at the ends move its ends: free where dropped, Alt onto a symbol, Alt+Shift onto a formula, Ctrl along the line. A drop with Alt asks where on the symbol with the compass, as for the line itself (Moving the ends). Its panel says where each end goes, the gap, an arc's degrees, and the stroke width, colour and opacity the line arrives with. The ends of a Transform block (1): where each goes, at which point, and the gap. An arrow is drawn anew between its new ends, with its tip and its stroke as an arrow of that length has them, and Manim plays Transform(arrow, new_arrow). A line and a dashed line keep their shape and their dashes, stretched and turned, as line.animate.put_start_and_end_on(a, b) does. An end on glyphs is measured when the Transform starts. Open the starting project as above (File ▸ Open…, then Other file…): the letters A, B and C, and an arrow from A to B. Select the arrow and move the playhead to 4 s. Choose Add animation ▸ Transform in the Animations panel, and put the playhead on the new block. Drag the end handle with Alt+Shift held onto C and choose its lower left corner in the compass. Play: the end travels there."],["reverse-become","Reverse points, in the line's menu and in the Line section, swaps the start and the end of a line where they are: an arrow points the other way, and what draws the line starts from its other end. Become ▸ makes the line another of the four kinds, keeping its ends, its curve and its tips; the Kind field does the same. An arrow that grew with GrowArrow and is left without a tip is drawn in with Create."],["path-shape","Curve ▸ Copy path shape takes the shape of a line's path: straight, an arc's degrees or a spline's points. Curve ▸ Paste path shape gives it to another line of any of the four kinds. From the menu of several selected clips it goes to every line among them. The path stretches and turns with the ends of the line that takes it, which keeps its ends, its kind, its tips and its stroke. Besides Ctrl+Z, pasting a straight path makes a bent line straight again, and pasting an arc makes a spline an arc again. The menu of a line, with Curve ▸ open. Beside each entry it says what it will do."],["menus","Right-click a line in the Preview or its clip on the timeline. Besides the entries every clip has, its menu offers only what a line takes: Entry What it does Intro ▸, Outro ▸ None (appear), Create, Write, FadeIn, and GrowArrow for an arrow; None (disappear), Uncreate, Unwrite, FadeOut Become ▸ another of the four kinds Reverse points swaps its start and its end Curve ▸ Arc, Spline or Edit curve, Copy path shape, Paste path shape Detach ▸ Detach start, Detach end, Detach all On the timeline, Add animation offers Appear (Create, Write, FadeIn and, for an arrow, GrowArrow), Disappear (Uncreate, Unwrite, FadeOut), one Transform (its ends, its stroke, its colour and its opacity) and the Emphasis effects that do not scale the line: Circumscribe, Flash and FocusOn. A block's own menu (Change animation, Change effect) and the Animation and Effect lists of its panel offer the same, so a block of a line never becomes one a line does not take. An arrow is animated whole. A block of an arrow or a double arrow takes all of it, its shaft and its tips together, as Manim does. Its panel and its dialog have no glyph picker, and its menu has no Split into runs, Split… nor Duplicate for the other glyphs. A block that names only a part of an arrow, as a project made with an earlier version may have it, is an error: All glyphs, in its panel, gives it the whole arrow."],["python","A line is built on the mobjects themselves, or on the points read off them: l1 = Line([-2, 1.9, 0], [5, 1.9, 0]) # ... l4 = DoubleArrow([-2, -2, 0], [5, -2, 0], buff=0, color=\"#FFFF00\", tip_length=0.45, tip_shape_end=ArrowCircleFilledTip, tip_shape_start=StealthTip) # ... l5 = Arrow(letter_a.get_corner(UR), letter_b.get_corner(UL), path_arc=-50 * DEGREES, color=\"#83C167\") # ... l6 = Arrow(letter_a.get_right(), letter_b.get_left()) An end with the point Auto is built on the formula itself, Arrow(a, b) in the example of the compass. An arc is the path_arc= of its class, and a spline the line bent by a small _spline helper. ManimGL's own Arrow is another object, so there an arrow is a Line with a tip of ManimCE's size added by add_tip, and a double arrow or another tip shape goes through a small _arrow helper. A bent arrow's GrowArrow plays _TraceArrow(l5), defined at the top of the file. A line is never found by position, as the glyphs of a MathJax formula are (the positional helper): its parts, the line, an arrow's tips, the dashes in their order, are the same in both libraries, so the Python names them through the line itself, even with Always use the positional helper ticked or in the other library than the project's."],["together","One scene with the four kinds, the points of a formula an end can be on, arcs, a spline, the three tip shapes, GrowArrow and a Transform that carries the ends. The same file is the example lines.mfe.json of the repository."],["next","Braces and shapes are the other objects fitted to glyphs. An identity with braces and arrows uses them all in one scene. Picking glyphs explains the Alt pick a line can start from. Custom paths use the same editing mode as a spline."]],"short":"Lines and arrows"},{"id":"layout/align","url":"layout/align.html","title":"Align and distribute","section":"Layout","level":"intermediate","description":"Line up several things in the Preview, and space them evenly.","headings":[["where","Where the commands are"],["align","Align"],["frame","Aligning to the frame"],["distribute","Distribute"],["takes-part","What takes part"],["together","Moving the aligned clips together"],["try-it","Try it"]],"chunks":[[null,"Line up several things in the Preview, and space them evenly. Select them, press a key or a button, and the edges meet or the gaps become equal, in one undo step."],["where","The same eight commands are in four places. Pick the one that is closest to your hands. The bar in the Preview. With two or more clips selected, a bar appears in the top-left corner of the Preview. The Edit menu: Edit ▸ Align and Edit ▸ Distribute. The right-click menu of a formula in the Preview: Align ▸ and Distribute ▸. The keyboard (the table below). The bar of a selection: (1) how many clips; (2) align left, centre, right, top, middle and bottom; (3) distribute horizontally and vertically; (4) the menu Match colors, with Paste properties before it. Edit ▸ Align lists the six edges with their keys, and the two ways in to Align by symbol. The right-click menu of a formula of the selection. Command Keys With Shift: to the frame Align left Alt+A Shift+Alt+A Align centre Alt+H Shift+Alt+H Align right Alt+D Shift+Alt+D Align top Alt+W Shift+Alt+W Align middle Alt+V Shift+Alt+V Align bottom Alt+S Shift+Alt+S Distribute horizontally Ctrl+Alt+H — Distribute vertically Ctrl+Alt+V — The letters are chosen by where the keys are, not by the names of the commands: A and D for the left and right edges and W and S for top and bottom, as in the WASD keys of a game, and H and V for the horizontal and vertical middles. In the Edit menu, hold Shift while you choose an edge to align to the frame."],["align","Aligning moves each selected clip along one axis only, so that one of its edges, or its middle, meets the same edge or middle of the group. Which line of the group it is depends on the command: Command The clips go to They move Left, Right the leftmost left edge, the rightmost right edge sideways Centre the vertical middle line of the group's box sideways Top, Bottom the highest top, the lowest bottom up or down Middle the horizontal middle line of the group's box up or down What is measured is the whole formula: every glyph counts where it is at the playhead, drawn or not, so a formula with a hidden glyph still measures the same. A rectangle round the three lines of a solution; Align left from the bar; Distribute vertically with Ctrl+Alt+V. Each command is one undo step: Ctrl+Z puts back the last, then the one before. If every box is already in place, nothing moves and no undo step is made."],["frame","A lone clip has nothing to line up with, so it aligns to the frame: Align centre puts it on the vertical middle line of the frame, Align left against the left edge of the frame, and so on. With Shift held, a selection of several clips aligns to the frame too: each clip goes to the frame's edge or middle, not to the group's. For example, a lone clip with Alt+H goes to the middle of the frame, and two clips with Shift+Alt+A both go to the left edge of the frame. In a scene with a camera, the frame is the camera’s at the playhead: what is seen at that instant."],["distribute","Distributing needs three or more clips. It leaves the two outermost ones where they are (by their centres, along the axis) and places the others so that the gaps between neighbouring boxes are all equal. Boxes that overlap give negative gaps, which are equalised too. The distribute buttons of the bar stay off until three clips are selected. Distributing horizontally spaces them from left to right; distributing vertically, from top to bottom. For a row of three formulas, Alt+V puts their middles on one line and Ctrl+Alt+H spaces them out evenly."],["takes-part","Align and distribute act on the selected clips that the Preview can edit right now. A clip takes part when it is on screen at the playhead; on a track that is not locked, and not locked in the preview (Locking a formula in the preview); not locked after its transformations (an edit at that time would belong to a transformation), and without the end state of one of its blocks under edit; placed by hand. The last one matters for braces, shapes, labels and lines: an object attached to glyphs of a formula is placed by them, so it is neither measured nor moved. Selected together with the formula it hangs on, it goes where that formula goes. Selected without it, it stays. The clips that stay are not a failure: a notice says how many, and names them. A formula locked in the preview was in the selection when the lines were aligned: it stays where it is, and the notice says so. When only one clip of the selection can move and other selected clips are left out, the clip has nothing to line up with and stays too: a clip aligns to the frame on its own only when nothing else of the selection is left out."],["together","After aligning, the clips are still a selection. Drag any of them and they all move, and the box round the whole group is what snaps to the other formulas and to the frame, with the guides of dragging and snapping. Hold Shift for the handles of the group, which scale or turn all of them about the group's centre. The three lines are dragged as one. Their box snaps: its left edge meets the left edge of the first formula of the row, and the yellow line says so."],["try-it","Open the start file, where seven formulas sit where someone placed them by eye. Open the file: choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… in the dialog and pick it (see Open). Click the ruler at 4 s, so that everything is on screen. Draw a rectangle around the three blue lines. Press Alt+A: their left edges meet. Then press Ctrl+Alt+V to space them out. Clear the selection, draw a rectangle around the three yellow formulas at the bottom, and press Alt+V and then Ctrl+Alt+H. Click the green title and press Alt+H: it goes to the middle of the frame. This is the scene once it has been tidied: the lines left-aligned and evenly spaced, the row on one line with equal gaps, the title in the middle. To line formulas up by one of their symbols — the equals signs of a derivation, say — see Align by symbol."]],"short":"Align and distribute"},{"id":"layout/align-symbol","url":"layout/align-symbol.html","title":"Align by symbol","section":"Layout","level":"advanced","description":"Align formulas by a symbol they share, also across time.","headings":[["idea","What it is for"],["by-symbol","Aligning by a symbol"],["halo","The halo"],["keys","From the keyboard"],["across-time","Across time"],["refusals","When G keeps nothing"],["locked","With a locked formula as the reference"],["good-to-know","Good to know"],["try-it","Try it"],["try-time","Try it across time"],["keys-list","Keys of this page"]],"chunks":[[null,"Align treats a formula as a box. Align by symbol treats it as a handle: pick one glyph of a formula, pick one glyph of another, and the first formula moves so that the two glyphs meet. It works for formulas on screen together, and for formulas that never are."],["idea","A derivation written line by line looks right when the equals signs are in a column. The lines have different lengths, so centring them, or aligning their left edges, does not do it. Aligning by the = does: each line moves until its = is under the one above."],["by-symbol","It is a mode of the Preview with two clicks. Start it with Alt+G, with the first button of the view toolbar (Align by symbol), with Edit ▸ Align ▸ By symbol… or with Align ▸ Align by symbol… in the right-click menu of a formula. A hint at the bottom of the Preview says what to click. Click the symbol to move, a glyph of the formula that will move. Click the reference symbol, a glyph of another formula. That formula stays where it is. A compass opens on the reference. Choose where on the reference symbol the first symbol lands. Alt+G; the = of the second line; the = of the first; Left edge on the compass. The whole second line moves so that its = is under the first's. The alignment is one undo step: Ctrl+Z puts the line back. The compass has nine places, the nine directions of Manim: the four edges, the four corners and the centre of the reference symbol. The compass opens beside the reference symbol. The title says what is being done: “Put “=” on the … of “=””. Compass The first symbol lands… What moves ↖ Upper left corner, ↗ Upper right corner, ↙ Lower left corner, ↘ Lower right corner with that corner of its box on the same corner of the reference's the formula, both ways • Centre with its middle on the middle of the reference both ways ← Left edge, → Right edge with its left (right) edge on the left (right) edge of the reference sideways only ↑ Top edge, ↓ Bottom edge with its top (bottom) edge on the top (bottom) edge of the reference up or down only Esc, or a click outside the compass, cancels the alignment. To put the equals signs of two lines in one column, the edge is the right choice: Left edge moves the second line sideways and leaves its height alone. Centre would move it onto the first line. The whole formula of the first symbol moves, not the symbol alone: the other glyphs keep their places in the formula. What is written is the clip's position, the same keyframe a drag writes, in one undo step."],["halo","While the mode is on, the pointer carries a halo, a circle. The symbol nearest to the pointer within the circle is outlined, and a click takes that one, so thin symbols — a minus, a dot, the bars of an equals sign — can be hit without aiming exactly. Shift and the wheel make the circle larger or smaller (from 6 to 60 pixels; 14 at the start). The reach is kept, and it is the same one the circle selector uses. The pointer is above the =, not on it; the halo reaches it and the outline says it is the one a click would take."],["keys","Every click has a key. While the mode is on, the arrow keys walk the glyphs on screen: the formulas are rows, from the top of the scene down, each with its glyphs in order; ← and → walk a row, ↑ and ↓ change row, keeping the column. The glyph under the cursor is outlined, and Enter takes it, as a click would. The compass takes the arrows and Enter too. Esc leaves the mode. The alignment above, with the keyboard alone: the first arrow puts the cursor on the first glyph; the arrows take it to the = of the second line and Enter takes it; the arrows again to the = of the first line and Enter; in the compass, ← and Enter."],["across-time","The lines of a derivation are often not on screen together: one comes in as the one before it leaves. The two symbols are measured at the playhead, so there is no moment to click both. G solves it. With the playhead where the formula to move is on screen, start the mode and click its symbol. Press G. The symbol is kept as it is at this instant, and the hint says so. Move the playhead to where the reference is on screen. The formula you keep stays in view, faint, where it is at the kept instant, so that you can see what the reference is for, and the ruler of the timeline marks the instant. Click the reference symbol and choose a side of the compass. In the derivation of the example at the end of the page, the second line is on screen alone at 4 s and the first alone at 1.6 s. At 4 s: Alt+G, click the = of the second line, G. Go to 1.6 s: the first line shows, and the second appears faint where it is at 4 s. Click the = of the first line and choose Left edge. Back at 4 s the second line has moved: its = is where the first line's is. Ctrl+Z puts it back. At 1.6 s: the first line, and the second one, faint, as it is at the kept instant. Only the keyframe in effect at the kept instant changes, and nothing else of the timeline: the playhead stays where you left it. A notice says what was done, and, when something moves beyond it, what. The ruler marks the kept instant (4 s). A click on the mark takes the playhead back there. The notice names both instants. G again lets the symbol go. Esc or Alt+G leave the mode and remove the mark and the faint formula. The faint formula does not answer the pointer: it cannot be the reference. Without G, a symbol whose formula is not on screen at the playhead cannot be measured: a notice reminds you to press G after clicking it, before moving the playhead."],["refusals","An alignment has to hold, so G refuses, with a notice that says why, when the formula is not on screen at the playhead; hangs on glyphs of another formula (a brace, a label: use Detach in the Animations panel first); is on a locked track, or locked in the preview; has the end state of one of its parts under edit (finish that edit first with Esc); is locked after its transformations and one has started by that instant: align it at a time before its first transformation, or at the end state of one (select its block and click its end icon), or unlock the clip with the padlock on the timeline; is moving at that instant: a transformation, a transformation of that part or an emphasis is running, so a shift written there would not hold. Pick a time when it is at rest. When the alignment is made but something moves beyond it, the closing notice lists that too: only the end of a transformation moved; a later transformation takes the formula to a position of its own, which did not move; the symbol was moved by a transformation of its part; the reference itself moves between the two instants."],["locked","A formula locked in the preview is out of the pointer's reach. To align to a symbol of one, use Shift+Alt+G (or Edit ▸ Align ▸ By symbol of a locked formula…): the first click is on a formula that is not locked, the one that moves; the second is on a locked formula, whose symbols can now be clicked, however many formulas lie over it. The locked formula stays, and the other one moves to it. The first line is locked in the preview, so it is faint. After Shift+Alt+G and a click on the = of the third line, a click on the = of the first one opens the compass on it: the third line will move there. With no formula locked, the shortcut says so in a notice and does not start the mode."],["good-to-know","The modes of the Preview (align by symbol, Edit path, Edit curve, drawing a line with Add line, colours in order, a shape's target) are on one at a time: starting one ends the one that was on. A formula edited while the mode is on keeps its symbol: the mode follows the glyph into the new render, and ends, with a notice, when the glyph is no longer there. The reference cannot be on the formula that moves. A formula that cannot be edited now (a locked track, locked in the preview, locked after its transformations) stays where it is, and a notice says so."],["try-it","Open the start file: four lines of a solution, each centred. Line them up by their equals signs. Open the file: choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… in the dialog and pick it (see Open). Put the playhead at 6 s, where the four lines are on screen. Press Alt+G. Click the = of the second line, then the = of the first, then the Left edge of the compass. Do the same for the third line, and then for the fourth: each time, click the = of the first line as the reference. Press Ctrl+Z three times and do it again with the arrow keys and Enter instead of the mouse."],["try-time","The second file is a derivation, x + 3 = 5 into x = 5 − 3 into x = 2, each line under the one before and centred on its own, so the = jumps sideways from line to line. Line them up by the = with G. Open the file and put the playhead at 4 s: only the second line is on screen. Press Alt+G, click the = of the line, then press G. Put the playhead at 1.6 s, click the = of the first line and choose Left edge. Do the same for the third line (kept at 6 s, reference on the second line at 4 s), and play the scene."],["keys-list","Keys What they do Alt+G Start Align by symbol, or leave it Shift+Alt+G The same, with the reference symbol on a formula locked in the preview ← → ↑ ↓, Enter Walk the glyphs on screen and take one; in the compass, choose a place G After the first symbol: keep it as it is at the playhead, for a reference at another time; again, let it go Shift + wheel Make the halo larger or smaller Esc Leave the mode, or close the compass Ctrl+Z Undo the alignment, one step See also Align and distribute to line up edges and centres, and Picking glyphs with Alt for choosing glyphs for a colour, a brace or a shape."]],"short":"Align by symbol"},{"id":"layout/glyph-picking","url":"layout/glyph-picking.html","title":"Picking glyphs with Alt","section":"Layout","level":"intermediate","description":"Pick single glyphs in the Preview with Alt, also across formulas.","headings":[["with-alt","Picking with Alt"],["bar","The bar"],["glyph-view-gestures","The same gestures in the glyph view"],["across-formulas","Across formulas"],["what-for","What picked glyphs are for"],["selection-colour","The selection colour"],["thin","Thin glyphs"],["circle","The circle selector in the Preview"],["glyph-view","The glyph view"],["try-it","Try it"]],"chunks":[[null,"A formula is made of glyphs: the letters, the digits, the signs. Hold Alt in the Preview and click a glyph to pick it. Pick a few, in one formula or across several, and colour them, hide them, or put a brace or a shape on them."],["with-alt","In the Preview, hold Alt and: click a glyph to pick it, in yellow. Click a picked glyph again to take it out; Alt+Shift+click a glyph to pick a range: from the last glyph you picked in that formula to this one; drag from a formula to draw a rectangle that picks every glyph it touches, as it grows. With Shift held as you press, the glyphs are added to those already picked; Esc during the drag puts the previous pick back. A click on the 2 of 2ab; Alt+Shift+click on the b picks the range; an Alt+drag from the formula picks what a rectangle touches (here the a²), in place of the glyphs picked before. A click does not have to hit the glyph's ink: a glyph whose box is within about 6 pixels of the pointer is picked, so a thin stroke or the gap of an = is not hard to aim at (the circle selector goes further). A click on a formula but not on a glyph clears the pick. Alt held before you press, on empty space, pans the view instead. The picked glyphs wear a faint yellow fill and a yellow outline, and so does the rectangle; the clip they belong to is selected. Their colour is a preference (below)."],["bar","While glyphs are picked, a bar appears in the top-left corner of the Preview. It says what is picked and offers what can be done with it. (1) What is picked; (2) Colour ▾; (3) Brace ▾; (4) Shape ▾; (5) the button that clears the pick. For glyphs of one formula the bar names the formula and numbers the glyphs, up to twelve (“3 glyphs of sum: 10, 11, 12”); past twelve, or over several formulas, it only counts them. They are the numbers the glyph view shows when you point at a glyph. A right-click on the ink of a picked glyph opens the same actions as a menu. In the hole of a letter, such as the middle of an o, it opens the clip's menu instead. Esc clears the pick, and so does selecting something else. The pick follows its formula when it is edited or rendered again: the picked glyphs keep their places in it, a glyph that is gone leaves the pick, and a pick that cannot be followed is cleared, with a notice."],["glyph-view-gestures","The formula editor's glyph view picks glyphs in the same three ways, without Alt: To pick In the Preview In the glyph view One glyph; again, take it out Alt+click click picks only that one; Ctrl+click toggles it A range Alt+Shift+click Shift+click What a rectangle touches Alt+drag from a formula; Shift adds drag over the drawing; Ctrl or Shift adds"],["across-formulas","Pick a glyph of one formula and then Alt+click a glyph of another: it joins the pick. A rectangle takes the glyphs it touches in every formula it crosses. The bar then says, for instance, “6 glyphs of 2 formulas”, and the formulas that have picked glyphs are the selected clips for as long as the pick lasts. (A range is always within one formula.) Six glyphs of two formulas, picked: the yellow glyphs, the two selected formulas, the bar, and the hint at the bottom. With glyphs of several formulas picked, the bar and the menu offer: a colour, or Clear colour, for all of them, as one undo step. The picked glyphs keep the selection colour until you clear the pick with Esc; then the colour shows, and Ctrl+Z takes it back; one brace or one shape over all of them. It is what Brace(VGroup(…), RIGHT, …) is in Manim: fitted to the group when its clip starts, and it follows each of the formulas. The last glyph of each identity, the exponent of its b², is picked in both formulas, and Brace ▾ ▸ Right puts one brace to their right, spanning both formulas. Deleting the clip of one of the formulas does not delete the brace: it stays where it is, detached. Hiding at the start, the colour sequence and On some glyphs… are for the glyphs of one formula, so they are not there for a pick across several. Because Alt+click adds, to pick glyphs of another formula alone you clear the pick first (Esc) or draw the rectangle there."],["what-for","Menu What it does with the picked glyphs Colour ▾ A colour for them — from the Manim palette, from the roles of the active editor palette if there is one, or any #RRGGBB typed in the Hex field — or, with Clear colour, none of their own. The colour belongs to the formula, so it shows in every clip of it. Copy colour to clipboard (for a single glyph) puts its colour on the system clipboard; Copy colour sequence and Paste colour sequence carry the colours of a pick to another pick of the same symbols. Hide at the start and Show at the start, for the glyphs of one formula: hidden glyphs wait out of the picture when the formula appears, until an Appear block or a relation brings them in. Brace ▾ A brace on them: Below, Above, Left or Right; for one formula, On some glyphs… opens the dialog with them picked. Shape ▾ A shape on them: Box, Circle, Underline, Cross, Cross line or Highlight; again On some glyphs… for one formula. The clip's own right-click menu does the same when the glyphs are picked: its Add brace and Add shape entries say “on the selected glyphs”. The Colour ▾ menu of glyphs of one formula. The Brace ▾ menu for glyphs of several formulas: four sides, and no dialog. Colours, braces and shapes are described in Colouring glyphs and objects, Braces and Shapes. Two neighbouring tools pick glyphs with plain clicks instead of Alt: Align by symbol and the copying of colours glyph by glyph in order (Shift+C, described in Colouring glyphs and objects)."],["selection-colour","The colour of picked glyphs, and of the rectangle that picks them, is a preference: Preferences ▸ Glyph selection colour. It offers the same six colours as the snapping guides — Yellow, Orange, Magenta, Cyan, Green and White — and starts at yellow. It is separate from Guide colour: changing one does not move the other. The choice is kept in the browser. Pick another colour if the glyphs you are working on are yellow themselves."],["thin","A minus, a dot, the bar of a fraction: the thin symbols are the hardest to hit. Two aids make them reachable."],["circle","Press S, or the second button of the view toolbar (Circle selector). The pointer becomes a dashed circle, and a click takes the symbol nearest to the pointer within the circle. A plain click takes its formula; Alt+click takes the symbol itself. Shift and the wheel change the reach, and S again gives the pointer back. The choice is kept. The two pair editors, the relation editor and the pair editor of a Morph block, always take glyphs with the same circle, at the same size. An Alt+click a little above the minus picks nothing. With the circle selector on (S), the same click picks the minus. S splits the selected clips at the playhead instead, and the circle selector does not change, while an audio clip is selected, or while a selected clip has the playhead inside it and the keyboard is not in the Preview (Splitting a clip). After a click on the scene the keyboard is in the Preview, and S is the selector's."],["glyph-view","The formula editor has a Glyphs view, which draws every glyph of the formula and lets you click, range-select (Shift), toggle (Ctrl) or drag a box over glyphs, to colour or hide them at the start. In it, every glyph has a frame that is at least 10 pixels each way, whatever the zoom, drawn under all the glyphs: the bar of a fraction and a minus sign take clicks, show their state — selected, hidden, coloured — and glow like a letter. The bar of the fraction and the minus sign, picked in the glyph view. The Glyph list button beside the Glyphs title lists the glyphs under the drawing, each with its index and label; with the list shown you can pick glyphs from the keyboard. It stays off until you press it."],["try-it","Open the start file: two identities, one under the other. Open the file: choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… in the dialog and pick it (see Open). Put the playhead at 4 s. Hold Alt and click the 2 of 2ab in the first line, then Alt+Shift+click the b. Do the same in the second line. The bar says “6 glyphs of 2 formulas”. Open Colour ▾ and choose a colour, then press Esc to clear the pick and see the colours. Alt+click the last glyph of each line, the exponent of b², and open Brace ▾ ▸ Right: one brace spans both lines. Press S and pick a minus sign with an Alt+click beside it."]],"short":"Picking glyphs"},{"id":"color/coloring","url":"color/coloring.html","title":"Colouring glyphs and objects","section":"Colour","level":"basic","description":"Colour a whole formula or single glyphs, copy colours between glyphs, and carry them from one formula to the next.","headings":[["where","Where a colour lives"],["formula-colour","The colour of a whole formula"],["creator","Glyph by glyph in the Creator"],["preview","Glyph by glyph in the Preview"],["any-colour","Any colour: the Hex field"],["order","Colours in order"],["sequence","Copying a colour sequence"],["patterns","Colouring by pattern"],["python","What reaches Manim"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A formula has one colour, and each of its glyphs can have a colour of its own. You set them in the Creator or straight in the Preview, and a glyph keeps its colour through every transformation."],["where","A colour belongs to one of three things: The formula has a colour. Every glyph without a colour of its own is drawn in it, in every clip of that formula. A glyph can have a colour of its own, kept with the formula. It shows in every clip too, and it moves with the glyph through a transformation. A clip can change colours while it plays, with the animation blocks of Animation blocks. A glyph is named by its place in the formula. The glossary calls that place its canonical index; you meet it as the number the glyph view shows under the pointer."],["formula-colour","Double-click a formula in the Creator (or select it and press Enter) to open its editor. The Colour row has a colour picker and a hex field: type a colour, such as #58C4DD, and press Enter, or leave the field. The Colour row of a formula's editor (1). A new formula is white, as Manim draws a formula by default. With a palette active, it takes the palette's formula colour instead; Palettes tells how. Do not mistake it for the Tag row above it. The tag only colours the dot that marks the formula in the Creator and on the timeline, and never the scene."],["creator","The right half of the editor is the glyph view. It draws every glyph of the formula inside a frame, and shows the index of the one under the pointer. A glyph you have not coloured is drawn in the colour of the formula. Click a glyph to select it. Shift+click another to take the range between them, and Ctrl+click to add or remove one. You can also drag a box over several glyphs, and Ctrl held adds them to what is selected. Click a swatch to colour the selection, or type any colour in the hex field beside the swatches. Clear selected gives the selected glyphs back the colour of the formula, and Clear colours does it for all of them. The glyph view with two glyphs selected: the drawing (1), the swatches (2) and the hex field (3). A click on the m and a Shift+click on the x select the pair, and a swatch makes it blue. Clear colours puts everything back. The twelve swatches are Manim's own colours, WHITE to PURPLE. With a palette active, its roles follow them. A right-click on the selection offers the same colours, and Hide at the start and Show at the start for the glyphs that should wait for a transformation. The key beside the Glyphs title lists the glyphs under the drawing, a key each with its index and label. It is also how you pick glyphs from the keyboard: Tab to a glyph's key, then Enter or Space to select it. The browser remembers whether you asked for the list. A LaTeX text or a font text colours its glyphs the same way, in its composition editor: the switch over the stage shows Glyphs in place of Placement. LaTeX texts and font texts tells more. Press + New in the Creator, type y = mx + b in the Raw LaTeX field and press Apply. Select the m and the x in the glyph view and click the blue swatch. Type f0ac5f in the hex field with the b selected, and press Enter."],["preview","You can also colour glyphs where they are on the scene. Hold Alt and click a glyph to pick it; Alt+click it again to let it go. Alt+Shift+click takes a range, and Alt+drag from a formula draws a rectangle that picks the glyphs it touches. The picked glyphs light up in yellow, and a bar in the corner of the Preview names them. Two glyphs picked, and the bar that acts on them (1). The bar's Colour ▾ menu has the same swatches. A right-click on the ink of a picked glyph opens the same actions, with the colours under Colour. Esc lets the glyphs go, and so does selecting something else. Alt+click on the m, Alt+Shift+click on the x, Colour ▾ and a swatch. Esc lets the glyphs go, and Ctrl+Z takes the colour back. The glyphs can be of several formulas at once: Alt+click a glyph of another formula and it joins the pick. A colour then goes to all of them as one undo step. Picking glyphs tells the rest. The picked glyphs are yellow because the preference Preferences ▸ Glyph selection colour is. It offers six colours, so you can choose one that stands out from your formula. The colours the picked glyphs can wear."],["any-colour","Every Colour menu has a Hex field under its swatches. Type #RRGGBB, or #RGB for a short one; the # is optional. The square beside the label shows the colour you typed, and Enter or Apply uses it. Something that is not a colour is marked, and Apply stays off. With one glyph picked, Copy colour to clipboard puts the colour that glyph is seen in on the system clipboard, as #RRGGBB, beside the colour itself. It is the glyph's own colour, or the formula's when it has none. Paste it into another Hex field with Ctrl+V, or into any other program. When the browser does not let the page write to the clipboard, a notice shows the colour so that you can type it. A colour typed in the Hex field (1), and Copy colour to clipboard (2), which shows the colour it would copy. A brace, a shape or a line has one colour and no glyphs to pick. The own menu of a brace or a shape has Colour ▸ with the same swatches and the same Hex field, and a line's colour is in the Line section of the Animations panel. See Braces, Shapes and Lines and arrows."],["order","Two lines of a derivation often say the same thing with other symbols: the m and the b of y = mx + b become the 2 and the 1 of y = 2x + 1. Colours in order copies the colours of some glyphs, one by one, and gives them in that order to others, whatever those are. Press Shift+C. The Preview says what to do, and the top of the Animations panel shows a strip, Colours in order. Click, one by one, the glyphs whose colours you want. Each click adds a chip to the strip, in the order of your clicks, with its number and its colour. A glyph without a colour of its own adds the colour of its formula. Press Enter (or Paste…). The first chip is marked as the next to give out. Click the glyphs that take the colours. Each click colours one glyph and removes its chip. With the last colour the mode ends by itself. Shift+C, a click on the m and one on the b, Enter, a click on the 2 and one on the 1. Ctrl+Z takes the whole paste back in one step. Copying: a chip for each click. Pasting: the marked chip is next. The result is the colouring of the next line, and it holds through the transformation between them: A few details help: Backspace takes the last chip off while you copy, and the last glyph back while you paste. Esc cancels a copy; during a paste it ends the mode and keeps what was pasted. Only clicks work while the mode is on: no Alt picks, no rectangle, no drag. The playhead can move between two clicks, so you can copy at one time and paste at another. The whole paste is one undo step. A pasted colour is the formula's, so it shows in every clip of that formula. The list is kept. Shift+V pastes it again from its first colour, onto other glyphs. The same two commands are in the Edit menu, and at the end of the Preview's right-click menu of a clip, as Copy colours in order… and Paste colours in order. Edit ▸ Colours in order. Download color-coloring-order.mfe.json, choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… and pick it. Double-click step2 in the Creator, press Clear colours in its glyph view and close the editor. Put the playhead at 1.5 s, press Shift+C, click the m and the b, and press Enter. Move the playhead to 4 s, where the second line is on screen, and click the 2 and the 1."],["sequence","When the glyphs are the same symbols in two places, a colour sequence is faster. The classic case is a formula and the sentence that explains it: colour the a, b and c of the equation, then give the same colours to the three letters of the sentence. Alt+click the glyphs of the first object. They need not be next to each other. Open Colour ▾ in the bar (or right-click a picked glyph) and choose Copy colour sequence. It takes the own colours of the picked glyphs. Press Esc, then Alt+click the glyphs of the other object, in this formula or another one. Open the menu again and choose Paste colour sequence. The three letters of the equation are picked: Copy colour sequence (1) takes their colours. Each glyph takes the colour of the copied glyph that is its symbol. Of several equal symbols, the first takes the first colour, the second the second, in glyph order. A glyph loses its own colour where the copied one had none. The two picks may list their glyphs in another order, but they must be the same symbols, each as many times. The line under Paste colour sequence says what would be pasted, or why it cannot be: Nothing copied yet., The copied sequence has 3 glyphs; this selection has 2. or The symbols differ followed by which. The line under Paste colour sequence (1). What you copied is kept in the page, not on the system clipboard, until you reload it. It survives opening another project, so you can copy in one project and paste in another. It is a copy of colours, not a link: changing the first object later does not change the second."],["patterns","To colour every x of a whole derivation without picking a glyph, give the editor a list of LaTeX patterns and their colours. That is Match colors."],["python","Colours leave the editor as plain values. The exported scene colours each glyph of a formula by its place, as you did: the line step1_g[2].set_color(\"#58C4DD\") below is the m of y = mx + b. The file needs no palette, no list and nothing of the editor to run."],["next","Match colors colours patterns across many formulas at once. Palettes by role names your colours and gives new objects the right ones. Picking glyphs covers the Alt pick in full. Keyboard and mouse shortcuts lists every key of these modes. The Pythagorean theorem, in colour builds a whole animation around colours."]],"short":"Colouring"},{"id":"color/palettes","url":"color/palettes.html","title":"Palettes by role and Chroma Lab","section":"Colour","level":"intermediate","description":"Name your colours by role, make a palette in the editor or design one in Chroma Lab, and use it for new objects and for a whole project.","headings":[["what","What a palette is"],["make","Making a palette"],["use","Using a palette"],["apply","Recolouring what exists"],["match","Roles and Match colors"],["chroma-lab","Chroma Lab"],["try","Try a palette on a scene"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A palette gives your colours names: one for the background, one for formulas, one for variables, and so on. You make it once, and the editor uses it for what you create from then on. Chroma Lab, the colour studio inside the app, designs whole palettes for you."],["what","A palette is a set of eleven roles, each with a colour. Every palette has all eleven, and the editor knows what to do with most of them: Role What the editor does with it background The background of a new project. formula The colour of a new formula. variable, constant, operator, result The colours of a Match colors list made from the palette. They are also offered wherever you choose a colour. text The colour of a new text. muted The colour of a new brace. rectangle The colour of a new box, highlight or underline. circle The colour of a new circle. arrow The colour of a new cross, cross line or line. You can add roles of your own to a palette: a name and a colour, such as Highlight. Nothing takes them by itself. They are offered wherever you choose a colour by role. A palette is a preference of the editor, kept in this browser. A project never stores one. It stores plain colours, and so does the exported Python. A project opened on a machine without your palette looks exactly the same."],["make","Choose Preferences ▸ Editor palettes…. The dialog holds up to 24 palettes. New palette starts one from the colours the editor uses without a palette, Import palette… reads a theme file, and Duplicate copies the one selected. Chroma Lab… (1) and Editor palettes… (2) are the last entries of Preferences. To make one: Press New palette. It appears selected, and not yet active. Give it a name in Display name and press Rename. Change a role: type a hex value in its field and press Enter, or use the picker beside it. The sample above the roles shows the palette at work on its own background. Under the eleven roles, Your roles adds roles of your own: type a name, choose a colour and press Add role (or Enter in the name). Press Activate. A colour is saved as soon as its field is accepted. There is no draft and no undo, so Duplicate a palette first if you want to keep it as it is. A role of your own is renamed and recoloured in place and removed with Delete; a palette holds up to 24 of them. Export JSON writes the palette to a file named after it. The file is the same palette when you import it again, here or in Chroma Lab. Your own roles travel in a field of their own, customRoles, beside the eleven colors."],["use","Activate makes a palette the one the editor works with, marked Active in the list. From then on: New projects start with its background, and new objects with the colour of their role (the table above). Colour fields show a Roles button beside their hex box, and the swatches of a colour menu are followed by the roles. A colour you change in the active palette applies from then on, with no need to activate it again. Nothing that already exists changes, and activating adds no undo step. To go back, select Manim defaults in the list and press Use Manim defaults. Deleting the active palette does the same, and the objects keep their colours. The Roles button opens every role as a block with its name beside its colour, and your own roles under Your roles. Choosing one puts its colour in the field. The swatches of a menu keep the names in their tooltips. The Roles button of a colour field (1)."],["apply","Edit ▸ Apply palette… recolours the objects of the project with the role of their kind. The entry is off until a palette is active. The dialog shows what would change before anything does. Apply palette… (1), and the lists of Match colors (2). The palette (1), what will change (2), the background (3) and what is skipped (4). Each object takes the colour of its role: a formula its formula, a brace its muted, and so on. Apply background also sets the background of the scene, behind everything, locked objects included. You can leave it off. An object is skipped, with its reason in the list, when it is used on a locked track, or when it has glyph colours of its own. Glyph colours, the colours that clips and animations override, tags, styles and timing are never touched. The whole change is one undo step. If the project or the palette changes while the dialog is open, it asks you to review a fresh preview first. Before. After Apply palette with the palette Chalkboard."],["match","The roles variable, constant, operator and result are for the glyphs of an equation. The editor cannot tell which glyph is a variable, so you say it with patterns. Edit ▸ Match colors ▸ New from active palette… makes a list of four rows coloured with those roles, and leaves the patterns empty for you to fill. It colours nothing by itself. Four rows, one for each role. Write the patterns and apply the list."],["chroma-lab","Chroma Lab is the editor's colour studio. It generates palettes from harmonies and seeds, keeps roles linked, checks their contrast against the scene's background, extracts colours from an image and tries a palette on animated scenes. It comes inside the app and works offline: open it with Preferences ▸ Chroma Lab…. You do not need it to use palettes, but it is the quickest way to a good one. Chroma Lab in its dialog. The title bar has Import palette… (1), Editor palettes… (2) and the information button (3); Send to Manim Formula Editor (4) is in the studio. A theme reaches the editor's palettes in two ways, and nothing is written to your project in either: Send. Send to Manim Formula Editor, under Use in your animation in Exploration and beside JSON in Seeds & themes, puts the theme in the palettes and opens Editor palettes on it, ready to activate. Sending the same theme again finds the entry it made; a changed one makes another, named like the first with (2). File. Save the theme with JSON in Chroma Lab, then use Import palette…, in the title bar of the dialog or in Editor palettes. You can also drag the file onto either dialog. After Send: the theme is selected in Editor palettes, ready to activate (1). The ⓘ button in the title bar says this again, and links to the notices of Chroma Lab. The general Export palette file of Chroma Lab has no named roles, and the editor cannot import it. Chroma Lab keeps its own work in the browser's storage, which can be unavailable. Send or export your theme before you close the dialog; it says so in its footer. A palette that you edit in the editor, adding roles of your own, is not sent back. If you send the theme from Chroma Lab again, it arrives as a new palette without your roles. Chroma Lab is a part of the editor that has a repository of its own, and it also opens alone from there. Getting the app running tells how the two fit together."],["try","The widget at the top of the page recolours a short derivation with the roles of a palette: pick a palette, and every glyph takes the colour of its role. A palette that looks good on a swatch row can look very different on a scene, so look at it on one before you activate it. The project below is that derivation: download it, choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… and pick it, to try a palette of your own on it. Open Preferences ▸ Editor palettes… and press New palette. Set variable to a colour you like and press Activate. Press + New in the Creator and open the Colour row's Roles button: your palette is there. Open Edit ▸ Apply palette… to see what it would change in your project."],["next","Match colors turns the four glyph roles into colours on your formulas. Colouring glyphs and objects covers colours by hand. Preferences lists every preference of the editor."]],"short":"Palettes"},{"id":"color/match-colors","url":"color/match-colors.html","title":"Match colors","section":"Colour","level":"intermediate","description":"Colour every occurrence of a LaTeX pattern in your formulas, as Manim’s tex_to_color_map does.","headings":[["why","Why a list"],["lists","Lists of patterns"],["patterns","How a pattern matches"],["overlap","When patterns overlap"],["apply","Applying a list"],["limits","What a list leaves alone"],["palette","A list that starts from a palette"],["python","What reaches Manim"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"A match list is a list of LaTeX patterns, each with a colour. Apply it to formulas and every occurrence of every pattern takes its colour, in all of them at once. It is the editor's version of Manim's tex_to_color_map."],["why","A derivation says the same letters again and again. Colouring glyphs by hand means picking each one, in each formula, and doing it again when you change a line. A list moves the work to the pattern: you say once that F is red, and every F of every formula you apply the list to is. The list belongs to the project and is saved with it. A project can hold as many lists as you like, each with its own name."],["lists","Choose Edit ▸ Match colors ▸ Create match colors… to make a list; it opens in the Match colors dialog under the name Match colors 1. The same submenu lists the lists you have, and a click on one opens it for editing. The dialog: the lists (1), the patterns with their colours (2), a preview (3) and the button that applies the list (4). A list is a name and some entries. Each entry is a pattern and a colour: Type the pattern as you would in a formula: x, \\frac{dy}{dx}, \\sin. A pair of $…$ around it is ignored. Enter or leaving the field commits it, and Esc takes the edit back. Click the colour chip for the Manim colours and, with a palette active, its roles; or type a hex value or use the picker beside it. ▲ and ▼ move an entry, and × removes it. + Add pattern adds one at the end. Duplicate copies the list; Delete removes it, and you hold the button for a moment to confirm. Every edit is one undo step, and Ctrl+Z works while the dialog is open. The preview draws one formula of the project with the colours the list would give it, before anything is applied. Choose the formula in the menu above it. It says how many glyphs of that formula take a colour, or that no pattern occurs in it."],["patterns","A pattern occurs wherever the formula's LaTeX repeats the pattern's, compared token by token. A token is a command (\\frac, \\sin, \\,), a brace, or one other character. That gives the rules you need: Spaces do not count: x y is xy. x is not found inside \\xi, which is one token of its own. A command and its arguments are matched as you write them: \\frac{dy}{dx} matches that fraction. A pattern that cuts a command from its arguments, such as \\frac alone, cannot be coloured. \\left( is one token. A delimiter cannot be coloured apart from its command, so a pattern takes \\left and \\right both or neither. For the line \\frac{dy}{dx} = 2x + 2: Pattern What it colours x the x of dx and the x of 2x y the y of dy 2x the 2 and the x of 2x together \\frac{dy}{dx} the whole fraction: its d, y, bar, d and x $x$ the same as x An entry the app cannot match says why under its pattern: an empty pattern (Type some LaTeX.), a row or column break (\\\\ or &), braces that do not balance, a \\left without its \\right. Such an entry is skipped."],["overlap","Two patterns can claim the same glyphs: x and \\frac{dy}{dx} both claim the x of dx. The lower entry wins. With the fraction last, the whole fraction is green. Move it up, and the fraction keeps only what no later entry claims: its y and its x take their own colours, nested inside it. The fraction moves up two places: the green fraction now holds a yellow y and a blue x. It goes back down for the loop. When an occurrence of one pattern only partly overlaps an occurrence of a lower one, it cannot be nested. It is dropped, and the preview names the pattern: Pattern 1 (“…”) overlaps a lower one here: the lower entry wins."],["apply","You apply a list to formulas: the formulas you select in the Creator, or the formulas of the clips you select in the Preview or on the timeline. Pick whichever you have at hand: In the Creator, select the formulas (Shift or Ctrl and click for several), right-click one and choose Apply match colors, then the list. In the Preview, right-click a clip and choose Apply match colors. On the timeline, do the same on several selected clips. With two or more clips selected, the bar in the corner of the Preview has Match colors ▾. In Edit together…, the Match colors button colours all of its formulas. In the dialog, Apply to 3 formulas does the same for the formulas selected behind it. Three formulas are selected in the Creator, and the list is applied from their right-click menu. The cards show the colours at once. Ctrl+Z takes them back. Each list in these menus shows its colours after its name, so you can tell lists apart without opening them. The menu of the selected formulas: each list with its colours. The Preview's bar, with two clips selected. Edit together… and its Match colors button (1). Applying a list replaces the glyph colours of the formulas: they become exactly what the list says, and a glyph no pattern reaches goes back to the formula's colour. All the formulas change in one undo step. A notice says how many glyphs took a colour, and which formulas were left alone and why. The notice after applying a list."],["limits","Objects without LaTeX. A Text (font) has colours glyph by glyph but no LaTeX to search; it keeps its colours, and the notice says so. A brace, a shape or a line has no glyph colours to set, so a list never changes one. Formulas that are not ready. A formula that has not been drawn yet, or whose glyphs changed while the colours were being worked out, is left alone and named in the notice. Patterns that cannot be drawn. A pattern that cuts through a macro's arguments cannot be told apart in the render; the notice says that the patterns cannot be rendered on that formula. The colours that a list wrote are ordinary glyph colours. You can recolour one glyph by hand afterwards, and a later Apply match colors will replace that colour too."],["palette","With a palette active, Edit ▸ Match colors ▸ New from active palette… makes a list of four rows, coloured with the palette's variable, constant, operator and result roles and with empty patterns for you to fill. Nothing is coloured until you write the patterns and apply the list. Palettes shows it."],["python","The exported scene has no list and no patterns, only the colours they gave: each glyph carries a set_color with a plain hex value. Here is the Newton example as ManimCE writes it. This second example is the one the dialog above shows: three lines of a derivation coloured by the list Course colours, where the fraction wins over the letters inside it. Download color-match-colors-newton.mfe.json, choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… and pick it. Open Edit ▸ Match colors ▸ Newton… and change the colour of F to purple. Close the dialog, select the three formulas in the Creator, right-click one and choose Apply match colors ▸ Newton. The F of all three lines is purple. Press Ctrl+Z twice to put the list and the colours back."],["next","Colouring glyphs and objects covers colours by hand. Palettes by role names colours and starts a list from them. Finding and organizing selects formulas by kind, so a list reaches all of them."]],"short":"Match colors"},{"id":"sound/audio","url":"sound/audio.html","title":"Audio","section":"Audio and video","level":"intermediate","description":"Import a recording, place it on an audio track, trim and split it, shape its volume, and export the sound or a video with it.","headings":[["parts","The parts of sound"],["recordings","Importing a recording"],["recording-row","The recording’s row"],["place","Putting it on the timeline"],["clip","Anatomy of a clip"],["trimming","Moving, trimming and splitting"],["move","Move"],["trim","Trim"],["split","Split"],["copy","Duplicate, delete and the clip’s menu"],["volume","Volume"],["editor","The volume editor"],["fades","Fades"],["loudness","When the mix is too loud"],["tracks","Audio tracks"],["recipes","Two recipes"],["recipe-pause","Take a pause out"],["recipe-duck","Make music quieter under a voice"],["keys","Keys and gestures at a glance"],["play","Hearing it"],["export","Exporting sound"],["save","Saving, and a recording that is missing"],["limits","Limits"]],"chunks":[[null,"Put a voice-over or a music bed under your animation. Import a recording, place it on an audio track, cut it, shape its volume, and hear it with the scene. The example The square of a sum with its narration: the recording in the Creator’s Audio tab, its clip on an audio track under the formulas, and the clip’s properties on the right."],["parts","Sound has three parts. A recording is a sound file that you import once; it lives in the Creator’s Audio tab. A clip is a piece of a recording placed on the timeline, with its own trim and its own volume. An audio track is a row of the timeline that holds clips. Nothing you do to a clip changes the recording. Trimming and splitting choose which part plays; the volume line says how loud. The sound plays with the scene in the Preview, goes into a video when you ask for it, and can be exported alone. The figures on this page use the example below and an eleven-second recording made for it: a synthetic hum in three phrases, with a pause after each, that stands in for a voice. Download both to follow along. audio-narration.mp3 (66 KB) is the recording. The project is the card below: download it, choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… and pick the file (Projects and files explains the dialog)."],["recordings","Open the Audio tab of the Creator. Press Import audio… and choose one file or several, or drag audio files from your desktop onto the tab: while they are over it, the tab says Drop audio to import it. The empty tab: Import audio… (1) and the search field (2). The line under them repeats what to do. MP3 and WAV are the formats to count on. The file picker also lists M4A, AAC, FLAC, OGG, Opus and WebM, and any other file comes in if your browser can decode its sound. When it cannot, a notice says Could not import and the file’s name, with the reason: for example This browser cannot decode the audio in this file. or This file does not contain an audio track. While a file is read, the tab says Preparing audio-narration.mp3… and then Importing audio-narration.mp3 · 42%, with a Cancel button. Files that you give while one is being read wait for their turn."],["recording-row","A recording’s row: its name (1), delete (2), its length, sample rate and how many clips use it (3), the part of it to place (4) and the button that places it (5). Name. A recording is named after its file. Click the name to change it; Enter or leaving the field keeps it and Esc takes the edit back. A name cannot be empty. Each rename is one undo step. Importing the same file twice does not add it twice: a notice says which recording it already is. A different file whose name is in use asks for a name of its own first (Name already in use, with the first free name proposed). Don’t import leaves the file out. Delete. The × deletes the recording and every clip made from it. When clips use it, a dialog counts them and you confirm by holding its button; Undo brings the recording and its clips back. While one of its clips is on a locked track, the × waits for you to unlock the track. Search. The field above the list filters it by name."],["place","A recording does nothing until a piece of it is on the timeline. Choose the piece with From (s) and To (s) — the whole recording by default — and press Add to timeline. The clip starts at the playhead, on the first unlocked audio track that has room there. When none has, the timeline adds a track, named Audio 1, Audio 2 and so on: the first number no track has. The new clip is selected and scrolled into view. You can also drag the button onto an audio track: the clip lands where you drop it. A locked track takes no drop. A drop on a track that has no room there, or a placement that would end after 60 minutes of project time, is refused with the notice Audio does not fit here: the sound is never shortened to fit. Add to timeline never runs out of room, because it adds a track. Place the same recording as many times as you like. Each clip has its own part, trim and volume, and the row counts them (2 uses). The Audio button of the timeline’s toolbar adds an empty audio track at the bottom of the list, named in the same way. Audio tracks mix: sounds on different tracks play together, and clips on one track cannot overlap. Download the project of the card at the top of this page and open it (File ▸ Open…, then Other file…). In the Creator, open the Audio tab and press Import audio…. Choose audio-narration.mp3. With the playhead at 0, press Add to timeline. Press Space. The scene plays with the narration."],["clip","An audio clip on its track. The name, with a ♪. Drag it to move the clip. Volume… opens the volume editor (later in this page). The volume-line button turns on the editing of the volume line on the clip itself. The strips at the top corners are the handles that trim the clip. The graph is the waveform of the recording, with the volume line over it. A clip on a muted track is dimmed. A clip whose recording is missing is hatched (see Saving)."],["trimming",""],["move","Drag a clip by its name — or from anywhere on it, until you turn the volume line on — to move it along its track or to another audio track. Snap pulls its edges to the playhead, to the edges of other clips and to the ruler’s small ticks; to place it freely, hold Shift while you drag (press the clip first: Shift at the press adds the clip to the selection, or takes it out when it was selected). Clips can only go to audio tracks. A selection of sounds and formulas together moves together in time. To start a sound exactly when a formula appears, right-click the formula’s clip and choose Timing › Copy timing; then right-click the sound and choose Timing › Paste start. A sound has no intro or outro, so the menu offers only Paste start, Paste end and Paste duration."],["trim","The end of the clip dragged left cuts the last phrase off. Dragged back, the phrase returns: the recording was never cut. Drag the handle at the start or at the end of a clip to trim it. Trimming never cuts the recording: drag the edge back and the sound comes back. Trimming the start leaves what remains where it was in time. An edge stops at the next clip of the track and at the ends of the recording. The edges snap like any other. The volume editor has two fields, Source start (s) and Source end (s), for a trim by the second."],["split","The playhead in the pause after the first phrase, S, a click on the second part to select it, and a drag that moves it later. Put the playhead where the cut goes, select the clip and press S. The clip’s menu has Split at playhead (S) too, and so does the Animations panel, with its button Split selected audio at playhead (the panel is titled Audio while only sounds are selected). Both parts keep the volume curve of the original, so the sound is the same with the cut as without it, even in the middle of a fade. Both are selected afterwards: a drag moves both, and a click on one part selects just that part. When the playhead is not inside a selected clip of an unlocked track, the menu entry and the button of the panel say so in a notice and change nothing. S does the same while a clip of an unlocked track is selected. With an audio clip of an unlocked track selected, S splits it. It also splits the selected clips of the scene that the playhead is inside, unless the keyboard is in the Preview, together with the sounds selected with them, in one step (Splitting a clip). Otherwise S switches the Preview’s circle selector on and off."],["copy","Right-click a clip for its menu. Ctrl+D copies the selected clips, each into the free time after it on its own track, and selects the copies. Delete removes the selected clips; Undo brings them back. Deleting a clip from the middle of a track leaves a gap: the clips after it do not move. The Timing › entry copies when a clip starts and ends and pastes it onto another (see Timing tools). Click the ruler in the pause after the first phrase, at about 3.7 s. Select the clip and press S. Click the second part to select it alone, then drag it about a second to the right. Press Ctrl+Z twice to go back."],["volume","The line over the waveform is the clip’s volume. 100% is the loudness of the recording and sits at mid-height of the graph; 0% is silence, at the bottom; 400% is the loudest the editor allows, at the top. Fades and reductions have the lower half of the graph, amplification the upper half. Until you turn it on, the line is a drawing: a drag anywhere on the clip moves the clip, so that a drag meant for the clip never changes its volume. The button beside Volume… turns the editing on, for that clip only. A scale (400%, 100%, 0%) appears, the line and its points become handles, and the clip then moves by its name alone. The line turned on, two points added with a double-click, and the last one dragged down: a fade out drawn by hand. Gesture What it does Drag the flat line up or down Changes the level of the whole clip. Double-click the line Adds a point at the level the line has there: the sound does not change. Drag a point Moves it in time and in level. It stops at its neighbours. Drag a segment between two points, up or down Moves both of its points by the same amount, so its slope stays. Arrow keys on a focused point ↑ ↓ change its level by 1% (Shift: 10%); ← → move it by 0.01 s (Shift: 0.1 s). Delete on a focused point Removes it. Enter on a focused point Opens the volume editor. Esc during a drag Takes the drag back. Each drag is one undo step. The line before its first point and after its last point stays at the level of that point."],["editor","The volume editor after Fade In and Fade Out of one second each. Open it with Volume… on the clip, with a double-click on the clip, with Edit volume and trim… in its menu, or with Volume, fades and trim… in the Animations panel. It is the numeric side of everything above, and what you change applies at once and can be undone. Source start (s) and Source end (s): the part of the recording the clip plays (the trim). Constant volume (%): one level for the whole clip, from 0 to 400. It replaces any curve; with a curve the field is empty and says Automated. New point source time (s) and Add point: a point of the line at that second of the recording, at the level the line has there. Enter in the field adds it, so a point can be made without the mouse. The table lists every point with its time and its level, each editable, and a × that removes it."],["fades","Fade In and Fade Out put ordinary points of the line at the clip’s trimmed edges: a ramp from silence at the start, or to silence at the end. Fade duration (s) says how long. Until you type one, the fade is half the clip, up to 0.5 s, and follows the clip when you trim it; a duration you type stays. Applying a fade again replaces the points of its stretch, and points outside it stay for when you restore a trim. Reset volume goes back to a constant 100%. The clip’s menu has both fades too, at the default length."],["loudness","Two sounds at 100% together, or a clip above 100%, can be louder than the output allows. While the scene plays, the playback bar shows the peak of the mix as ♪ 85%; over 100% it adds Clip and turns to the warning colour. Lower a clip or a track, or reshape the line, until it stays under."],["tracks","Two audio tracks, the second one muted: the row of keys of its header, from mute to delete (1), and the two small keys that make a track lower or taller (2). An audio track’s header has the keys of a visual track’s, except that the eye is a speaker. Name. Double-click it, or press Enter or F2 on it, to rename the track. Mute keeps the track out of playback and out of every export. Its clips stay, dimmed. Lock keeps its clips from being edited. A locked track still plays. Move up and Move down change the track’s place in the list. They do not change how the formulas are layered. Delete removes the track and its clips after a question you confirm by holding the button; Undo brings it back. The two small keys beside the name make that track one step lower or taller. That changes the view only. The Animations panel — titled Audio while only sounds are selected — shows an Audio clip card for a selected clip: its times, its part of the recording, whether its track is muted or locked, and two buttons, Volume, fades and trim… and Split selected audio at playhead. The Animations panel, titled Audio because only a sound is selected: split at the playhead (1) and the volume editor (2)."],["recipes",""],["recipe-pause","Put the playhead at the start of the pause and split the clip (S). Put the playhead at the end of the pause, click the part on its right to select it, and split again. Select the middle part, the pause, and press Delete. Drag the part on the right to the left until its start meets the end of the part on the left: with Snap on, it jumps onto that edge."],["recipe-duck","Import the music and place it on its own audio track, under the voice. Turn on the music’s volume line, and double-click it four times: just before the voice starts, as it starts, as it ends, and just after. Drag the segment between the middle two points down to about 30%. The two ramps on its sides bring the music down and back up."],["keys","What How Move a clip Drag its name. Hold Shift during the drag to place it freely. Trim a clip Drag a handle at its top corners. Split at the playhead S Duplicate Ctrl+D Delete Delete or Backspace Undo, redo Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Shift+Z Add a point to the volume line Double-click the line, once it is turned on. Play, pause Space"],["play","Press Space or Play in the bar under the Preview: the sound plays with the scene. The first time, the bar says Preparing audio… while the editor reads the recordings; press the button again to cancel it. Moving the playhead, muting a track and editing a volume line all work while it plays. The sound follows the audio device’s clock, so picture and sound stay together. A scene lasts until its last clip ends, a sound clip included. When the narration is longer than the formulas, the scene — and a video exported by the browser or by ffmpeg — lasts as long as the narration. File ▸ Project settings… can fix the length instead (Duration ▸ Fixed)."],["export","Export ▸ Audio… writes the mix without drawing any formula. Export ▸ Audio… writes the sound of the scene as a file: 48 kHz, stereo, with the volume curves and the track mutes applied. Choose a Format, a Start (s) and an End (s) — the whole scene by default, up to 60 minutes — and press Export audio. Format What it is WAV · PCM 16-bit Uncompressed, about 11 MiB a minute; the dialog shows the estimated size. WebM · Opus Compressed, small. M4A · AAC Compressed. Only where the browser can encode AAC (Chrome and Edge on Windows and macOS); the dialog says so where it cannot. A video takes the sound along: Export ▸ Video… has an Include audio option, described in Exporting video. The exported Python has none: its dialog says Audio is omitted from Manim exports. To add the sound to a scene rendered by Manim yourself, call Manim’s self.add_sound(\"audio-narration.mp3\") in the scene (The generated Python). An export needs the recordings of its clips that are not muted. When one is missing, the export stops before it starts and names it."],["save","A project with recordings is saved as an .mfe package: one file with the project and every recording in it, the unused and the muted ones too. A project without recordings is saved as .mfe.json, unless it was opened from a package (File ▸ Save keeps it a package) or holds more font-text shapes than a plain file carries. Open a package like any project (File ▸ Open…, then Other file… for one from your disk): the editor checks each recording and copies it into the browser’s storage, with a progress dialog and a Cancel button for a large file. While you work, the recordings live in the browser’s storage, which the browser may clear. A saved package is the backup. Save before you close the tab, and keep the package. When the browser no longer holds a recording, its clips stay where they are, hatched and marked missing media, and the playback skips them. The recording’s row says Original unavailable, and a notice names the files. A clip whose recording the browser does not have (1). Import the same file again in the Audio tab: the clips come back to life, with their trims and volumes. File ▸ Save first looks for the missing recordings in the file it is about to replace, and brings them back from it. For what is still missing it asks; Save without the missing media writes a file whose clips stay marked, and Cancel writes nothing. An export stops at a recording that is missing and says which. More about files and recovery: Projects and files. The layout of both kinds of file is in Project files and their format."],["limits","A placement ends within 60 minutes of the project, and an export mixes an hour of sound at most. Projects of 40 to 60 minutes work; the usual size is under 15. A scene that is longer plays whole with its sound, but a video of more than 60 minutes is exported without it, and its dialog says so. A recording longer than an hour supplies the pieces you choose, up to 60 minutes each time. Importing MP3 works. Encoding new MP3 files does not. The editor does not record sound, reduce noise, equalise, stretch time or crossfade clips on its own: a fade is yours to draw."]],"short":"Audio"},{"id":"sound/video","url":"sound/video.html","title":"Exporting video","section":"Audio and video","level":"intermediate","description":"Export a video of your scene: encoded in the browser, encoded by the companion's ffmpeg, or rendered by your own Manim.","headings":[["dialog","The dialog"],["encoders","Three ways to make the video"],["browser","In the browser"],["companion","With the companion’s ffmpeg"],["manim","Rendered by Manim"],["size","Size, frame rate and range"],["sound","Sound in the video"],["saving","The file, and stopping"],["messages","What the dialog tells you"]],"chunks":[[null,"Turn the scene into a video file. Your browser can encode it, the companion’s ffmpeg can, or your own Manim can draw it: pick the one that fits what you have."],["dialog","Choose Export ▸ Video…. The same entry is in the list that opens from the arrow of the Export… button, next to the menus. Exporting says what each export is for. The dialog with no companion running: Browser is the encoder that works, and the other two say why they do not. Encoder: what makes the video (next section). One that is not available stays on the list, greyed out, with the reason. Format: the file type. The list depends on the encoder. Size: the project’s own size, a preset or a size of your own. Frame rate: the project’s, a preset or your own. Range: the whole scene, or a stretch of it. Export starts it. Under the fields, one line says what you will get — for example 672 frames · 11.2 s · 1920 × 1080 at 60 fps. The file is named after the project and lasts exactly its frames divided by its frame rate, to the frame. Open a project and choose Export ▸ Video…. Under Size choose Custom… and type 320 and 180. Under Frame rate choose Custom… and type 10. Under Range choose Custom and type 0 to 1.5. The line under the fields reads 15 frames · 1.5 s · 320 × 180 at 10 fps. Press Export: a very small file, in a moment."],["encoders","The encoder decides who draws and encodes the frames. Each one changes the fields under it; the sections that follow show them one by one. Browser Companion · ffmpeg Render with Manim What draws the picture The editor, as in the Preview The editor, as in the Preview Manim, from the exported Python What you need A browser that can encode video (Chrome and Edge can) The companion running, and ffmpeg on its machine The companion started with --allow-manim-render, and a ManimCE or ManimGL it can reach Formats MP4 (H.264 or AV1), WebM (VP9) MP4 (H.264), WebM (VP9), MOV (ProRes), GIF MP4 Range Whole scene or custom Whole scene or custom Whole scene Sound Yes Yes, except in a GIF Yes Start with Browser: it needs nothing. Choose Companion · ffmpeg for a cleaner H.264 file, for ProRes or for a GIF. Choose Render with Manim when the video must be Manim’s own picture, for example to put it next to scenes you wrote by hand."],["browser","The browser encodes the frames itself, with WebCodecs, and nothing else has to run. The frames are drawn by the same renderer as the Preview, so the video matches what you see. A scene with a camera is filmed through it, as the camera view shows it. MP4 · H.264, MP4 · AV1 and WebM · VP9 are the formats. A format your browser cannot encode at the size you chose is greyed out and says not supported by this browser at this size. When none can be, the dialog says so and suggests a smaller size, or the companion. Where the browser has no WebCodecs at all, Browser is greyed out: This browser cannot encode video (WebCodecs is missing). Use Chrome or Edge, or the companion. The dialog estimates the size: About 25.2 MB (H.264 at 18.7 Mbit/s, written to a file as it is encoded). It is the bitrate the editor picks, times the length, and the sound when you include it. Where the browser keeps the file in memory it says assembled in memory, then downloaded instead. A simple scene comes out far smaller than the estimate: the one in the last figure of this page was estimated at 92 kB and made 5 kB. Chrome and Edge ask where to save the file before they start, and write it as it is encoded, so a long video never has to fit in memory. Other browsers keep the whole file in memory and download it at the end; the dialog tells which will happen, and warns when the estimate goes over 1 GB. Specks in H.264. Chrome’s own H.264 encoder can leave a few pixels of a moving object behind it: small dots that stay until the next keyframe, one every two seconds. For an MP4 without them, choose Companion · ffmpeg, which encodes H.264 with x264. Without a companion, WebM · VP9 leaves far fewer."],["companion","The editor draws the same frames and sends them to the companion, a small program on your machine, whose ffmpeg encodes them. It adds formats the browser does not have. With a companion that has ffmpeg: the encoder (1), its format (2), its quality (3) and how large a video this companion writes (4). Format What it is for MP4 · H.264 The usual file for the web and for sharing. ffmpeg encodes it with x264. WebM · VP9 An open format for the web. MOV · ProRes 422 HQ A large, high-quality file to edit in a video editor. GIF A looping picture without sound. At most 50 frames a second and 1920 pixels on a side. A long or large GIF can run over the companion’s limit: it then refuses the job, and the dialog shows why. Quality — Low (small file), Medium or High (large file) — sets how much the encoder keeps. MOV and GIF have no such choice. Under the quality the dialog says how large a video this companion writes: Up to 4 GiB per video: the companion’s limit. Start it with --max-video-gib to give it another. A video that passes the limit is never saved cut short: the export fails and says so, and nothing is written. The companion also stops an export when its disk is nearly full. How large a video can be has both limits and how to change them. Two things must be in place: the companion running, and ffmpeg on its machine. When either is missing the option says which. The page about the companion shows how to start it and how to check that ffmpeg is found."],["manim","The editor exports the Python scene (the same one as Export ▸ Python) and the companion has your own Manim render it: the video is Manim’s, frame for frame. Render with Manim (1): the library to use (2) and a range that is always the whole scene (3). The Manim list offers ManimCE (Community Edition) and ManimGL (3b1b). For a library the companion has no Python for, the dialog says so in red and Export stays off. When it is not the library the project is made for, the dialog says that the exported scene is converted. The size and the frame rate are the ones of the dialog. The range is always the whole scene: the exported Python has no range. It runs code. The Python that Manim renders is generated by the editor, but it is still run on your machine, so the companion keeps this off until you start it with --allow-manim-render. On Linux with bubblewrap it runs in a sandbox with no network; elsewhere the dialog warns that Manim runs without a sandbox: the exported Python code gets all your permissions. While it works, the dialog says Manim CE is rendering (animation 2) with the frame it has reached. A render that takes longer than ten minutes is stopped; the companion’s --manim-timeout sets the limit. A formula on the timeline that does not render holds this encoder back: it needs the LaTeX of every formula to be right, and says which to fix in the Creator. The video ends with the last animation. Sound that goes on after it is cut there, and the dialog says by how much."],["size","Everything custom. The warning (1) is about the shape of the picture; the summary (2) says what the file will be. Size: Project uses the size of the project, the Resolution of File ▸ Project settings…. The presets are 720p (1280 × 720), 1080p (1920 × 1080), 1440p (2560 × 1440) and 2160p (3840 × 2160). Custom… takes a width up to 7680 and a height up to 4320, each at least 16. Both must be even, except for a GIF. Frame rate: the project’s (also in Project settings…), 24, 25, 30 or 60, or a number of your own from 1 to 240. A GIF takes at most 50. Range: Whole scene (its length in seconds), or Custom with a start and an end in seconds. Render with Manim always uses the whole scene. The frame of the scene is always 8 units tall. A picture of another shape than the project’s shows more or less space at the sides than the Preview does, and the dialog warns you."],["sound","A project with sound clips gets an option at the top of the dialog, Include audio · volume curves and track mute. It is on by default. The sound is the same mix as Export ▸ Audio… (Audio): the volume lines and the track mutes are applied. The option (1); in Chrome on Linux, the warning that comes with an MP4 (2); and, with a companion running, the button that goes to its ffmpeg (3). Browser. WebM carries Opus. MP4 carries AAC where the browser has an AAC encoder (Chrome and Edge on Windows and macOS). Where it has none — Chrome on Linux — the MP4 carries Opus: a valid file that browsers and players such as VLC play, and some players and video editors do not. The dialog says so before you export. If a companion with an AAC encoder is running, a button under the warning, Use Companion · ffmpeg for AAC (MP4 · H.264), switches to Companion · ffmpeg with MP4 in one click. Companion · ffmpeg. The editor sends the mix with the frames and ffmpeg encodes it into the file: AAC in MP4 and MOV, Opus in WebM. A GIF has no sound. Render with Manim. ffmpeg adds the mix, as AAC, to the video Manim renders. That video ends with the last animation, so sound that goes on longer is cut there. Length. An export mixes an hour of sound at most. A longer video goes without it, and the dialog says so. The companion needs to be version 1.5 or newer, with an ffmpeg that has the encoder, to add sound; the dialog says what is missing."],["saving","After the small export of the Try it, through a browser that downloads: the estimate made before it (1), and the line that names the file and its size (2), with Download again under it. The file is named after the project: The square of a sum.mp4. In Chrome and Edge a window asks where to save it before the export starts, and the dialog ends by saying Written to The square of a sum.mp4 and its size. Elsewhere the browser downloads the file when it is done, and the dialog says Saved and offers Download again. While it runs, the dialog shows what it is doing — Encoding, Sending frames, Mixing audio — and a progress bar with the frame it has reached. Cancel export stops it and throws the partial file away. Close says Cancel and close while it runs. When the button says Saving…, the file is being closed: that cannot be cancelled, and closing the dialog only hides it."],["messages","Message What it means Waiting for a formula to render (name)… A formula on the timeline is still being drawn. Export stays off until it is done, so that no old picture is filmed. name does not render with its current LaTeX: the video draws its last good render. Browser and ffmpeg use the last picture that worked. Render with Manim cannot: fix the formula in the Creator. name is empty and will be missing from the video. A formula with no content has nothing to film. The companion is not running. Start it: … The two companion encoders need it. The line gives the command (Starting it). Rendering with Manim is off. Restart the companion with --allow-manim-render… Render with Manim is off until the companion is started with that option. ffmpeg is not installed on the companion’s machine (or not on PATH). Install ffmpeg where the companion runs. The health command tells how. Up to 4 GiB per video: the companion’s limit. Start it with --max-video-gib to give it another. How large a video Companion · ffmpeg may write. The number is the one of the companion that is running. This companion is older than 1.7: a video over 4 GiB may come back cut short, with no warning. Update companion/mfe_companion.py. An older companion did not check the size of what it wrote. Use the mfe_companion.py of this version. The video is larger than 4 GiB, the most this companion writes for one video, so it was stopped… The export failed at the companion’s limit, and nothing was saved. Export a shorter range, a smaller size or a lower quality, or restart the companion with a higher limit (How large a video can be). The disk of the companion’s temporary folder (…) is nearly full: less than 1 GiB is free there, so the job was stopped… The companion keeps 1 GiB of that disk free. Free some room in the folder the message names, or start the companion with TMPDIR naming a folder on a larger disk."]],"short":"Video"},{"id":"manim/python","url":"manim/python.html","title":"The generated Python","section":"Manim","level":"intermediate","description":"Read an exported scene line by line, run it with ManimCE or ManimGL, and change it afterwards.","headings":[["export","Getting the file"],["what","What the file holds"],["header","The header"],["scene","The scene"],["formulas","The formulas"],["steps","The timeline: plays and waits"],["video","What it makes"],["glyphs","How the scene finds the glyphs"],["exact","Exact indices"],["positional","The positional helper"],["running","Running it"],["changing","Changing it afterwards"],["faithful","How close it is to the Preview"]],"chunks":[[null,"Every project exports as a plain Manim scene that you can read, run and change. This page reads one scene line by line, runs it, and says what is safe to edit."],["export","Choose Export ▸ Python — ManimCE… or Export ▸ Python — ManimGL…. The Export… button next to the menus opens the last one you used, and its arrow lists every export (Exporting compares them). The dialog for the scene of this page. Scene name: the name of the Python class, and of the file. It must be a valid class name that the generated code does not use itself; when it is not, the dialog suggests a safe spelling. Always use the positional helper: how the scene finds the glyphs of a formula (further down). Comments (formulas, timing): the comment lines that name each formula and say which seconds each step covers. Turn them off for a shorter file. The notes list what the code cannot guarantee. An empty list says The scene reproduces the preview: exact glyph indices, whole frames., or, with the positional helper ticked, The scene reproduces the preview: glyphs matched by position when it runs, whole frames. The code, to read. Copy puts the code on the clipboard, and Download SceneToRead.py saves it. While a formula of the timeline is still being drawn, the dialog says Waiting for a formula to render and gives no code, so that it never exports a partial scene. Copy and Download stay off while a formula has no render of its current LaTeX; the Creator shows the error. Should the editor fail to write the scene, the dialog says so where the code would be, The scene could not be written as Python (…), with nothing to copy or download. It is a fault of the editor, not of your scene; the project is as it was, so save it, and fixing the problems it has may get around the fault."],["what","The file is a normal Manim scene. This is the exported file of the scene above, with the tabs for both libraries; the rest of the section reads the ManimCE tab. Play the scene above and watch its strip: the first formula writes itself during seconds 0 to 1, the first transformation runs from 2 to 3, the second from 4 to 5. The comments of the code name the same seconds."],["header","# Scene to read — exported by Manim Formula Editor for ManimCE 0.22. # Render at 1920×1080, 60 fps: # manim -r 1920,1080 --fps 60 SceneToRead.py SceneToRead # Every play and wait lasts a whole number of frames at 60 fps, so the video has # exactly 360 frames, like the editor's video export. from manim import * The first line of the comment names the project and the library it is written for (ManimCE 0.22 here; ManimGL 1.7 for the other export). The next two give the size and frame rate of the project and the command that renders the scene at them. Two more lines, which come with the option Comments (formulas, timing) (on by default), say that every play and wait lasts a whole number of frames and how many the video has: the scene lasts 6 seconds at 60 frames a second, which is 360 frames, exactly the frames of the timeline. When the project has sound that goes on after the last animation, the end of that comment says so instead (again only with Comments on): The editor’s video export is longer (… frames): it lasts until the project’s audio ends, and this scene has no audio. The Python is the picture alone."],["scene","class SceneToRead(Scene): def construct(self): self.camera.background_color = \"#000000\" One Scene class holds everything. Its name is the one in the dialog, and the project’s background colour (File ▸ Project settings…) is set first. A scene with a camera is a MovingCameraScene in ManimCE, and each camera move is a play of its own among the others."],["formulas","Each formula that the timeline uses is built once, before anything plays. Here is the first one: # step1: 2x + 1 = 7 step1 = MathTex(r\"2x + 1 = 7\", font_size=96) step1_g = _glyphs(step1, [ (-2.7467, 0.0475, 0.5264, 0.8786), (-2.0488, -0.1075, 0.6425, 0.5976), (-0.8553, -0.0613, 0.8786, 0.8773), (0.2875, 0.0475, 0.4538, 0.8786), (1.4904, -0.062, 0.8786, 0.3087), (2.7263, 0.0396, 0.5673, 0.9208), ]) step1_g[1].set_color(\"#F4C867\") step1.move_to([-1.0921, 0, 0]) step1.set_z_index(1) The comment is the formula’s name in the editor, and its LaTeX. MathTex is Manim’s class for a formula, with the LaTeX as a raw string and the formula’s size as font_size. A formula that is on the timeline twice gets one object per clip (step1, step1_2). step1_g is the list of the formula’s glyphs, numbered as the editor numbers them (below). In 2x + 1 = 7, glyph 1 is the x. set_color gives that glyph the colour it has in the editor. move_to is where the clip puts the formula, in scene units: the origin is the middle of the frame, y points up, and the frame is 8 units tall. set_z_index is the layer. The front track of the timeline gets the higher number, so a formula in front is drawn over one behind it."],["steps","# 0 s – 1 s: step1 write self.play(Write(VGroup(*step1_g[:])), run_time=1) # 1 s – 2 s self.wait(1) # 2 s – 3 s: step1 fade_out, step1 → step2 self.play( FadeOut(VGroup(step1_g[2], step1_g[3])), ReplacementTransform(step1_g[0], step2_g[0]), ReplacementTransform(step1_g[1], step2_g[1]), ReplacementTransform(step1_g[4], step2_g[2]), ReplacementTransform(step1_g[5], step2_g[3]), run_time=1, ) The timeline is cut at every moment when something starts or stops. A stretch with nothing running is self.wait(…); a stretch with animations is one self.play(…) that holds all of them, so they run together. The comment above each line says which seconds it covers and what is in it. Every duration is a whole number of frames, so the scene lasts exactly as long as the editor’s own video. What each line is, and where it comes from in the editor: In the code In the editor Write(VGroup(*step1_g[:])) The Write intro of the first clip, on every glyph of the formula. ReplacementTransform(step1_g[0], step2_g[0]) A pair of a relation: glyph 0 of step1 becomes glyph 0 of step2. FadeOut(VGroup(step1_g[2], step1_g[3])) The glyphs of the source that no pair takes (the + and the 1) leave with the clip’s outro. FadeTransform(step2_g[3], step3_g[2]) A pair whose style is fade, in the next relation. self.wait(1) A stretch with no animation. When a formula comes on a track in front of the one it came from, its glyphs are moved to the new layer just before they move, so that they are drawn in front all the way: step2_g[1].set_z_index(1) step2_g[2].set_z_index(1) step2_g[3].set_z_index(1)"],["video","The video that ManimCE renders from the file above, at 480p and 15 frames a second to keep it small:"],["glyphs","A transformation names glyphs: this one becomes that one. The editor numbers the glyphs of a formula in the order its renderer gives them. Manim numbers them in its own order, and the two can differ: MathJax puts the bar of a fraction after its numerator and denominator, for example. There are two ways to keep them in step."],["exact","A formula that the editor drew with the companion has Manim’s own glyphs, in Manim’s order. The scene then names them directly, by their place in the formula (in ManimCE the glyphs sit one level down, so the index starts with [0]). This is the scene of the example below, exported for the library it was drawn for: # sum: 1+2+3+4+5+6 sum = MathTex(r\"1+2+3+4+5+6\", font_size=60) sum.move_to([0, 0.6, 0]) frame = SurroundingRectangle(sum[0][:5], buff=0.15, color=\"#FFFF00\", corner_radius=0.12).set_fill(\"#FFFF00\") There is no helper in the file, and sum[0][:5] means the first five glyphs. This is the shortest, most readable form of the scene."],["positional","A formula that the editor drew with MathJax, which is what you get without the companion, cannot promise Manim’s order. The scene then carries a small function that finds the glyphs when it runs: def _glyphs(tex, layout): \"\"\"The glyphs of `tex` in the editor's order. That order (MathJax's, or another template's) can differ from Manim's, so the glyphs are matched by position: `layout` holds each glyph's (x, y, width, height) relative to the formula centre, divided by the formula height, and every real glyph is assigned to one entry with the least total distance. Entries without a counterpart get an empty VMobject.\"\"\" from scipy.optimize import linear_sum_assignment real = tex.family_members_with_points() glyphs = [VMobject() for _ in layout] if len(real) != len(layout): print(f\"Warning: Manim made {len(real)} glyphs of a formula the editor split into \" f\"{len(layout)}; unmatched glyphs are not animated.\") if not real or not layout: return glyphs # nothing to match (a formula without ink, such as \\quad) centre, height = tex.get_center(), tex.get_height() if height < 1e-6: height = 1.0 # a flat mobject (a rule): the editor's layout is divided by 1 then too have = np.array([ [*((g.get_center() - centre)[:2] / height), g.get_width() / height, g.get_height() / height] for g in real ]) want = np.array(layout, dtype=float) cost = np.linalg.norm(want[:, None, :] - have[None, :, :], axis=2) for i, j in zip(*linear_sum_assignment(cost)): glyphs[i] = real[j] return glyphs Each formula brings a list, step1_g = _glyphs(step1, [ … ]), with one entry of four numbers per glyph: where that glyph sits in the editor’s drawing, relative to the centre of the formula and divided by its height, with its width and its height. When the scene runs, _glyphs asks Manim for the glyphs it really built and pairs each with the closest entry of the list, so that the total distance is as small as it can be. After that, step1_g[1] is the editor’s glyph 1, whatever Manim’s own number for it is. What you need. Nothing beyond Manim. The matching uses scipy, which both ManimCE and ManimGL need and install with themselves; the function imports it only when the scene runs. A plain Manim install is enough. When it is there. For every formula drawn by MathJax; for a formula drawn for the other library than the one you export for; and for every formula when you tick Always use the positional helper, which is also the safer choice when the scene must work across Manim versions and TeX templates. A scene made only of exact formulas has no helper at all. A line or an arrow never uses it: its parts are the same in both libraries, and the scene names them through the line itself. What it says. With the tick off, the dialog lists each formula that uses it, with the reason; with the tick on, every formula uses it, the notes are not written and the line of an empty list says that the glyphs are matched by position. If Manim builds a different number of glyphs than the editor split the formula into, the scene prints Warning: Manim made N glyphs of a formula the editor split into M; unmatched glyphs are not animated. when it runs. Which formulas those are, and what the video then shows, is on Without the companion. To get exact indices, run the companion before you export, and let the formulas render with it: the badge in the menu bar says Renderer: LaTeX when they did. Download the project of the card above (A box and a brace) and open it: choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… and pick the file. Choose Export ▸ Python — ManimCE… and read the notes: there is one, which says that the scene reproduces the Preview. Tick Always use the positional helper and watch the code: the helper _glyphs appears at its top, and the glyphs are named through lists such as sum_g[:5] instead of sum[0][:5]. (In a project whose formulas were drawn by MathJax, like the scene at the top of this page, the code does not change when you tick it: every formula already uses the helper, and only the notes about matching by position go away.)"],["running","You need Manim, and a LaTeX installation that Manim can find, because Manim typesets formulas with LaTeX. The page ManimCE and ManimGL has the links to install each. Save the code as SceneToRead.py (the dialog’s Download does) and run the command of its header, from the folder that holds it: # ManimCE manim -r 1920,1080 --fps 60 SceneToRead.py SceneToRead # ManimGL manimgl SceneToRead.py SceneToRead -w -r 1920x1080 --fps 60 ManimCE writes the video to media/videos/SceneToRead/1080p60/SceneToRead.mp4. For a quick look, take -r … and --fps … out of the command and use the low quality: manim -pql SceneToRead.py SceneToRead renders at 480p and 15 frames a second and opens the result (-p). A quality flag added to the command above changes nothing, because -r and --fps come after it; lowering those two numbers works too. ManimGL writes the video into a videos folder with -w. Without -w it opens a window that plays the scene. For a quick look, leave -r out and add -l (480p): manimgl SceneToRead.py SceneToRead -w -l. The scene is written for the frame rate of its header: its durations are whole frames at that rate. Render it with the same --fps to get the editor’s timing to the frame."],["changing","The file is yours: read it, change it, build on it. A few edits are safe and common. Rename the scene. Change the class name and the command in the header comment, or pick the name in the dialog before you download. Colours. Change the strings of set_color(…) and of self.camera.background_color. Add your own animations between the self.play and self.wait lines of the scene, not inside them: # 3 s – 4 s self.wait(1) self.play(Indicate(step2_g[1])) # yours What you add takes time of its own: the video gets longer than the editor’s timeline. Add sound. The exported Python has none: its dialog says Audio is omitted from Manim exports. Manim can add a file at any moment of the scene, with self.add_sound(\"narration.mp3\"), in both libraries. To get the sound mixed as it plays in the editor, export a video with sound instead (Exporting video). Leave the glyph lists (_glyphs(step1, [ … ])) and the helper alone: they carry the editor’s drawing of each formula, and the glyph numbers of the animations rest on them. Exporting again writes a new file; it does not merge with your edits. Make the changes to the animation in the editor, where they can be repeated, and keep to the Python for what the editor cannot do."],["faithful","The scene is built to reproduce what the Preview shows: the same glyphs in the same places and colours at every frame, with the timing of the timeline in whole frames. The editor’s own tests check it: they export scenes, render them with ManimCE and ManimGL, and compare the number of frames and the glyphs drawn in every frame with the editor’s. What the code cannot promise is in the dialog’s notes: formulas matched by position, glyph counts that differ between MathJax and LaTeX, packages MathJax does not know, hidden tracks (exported anyway), an export for the other library than the project’s, and animations that were shorter than a frame and had to be stretched to one. A scene exported for ManimGL is not frame-for-frame the scene exported for ManimCE; the next page says where they differ. Next: Without the companion, where matching by position falls short; ManimCE and ManimGL; and the companion for exact glyphs."]],"short":"The Python"},{"id":"manim/without-companion","url":"manim/without-companion.html","title":"Without the companion","section":"Manim","level":"intermediate","description":"What the editor does opened from disk with no companion, what the companion would add, and the one place its absence shows: formulas that Manim splits into other glyphs.","headings":[["from-disk","Opened with a double click"],["adds","What the companion adds"],["python","Where it shows: the exported Python"],["differ","What makes the numbers differ"],["video","What the video shows"],["glyph-by-glyph","A formula that comes in glyph by glyph"],["whole","A formula that comes in whole"],["dialog","Why the export does not warn you"],["storage","Your work, opened from disk"]],"chunks":[[null,"You can open the editor with a double click and never start the companion. This page says what you have then, what the companion would add, and the one place where its absence shows in what you make: the Manim video of a few formulas."],["from-disk","The built editor opens from app/dist/index.html (Getting the app running): no server, no companion and no network. Keep the whole dist/ folder together: the fonts, Chroma Lab and the licence files are beside the page. Opened so, the editor does all of its own work: formulas, drawn by MathJax; the timeline with every block, relation and path, the camera, braces, shapes and lines; projects opened and saved in place (in Chrome and Edge), the autosave, the templates, the palettes and Chroma Lab, and the examples of Help ▸ Examples; every export the browser makes by itself: Python for ManimCE and ManimGL, video (MP4 and WebM), the frame as a PNG image, audio, and the scene as a web page (Exporting)."],["adds","What you have without it, and what it gives once it runs (The companion says how to start it): Without the companion With it The glyphs of a formula MathJax’s: the same formula, at times split into other glyphs than Manim’s (below). The menu bar says Renderer: MathJax. LaTeX’s, which are Manim’s own: Renderer: LaTeX. Accented letters in a LaTeX text Drawn without their accents. Drawn; for ManimCE the editor suggests the fontenc line. A LaTeX text with a width Its lines are broken about where LaTeX would break them, and the text says so. LaTeX breaks them. Font text Placeholder boxes, one a character, composed, placed, coloured and animated all the same. The outlines of the font (shaped on Linux in version 1.0). Video The browser’s encoders: MP4 (H.264, AV1) and WebM (VP9). ffmpeg too: x264, ProRes, GIF. AAC sound in an MP4 Only where the browser encodes AAC (not Chrome on Linux); WebM with Opus otherwise. ffmpeg encodes it. Render with Manim Not offered: the video dialog greys it out and says why. The exported scene, rendered by your own Manim."],["python","Inside the editor a formula drawn by MathJax is as good as one drawn by LaTeX: it is coloured, animated and saved the same. The exported scene is another matter, because there the glyphs are Manim’s, and the script has to find, when it runs, which glyph of Manim is each glyph of the editor. It finds them by where they are, with the positional helper. That works when MathJax and LaTeX split the formula into the same number of glyphs, in whatever order. Measured on 862 formulas (the 257 templates, 338 formulas of the author’s own projects and 267 written to cover the cases), 9 in 10 are split into the same number, and among those the helper’s pairing was right in 771 of 777. It does not work for the rest, and those are a short list of things."],["differ","In the formula MathJax draws LaTeX (and Manim) draws \\cdots, \\ldots, \\dots, \\vdots, \\ddots one glyph three dots \\left|…\\right|, vmatrix, \\middle| around something tall one bar several pieces, stacked \\iint, \\iiint, \\oint one glyph two or three (ManimGL draws \\iint as one) \\neq, \\ne, \\notin, \\not= one glyph a stroke over = or ∈: two \\Longrightarrow, \\iff, \\longmapsto one long arrow pieces A tall \\sqrt; \\cancel, \\boxed, \\underbrace, \\xrightarrow, \\overrightarrow other pieces other pieces Of the measured formulas that differ, \\cdots and its kin are the most common (24 of 79), then the tall bars (18) and \\neq and \\notin (13)."],["video","Take 1+2+\\cdots+n turning into 1+2+\\cdots+n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2} through a relation of seven pairs, exported with no companion. MathJax draws the three dots as one glyph, so the editor splits the sum into seven glyphs; Manim makes nine of it, and the script reaches seven of them. The two dots it does not reach are left out of every animation of glyphs. What the video shows then depends on how the formula comes in. ManimCE 0.22.1 rendered the videos below; ManimGL 1.7.2 does the same."],["glyph-by-glyph","Here the sum writes itself in. The Write of the scene plays the sum’s list of glyphs, the seven the script reached: self.play(Write(VGroup(*step1_g[:])), run_time=1) A formula that comes in so, glyph by glyph (Write, Create, any intro with a lag), is drawn without the two dots that are in no list: one dot of three, from the first frame to the last. Rendered by ManimCE: only the middle dot, in the sum and in the equation it turns into, from the first frame to the last."],["whole","With no intro, or a fade, the scene adds the formula whole, and its three dots are there: self.add(step1) When its glyphs go to the next formula, the relation moves the seven the script reached, and two of the dots stay where they were. This is the same scene with no intro on the sum, and centred, so that its glyphs travel to the equation: Rendered by ManimCE: three dots until the sum moves; then one goes with it, and two stay behind, on the n and in the equals sign of the equation. Either way the scene says so when it runs, in a line for each such formula. For the sum it prints Warning: Manim made 9 glyphs of a formula the editor split into 7; unmatched glyphs are not animated."],["dialog","The export dialog cannot say it sooner. With no companion it has no LaTeX render to count against, so every formula drawn by MathJax gets the same note there, the ones that will come out right and the others alike: Glyphs of \"step1\" are matched by position when the scene runs, because it was rendered with MathJax, whose glyph order can differ from LaTeX. Render with the companion online for exact indices. Nothing in the editor warns of the things of the list above while you type them. The way out is the companion. With it online the formulas are rendered by LaTeX, the script names Manim’s glyphs by their index, with no helper (Exact indices), and all of them are drawn. Start it before you export, and let the formulas render with it: the badge of the menu bar says Renderer: LaTeX when they did (Is it working?). Download the project of the first card above and open it: choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… and pick the file. Choose Export ▸ Python — ManimCE… and read the notes: each formula has the same one, whatever it holds. Download the scene and run it with ManimCE (Running it). The terminal prints the warning above, and the video has one dot of three."],["storage","Chrome gives every page opened from a file the same storage. The autosave, your templates, your palettes and the recordings you imported live in that browser profile, shared with any other page opened from disk. Clearing the site’s data clears them too. A project saved to a file is what keeps the work (Projects and files). A companion you start later is found by itself, but it refuses a page opened from a file unless it was started with --allow-file-origin (the editor shows the command), or serves the build itself: serve --app app/dist, then open http://127.0.0.1:7799/app/ (Starting it; Security says what the first option costs). Next: ManimCE and ManimGL, and the companion for exact glyphs."]],"short":"Without the companion"},{"id":"manim/ce-vs-gl","url":"manim/ce-vs-gl.html","title":"ManimCE and ManimGL","section":"Manim","level":"advanced","description":"The two Manims the editor exports to: what differs in the code it writes and in what you see, which one to use, and how to install each.","headings":[["two","Two Manims"],["choosing","Which one to use"],["differences","What differs in the code"],["result","What differs in what you see"],["converting","Exporting for the other library"],["installing","Installing each"],["running","Running the same scene in both"]],"chunks":[[null,"The editor exports for two versions of Manim. They share one idea and differ in many details: what the code looks like, how some animations move, how a video is timed. This page says where, helps you choose, and links to the installation of each."],["two","ManimCE is Manim Community Edition: the version maintained by a community of contributors, with documentation of its own. You install it with pip install manim and write from manim import *. The editor writes for ManimCE 0.22. ManimGL is the version 3Blue1Brown writes his videos with. It draws with OpenGL and can play a scene in an interactive window. You install it with pip install manimgl and write from manimlib import *. The editor writes for ManimGL 1.7. The editor’s Preview mirrors each of them: its easing curves, its lags and its sizes follow the library of the project. You set that in File ▸ Project settings…, under Manim target: ManimCE (Community Edition), the default, or ManimGL (3b1b). The arrow of the Export… button offers both libraries whatever the project’s is, and the editor converts what differs. Switch the tabs above to compare the two files. The rest of the page is a guide to what you see."],["choosing","If… Use You already work with one of them That one. Export for it, and the scene runs in the project you have. You have neither and want to start ManimCE: a new project is made for it, so it is the library the Preview mirrors by default. You follow 3Blue1Brown’s code, or want an interactive window to play with the scene ManimGL. You are not sure Export both. The two files come from one project, and the dialogs say what each will do. You have decided Set the Manim target of the project to that library: the Preview then moves as it will, and the export needs no conversion. For the formulas and the transformations the two give the same picture. The differences below are in details of the code and in a few effects."],["differences","The table shows the same scenes exported twice. The first six rows come from the scene of The generated Python, the next four from the scene above. What ManimCE ManimGL The import from manim import * from manimlib import * The command in the header manim -r 1920,1080 --fps 60 SceneToRead.py SceneToRead manimgl SceneToRead.py SceneToRead -w -r 1920x1080 --fps 60 The background self.camera.background_color = \"#000000\", in construct default_camera_config = dict(background_color=\"#000000\"), in the class A formula MathTex(r\"2x + 1 = 7\", font_size=96) Tex(r\"2x + 1 = 7\", font_size=98.9888) The intro Write Write(VGroup(*step1_g[:])) Write(VGroup(*step1_g[:]), stroke_color=\"#FFFFFF\") One glyph leaving FadeOut(step2_g[0]) FadeOut(VGroup(step2_g[0])) The intro Create Create(frame) ShowCreation(VGroup(frame_g[0])) A rounded box SurroundingRectangle(sum[0][:5], buff=0.15, color=\"#FFFF00\", corner_radius=0.12) SurroundingRectangle(VGroup(*sum_g[:5]), buff=0.15, color=\"#FFFF00\").round_corners(0.12) A brace and its colour Brace(frame, DOWN, buff=0.2, color=\"#58C4DD\") Brace(frame, DOWN, buff=0.2), then under.set_color(\"#58C4DD\") A size font_size=60 font_size=61.868 And a few more that the examples above do not show: What ManimCE ManimGL A glyph by its exact index poly[0][3:5].set_color(\"#FFFF00\") poly[3:5].set_color(\"#FFFF00\") A Copy pair ReplacementTransform(f1_g[0].copy(), f2_g[0]) TransformFromCopy(f1_g[0], f2_g[0]) A LaTeX text Tex(r\"A brace on text\", font_size=40, color=\"#83C167\") TexText(r\"A brace on text\", font_size=40), then title.set_color(\"#83C167\") The size of a font text font_size=56 font_size=82.1968, which is 56 × 1.4678 The alignment of a font text None written. alignment='LEFT' The outro Unwrite Unwrite(…), Manim’s own _Unwrite(…), a small class in the file that plays a Write backwards: ManimGL has no Unwrite A scene with a camera class Pinned(MovingCameraScene): class Pinned(Scene):, whose frame can move already The size. At the same font_size a ManimGL formula comes out slightly smaller than a ManimCE one, by a factor of about 1.031. When you export a project for the other library the editor multiplies the size by that factor (96 becomes 98.9888), so the formula keeps the size you see in the Preview. Glyph references. A formula that the editor drew exactly (with the companion) is named by index in the library it was drawn for: sum[0][:5] in the ManimCE file above. A formula drawn for the other library, or by MathJax, goes through the positional helper, which both libraries use the same way (How the scene finds the glyphs). In the scene above the ManimCE file is exact and the ManimGL file has the helper, for that reason. Groups. ManimGL counts a group as one more slot in the stagger of an animation, so the exported code wraps even a single glyph in VGroup(…) to keep the animation as the Preview draws it. ManimCE takes the glyph as it is. Packages and preamble. The LaTeX packages of a formula and the project’s LaTeX preamble (in File ▸ Project settings…) go through a TexTemplate in ManimCE and through additional_preamble in ManimGL."],["result","The Preview draws the behaviour of the project’s Manim target, and the editor’s tests compare scenes exported for each library with the frames Manim makes. A scene exported for the other library keeps your timeline but takes that library’s own easing curves and default lags, so its motion differs slightly from the Preview. These are the differences you can notice: Where ManimCE ManimGL The smooth easing A sigmoid curve. A quintic polynomial. The same name, a slightly different ease. The stagger of a Write over n glyphs Lag ratio min(4/max(1, n), 0.2). Lag ratio min(4/(n + 1), 0.2), and the group itself takes one more slot of the stagger. The outline of a Write Each glyph is traced in its own colour. Every glyph is traced in one colour, which the exported call names (stroke_color). Fade in with a scale Starts at the scale you set. Starts at its inverse. The frames of a video Each play is drawn at the start of every frame interval, as the editor’s video is. Each play is drawn at the end of every frame interval: every instant shows one frame later, and the video ends on the final state. A brace Honours the sharpness you set. Draws the brace from a font and ignores the sharpness; the dialog says so. A font text Comes out about 1.47 times as large at the same size. Its lines are 0.975 em apart whatever the font, and a tab is four spaces. Spaces its lines by the font’s own line height and has tab stops of its own. The editor converts what it can: the size of a font text is converted from the library that shaped it, and the sizes of formulas as above. What it cannot convert is listed in the notes of the export dialog."],["converting","Choose Export ▸ Python — ManimCE… or Export ▸ Python — ManimGL…: both are always on offer, whatever the Manim target of the project is. The two dialogs of the same project, made for ManimCE: The scene for the library it was drawn for: the notes (1) say that it reproduces the Preview. The same scene for the other library: the notes (1) say that the font sizes are converted, that the glyphs are matched by position, and that the motion differs slightly from the Preview. The first note of the second dialog says it plainly: The project targets ManimCE; exporting to ManimGL converts the font sizes of formulas and LaTeX texts (× 1.0311) and matches their glyphs by position. Easing curves and default lag ratios follow ManimGL, so the motion differs slightly from the preview. Read the notes of every export: they are the list of what a converted scene does not promise. Download the project above and open it: choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… and pick the file. Choose Export ▸ Python — ManimGL… and read its notes. Close the dialog. Choose File ▸ Project settings…, set Manim target to ManimGL (3b1b) and press Apply. Export for ManimGL again: the note that says the project targets ManimCE is gone. Export for ManimCE instead, and the same kind of note comes up the other way round."],["installing","Follow the official instructions for your system: they cover the libraries Manim needs beyond Python. Both need a LaTeX installation with dvisvgm, because Manim typesets formulas with LaTeX. ManimGL also needs ffmpeg to write a video file; ManimCE brings its own, through the av package it installs. ManimCE 0.22 ManimGL 1.7 Python 3.11 or newer 3.10 or newer Install python -m pip install manim python -m pip install manimgl Official guide docs.manim.community 3b1b.github.io/manim Also needs LaTeX; on some systems Cairo and Pango LaTeX and ffmpeg; a graphics setup that supports OpenGL Put each in a virtual environment of its own. The editor’s companion takes the path of the Python of each environment (--ce-python and --gl-python) to render videos with them; see The companion."],["running","# ManimCE: writes media/videos/SceneToRead/1080p60/SceneToRead.mp4 manim -r 1920,1080 --fps 60 SceneToRead.py SceneToRead # ManimGL: writes the video into a videos folder (without -w it plays in a window) manimgl SceneToRead.py SceneToRead -w -r 1920x1080 --fps 60 Each command is in the header of the file exported for its library, already filled in with the size and the frame rate of the project. For a quick preview, take -r … and --fps … out and add the low-quality flag (ManimCE: -pql, which also opens the video; ManimGL: -l), or lower those two numbers: a quality flag added to the commands above changes nothing, because both libraries apply -r and --fps after it. ManimGL also plays the scene in its window when you leave out -w."]],"short":"CE and GL"},{"id":"manim/companion","url":"manim/companion.html","title":"The companion","section":"Manim","level":"advanced","description":"The optional local helper: exact LaTeX glyphs, font text, ffmpeg video and rendering with your Manim. How to start it, check it and use it safely.","headings":[["what","What it adds"],["start","Starting it"],["badge","Is it working?"],["health","Checking what you have: health"],["options","The options of serve"],["exact-glyphs","Exact glyphs"],["font-text","Font text"],["ffmpeg","ffmpeg"],["video-limit","How large a video can be"],["rendering","Rendering with Manim"],["security","Security"],["platforms","Linux, macOS and Windows"],["problems","When the editor does not see it"]],"chunks":[[null,"The editor works on its own. The companion is a small program that you start on your machine to give it more: exact LaTeX glyphs, font text, cleaner video, and rendering with your own Manim."],["what","Without the companion With it Formulas Drawn by MathJax in the browser: close to LaTeX, but its glyphs can come in another order than Manim’s. Compiled by LaTeX itself, the way Manim does it: Manim’s glyphs, in Manim’s order. Exported Python The scene finds the glyphs by position when it runs. The scene names the glyphs by index, with no helper (The generated Python). Font text Drawn as placeholder boxes in the Preview and in videos. Shaped by the Pango of your own Manim: its real glyphs. Linux for now. Video Encoded by the browser. Also by ffmpeg: MP4, WebM, ProRes and GIF, with sound. Manim Nothing: you run the exported Python yourself. Render with Manim runs it for you, with your own ManimCE or ManimGL. Writing, animating and exporting Python never needs it. Each row is an extra, and each one is on only when the companion has what it needs: health, below, tells what. Without the companion says what the editor does with none, and the one place where that shows in the result: formulas that Manim splits into other glyphs than MathJax. The editor and the companion talk over HTTP, on your machine only. The companion runs the other programs."],["start","The companion is one Python file, companion/mfe_companion.py in the repository. It uses only Python’s standard library and needs Python 3.10 or newer. For exact glyphs it also needs latex and dvisvgm on the PATH (TeX Live, MacTeX or MiKTeX); ffmpeg is optional. python3 companion/mfe_companion.py serve It prints one line, for example mfe-companion 1.7.0 listening on http://127.0.0.1:7799 (LaTeX sandbox: bubblewrap), and keeps running. Leave it so, and stop it with Ctrl+C in its terminal; on Linux and macOS, closing the terminal stops it in order too, and clears its temporary files. You do not tell the editor where it is. The editor looks at http://127.0.0.1:7799 every five seconds until it finds it, every fifteen once it has, and each time its window gets the focus. How you opened the editor decides whether it may talk to the companion: The editor is opened… Start the companion with With npm run dev (an address on localhost) serve From the built app/dist/index.html, as a file serve --allow-file-origin By the companion itself serve --app app/dist, then open http://127.0.0.1:7799/app/ The last one is the simplest: the companion serves the built app, and the app talks to it on its own address, whatever the port. The second option has a catch, explained under Security. To use your Manim, give the companion the Python of each environment that has it: python3 companion/mfe_companion.py serve --app app/dist \\ --ce-python ../manim_ce/venv/bin/python \\ --gl-python ../manim_gl/venv/bin/python \\ --allow-manim-render Font text needs the path of the library the project is made for (both, to check an export for the other one). Render with Manim needs the path of the library it renders with, and --allow-manim-render."],["badge","The right end of the menu bar says which renderer the formulas really use. The badge (1) with no companion running. Renderer: LaTeX: every formula is drawn from an exact LaTeX render. Renderer: MathJax: the formulas are drawn by MathJax. This is also what a new formula gets while no companion is there. Renderer: Pango: the only texts are font texts, shaped. Renderer: mixed: some formulas are exact and some are not yet, or a font text is still a row of boxes beside formulas. Hover the badge for the details: how many formulas are exact, how many use MathJax, and the state of the companion. With none running it says No companion at http://127.0.0.1:7799. Start it for exact LaTeX rendering: python3 companion/mfe_companion.py serve, with the command that fits how you opened the editor. With one running it gives its version, the tools it found (latex, dvisvgm, ffmpeg) and the Manims it can reach. Help ▸ About gives the version too. The three Glyph source items of the View menu (1): where the glyphs of formulas come from. Glyph source: Automatic uses the companion’s LaTeX whenever it is online, and MathJax otherwise. Glyph source: MathJax always uses MathJax, even with a companion. Glyph source: LaTeX (companion) asks for LaTeX only. When the companion comes up, the editor draws every formula again with LaTeX: the badge passes from Renderer: MathJax through mixed to Renderer: LaTeX within moments."],["health","When something of the companion does not work, start here. It does not listen on any port and changes nothing: python3 companion/mfe_companion.py health python3 companion/mfe_companion.py health --ce-python ../manim_ce/venv/bin/python The report has a section for each thing the companion does. A line starts with [ok] when the piece is there, [--] when it is missing and [!!] when it is there and cannot be used. Under each line that is not [ok] come what it costs and the command that gets it for your system. Here are two examples, with made-up versions and paths: the whole report of a Linux machine that has everything but ManimGL, and the part that matters in the report of a Mac without ffmpeg. Manim Formula Editor companion 1.7.0 Python 3.12.3 at /usr/bin/python3 (linux) LaTeX: the exact glyphs of formulas and LaTeX texts [ok] latex: pdfTeX 3.141592653-2.6-1.40.26 (TeX Live 2024) (/usr/bin/latex) [ok] dvisvgm: dvisvgm 3.2.2 (/usr/bin/dvisvgm) [ok] sandbox: bubblewrap (LaTeX reads nothing of yours but its own files) ffmpeg: Export > Video > Companion (ffmpeg), and the sound of Render with Manim [ok] ffmpeg: 6.1.1 (/usr/bin/ffmpeg) [ok] MP4 (H.264): libx264, sound aac [ok] WebM (VP9): libvpx-vp9, sound libopus [ok] MOV (ProRes): prores_ks, sound aac [ok] GIF: gif note: a video is written in /tmp (15.5 GiB free) while it is encoded, 4 GiB at most; `serve --max-video-gib N` gives another limit (1 to 64), and TMPDIR another folder. Manim: Render with Manim, and the shapes of font text [ok] ManimCE 0.22.1: /home/you/manim-ce/venv/bin/python [ ] ManimGL: not checked. Add --gl-python PATH (or set MFE_GL_PYTHON) to check it too. note: Render with Manim is off unless `serve` gets --allow-manim-render; it would run inside the bubblewrap sandbox. Font text: Text (font), shaped with the Pango of each Manim [ok] with ManimCE: 312 font families Everything that was checked is ready here. The same as the app reads it, in JSON: health --json ffmpeg: Export > Video > Companion (ffmpeg), and the sound of Render with Manim [--] ffmpeg: not found on PATH Without it the companion encodes no video; the app still exports with the browser's encoder. To get it: brew install ffmpeg Then open a new terminal, so that it is on PATH, and run this command again. 1 thing marked [--] or [!!] above: each says what it costs and how to get it. health --json prints what the editor itself reads from the companion. With no --ce-python or --gl-python the report checks LaTeX and ffmpeg and says how to ask for the rest. Run python3 companion/mfe_companion.py health in a terminal, from the folder of the repository. Read the sections. Fix the lines that start with [--] or [!!] with the commands under them, and run it again. Then run serve, open the editor, and watch the badge in the menu bar change from Renderer: MathJax to Renderer: LaTeX."],["options","Option What it does --app DIR Also serves the built app in DIR (app/dist) at http://127.0.0.1:<port>/app/. The app then reaches the companion on its own address. --allow-file-origin Also accepts the app opened from a file. Any web page can then reach the companion: see Security. Environment: MFE_ALLOW_FILE_ORIGIN=1. --ce-python PATH, --gl-python PATH The Python of an environment with ManimCE, and with ManimGL. For font text and Render with Manim. Environment: MFE_CE_PYTHON, MFE_GL_PYTHON. --allow-manim-render Turns on Render with Manim, which runs the Python code it receives. In a sandbox where one is available. Environment: MFE_ALLOW_MANIM_RENDER=1. --manim-timeout SECONDS The time limit of one render (600 by default). --max-video-gib N The largest video one export through ffmpeg may write, in GiB: a whole number from 1 to 64 (4 by default). A larger video fails and says so: see How large a video can be. --sandbox-gpu Lets the sandboxed Manim use the graphics card. Without it Mesa draws in software. Environment: MFE_SANDBOX_GPU=1. --no-sandbox Turns bubblewrap off: LaTeX, Render with Manim, the pass of ffmpeg that adds the sound to its video, and the font-text worker then run with all your permissions. Environment: MFE_NO_SANDBOX=1. --no-disk-cache Keeps the cache in memory only. Formulas are otherwise cached on disk, so each is compiled once. --port PORT The port (7799 by default). The editor looks at 7799, so change it only together with --app. -v, --verbose Logs every request."],["exact-glyphs","For each formula the companion writes the same TeX document that Manim writes (a MathTex for ManimCE, a Tex for ManimGL), compiles it with latex, converts it with dvisvgm, and sends the drawing back with one label per glyph. The editor draws that. So the glyphs are Manim’s own: their order, their shapes, their places. A LaTeX text (a Text (LaTeX) object) is compiled the same way, in text mode. A formula takes a moment the first time. The result is cached, so it does not again. A LaTeX error comes back with its message and its line, and the Creator shows it. The packages of a formula and the project’s LaTeX preamble (File ▸ Project settings…) are part of the document. Help ▸ Engine inspector… shows the glyphs a formula gets from each renderer, side by side, so you can see how MathJax and LaTeX differ for it."],["font-text","A font text is set by Manim’s own text engine, Pango. The companion asks it, through the Python you gave it, for the shape of every glyph, so that the editor draws the text as Manim will. It needs --ce-python or --gl-python, and a system that has Pango 1.50 or newer, Cairo, Fontconfig and ManimPango: that is Linux for now. It starts the first time a text needs it, never before. Without it a font text is a row of placeholder boxes. It still exports to Python, and Render with Manim draws the real glyphs. health has a Font text section that says whether it works."],["ffmpeg","With ffmpeg on the PATH of the machine that runs the companion, Export ▸ Video… offers Companion · ffmpeg: MP4 with x264, WebM with VP9, MOV with ProRes, and GIF, with the sound of the project in all of them but the GIF. health lists each format with the encoder it needs (libx264, libvpx-vp9, prores_ks, gif; for sound aac and libopus). All of this is in Exporting video."],["video-limit","While it is encoded, a video is written in the temporary folder of the machine that runs the companion. What a long video needs is room on that disk, not memory. Two limits keep an export from filling it: One video is at most 4 GiB. A larger one is never delivered cut short: the export fails, and the dialog says the limit and what you can do. To give the companion another limit, start it with --max-video-gib N, a whole number of GiB from 1 to 64, for example serve --max-video-gib 16. The Export video dialog shows the limit of the companion it found. 1 GiB of that disk stays free. An export is not begun, and one that is running is stopped, when less is left. This covers Render with Manim too, whose video has no size limit of its own. The message names the folder. To write the videos on another disk, start the companion with the environment variable TMPDIR naming a folder there: TMPDIR=/data/tmp python3 companion/mfe_companion.py serve --max-video-gib 16 Started with a limit that the disk of the folder cannot hold, the companion says so at once. health shows the folder, the room it has and the limit. On some Linux systems, Fedora among them, /tmp is kept in memory (a tmpfs, usually half the RAM). There a large video does take memory, and TMPDIR on a real disk is the way to export one. In Git Bash the line above works as it is, with a path such as TMPDIR=D:/tmp. In the Command Prompt, give the variable first: set TMPDIR=D:\\tmp, then the command."],["rendering","The editor sends the scene it would export as Python, and the companion has your Manim render it. It is off until you start the companion with --allow-manim-render and give it at least one of --ce-python and --gl-python. The code runs in a folder of its own, with a minimal environment, and the companion stops it after --manim-timeout seconds. On Linux with bubblewrap it runs in a sandbox: the file system read-only, your home folder empty, no network. Elsewhere it runs with all your permissions, and the dialog says so. The result is an MP4. With sound in the project, ffmpeg adds the mix to it as AAC, in a sandbox of the same kind whenever Manim ran in one."],["security","The companion runs programs on text that a web page sends, so it is locked down. What to know: It listens on 127.0.0.1 only, and refuses requests whose address is not 127.0.0.1 or localhost, and pages whose origin is not a local one. Only pages served from your own machine can use it. --allow-file-origin widens that. A page opened from a file has no origin, and neither does a frame sandboxed inside any website, so with the option on any web page can send requests to the companion. Serve the app with --app instead, and leave the option off. LaTeX runs without shell escape, in a temporary folder, for at most fifteen seconds. From TeX Live 2026 on, LaTeX can no longer be forbidden from reading files, so on Linux with bubblewrap the companion runs LaTeX in a sandbox that hides your home folder and has no network. Without bubblewrap — macOS, Windows, or Linux without it — LaTeX can read what you can, and the only protection is the origin rules above. serve says which case you are in when it starts. Render with Manim runs code. It is the reason for --allow-manim-render. Use it only with pages you trust. --app serves regular files under its folder, nothing else: no listing, no hidden files, no way out of the folder. It is a convenience for your own machine, not a web server."],["platforms","health gives the install command for your system. These are the ones it gives, and what differs. Install LaTeX and ffmpeg with your package manager, for example sudo apt install texlive texlive-latex-extra dvisvgm ffmpeg. Install bubblewrap to confine LaTeX and Render with Manim. Font text works here. Install MacTeX with brew install --cask mactex-no-gui and ffmpeg with brew install ffmpeg. There is no sandbox: use the companion with pages you trust. Version 1.0 shapes font text on Linux only: here it stays a row of boxes. The command is python companion/mfe_companion.py serve. Where no Python is installed, Windows answers python with the Microsoft Store’s alias, which runs nothing: use the launcher (py companion/mfe_companion.py serve) or the full name of an interpreter. Install MiKTeX for LaTeX and winget install Gyan.FFmpeg for ffmpeg. Write a path in Git Bash with / (--ce-python C:/manim/ce/venv/Scripts/python.exe). There is no sandbox, and version 1.0 shapes font text on Linux only. Stop the companion with Ctrl+C; closing its console window ends it at once and leaves its temporary folder (mfe-jobs-… in %TEMP%)."],["problems","The badge still says MathJax, and the tooltip says no companion. Is it running? Is the page opened the way the table above says? A built app opened as a file needs --allow-file-origin or --app. Cannot listen on 127.0.0.1:7799. Another program, or another companion, has the port. Stop it. It runs, but the formulas are still MathJax. Run health: a [--] on latex or dvisvgm means LaTeX is not on the path of the terminal that started it. Open a new terminal after installing it. Also look at the View menu: Glyph source: MathJax keeps MathJax even with a companion. A Manim is not found. health --ce-python PATH says why, and gives the command that shows the error. The video dialog greys out ffmpeg or Manim. It says why. Then health says how to put it right."]],"short":"The companion"},{"id":"reference/shortcuts","url":"reference/shortcuts.html","title":"Keyboard and mouse shortcuts","section":"Reference","level":"reference","description":"Every keyboard shortcut and mouse gesture of the editor, by what you are doing, with a filter and the page that teaches each one.","headings":[["keyboard","Keyboard"],["keys-files-and-history","Files and history"],["keys-playback","Playback"],["keys-editing","Editing"],["keys-preview-and-timeline","Preview and timeline"],["keys-dialogs-and-help","Dialogs and help"],["mouse","Mouse"],["sheet","The sheet in the app"]],"chunks":[[null,"Every key and mouse gesture of the editor, grouped by what you are doing, each with the page that teaches it. It is the sheet that Help ▸ Keyboard shortcuts… shows, and ? opens that sheet anywhere outside text fields and dialogs. The editor takes ⌘ where other systems take Ctrl, because a Ctrl+click is the secondary click on a Mac. Alt is ⌥ and Shift is ⇧, written before ⌘ as a Mac's menus write them (⇧⌘Z), and Enter is ↩. A few rows have keys of their own there, where the system keeps the usual ones: Save As (⇧⌘S), Sync folder (⌥⌘S), Distribute (⌃⌥H and ⌃⌥V, with the Control key) and the key that adds a formula of the list to the selection (⌘↩). A trackpad zooms with a pinch and moves the Preview's view with a scroll. The keys on this page are written for the system you read it on."],["keyboard","The keys joined by + are pressed together, and / or or separates alternatives. A bracket says what holding another key changes: (Shift: 0.1) is a bigger step. Where says where the key works; anywhere but text fields means that a text field with the focus takes the key as text, and outside fields and dialogs adds that an open dialog or menu keeps it for itself. On a keyboard of another script (Russian, Greek, Hebrew…), the letter of a shortcut is the key where a Latin keyboard has it: Ctrl+Z is the key of Я on a Russian layout. A Latin layout that puts the letters elsewhere, such as Dvorak or AZERTY, keeps its own: the shortcut is the key that types the letter. If you remember only a few: Space plays and pauses; ← and → step a frame. Ctrl+Z undoes and Ctrl+Shift+Z redoes; Ctrl+S saves. Ctrl+D duplicates the selected clips on the timeline. Ctrl+F searches the formulas. Hold Shift over a selected clip for its handles; Alt + click picks a glyph; Alt+G aligns by symbol. ? opens the sheet this page is made from. Filter the keyboard shortcuts No shortcut matches. Try fewer words, or one key."],["keys-files-and-history","Files and history Keys What it does Where See Ctrl+Alt+N, Ctrl+O New project, open a project (the Projects dialog). In a text field or a dialog the browser may take these keys instead: click out of the field, close the dialog, or use the File menu. outside text fields and dialogs Projects and files Ctrl+S, Ctrl+Alt+SCtrl+S, Ctrl+Shift+S Save, Save As. In a text field or a dialog the browser may take these keys instead (Ctrl+S offers to save the page): click out of the field, close the dialog, or use the File menu. outside text fields and dialogs Projects and files Ctrl+Shift+SCtrl+Alt+S Sync folder (the project library) outside text fields and dialogs; anywhere in the Projects dialog Projects and files Ctrl+Z Ctrl+Shift+Z or Ctrl+YCtrl+Z Ctrl+Shift+Z Undo; redo (Ctrl+Y on Windows and Linux only; also in immediate-edit dialogs); cancel an active gesture firstUndo; redo (also in immediate-edit dialogs); cancel an active gesture first anywhere but text fields"],["keys-playback","Playback Keys What it does Where See Space Play / pause (a button reached with Tab takes Space itself) outside fields, checkboxes, menus and dialogs The Preview Home, End ← / → (Shift: 1 s) Playhead to the start or the end; step a frame outside fields and dialogs The timeline Ctrl (held while dragging on the ruler) Move the playhead tick by tick: in steps of dt, the time between two small ticks of the ruler (the timeline’s toolbar shows it, and its − and + set one of your own) timeline The timeline"],["keys-editing","Editing Keys What it does Where See Ctrl+A Select every clip on screen at the playhead outside text fields and dialogs The Preview Ctrl+F Esc Search formulas: a panel in the place of the Preview lists the formulas, LaTeX texts and font texts whose name or source has what is typed (pressed again, back to its field); Esc closes it anywhere but dialogs Organizing Alt+A / Alt+H / Alt+D (Shift: to the frame) Align left / centre / right preview selection Align and distribute Alt+W / Alt+V / Alt+S (Shift: to the frame) Align top / middle / bottom preview selection Align and distribute Ctrl+Alt+H / Ctrl+Alt+VCtrl+Alt+H / Ctrl+Alt+V Distribute horizontally / vertically (3+ clips) preview selection Align and distribute ← / →, ↑ / ↓ (Shift: 0.1) Move the selected clips 0.01 (Shift: 0.1) at the playhead, as a drag would move them; a run of presses is one undo step. With nothing selected, ←/→ step the playhead preview, after a click on it, with clips selected The Preview Alt+G Align by symbol: click the symbol to move, then the reference symbol (or ←/→, ↑/↓ and Enter); a compass picks the side; Shift+wheel sets the reach preview Align by symbol Shift+Alt+G Align by symbol, the reference symbol on a formula locked in the preview: it stays, and the formula of the first symbol moves to it preview Align by symbol S Circle selector of the preview on or off: a click takes the symbol nearest to the pointer within a circle (Shift+wheel sets its reach); with a selected clip under the playhead and the keyboard out of the preview, or with a sound selected, S splits the selected clips at the playhead instead — the second half of a formula’s clip becomes a clip of a copy of it (Preferences → Split suffix names it) outside fields and dialogs Picking glyphs G Keep the symbol to move as it is at the playhead, to click its reference at another time (its formula stays in view, faint, and the ruler marks the instant); G again lets it go preview, aligning by symbol Align by symbol Shift+C Copy colours in order: click, one by one, the glyphs of the preview whose colours to copy (the Animations panel lists them); Enter then pastes them, in that order, on the glyphs clicked next; Shift+C again leaves outside fields and dialogs Colouring Shift+V Paste the colours last copied in order once more, from the first one outside fields and dialogs Colouring Enter Backspace Esc Go on from the copy to the paste; take the last click back; leave, keeping what was pasted preview, colours in order Colouring Delete / Backspace Delete the selected formula (held to confirm), clips, relation, block, camera move or target camera Creator, timeline The timeline F2 Enter Rename; edit the formula formula card Formulas F2 Enter (→/← too in the list view) Rename the group; open or close it group of formulas Organizing ↑ / ↓, Home, End Shift+↑ / Shift+↓ Ctrl+Space (←/→ too in the block view)↑ / ↓, Home, End Shift+↑ / Shift+↓ Ctrl+Enter (←/→ too in the block view) Move and select; extend the selection; add or remove a formula formula list Ctrl+D Duplicate the selected clips, block or camera move (a formula: Duplicate in its menu) timeline The timeline Tab ← / →, ↑ / ↓, Home, End Shift+← / Shift+→ Into the clips; between the clips of a track, the tracks and a clip's block lanes; extend the selection along the track timeline The timeline Enter Shift+F10 or MenuEnter Shift+F10 Open the clip's formula or the block's menu; the clip's Add animation menu (at the playhead), the menu of the selected clips when there are several, or the block's menu timeline clip or block Menus Alt+← / Alt+→ (Shift: 5 frames) Alt+↑ / Alt+↓ Move the clip a frame; to the track above or below timeline clip The timeline Alt+← / Alt+→ (Shift: 5 frames) Nudge the selected block or camera move a frame; it stops against what it cannot run alongside timeline block, camera move Animation blocks Tab ← / →, Home, End Enter, Shift+F10 or MenuTab ← / →, Home, End Enter, Shift+F10 Into the camera's moves; between them, in the order they run; the menu of a move timeline, Camera row The camera P Shift+P Add a pause at the playhead; add a comment there, its words asked for first — where the scene rests (elsewhere a notice says what runs there; a second pause on a frame is not added) outside fields, menus and dialogs, with the Slides mode on Slides and HTML Tab ← / → Delete / Backspace Enter Shift+F10 or MenuTab ← / → Delete / Backspace Enter Shift+F10 Reach the marks one by one; move the focused mark a frame inside its zone (↑/↓ in Zen mode); delete it; write its words; its menu a pause or a comment on the ruler (Slides mode) Slides and HTML"],["keys-preview-and-timeline","Preview and timeline Keys What it does Where See Shift (held) Transform handles on the selected clips (a group when several); link ports on every clip preview, timeline The Preview Alt (held) Only the edges answer the pointer — of the clips, and of their intros and outros: trim a clip, or make an intro or an outro longer or shorter, where a block or a relation window covers the edge timeline Intro and outro Ctrl (held while dragging) Free scale and rotation (no scale step — Preferences → Scale step — nor 15° steps); no snapping to other formulas preview The Preview Alt Ctrl+Alt (pressed while dragging) Move only horizontally or only vertically — the way the pointer has gone further from where the drag began —, still snapping along that axis to other formulas and to the grid; with Ctrl/⌘ too, without snapping. Let go of Alt and the drag is free again preview The Preview Ctrl + wheel Alt + drag on empty space, or the middle buttonpinch or Ctrl + wheel scroll, Alt + drag on empty space, or the middle button Zoom about the pointer; pan preview The Preview Ctrl + wheel middle button (held)pinch or Ctrl + wheel middle button (held) Zoom about the pointer; drag the view both ways at once (Esc puts it back) timeline The timeline T (held while moving the mouse) Move the timeline’s view along time as dragging its scrollbar would, wherever the pointer is: sideways, or up and down in Zen mode; the view stays where it is when T is released outside fields, menus and dialogs The timeline Shift+T (held while moving the mouse) Move the playhead to the time the pointer is at, at once and as it moves, wherever the pointer is: its place sideways, or up and down in Zen mode; the playhead stays where it is when the keys are released, and Shift pressed or let go while T is held changes between the playhead and the view outside fields, menus and dialogs The timeline ↑ / ↓, ← / → (in Zen mode) The timeline is vertical there and the arrows follow the screen: ↑/↓ go along time and ←/→ across the tracks, → towards the ruler, where the first track is; Alt+↑/↓ moves the clip a frame, Alt+←/→ to the track on that side timeline The interface Shift (held while dragging on the ruler) Mark a region, its start in green and its end in red: what is created next (an animation block, a relation, a formula brought to the timeline) takes it, from its start to its end, or is not made when it does not fit there timeline The timeline I, O Esc Put the start, the end of the timeline’s region at the playhead; remove the region (when Esc has nothing else to cancel or close) outside fields, menus and dialogs The timeline Ctrl Alt Alt+Shift (held while dragging an end of a line) Along the line: longer or shorter, in the direction it had; onto the symbol under the pointer; onto the whole formula there (a compass asks the point of them). With none, the end is left free where it is dropped preview Lines and arrows ↑ / ↓ (Shift: ×10) wheel with the mouse button held on the number (Shift: ×10, Alt: ×0.1) Nudge the value by the field's own step, up for more. The wheel writes as it turns — the preview follows —, its turns are one undo step when the button is let go, and Esc takes them back; without the button held the wheel scrolls the panel as always number boxes of the Animations and Relations panels Alt + click Alt + drag from a formula Select a glyph of a formula, with those selected in other formulas (again: take it out; Shift: a range within the formula); a rectangle that selects the glyphs it touches, of every formula (Shift adds) preview Picking glyphs Enter / Esc Make the Transform that carries the shape to what is chosen, at the playhead; leave without making it preview, while the new target of a shape is chosen Shapes ← / →, ↑ / ↓ (Shift: 10 px) Delete / Backspace Enter, Esc Move the selected anchor or handle a pixel (Shift: ten); remove the selected anchor, or bring the selected handle back to it; finish, as Done does preview, Edit path Custom paths Delete / Backspace Enter Esc Remove the selected point of the line; keep the curve, as Done does; put back the curve the mode started with preview, Edit curve Lines and arrows Alt + click Esc Put an end of the line on the symbols the preview picks, not on the whole formula; give up the end being chosen, then leave the mode preview, Add line Lines and arrows Ctrl+' Show or hide the grid outside text fields and dialogs The Preview Alt+↑ / Alt+↓ Denser / sparser grid while the grid is shown The Preview"],["keys-dialogs-and-help","Dialogs and help Keys What it does Where See ↓ / ↑, Enter ← / → Home, End a letter Tab Open a menu on its first / last item; the previous or next menu; the first or last item; the next item with that initial; leave the menu menu bar Menus Ctrl+Enter Apply: the formula's source as typed, or the text's draft (one undo step) formula editor, text editor Formulas Esc Cancel the drag; close the dialog (a field's own edit first) or one level of menu while dragging, in dialogs and menus C A Show or fold the Creator; the Animations panel — in Zen mode, open or close its half of the drawer over the Preview outside fields, menus and dialogs The interface Alt+M Multicamera on or off: the scene gets a camera (the Camera row of the timeline) or loses it — off only once every target camera and every camera move is deleted outside fields, menus and dialogs The camera V Camera view on or off: the Preview looks through the scene’s camera at the playhead, as the video does, or shows the world with the camera’s rectangles outside fields, menus and dialogs, with the multicamera on The camera F (held) The pointer takes the objects of the scene, as in the camera view, instead of the camera’s rectangles: they are selected, moved and transformed while the key is down, and the scene does not play the Preview with the multicamera on, in the world view The camera ? Show the keyboard shortcuts outside text fields and dialogs Menus Alt turns up in many rows, and what it does depends on where you are. Held over the timeline it lets the pointer reach the edges of clips and of their intros and outros; pressed during a drag in the Preview it locks the move to one axis; with a click or a drag it picks glyphs. The filter above finds them all: type alt."],["mouse","The gestures that go with the keys. A bracket after a key says that you hold it while you do it. The Ctrl of these rows is ⌘ on a Mac. Mouse gestures Gesture Where What it does See Click Creator, timeline, Preview Selects what is under the pointer. In the Preview, hovering outlines a glyph before you click. A tour of the interface Shift + click Creator, timeline, Preview Selects a range in a list; in the Preview and on the timeline it adds a clip to the selection or takes it out. The timeline Ctrl + click Creator, timeline, Preview Toggles one item in or out of the selection. The Preview Double-click Formula card, clip Opens the formula editor. On a card it edits the formula; on its name it renames it (so does F2). On a clip, in the Preview or on the timeline, it opens the editor of the formula or text. Writing formulas Double-click Animation block, relation band Opens the block’s dialog, or the relation editor. Animation blocks Right-click, Ctrl + click, or a click with two fingers Clips, blocks, relations, formula cards, glyphs, empty space Opens the menu of what is under the pointer. Context menus Drag a formula card onto the timeline Creator ▸ timeline Puts a clip of the formula on the track where you drop it. The timeline Drag a clip Preview Moves the selected clip: a keyframe edit at the playhead. It snaps to the other formulas, the frame and the grid; Ctrl held after pressing frees it, Alt locks one axis; Esc cancels. The Preview Shift held over a selected clip Preview Shows the transform handles: a corner scales in steps, the circle above rotates in 15° steps, and Ctrl frees both. The Preview Drag on empty space Preview, timeline lanes Draws a rectangle that selects the clips it touches; with Ctrl or Shift it adds them; Esc cancels. The Preview Alt + click, Alt + drag from a formula Preview Picks a glyph, with those picked in other formulas; the drag draws a rectangle that picks the glyphs it touches. Picking glyphs Scroll, Alt + drag on empty space, or the middle button Preview Pans the view. A pinch on the trackpad, or Ctrl + wheel, zooms about the pointer and Fit shows the whole frame again. The Preview Drag an end handle of a line Preview Moves that end, free where you drop it. With Ctrl it goes along the line, with Alt onto the symbol under the pointer, with Alt+Shift onto the whole formula. Lines and arrows Drag a clip Timeline Moves it along its track and between tracks. Its edges snap to the ticks of the ruler, the playhead and the edges of other clips; Shift held during the drag frees it. Pressed on the clip, Shift adds the clip to the selection or takes it out. The timeline Drag the edge of a clip Timeline Trims the clip. Drag the inner edge of the hatched intro or outro to make that animation longer or shorter; hold Alt to reach the edge through a block or a relation window that covers it. Appearing and disappearing Click or drag on the ruler Timeline Puts the playhead there, and scrubs. The timeline Wheel; pinch or Ctrl + wheel; middle button held Timeline Scrolls the view; zooms about the pointer; drags the view both ways at once (Esc puts it back). The timeline Shift held Timeline Shows a link port in the lower half of every clip: click one, then another clip, to relate the two. The relation editor opens, or a menu first when they are related already. How relations work Rest the pointer on a clip Timeline Shows its formula in a tooltip. The timeline Drag the top or bottom edge of a track header Timeline Makes that track lower or taller; a double-click gives it the standard height again. The timeline Wheel with the button held on a number Animations and relation panels Writes the value as the wheel turns; Shift makes the steps ten times bigger, Alt ten times smaller. Let go of the button and the wheel scrolls the panel again."],["sheet","The table above is built from the sheet the app itself shows. Open it with Help ▸ Keyboard shortcuts… or ?, and close it with Esc. The sheet: the keys as caps on the left, what they do in the middle, where they work on the right. For what a command does, and the other ways to reach it, see Every menu: the entries that have a key show it."]],"short":"Shortcuts"},{"id":"reference/menus","url":"reference/menus.html","title":"Every menu","section":"Reference","level":"reference","description":"Every menu of the menu bar and every right-click menu of the editor, entry by entry: its keys, what it does and the page that teaches it.","headings":[["menu-bar","The menu bar"],["file","File"],["edit","Edit"],["view","View"],["preferences-menu","Preferences"],["export","Export and the Export… button"],["help","Help"],["context-menus","Context menus"],["timeline-clip","A clip on the timeline"],["timeline-clips","Several clips"],["timeline-block","An animation block"],["timeline-group","A group of blocks"],["timeline-relation","A relation"],["timeline-audio","An audio clip"],["preview-clip","A clip in the Preview"],["preview-glyphs","Glyphs picked in the Preview"],["preview-space","Empty space in the Preview"],["preview-camera","The camera in the Preview"],["timeline-camera","The Camera row of the timeline"],["slides-ruler","The ruler in the Slides mode"],["slides-mark","A pause or a comment"],["line-end","The end of a line"],["creator","In the Creator"],["glyphs","On glyphs and pairs"]],"chunks":[[null,"Every menu of the menu bar and every right-click menu of the editor, entry by entry: its keys, what it does, and the page that teaches it. The menu bar runs along the top of the window. Click a menu to open it, or focus its button and press ↓ to open it on its first entry and ↑ on its last; ← and → move along the bar, a letter goes to the next entry that starts with it, and Esc closes one level. An arrow at the end of an entry opens a submenu. A tick marks the choice in use, or a switch that is on. An entry is dimmed while nothing can use it yet: Undo before the first change, the entries of Align before something is selected. While a menu is open, of the bar or of a right-click, the keys you press are its own: no shortcut acts on what is under it until it closes, so an arrow does not move the playhead and Delete deletes nothing. The tables below were read out of the app as it opens, with nothing selected, so the entries that act on a selection are dimmed there; a description says what an entry needs where it matters. Where an entry is a switch or a choice, the table says what it is by default. The keys are written for your system (Keyboard and mouse shortcuts has them all, by what you are doing). A few entries show only in a state the tables were not read in (a group in the Creator, glyphs picked in the Preview, glyphs selected on both sides of the relation editor): each is listed after the table of its menu."],["menu-bar","Five menus, then the Export… button, then the name of the project, the Renderer badge and the theme button. The name and the badge are described in A tour of the interface."],["file","The File menu: projects, saving, and the settings that belong to the project. The File menu Entry Keys What it does See New Ctrl+Alt+N Starts an empty project. When the project has unsaved changes it asks first: Save, Don't save or Cancel. Projects and files Open… Ctrl+O Opens the Projects dialog: the projects of your synced folder as cards, with a search box and favourites. Other file… in that dialog is the browser’s own file picker, for a file that is not in the folder. Projects and files Sync folder… Ctrl+Shift+S Picks the folder that holds your projects, once. Chrome and Edge keep it and list it again each time; other browsers keep a snapshot of it until you sync again. Projects and files Recover autosave Lists the autosaved work of other tabs that were closed, kept for a week, the five most recent, so you can bring it back. Projects and files No autosave of another tab What the submenu says when there is nothing to recover. Projects and files Save Ctrl+S Saves the project to its file, and asks where when it has none. A browser without file access downloads a new file each time. Projects and files Save As… Ctrl+Alt+S Saves the project under a new name or in a new place; the picker starts in the synced folder when there is one. Projects and files Project settings… Opens Project settings: name, target (ManimCE or ManimGL), resolution, frame rate, background, LaTeX preamble and duration. They belong to the project and are saved in its file. Preferences"],["edit","The Edit menu with three clips selected in the Preview, so that Align and Distribute can act. The first group undoes and deletes, the next copies and pastes the properties of clips, then come aligning, colours, and last the editor preferences for new clips and Morph blocks. Those last four entries are kept in the browser, not in the project; see Preferences. The Edit menu Entry Keys What it does See Undo Ctrl+Z Takes back the last change. During a drag it cancels the drag first. The interface Redo Ctrl+Shift+Z Brings back what Undo took back. The interface Delete formula Delete Deletes the formula selected in the Creator, with its clips and their relations, as one undo step. It asks first, and the confirm button is held down. Formulas Copy properties Takes the state of the one clip selected in the Preview as it is at the playhead: position, scale, rotation, flips, opacity, colour and glyph colours. Timing tools Paste properties Gives the copied properties to the selected clips, all of them but the position or one group. Each entry shows what it would paste; a paste is one undo step. Timing tools All but position Everything but the position. Timing tools Transformation Scale, rotation and flips. Timing tools Appearance Opacity and colour. Timing tools Glyph colours The colours of the glyphs, onto clips of the same formula only, since they go by glyph. Timing tools Position The position; several clips end up stacked on one point. Timing tools Align Lines up the clips selected in the Preview. A lone clip, or Shift held while you choose, aligns to the frame. Align and distribute Left Alt+A Aligns on the outermost left edge of the selection. Align and distribute Centre Alt+H Aligns on the middle, horizontally. Align and distribute Right Alt+D Aligns on the outermost right edge. Align and distribute Top Alt+W Aligns on the outermost top edge. Align and distribute Middle Alt+V Aligns on the middle, vertically. Align and distribute Bottom Alt+S Aligns on the outermost bottom edge. Align and distribute By symbol… Alt+G A two-click mode: click the symbol to move, then the reference symbol; a compass asks on which side or corner it lands. Align by symbol By symbol of a locked formula… Shift+Alt+G The same, with the reference symbol on a formula locked in the Preview: it stays where it is and the other formula moves to it. Align by symbol Distribute Spaces three or more selected clips evenly. Align and distribute Horizontally Ctrl+Alt+H Evens out the gaps between the clips, left to right. Align and distribute Vertically Ctrl+Alt+V Evens out the gaps between the clips, top to bottom. Align and distribute Apply palette… Previews what the active palette would change in the formulas and objects that exist, and applies it as one undo step. Off while no palette is active. Palettes Match colors The project’s lists of LaTeX patterns and the colours they give. Each list of the project is also an entry here, below the two commands. Match colors Create match colors… Opens the Match colors dialog on a new, empty list. Match colors New from active palette… Makes a list with four rows (variable, constant, operator and result), coloured from the active palette, for you to fill with patterns. Match colors Colours in order Copies colours from glyphs in the Preview, one by one, and pastes them in the same order onto other glyphs. Colouring Copy… Shift+C Starts the copy: click the glyphs whose colours to copy, in order; Enter then pastes them on the glyphs you click next. Colouring Paste Shift+V Pastes the colours last copied once more, from the first one. Off until something was copied. Colouring Always add intro (Write) New clips get the intro chosen in Default intro & outro… (the animation in brackets) instead of appearing at once. Off by default. Intro and outro Always add outro (FadeOut) New clips get the outro chosen in Default intro & outro… (the animation in brackets) instead of disappearing at once. Off by default. Intro and outro Default intro & outro… Chooses the animation, duration, easing and lag ratio of the intro and the outro that the two entries above give. Intro and outro Suggest morph pairs A new Morph block starts with the pairs Suggest finds, instead of none. Off by default. Morph blocks"],["view","The View menu with Theme open; Dark is ticked until you choose another. The View menu Entry Keys What it does See Toggle theme Flips between the dark and the light theme, as the round, half-filled button at the end of the menu bar does. The interface Theme System follows your operating system as it changes; Dark and Light fix one. Default: Dark. Choices: System, Dark, Light. The interface Panels Shows or folds the Creator, the Animations panel and the Timeline to their title bars: a tick means the panel is shown. The layout remembers it. Off in the stacked layout of a narrow window, where nothing folds. All three are shown by default. Outside a text field, the key C does it for the Creator and A for the Animations panel. The interface Multicamera Alt+M Gives the scene a camera, or takes it away: with it the timeline has a Camera row for its moves, and the Preview shows the world around the frame, with the camera drawn as a rectangle. It stays on while the camera has target cameras or moves: delete them first, and a notice says so. Off by default. The camera Camera view V With the multicamera on, looks through the camera at the playhead, as the video does, or goes back to the world with the camera’s rectangles. Greyed out without a camera. Off by default. The camera Slides Turns the Slides mode on or off: pauses and comments on the scene’s time, where it rests, for the exported web page and the Python. It stays on while the project has a pause or a comment: delete them first, and a notice says so. Off by default. Slides and HTML Add pause at the playhead P Adds a pause at the playhead, where the scene rests; where something animates, a notice says what runs there. Greyed out while the Slides mode is off. Slides and HTML Add comment at the playhead… Shift+P Adds a comment at the playhead, its words asked for first. Greyed out while the Slides mode is off. Slides and HTML Add a pause after every animation Adds a pause where each animation ends and another follows, but in a rest that holds one already, as one undo step: then delete the ones you do not want. Greyed out while the Slides mode is off. Slides and HTML Skip the wait at every pause Makes every pause that has a wait to skip skip it: going on from it, the exported page and the rehearsal jump to where the next animation starts. The others stay as they are, and the notice counts them. One undo step; greyed out while the project has no pause. Slides and HTML Keep the wait at every pause Makes every pause keep its wait again, as one undo step. Greyed out while the project has no pause. Slides and HTML Delete every pause… Deletes every pause, with its note; the comments stay. It asks first, saying how many go, and its button is held down; one undo step brings them back. Greyed out while the project has no pause. Slides and HTML Glyph source: Automatic Formulas use the companion’s exact LaTeX glyphs when it is running, and MathJax otherwise. The default. The companion Glyph source: MathJax Forces MathJax, the browser’s own renderer, even when the companion is running. Formulas Glyph source: LaTeX (companion) Forces the companion’s exact LaTeX glyphs. Without the companion running, a formula shows an error that says so. The companion"],["preferences-menu","The Preferences menu with Guide colour open: each choice has its colour square. Preferences says more about each one, and lists the preferences that are set elsewhere. The Preferences menu Entry Keys What it does See Snap to objects A dragged formula sticks to the edges and centres of the others and of the frame. Hold Ctrl/⌘ after pressing to drag freely. On by default. The Preview Guide colour The colour of the guide lines a snapped drag shows in the Preview. Default: Yellow. Choices: Yellow, Orange, Magenta, Cyan, Green, White. The Preview Guide width How thick those guide lines are. Default: Thin. Choices: Thin, Regular, Thick. The Preview Glyph selection colour The colour of the glyphs picked with Alt in the Preview, and of the rectangle that picks them. Default: Yellow. Choices: Yellow, Orange, Magenta, Cyan, Green, White. Picking glyphs Keep the transform handles shown Keeps the transform handles on the selected clips without Shift held. Off by default. The Preview Scale step The step a corner drag’s scale snaps to. Custom… takes a number or a fraction, such as 1/3. Default: 0.05. Choices: 0.05, 0.1, 0.15, 0.2, Custom…. The Preview Locked formulas How opaque the Preview draws a formula locked in it. The Preview only: nothing exported is faded. Default: 40 %. Choices: 20 %, 30 %, 40 %, 50 %, 60 %, 80 %, 100 % (not faded). The Preview Show grid Draws a grid in the Preview, under the formulas, and makes a dragged formula snap to it (Ctrl/⌘ frees it). Never part of an export. Off by default. The Preview Grid spacing The spacing of the grid, in Manim units. Default: 1 unit. Choices: 2 units, 1 unit, 0.5 units, 0.25 units, 0.1 units. The Preview Grid snaps What the grid holds while you drag: the formula’s centre, or whichever of its edges is nearer to a line. Default: The formula's centre. Choices: The formula's centre, Its nearest edges. The Preview Show the camera’s route In the world view of a scene with a camera, draws the line with arrowheads that joins the camera’s frames in the order its moves run. On by default. The camera Dim what the camera leaves out In the world view of a scene with a camera, dims what the camera leaves out at the playhead. On by default. The camera Standard scale… The size new formulas and new LaTeX texts are born with (1 is Manim’s font size 48). In its dialog, Apply to all formulas and Apply to all texts give it to the ones that exist. Formulas Split suffix (_s)… The suffix that names the copy a split makes: a clip cut at the playhead (S) gives its second half a copy of its formula, named after it with this suffix. The brackets show the suffix in use; its dialog takes letters, digits and “_”, at most 16. The timeline Zen mode For small windows: the timeline becomes a column on the left, drawn vertical, and the Preview takes the rest; the Creator and the Animations panel open over it, side by side, from two buttons of a strip. It only rearranges the window. Off by default. The interface Chroma Lab… Opens Chroma Lab, the colour studio that comes inside the editor, to make a palette and send it to the editor. Palettes Editor palettes… Opens the palette library: import, make, edit, activate and delete palettes of named colour roles. Palettes"],["export","The Export… button beside the menus exports Python for the target of the last Python export you opened (ManimCE at first). Its arrow lists the six exports; there is no Export menu in the bar, and this documentation writes an entry as Export ▸ Video…. The arrow of the Export… button lists every export. The Export… button’s menu Entry Keys What it does See Python — ManimCE… Exports the scene as a Python file for ManimCE. Exporting Python — ManimGL… Exports the scene as a Python file for ManimGL. Exporting Video… Renders the scene to a video file: in the browser, with the companion’s ffmpeg, or by Manim itself. Video Image… Writes the frame at the playhead as a PNG picture, the one a video shows there: at the project’s size or twice it, over the scene’s background or transparent. Exporting Audio… Exports the sound of the project as one audio file: WAV or WebM with Opus, and M4A where the browser can encode AAC. Audio HTML… Writes the scene as a web page that plays it live and stops at each pause of the Slides mode: a folder to work on and embed, its zip, or one file. Slides and HTML"],["help","The Help menu, with Examples open. The Help menu Entry Keys What it does See Keyboard shortcuts… ? Shows the table of keyboard shortcuts and click gestures. Shortcuts Examples Opens one of the example projects the app carries, as a file opens: it asks first about unsaved work, and the example becomes a project of yours without a file, so Save asks where it goes. Projects and files Completing the square The quadratic formula derived step by step. Try an example, in the Creator of a project with no formula, opens it too. Projects and files Hello: one-to-one Two formulas and the relation between them. Projects and files Camera The scene’s camera and its moves. Projects and files Braces Braces over parts of a sum, each with its label. Projects and files Engine inspector… Shows how a formula splits into glyphs with each renderer, MathJax and the companion’s LaTeX. The companion Third-party licenses… Lists the open-source components the app contains and opens their notices. About Shows the version of the app, the project file format version, the MathJax and companion versions and the licence. Files and format"],["context-menus","Right-click what you want to act on. The menu is for what is under the pointer; a menu of a selection acts on the whole selection. On the timeline, Shift+F10 or the Menu key opens the menu of the clip or block that has the keyboard focus. Esc closes it, and → and ← open and close a submenu. The tables read like the ones above; where one menu differs with what you click, a For column says when you see an entry."],["timeline-clip","Right-click a clip. The menu starts with Add animation: its header names the clip and where a block you add starts: at the playhead when the playhead is on the clip, else at the time under the pointer, and then the header says the playhead is outside this clip. Then come the objects you can put on the clip, and last the clip’s own entries. On a locked track the menu opens with what would change the clip greyed out; its timing can still be copied. The menu of a formula clip, with Add shape open beside it. The right-click menu: formulas, LaTeX texts, font texts, braces, shapes, lines, arrows Entry For What it does See Appear all Adds a block that brings the glyphs in at the playhead, or at the time you right-clicked when the playhead is not on the clip. GrowArrow is for arrows. Choices: Write, Create, FadeIn, GrowArrow, GrowFromCenter, DrawBorderThenFill. Animation blocks Disappear all Adds a block that takes the glyphs out at that time. Choices: FadeOut, Unwrite, Uncreate, ShrinkToCenter. Animation blocks Transform braces, lines and arrows Adds the Transform of a brace, a shape, a line or an arrow: its place, size, colour and, for a line, its ends and stroke change over the block. Animation blocks Transform formulas and texts, shapes Adds a Transform block: the whole object moves, turns or rescales, or (a shape) goes to surround something else. Animation blocks Whole formula (keyframe) formulas and texts Adds a keyframe for the whole formula or text: position, scale, rotation, opacity and colour change over the block. Animation blocks Part of the formula formulas and texts Adds a block that moves, turns or recolours some of the glyphs of the formula while the rest stays. Animation blocks Transform shapes Adds the Transform of a brace, a shape, a line or an arrow: its place, size, colour and, for a line, its ends and stroke change over the block. Animation blocks To another formula or glyphs… shapes Carries the shape to surround another formula or other glyphs: pick the new target in the Preview, then confirm. Shapes Morph within the formula formulas and texts Adds a Morph block: a transformation between the glyphs of this same formula, for a derivation on one clip. Morph blocks Emphasis all Adds a block that draws attention at that time. A line or an arrow offers only Circumscribe, Flash and FocusOn. Choices: Indicate, Wiggle, Circumscribe, Flash, FocusOn. Animation blocks Add label braces Puts a label at the tip of the brace: a formula or a LaTeX text. Choices: Formula, Text (LaTeX). Braces Colour braces, shapes The colour of the brace or shape: the twelve Manim colours, the roles of the active palette, or any #RRGGBB typed in the field and applied. Colouring Direction braces Moves the brace to another side of what it spans. Choices: Below, Above, Left, Right. Braces Add brace formulas and texts, shapes, lines and arrows Puts a brace on the clip, on the side you choose. On a formula or a text, On some glyphs… opens a dialog to pick which glyphs it spans. Choices: Below, Above, Left, Right, On some glyphs…. Braces Add shape formulas and texts, lines and arrows Puts a shape around the clip. On some glyphs… picks which glyphs it surrounds. Choices: Box, Circle, Underline, Cross, Cross line, Highlight, On some glyphs…. Shapes Add line formulas and texts, lines and arrows Draws a line or an arrow that starts on this clip: a compass asks where on it, then one click puts the other end. Choices: Line, Dashed line, Arrow, Double arrow. Lines and arrows Intro all The animation the clip starts with, the current one ticked. None makes the formula appear at the clip’s start. Lines and arrows offer fewer; GrowArrow is for arrows. Choices: None (appear), Write, FadeIn, GrowFromCenter, DrawBorderThenFill, Create, GrowArrow. Intro and outro Outro all The animation the clip ends with, the current one ticked. None makes it disappear at the clip’s end. Choices: None (disappear), Unwrite, FadeOut, ShrinkToCenter, Uncreate. Intro and outro Timing all Copy when this clip starts and ends and how long its intro and outro are, or paste one of those onto this clip. Timing tools Copy timing all Copies the clip’s start, end, duration and intro and outro times. Timing tools Paste start all Moves the clip to start when the copied clip does. Timing tools Paste end all Moves the clip to end when the copied clip does. Timing tools Paste duration all Makes the clip last as long as the copied one, from where it starts. Timing tools Paste intro time all Gives its intro the length of the copied clip’s intro. Timing tools Paste outro time all Gives its outro the length of the copied clip’s outro. Timing tools Remove animations all Takes off the clip’s intro, its outro and its blocks, but the Morph ones. Intro and outro Remove relations formulas and texts Takes off the clip’s transformations of glyphs: its relations with other clips and its Morph blocks. Relations Unlink formula (make a copy) formulas and texts Gives this clip a copy of its formula of its own, so editing one no longer changes the other. Off, its tooltip saying why, for a formula’s only clip. Timing tools Split at playhead (S) all Cuts the clip in two at the playhead: the second half becomes a clip of a copy of its formula, that starts as the object is then. Off, its tooltip saying why, while the playhead is outside the clip or the track is locked; when something keeps the clip whole, a notice says what. The timeline Become lines and arrows Turns the line into another kind of line: it keeps its ends, its curve and its tips. Choices: Line, Dashed line, Arrow, Double arrow. Lines and arrows Reverse points start ⇄ end lines and arrows Swaps the start and the end of the line. Lines and arrows Curve lines and arrows Bends the line into an arc or a spline, or shapes the spline it has; copies the shape of its path, or gives it the copied one. Lines and arrows Arc lines and arrows Bends the line into an arc of a circle (Manim’s path_arc). Lines and arrows Spline points and handles lines and arrows Turns it into a spline with points and handles you can edit in the Preview. Once the line is a spline the entry reads Edit curve. Lines and arrows Copy path shape lines and arrows Copies the shape of the line’s path. Lines and arrows Paste path shape lines and arrows Gives the line the shape last copied. Off until something was copied. Lines and arrows Detach lines and arrows Frees one end of the line or both from the glyphs they are on; off when both are free already. Lines and arrows Detach braces, shapes Frees the brace or shape from what it hangs on: it stays where it is and no longer follows that clip. Braces Lock in the preview all The Preview then draws the clip and leaves it alone: a press goes through it to what is under it, and nothing done in the Preview moves it. The Preview"],["timeline-clips","With two or more clips selected, right-click one of them. The menu offers what the Creator offers their formulas, one brace over them all, one shape around them all, and the clips’ own Duplicate and Delete. Its first entry is Edit… when the clips are all of one formula and Edit together… when they are of several, and Paste path shape is there when a line is among them. The right-click menu: clips of several formulas, clips of one formula, a line among them Entry For What it does See Edit together… clips of several formulas, a line among them Opens the formulas of the selected clips in one editor, to set their scale or glyph colours together. Organizing Edit… clips of one formula Opens the formula of these clips in its editor. Formulas Add brace all Puts one brace over all the selected clips. Not offered when a brace is among them; off, its tooltip saying why, when the clips are never on screen together. Choices: Below, Above, Left, Right. Braces Add shape all Puts one shape around all the selected clips. Not offered when a brace or a shape is among them; off, its tooltip saying why, when the clips are never on screen together. Choices: Box, Circle, Underline, Cross, Cross line, Highlight. Shapes Apply match colors all Colours the glyphs of the selected clips’ formulas with one of the project’s match colour lists. Match colors Create match colors… all Opens the Match colors dialog on a new list (what the submenu offers while the project has none). Match colors Paste path shape a line among them Gives each line among the selected clips the shape of the path last copied. Off until something was copied that fits one of them. Lines and arrows Timing all Pastes a part of the copied timing onto every selected clip. Choices: Paste start, Paste end, Paste duration, Paste intro time, Paste outro time. Timing tools Unlink formula (make a copy) all Gives each formula of the selection a copy of its own, shared by its selected clips. Timing tools Split at playhead (S) all Cuts in two at the playhead the selected clips it is inside, the sounds among them too, as one undo step. Off while it is inside none of them, or only inside clips on locked tracks. The timeline Lock in the preview all Locks them all in the Preview, or unlocks them when they all are. The Preview Duplicate all Duplicates the selected clips on the timeline. The timeline Delete N clips all Deletes the selected clips as one undo step; the formulas stay in the Creator. When braces, shapes or lines attached to them would go too, it asks first. The timeline"],["timeline-block","Right-click a block in the top half of a clip. What it offers depends on its kind: an Appear or a Disappear block changes its animation, an Emphasis block its effect, a Morph block opens its pairs. A block of an unfolded group has the group’s entries too. A block that starts with other blocks of its clip and lasts as long as they do also offers Group with the N blocks that run with it, which makes one group of them all. The right-click menu: Appear and Disappear blocks, Emphasis blocks, Morph blocks, Transform blocks, blocks of an unfolded group, a block that runs with others Entry For What it does See Change animation Appear and Disappear blocks, a block that runs with others Changes the animation of an Appear or Disappear block to another of the nine, the current one ticked. A block of a line lists only what a line takes. Choices: Appear · Write, Appear · Create, Appear · FadeIn, Appear · GrowFromCenter, Appear · DrawBorderThenFill, Disappear · FadeOut, Disappear · Unwrite, Disappear · Uncreate, Disappear · ShrinkToCenter. Animation blocks Change effect Emphasis blocks Changes the effect of an Emphasis block to another of the five, the current one ticked. A block of a line lists Circumscribe, Flash and FocusOn. Choices: Indicate, Wiggle, Circumscribe, Flash, FocusOn. Animation blocks Edit pairs… Morph blocks Opens the pair editor of the Morph block. Morph blocks Fold the group blocks of an unfolded group Draws the group of this block as one block again. Block groups Take out of the group blocks of an unfolded group Takes this block out of its group. Block groups Ungroup blocks of an unfolded group Undoes the group; its blocks stay. Block groups Group with the N blocks that run with it blocks of an unfolded group, a block that runs with others Makes one group of this block and the blocks of its clip that start with it and last as long; N is how many. In a block of an unfolded group the entry ends “with its group”. Shown when there are some. Block groups Duplicate all Makes a copy of the block next to it. A Morph block’s copy has no pairs. Animation blocks Duplicate for the other glyphs Appear and Disappear blocks, Emphasis blocks, Transform blocks, blocks of an unfolded group, a block that runs with others Makes a copy that animates the other glyphs of the formula, so every glyph is covered once. Not on a block of an arrow, which takes all of it. Animation blocks Split into runs Appear and Disappear blocks, Emphasis blocks, Transform blocks, blocks of an unfolded group, a block that runs with others Splits a block that names glyphs into one block per run of consecutive glyphs. Not on a block of an arrow, which takes all of it. Animation blocks Split… Appear and Disappear blocks, Emphasis blocks, Transform blocks, blocks of an unfolded group, a block that runs with others Opens the Split dialog to divide a block that names glyphs as you choose. Not on a block of an arrow, which takes all of it. Timing tools Delete… all Deletes the block, after a question whose confirm button is held down. Animation blocks"],["timeline-group","A group of blocks is drawn as one block until you unfold it. Right-click it for its own menu. It offers Group with the N blocks that run with it too, when other blocks of the clip start with the group and last as long. The right-click menu: a folded group Entry What it does See Unfold Shows the group’s blocks on lanes of their own. Block groups Ungroup Undoes the group; its blocks stay. Block groups Group with the N blocks that run with it Takes into the group the blocks of its clip that start with it and last as long; N is how many. Shown when there are some. Block groups Duplicate Copies the whole group. Block groups Delete… Deletes the whole group, after a question whose confirm button is held down. Block groups"],["timeline-relation","Related clips are joined by an arrow, and the part of the target clip where the transformation happens is a band. Right-click the band. The right-click menu: a relation Entry What it does See Edit pairs… Opens the relation editor on this relation. Relation editor Choose a pair… Edits the path of one pair in the Preview. A relation of up to five pairs lists them under the heading Edit path instead; from six on this entry opens a dialog with the relation’s diagram and a picture of each pair’s path. Custom paths Delete relation Deletes the relation. Relations"],["timeline-audio","Right-click a clip on an audio track. The menu is short, because a sound has no intro, outro, blocks or relations. The right-click menu: an audio clip Entry What it does See Edit volume and trim… Opens the editor of the clip: its trim and its volume curve. Audio Split at playhead (S) Cuts the clip in two at the playhead. Audio Fade In Makes the volume rise from silence over the first half second of the clip, or its first half when the clip is shorter than a second. Audio Fade Out Makes the volume fall to silence over the last half second of the clip, or its second half when the clip is shorter than a second. Audio Timing Copy or paste when the clip starts and ends. A sound has no intro or outro. Timing tools Copy timing Copies the clip’s start, end and duration. Timing tools Paste start Moves the clip to start when the copied clip does. Timing tools Paste end Moves the clip to end when the copied clip does. Timing tools Paste duration Makes the clip last as long as the copied one. Timing tools Duplicate Duplicates the clip, or the clips that are selected. Audio Delete Deletes the clip, or the clips that are selected. Audio"],["preview-clip","Right-click a formula, text or object in the Preview. Copy properties takes the clip under the pointer, and each section of the menu opens as a submenu beside its entry. If you picked glyphs of the clip with Alt, Add brace and Add shape work on those glyphs. The clip’s own entries are the same as on the timeline, but the Preview’s menu has no Timing and no Lock in the preview. On a brace it offers Add label instead of the brace, shape and line entries; on a shape, Transform to another formula or glyphs… and no Add shape. The right-click menu: a formula or text, a brace, a shape Entry For What it does See Copy properties all Copies the properties of the clip under the pointer. Timing tools Paste properties all Pastes the copied properties onto the selected clips, all but the position or one group. Choices: All but position, Transformation, Appearance, Glyph colours, Position. Timing tools Align all Lines up the selected clips; Align by symbol… starts the two-click mode. Choices: Align left, Align centre, Align right, Align top, Align middle, Align bottom, Align by symbol…. Align and distribute Distribute all Spaces three or more selected clips evenly. Choices: Distribute horizontally, Distribute vertically. Align and distribute Add label a brace Puts a label at the tip of the brace under the pointer: a formula or a LaTeX text. Choices: Formula, Text (LaTeX). Braces Colour a brace, a shape The colour of the brace or shape: the twelve Manim colours, the roles of the active palette, or any #RRGGBB typed in the field and applied. Colouring Direction a brace Moves the brace to another side of what it spans. Choices: Below, Above, Left, Right. Braces Add brace a formula or text, a shape Puts a brace on the clip under the pointer, or on the glyphs picked on it with Alt, or over all the selected clips. Choices: Below, Above, Left, Right, On some glyphs…. Braces Transform to another formula or glyphs… a shape Carries the shape to surround another formula or other glyphs: pick the new target in the Preview, then confirm. Shapes Add shape a formula or text Puts a shape around the clip under the pointer, or around the glyphs picked on it, or around all the selected clips. Choices: Box, Circle, Underline, Cross, Cross line, Highlight, On some glyphs…. Shapes Add line a formula or text, a shape Draws a line or an arrow that starts on this clip: a compass asks where on it, then one click puts the other end. Choices: Line, Dashed line, Arrow, Double arrow. Lines and arrows Intro all The animation the clip starts with, the current one ticked. Choices: None (appear), Write, FadeIn, GrowFromCenter, DrawBorderThenFill, Create. Intro and outro Outro all The animation the clip ends with, the current one ticked. Choices: None (disappear), Unwrite, FadeOut, ShrinkToCenter, Uncreate. Intro and outro Remove animations all Takes off the clip’s intro, outro and blocks, but the Morph ones. Intro and outro Remove relations a formula or text Takes off the clip’s relations and Morph blocks. Relations Unlink formula (make a copy) a formula or text Gives this clip a copy of its formula of its own. Timing tools Split at playhead (S) all Cuts the clip in two at the playhead: the second half becomes a clip of a copy of its formula. The timeline Detach a brace, a shape Frees the brace or shape from what it hangs on: it stays where it is and no longer follows that clip. Braces Apply match colors all Colours the glyphs of the selected clips’ formulas with a match colour list. Choices: Create match colors…. Match colors Copy colours in order… all Starts Colours in order: click glyphs whose colours to copy, in order. Colouring Paste colours in order all Pastes the colours last copied, in order, from the first one. Off until something was copied. Colouring"],["preview-glyphs","Right-click the ink of a glyph you picked with Alt. The menu acts on the picked glyphs. Colour holds the swatches, the Hex field and Clear colour of the glyph view’s menu, and Add brace and Add shape put one on the picked glyphs. For glyphs of one formula the menu also has Hide at the start, Show at the start, On some glyphs… under Add brace and Add shape, and these: The right-click menu of glyphs picked with Alt: the entries of their colours Entry What it does See Copy colour to clipboard With one glyph picked: puts the colour it is seen in on the system clipboard, as #RRGGBB. The entry shows that colour. Colouring Copy colour sequence Takes the own colours of the picked glyphs, in order. Colouring Paste colour sequence Gives the colours copied to the picked glyphs, when they are the same symbols. The line under it says what would be pasted, or why it cannot be. Colouring"],["preview-space","Right-click where there is nothing. Add line: choose a kind and click where the line starts and where it ends. With the multicamera on, the menu also adds to the camera. The right-click menu: without a camera, with the multicamera on Entry For What it does See Line all Draws a line: click where it starts, then where it ends. Lines and arrows Dashed line all Draws a dashed line the same way. Lines and arrows Arrow all Draws an arrow, with its tip at the end. Lines and arrows Double arrow all Draws a double arrow, with a tip at each end. Lines and arrows Add target camera here with the multicamera on Adds a target camera centred where you clicked, as large as the camera is at the playhead, and selects it. The camera Add camera move with the multicamera on Adds a camera move at the playhead, a second long, where the scene is free; the playhead goes to its end, where the camera is then put. The camera"],["preview-camera","With the multicamera on, right-click a rectangle of the camera by its edge or its name: a target camera, the camera itself, the frame of 0 s or the frame a move goes to on its own. The right-click menu: a target camera, the camera Entry For What it does See Move camera here a target camera Adds a camera move to this target camera, at the playhead. The camera Rename… a target camera Asks for the target camera’s new name. The camera Delete (Del) a target camera Deletes the target camera, after a question when moves go to it: those moves stay, each going to a frame of its own where the target camera was. The camera Add target camera here the camera Adds a target camera in the frame of this rectangle. The camera Add camera move the camera Adds a camera move at the playhead, a second long, where the scene is free. The camera"],["timeline-camera","Right-click a move of the Camera row, or the row where it has none. The right-click menu: a camera move, the Camera row Entry For What it does See Isolate in the Preview a camera move Leaves the rectangle of the frame this move goes to as the only one that answers the pointer in the Preview, for frames that lie one over another. Stop isolating ends it. The camera Duplicate (Ctrl+D) a camera move Copies the move into the first place after it with room for its whole length. The camera Delete (Del) a camera move Deletes the move. The camera Add camera move here the Camera row Adds a camera move at the time you clicked, a second long, where the scene is free. The camera"],["slides-ruler","With the Slides mode on, right-click the ruler (Slides and the HTML page). Off, the ruler has no menu. The right-click menu: where the scene rests, where something animates Entry For What it does See Add pause here (time) all Adds a pause at the time clicked, on a frame; with Snap on, pulled onto the playhead or the edge of the zone when they are near. Greyed out where something animates, and a line says what runs there. Slides and HTML Add comment here… all Adds a comment there, its words asked for first. Greyed out where something animates. Slides and HTML Add a pause after every animation all Adds a pause where each animation ends and another follows, but in a rest that holds one already, as one undo step. On the menu wherever it is opened. Slides and HTML Skip the wait at every pause all Makes every pause that has a wait to skip skip it, as one undo step; the others stay as they are. On the menu wherever it is opened; greyed out while the project has no pause. Slides and HTML Keep the wait at every pause all Makes every pause keep its wait again, as one undo step. On the menu wherever it is opened; greyed out while the project has no pause. Slides and HTML Delete every pause… all Deletes every pause, with its note; the comments stay. It asks first, saying how many go, and its button is held down; one undo step brings them back. On the menu wherever it is opened; greyed out while the project has no pause. Slides and HTML"],["slides-mark","Right-click a pause or a comment on the ruler, or press Shift+F10 while it has the keyboard focus. The menu’s header says what the mark is and when. The right-click menu: a pause, a comment Entry For What it does See Edit note… a pause Writes the note of the pause, for whoever presents; emptied, the note goes. A pause without one reads Add a note…. Slides and HTML Skip the wait after it a pause A pause’s box: going on from it, the exported page and the rehearsal jump over the rest that follows, to where the next animation starts. Greyed out, with a line that says why, where there is nothing to skip. One undo step. Slides and HTML Edit comment… a comment Writes the words of the comment; a comment is not kept without them. Slides and HTML Delete (Del) all Deletes the mark, as one undo step. Slides and HTML"],["line-end","Select a line or an arrow and right-click the handle at one of its ends. The right-click menu: an end of a line Entry What it does See Detach Frees this end from the glyphs it is on. Off when it is free already. Lines and arrows Change its point… Opens the compass to choose another point of what the end is on. Lines and arrows"],["creator","Right-click a card of the list or the blocks. The menu acts on the selected formulas: a right-click on a card outside the selection selects that card first. Its entries depend on what is selected (Edit… for one formula, Edit together… for several, the group entries) and on whether the formulas can take match colours. The right-click menu: several formulas, one formula, a group Entry For What it does See Edit together… several formulas, a group Opens the selected formulas in one editor: one scale and glyph colours for all. Organizing Edit… one formula Opens the formula in its editor. Formulas Add to the timeline all Puts a clip of each selected formula on the timeline at the playhead, or in the region when one is marked on the ruler. The timeline Rename group a group Renames the group. Organizing Ungroup a group Undoes the group; its formulas stay. Organizing Group several formulas Makes a group of the selected formulas, in the Creator’s list. Organizing Apply match colors all Colours the glyphs of the selected formulas with one of the project’s match colour lists. Match colors Create match colors… all Opens the Match colors dialog on a new list (what the submenu offers while the project has none). Match colors Duplicate all Makes a copy of each selected formula, under the next free name, right after the original. Organizing Delete… all Deletes the selected formulas with their clips and relations, as one undo step, after a question whose confirm button is held down. With several selected it says how many: Delete N formulas…. Formulas With groups in the project, the menu of selected formulas that is not opened on the row of a group can have two more entries: The right-click menu of the Creator: the entries that change what is in a group Entry What it does See Add to group A submenu of the groups there are: puts the selected formulas in the one you choose. Shown while one of them is in no group. Organizing Remove from group Takes the selected formulas out of their groups; a group left without formulas goes. Shown while one of them is in a group. Organizing The arrow of + New beside the button is a menu of its own: The right-click menu: the arrow of + New Entry What it does See Formula A new formula, written in LaTeX math. Formulas Text (LaTeX) A new LaTeX text: prose with formulas between dollar signs. Texts Text (font) A new font text: plain text in any font, shaped by the companion. Texts"],["glyphs","In the formula editor’s glyph view, right-click a glyph for what acts on the selected glyphs. In the relation editor, right-click a glyph for the menu of the pairs it is in. The right-click menu: a glyph in the formula editor, a pair in the relation editor Entry For What it does See Colour swatches a glyph in the formula editor The twelve Manim colours, then the roles of the active palette: one click colours what the menu acts on. Colouring Apply a glyph in the formula editor Colours the selected glyphs with the #RRGGBB typed in the field above it. Colouring Clear colour a glyph in the formula editor Gives the selected glyphs back the colour of the whole formula. Colouring Hide at the start a glyph in the formula editor Hides the selected glyphs at the start of the clip, so that an Appear block can bring them in later. Animation blocks Show at the start a glyph in the formula editor Shows glyphs that were hidden at the start. Animation blocks Inherit: Transform a pair in the relation editor The pair takes the style of the relation. Relations Transform a pair in the relation editor The glyph moves from the source to the target. Relations Copy (source stays) a pair in the relation editor A copy of the source glyph moves to the target; the source stays. Relations Fade transform a pair in the relation editor The source glyph fades out as the target glyph fades in (Manim’s FadeTransform). Relations Fade copy (source stays) a pair in the relation editor A copy of the source glyph fades out as the target glyph fades in; the source stays. Relations Edit path… a pair in the relation editor Shapes the path of this pair in the Preview. Custom paths Delete link… a pair in the relation editor Deletes the link of the selected glyphs, after a question. Relation editor The menu of the relation editor has more entries in two cases: with glyphs selected on both sides the link comes first, and a pair of several sources onto one target, or one whose target other pairs bring too, can be taken apart or joined. The pair editor of a Morph block has the same menu. The right-click menu of the relation editor: the link, and pairs that bring one target Entry What it does See Link as Transform, Link as Copy, Link as Fade transform, Link as Fade copy Links the selected glyphs as a pair of that style. A style they cannot take is greyed out, with the reason under it. Onto targets that a pair brings already, the menu offers Join pair N instead, and Separate pair as … for a pair of its own onto them. Relation editor Split into separate pairs Takes a pair of several sources onto one target apart: one pair for each source, each with the style it played and the path it followed. Relation editor Join into one pair Puts back into one the pairs that bring the same target. Greyed out, with the reason under it, when one pair could not play what they play. Relation editor Several panels have a button that opens a menu of its own beside its label, such as Paste properties and Match colors in the bar that appears in the Preview when two or more clips are selected. Those menus offer the same commands as the ones above."]],"short":"Menus"},{"id":"reference/preferences","url":"reference/preferences.html","title":"Preferences","section":"Reference","level":"reference","description":"Every preference of the editor: what it does, where you change it, what it is by default, and how it differs from the settings of a project.","headings":[["where-kept","Where preferences are kept"],["menu","The Preferences menu"],["look","Look and layout"],["preview","The Preview"],["new","New objects and animations"],["output","Glyphs and export"],["palettes","Palettes"],["project-settings","Project settings are not preferences"],["remembered","What else the browser remembers"]],"chunks":[[null,"Every preference of the editor: what it does, where you change it, and what it is the first time you open the app. Preferences belong to you and your browser; the settings of a project are a different thing, at the end of the page."],["where-kept","A preference is kept in the browser you use, for you, and not in a project file. It is the same for every project you open, and it does not travel with a project: open the same file on another computer and the editor behaves as that computer’s browser remembers. Change one and it takes effect at once; there is nothing to save and nothing to undo. If the browser blocks its storage (some private windows do), the preferences still work while the tab is open, but they are not kept: the next visit starts from the defaults. Clearing the site data of the editor in your browser resets all of them, and also removes the autosaves, your templates, your palettes and the recordings the browser holds, so save your projects to files first."],["menu","Most preferences are in the Preferences menu, grouped by what they do. The others are in the Edit and View menus, or on a button of the panel they change, and the tables below say where. The Preferences menu with Scale step open: a tick marks the choice in use. The entries are described one by one in Every menu; here they are described by what they change."],["look","How the editor looks and how its panels are arranged. Look and layout Preference Where you change it What it does Default Theme View ▸ Theme View ▸ Toggle theme the button at the end of the menu bar Dark, Light, or System, which follows the colour scheme of your operating system as it changes. The button and Toggle theme flip between dark and light and keep that choice. See The interface. Dark Panels View ▸ Panels the chevron in a panel’s header dragging the gaps between panels Folds the Creator, the Animations panel and the Timeline to their title bars, and sets their sizes. The browser remembers both, so the layout comes back as you left it. In the stacked layout of a narrow window there is nothing to fold. See The interface. All three shown, at their standard sizes Zen mode Preferences ▸ Zen mode An arrangement for small windows: the timeline is a column on the left, drawn vertical, and the Preview takes all the rest. The Creator and the Animations panel are behind two buttons of a strip along the Preview, each opening its panel as a half of a drawer over it. The browser also remembers the side of the Preview the strip is on, which halves are open, the drawer’s height and where its halves meet. See The interface. Off Creator list the key beside the search in the Creator, which switches to the other view Shows the formulas as rows with their rendering, or as a grid of blocks. See Formulas. List Previews of the Creator list the +/− key of the Creator’s toolbar (a click larger, a right-click smaller), in the list view How tall the drawing of each card of the list view is: 1×, 1.5×, 2× or 3× its standard height of 52 pixels. The block view has a size of its own. See Formulas. 1× Preview tooltip of the blocks the +/− key of the Creator’s toolbar (a click larger, a right-click smaller), in the block view How large the tooltip beside a block is, with its drawing: 1×, 1.5×, 2× or 3×, the drawing as many times wider and taller. The list view has a size of its own. See Formulas. 1× Glyph list the Glyph list key beside the Glyphs title in the formula editor and the text editor Lists the glyphs under the drawing as keys, each with its index and label: the way to pick glyphs from the keyboard. It takes room, so it shows only when asked for. See Picking glyphs. Off Glyph list of the pair editors the Glyph list key in the relation editor and in the pair editor of a Morph block The same list under each of the two formulas of a pair editor: one choice for the two editors. See Relation editor. Off Formula keypad the ⌨ Keyboard key beside the LaTeX title of the formula editor, which shows and hides it Whether the on-screen keypad of the formula editor, under the visual field, was left shown: it is there again the next time the editor opens, until its key hides it. See Formulas. Hidden"],["preview","Aids for placing things in the Preview. Three of them are also buttons in the toolbar at the end of the Preview’s title bar, so you can switch them without opening a menu. The Preview’s toolbar: the circle selector (1), the transform handles kept shown (2) and snapping to objects (3) are preferences you switch here or in the menu. The Preview Preference Where you change it What it does Default Snap to objects Preferences ▸ Snap to objects the magnet button in the Preview’s toolbar A dragged formula sticks to the edges and centres of the other formulas and of the frame, and to the gaps between them. Hold Ctrl/⌘ after pressing to drag freely. See The Preview. On Guide colour Preferences ▸ Guide colour The colour of the guide lines that a snapped drag shows: yellow, orange, magenta, cyan, green or white. See The Preview. Yellow Guide width Preferences ▸ Guide width How thick those lines are: thin, regular or thick. See The Preview. Thin Glyph selection colour Preferences ▸ Glyph selection colour The colour of the glyphs picked with Alt in the Preview, and of the rectangle that picks them. The same six colours as the guides. See Picking glyphs. Yellow Keep the transform handles shown Preferences ▸ Keep the transform handles shown the handles button in the Preview’s toolbar Keeps the handles for scaling and rotating on the selected clips. Off, they show only while you hold Shift. See The Preview. Off Scale step Preferences ▸ Scale step Preferences ▸ Scale step ▸ Custom… The step that a corner drag’s scale snaps to. Choose a preset, or Custom… for a number or a fraction such as 1/3, from 0.01 to 2. See The Preview. 0.05 (presets 0.05, 0.1, 0.15, 0.2) Locked formulas Preferences ▸ Locked formulas How opaque the Preview draws a formula that is locked in it, of its own opacity: 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 80 or 100 %. Only the Preview is faded; nothing exported is. See The Preview. 40 % Show grid Preferences ▸ Show grid the # button in the bar under the Preview Ctrl+' Draws a grid under the formulas and makes a dragged formula snap to it (Ctrl/⌘ held frees it). The grid is never part of an export. See The Preview. Off Grid spacing Preferences ▸ Grid spacing − and + beside the # button Alt+↑ and Alt+↓ while the grid is shown The distance between grid lines, in Manim units: 2, 1, 0.5, 0.25 or 0.1. See The Preview. 1 unit Grid snaps Preferences ▸ Grid snaps the last button of the grid controls, under the Preview What the grid holds while you drag: the formula’s centre, or whichever of its edges is nearer to a line. See The Preview. The formula’s centre Show the camera’s route Preferences ▸ Show the camera’s route In the world view of a scene with a camera, draws the line with arrowheads that joins the camera’s frames in the order its moves run. See The camera. On Dim what the camera leaves out Preferences ▸ Dim what the camera leaves out In the world view of a scene with a camera, dims what the camera leaves out at the playhead. See The camera. On Circle selector the Circle selector button in the Preview’s toolbar S A click takes the symbol nearest to the pointer within a circle, instead of what is right under it: the formula, or with Alt the symbol itself. See Picking glyphs. Off Reach of the circle Shift + wheel in the Preview while the circle selector or Align by symbol is on, and over the drawing of a pair editor How far from the pointer, in screen pixels, a symbol is still picked: the radius of the circle selector, of Align by symbol and of the circle that takes glyphs in the pair editors. See Picking glyphs. 14 px (from 6 to 60) The grid controls in the bar under the Preview: show it, then sparser, the spacing, denser, and what the grid holds. Scale step ▸ Custom… opens a small dialog. It takes a number or a fraction, such as 1/3 or 0.3/0.2, with a dot or a comma for the decimals, and shows its value when you type a fraction. The dialog behind Scale step ▸ Custom…."],["new","What the objects you create from now on start with. None of these changes what is already in the project. New objects and animations Preference Where you change it What it does Default Standard scale Preferences ▸ Standard scale… The size that new formulas and new LaTeX texts are born with: 1 is Manim’s font size 48, 1.5 is 72. Saving leaves the objects that exist as they are; Apply to all formulas and Apply to all texts give the scale typed to every one of the project. See Organizing. 1 for formulas and 1 for texts (font size 48) Split suffix Preferences ▸ Split suffix… The suffix that names the copy a split makes: cutting a clip at the playhead (S) gives its second half a copy of its formula, named after it with this suffix — f1 becomes f1_s, or f1_s1, f1_s2… when that name is taken. Letters, digits and “_”, at most 16, so that the name stays a Python variable. See The timeline. _s Always add intro Edit ▸ Always add intro New clips get the intro chosen in Default intro & outro…, instead of appearing at once. A formula can choose its own with Appears with in its editor. See Intro and outro. Off Always add outro Edit ▸ Always add outro New clips get the outro chosen in Default intro & outro…, instead of disappearing at once. See Intro and outro. Off Default intro & outro Edit ▸ Default intro & outro… The animation, duration, easing, lag ratio and, for the fades, shift and scale of the intro and of the outro that the two switches above give. Restore defaults puts the standard ones back. See Intro and outro. intro Write, 1 s, linear; outro FadeOut, 1 s, smooth Suggest morph pairs Edit ▸ Suggest morph pairs A new Morph block starts with the pairs that Suggest finds for it where it lands, instead of none. A copy of a block is never given pairs. See Morph blocks. Off Standard scale: one value for new formulas and one for new LaTeX texts. Each has its own Apply to all button, which gives that scale to every such object of the project at once, as one undo step. Split suffix… opens a small dialog with one field. The menu shows the suffix in use between brackets, and the dialog shows the name it gives under the field, such as f1 → f1_s. A text that is not a suffix is refused, with the reason under the field. Split suffix: the suffix, and the name it gives a copy of f1. Default intro & outro: the animation, duration, easing and lag ratio of each, and Restore defaults."],["output","Glyphs and export Preference Where you change it What it does Default Glyph source the three Glyph source items of the View menu Which renderer draws the formulas: Automatic (the companion’s exact LaTeX glyphs when it runs, MathJax otherwise), MathJax, or LaTeX (companion). This one is not remembered: after a reload it is Automatic again. See The companion. Automatic Python target of the Export… button Export ▸ Python — ManimCE… Export ▸ Python — ManimGL… The Export… button exports Python for the target of the last Python export you opened. The browser remembers it. See Exporting. ManimCE"],["palettes","Preferences ▸ Chroma Lab… opens the colour studio that comes inside the editor, to make a palette and send it to the editor. Preferences ▸ Editor palettes… is the library of palettes you can use: you import, make, edit, activate and delete palettes of named colour roles there, up to 24. Until you activate one, the editor uses its own standard colours (the entry Manim defaults). Activating a palette changes the colours the editor offers and gives new objects and new projects, and does not recolour the project you have open; that is what Edit ▸ Apply palette… is for. The library is kept in the browser like the preferences above. Palettes and Chroma Lab teaches all of it. Editor palettes before any palette is added: Manim defaults is the one in use."],["project-settings","File ▸ Project settings… holds what belongs to the project itself: its name, the size and speed of its scene, its background, and the library it is exported for. They are saved in the project file, so they travel with it, and Apply makes all your changes one undo step. Project settings. Project settings Setting What it does New project Name The project’s name, shown in the menu bar. You can also click it there to rename the project in place. An empty name becomes Untitled. Untitled Manim target ManimCE (Community Edition) or ManimGL (3b1b): the library the formulas are measured for and the Python is written for. ManimCE Resolution The size of the scene in pixels: 1920 × 1080 (Full HD), 1280 × 720 (HD), 3840 × 2160 (4K), or a width of 16 to 7680 and a height of 16 to 4320 of your own. 1920 × 1080 Frame rate 24, 25, 30 or 60 frames a second, or a whole number from 1 to 240 of your own. Every play and wait of the exported scene lasts a whole number of frames. 60 Background The colour of the scene’s background. Black, or the background of the active palette Duration Until the last clip ends, or Fixed at a number of seconds above 0. Until the last clip ends LaTeX preamble Lines added after the packages of every formula. The companion’s LaTeX compiles them; MathJax understands \\newcommand and \\DeclareMathOperator. Empty"],["remembered","These are not preferences you set, but you will see them come back: The autosave of your work, one per tab, so a reload or a crash brings it back (see Recovering your work). The folder of File ▸ Sync folder… (Chrome and Edge keep it), and the stars you give projects in the Projects dialog. Your templates, My templates and Favorites, in the template library. The palette library of Editor palettes…. The view of the Preview (its zoom and pan) and the zoom and the track heights of the timeline are not remembered: they start afresh each time you open the editor."]],"short":"Preferences"},{"id":"reference/glossary","url":"reference/glossary.html","title":"Glossary","section":"Reference","level":"reference","description":"The words of the editor and of Manim, each defined in a sentence or two and linked to the page that teaches it.","headings":[["a","A"],["b","B"],["c","C"],["d","D"],["e","E"],["f","F"],["g","G"],["h","H"],["i","I"],["k","K"],["l","L"],["m","M"],["o","O"],["p","P"],["r","R"],["s","S"],["t","T"],["u","U"],["w","W"]],"chunks":[[null,"The words of the editor and of Manim, each in a sentence or two, with the page that teaches it. A term in italics inside an entry has an entry of its own. A B C D E F G H I K L M O P R S T U W"],["a","Anchor The point of a formula’s box where an end of a line sits: a corner, the middle of an edge or the centre, picked on a compass, or Auto, which lets Manim find where the line leaves the glyphs towards its other end. Lines and arrows Animation block See block. Appear and Disappear Two kinds of block: an Appear block brings some glyphs in at a time you choose (Write, Create, FadeIn…), a Disappear block takes them out. Animation blocks Appears with The field of a formula’s editor that says which intro its new clips get, when it should not be the one chosen in the preferences. Appearing and disappearing Audio clip A recording, or a part of one, placed on an audio track, with its own trim and volume curve. Audio Autosave The copy of your work that each browser tab keeps by itself, so a reload or a crash does not lose it. It is a safety net: File ▸ Save still writes the real file. Recovering your work"],["b","Background The colour of the scene behind everything. A setting of the project. Project settings Block A bar in the top half of a clip that animates some of its glyphs at a time: Appear, Disappear, Transform, Morph or Emphasis. Blocks that start together and last the same can form a group. Animation blocks Brace An object: a curly brace fitted to the glyphs of another clip, on the side you choose, that can carry a label. It follows what it hangs on. Braces"],["c","Camera The frame the scene is filmed through, once the multicamera is on: it can move and zoom while the video plays. In the Preview it is the sky-blue rectangle. The camera Camera move A block of the timeline’s Camera row that takes the camera to another frame over a time: a frame of its own, a target camera or the frame of 0 s. Nothing else animates while it runs. The camera Camera view The Preview looking through the camera at the playhead, as the video does, instead of showing the world with the camera’s rectangles. The camera Canonical index The position of a glyph in the saved list of glyphs of its formula (its snapshot). Every reference in a project to a glyph — a colour, a pair, a block — uses it, so a project does not depend on which renderer drew the formula. Glyph order Chroma Lab The colour studio that comes inside the editor: you make a palette there and send it to the editor’s palettes. Palettes and Chroma Lab Clip One appearance of an object on the timeline, from a start to an end. A formula can have as many clips as you like, and each clip becomes one Manim object. The timeline Comment (Slides mode) Words at an instant of the scene for whoever works on it after the export: a # NOTE in the Python, a note of the exported page and a task of its folder’s guide. Slides and the HTML page Companion An optional helper that runs on your own computer, one Python file. With it the editor draws formulas with real LaTeX, shapes font text, encodes video with ffmpeg and can render with Manim. The companion Compass The small choice of a side, a corner or the centre that appears when you align by symbol or put the end of a line on a formula. Align by symbol Copies One of the three ways (extras) several sources reach one target: the principal source transforms, and the others send a copy and stay where they are. How relations work Creator The panel on the left that holds the formulas and texts of the project, the template library and the audio recordings. A tour of the interface Custom path The path one pair of glyphs follows, shaped by hand as a spline in the Preview, instead of a straight line or the relation’s path arc. Custom paths"],["d","Distribute Spaces three or more selected clips so the gaps between neighbours are equal, horizontally or vertically. Align and distribute dt The time between two small ticks of the timeline’s ruler: the zoom’s own, or one of your own set with the − and + beside it. Clips snap to it. The timeline"],["e","Easing How the progress of an animation runs over its time: linear, smooth, double_smooth, there_and_back and the others the app offers. Manim calls it a rate function. Appearing and disappearing Emphasis A kind of block that draws attention to some glyphs without changing the scene: Indicate, Wiggle, Circumscribe, Flash or FocusOn. Animation blocks Exact mode The editor with the companion running and LaTeX installed on its machine: formulas are drawn by real LaTeX, so their glyphs and their order are the ones Manim will build. The companion Export Taking the scene out of the editor: as a Python file for ManimCE or ManimGL, as a video, as an audio file or as a web page that plays it. A project file is not an export. Exporting Extras The setting of a pair with several sources and one target that says how they reach it: Separate (each source makes its own transform), Merge (Manim) (Manim’s own alignment, where the sources beyond the first fade into the target) or Copies. How relations work"],["f","Font text An object that is plain text in any font you choose, with no LaTeX: Text (font) in the Creator. Shaping it needs the companion; until then it is drawn as placeholder boxes, unless the project file already carries its shape. LaTeX texts and font texts Formula An object of the Creator whose source is LaTeX in math mode. One formula can have many clips; its name becomes the Python variable of the exported scene. Writing formulas Frame The rectangle of the scene that the video shows. In Manim’s units it is 8 tall, and 14.22 wide at 16:9, with its origin at the centre and the y axis pointing up. The Preview"],["g","Glyph One drawn piece of a formula: a letter, a digit, an operator, a fraction bar. Colours, pairs, blocks, braces and lines name glyphs. Each has an index and a label. Picking glyphs Glyph label What a glyph is, as the editor names it: its character, or a dash for a rule such as a fraction bar. Suggest links by label in the relation editor pairs glyphs that have the same label. The relation editor Glyph source The renderer you ask for with the three Glyph source items of the View menu: Automatic, MathJax or LaTeX (companion). Preferences Group (of blocks) Blocks of one clip that start together and last the same, drawn as one block until you unfold them. Groups of animation blocks Group (of formulas) A named set of formulas that the Creator’s list keeps together, to fold and select as one. Finding and organizing Guides The coloured lines the Preview draws while a drag snaps to other formulas, the frame or the grid. The Preview"],["h","Hidden at the start A glyph that is off the screen when its clip starts, until an Appear block, a relation or a Morph block brings it in. Animation blocks Hidden track A track that the Preview does not draw. It is still exported, and the export dialog says so. The timeline"],["i","Intro and outro The animation a clip starts with and the one it ends with, drawn as the hatched ends of the clip on the timeline. The choice None makes the clip appear or disappear at once. Appearing and disappearing"],["k","Keyframe The state of a clip — position, scale, rotation, flips, opacity and colour — at a time. A Transform block of the whole formula adds one, and the clip moves to it over the block. Animation blocks"],["l","LaTeX text An object that is text with formulas inside it between dollar signs: Text (LaTeX) in the Creator. LaTeX texts and font texts Line and arrow An object drawn between two ends: a line, a dashed line, an arrow or a double arrow. Each end is free in the scene or on the glyphs of another clip. Lines and arrows Link port The small control in the lower half of a clip that appears while you hold Shift: click it, then another clip, to start a relation. How relations work Locked region The time of a camera move, drawn as a hatched band across the tracks: no animation may run in it. The camera Lock Three different things. A locked track cannot be edited on the timeline. A clip locked in the Preview is drawn and left alone there, so a click goes through it to what is under it; the timeline and the panels still edit it. And a clip locked after its first transformation (a padlock by its name): the Preview edits its transformations only through the end icon of their blocks. The timeline"],["m","ManimCE and ManimGL The two Python libraries the editor writes for: ManimCE, the Community Edition, and ManimGL, 3Blue1Brown’s original. A project has one target, and exports can be made for either. ManimCE and ManimGL Match colors A list of LaTeX patterns and the colour each one gives, applied to the glyphs of formulas across the project. Match colors MathJax The renderer that is in the browser and needs nothing installed. It draws formulas that look like LaTeX, but the order of its glyphs can differ from LaTeX’s. Glyph order Morph A kind of block that moves glyphs of a formula onto other glyphs of the same formula, the way one line of a derivation grows out of the last. Morph blocks Multicamera The switch that gives a scene its camera: with it on, the timeline has a Camera row and the Preview shows the world. The camera"],["o","Offset Where the window of a relation starts, counted from the start of the target clip. How relations work Outro See intro and outro."],["p","Package (LaTeX) A LaTeX package that a formula loads, chosen in its editor under Packages, such as physics or cancel. Writing formulas Package (.mfe) A project file that also carries its recordings, so audio travels with it. A project with no audio is usually a plain .mfe.json. Project files Pair A link in a relation between glyphs of the source formula and glyphs of the target formula: one to one, one to many or many to one. The pair says which glyph becomes which, and in what style. How relations work Palette A set of colours with names, kept in the editor’s palette library. An active palette changes the colours the editor offers and gives new objects. Palettes and Chroma Lab Pango The text library Manim uses for font text. The companion shapes text with it on Linux; on other systems font text stays as placeholder boxes. LaTeX texts and font texts Path arc The bend of the way glyphs travel in a relation, in degrees: 0 is a straight line. It is Manim’s path_arc. Custom paths Pause (Slides mode) An instant where the scene rests and the exported web page stops until its reader goes on; it may carry a note for whoever presents, and skip the wait after it. Slides and the HTML page Playhead The vertical line of the timeline that marks the time the Preview shows. Move it by clicking the ruler. The timeline Positional matching How an exported scene finds the glyphs of a formula without the companion: by where they are, not by their number, with a small helper at the top of the Python. The generated Python Preamble The LaTeX lines added after the packages of every formula, in File ▸ Project settings…. Project settings Preview The panel in the middle that draws the scene at the playhead, where you select, move and scale clips. Placing things in the Preview Project The document you work on: formulas, tracks, clips, relations and the settings of the scene, saved as one file. Project files"],["r","Rate function See easing. Region A stretch of time marked on the ruler of the timeline, from a green bracket to a red one: the next animation block, relation or clip of a formula you create takes it, from its start to its end. The timeline Relation A transformation of one clip into another: it links a source clip to a target clip through pairs of glyphs and runs during a window of the target clip. Two clips can have several. How relations work Render with Manim A way to make a video in Export ▸ Video…: the companion runs the exported Python with your own Manim. It is off unless the companion is started for it. Exporting video Renderer What draws the glyphs of a formula: MathJax in the browser, LaTeX through the companion, or Pango for font text. The Renderer badge in the menu bar says which one the project is using. A tour of the interface Role A named colour of a palette, such as background or formula: eleven are standard, and you can add roles of your own. Palettes and Chroma Lab"],["s","Scale step The step a corner drag of the Preview’s handles snaps its scale to. A preference. Preferences Shape An object fitted to a clip or to some of its glyphs: a box, a circle, an underline, a cross, a cross line or a highlight. Shapes Skip the wait (Slides mode) What a pause can do: going on from it, the exported web page and the rehearsal jump over the rest that follows, to where the next animation starts. Only where the scene stopped there: played through, in the Python and in a video, the rest stays. Slides and the HTML page Slides mode The switch of a project that puts pauses and comments on the scene’s time, for a talk: View ▸ Slides. The ruler then shows where the scene rests. Slides and the HTML page Snap The pull that makes a dragged clip or formula stick to the edges, centres and ticks near it. The Snap key of the timeline and the magnet of the Preview turn it on and off. The timeline Snapshot The list of a formula’s glyphs that the project saves, whichever renderer drew them. It defines the canonical indices. Project files Source and target Two meanings. The source of a formula is the LaTeX you type. In a relation, the source clip is where the glyphs come from and the target clip where they go. How relations work Split Dividing a block that names glyphs into several, by runs of consecutive glyphs or as you choose. Timing tools Standard scale The size new formulas and texts are born with, where 1 is Manim’s font size 48. A preference. Preferences Starts empty A box of a clip’s intro. Ticked, no glyph of the clip comes in with the intro: each appears only when a relation, a Morph block or an Appear block brings it. It makes the target of a transformation show only what was transformed. A clip can also leave only some glyphs out of its intro: the first half of a split clip leaves out those that came on screen after the cut, and its intro says how many, with Bring them back. How relations work, Splitting a clip Style (of a pair) How a glyph reaches its target: Transform moves it, Copy (source stays) sends a copy and leaves the source, Fade transform fades one out as the other fades in, Fade copy (source stays) fades a copy and leaves the source. A pair can have its own style or follow its relation’s. How relations work Suggest The buttons that propose pairs for you, linking glyphs that have the same label in reading order: Suggest links by label in the relation editor and Suggest pairs by label in the Morph editor. The relation editor, Morph blocks Sync folder The folder you pick once with File ▸ Sync folder… to hold your projects, which the Projects dialog then lists. Projects and files"],["t","Target The Manim library a project is for, ManimCE or ManimGL: a project setting. In a relation, the target is also the clip the glyphs arrive in. Project settings Target camera A frame with a name, drawn in pink in the Preview, set aside for camera moves to go to. Several moves can share one. The camera Template A formula written once, with gaps called placeholders (#1, #2…) that you fill in when you use it. The template library Timeline The panel at the bottom: tracks of clips under a ruler, with the playhead. It says when each object is on screen. The timeline Track A row of the timeline that holds clips. Visual tracks can be hidden or locked, and the first one is drawn in front; audio tracks can be muted and locked. The timeline Transform A kind of block that moves, rotates, rescales or recolours a whole object or some of its glyphs. Not to be confused with a relation, which turns one clip into another. Animation blocks"],["u","Unlink formula Giving a clip a copy of its formula of its own, so that editing one no longer changes the other. Timing tools Unpaired glyphs Glyphs of the source that no pair takes, which leave with the source clip’s outro, and glyphs of the target that no pair brings, which arrive with the target clip’s intro, or stay out when the target starts empty. A glyph that a block of its clip takes away or brings in is the block’s. How relations work"],["w","World (view) What the Preview shows while the multicamera is on: the scene around the frame, nothing cut, with the camera and the target cameras drawn as rectangles. The camera Window (of a relation) The stretch of time of the target clip during which a relation’s transformation runs, set by its offset and its duration. On the timeline it is a band you can drag and resize. How relations work"]],"short":"Glossary"},{"id":"reference/troubleshooting","url":"reference/troubleshooting.html","title":"Troubleshooting and questions","section":"Reference","level":"reference","description":"When something does not work as you expect: LaTeX errors, glyph order, glyphs missing in a Manim video, the companion, font text, files, video, locked clips, recovering work, and common questions.","headings":[["problems","Problems"],["latex-error","A formula shows an error"],["glyph-order","Pairs and colours moved when the renderer changed"],["missing-glyphs","Dots missing, or left behind, in the Manim video"],["companion-offline","The badge says MathJax and the companion is running"],["latex-missing","The companion runs but formulas still use MathJax"],["font-text","A font text is drawn as boxes"],["file-access","Open and Save work on copies"],["video","The video will not export"],["hidden-track","A hidden track is still in the export"],["locked","A clip will not move or be selected"],["dimmed","A menu entry is dimmed"],["recover","Recovering your work"],["one-tab","Use one tab at a time"],["open-fails","A file will not open"],["slow","Things take a moment"],["questions","Questions"],["online","Does the editor need the internet?"],["which-browser","Which browser should I use?"],["need-manim","Do I need Manim installed?"],["scipy","Why does my exported Python import SciPy?"],["narrow","Can I use a narrow window?"],["report","How do I report a problem?"]],"chunks":[[null,"What to do when the editor does not do what you expect. Each problem says what you see, why it happens and what to do, with the app’s own words in quotation marks so you can find the message you have."],["problems",""],["latex-error","You see. A red message on the formula’s card in the Creator, and in its editor, and the badge of the card says error. The card of a formula that uses \\qty without the physics package. The drawing is the last one that rendered; the badge and the message say that the new source did not. Why. The renderer could not typeset the source. The editor puts the renderer’s message in plain words. The ones you will meet: “Undefined control sequence \\qty.” The command is not known. When it belongs to a package the formula does not load, the message goes on: “It comes from the \"physics\" package: add it under Packages.” “\\begin{…} is never closed: add \\end{…}.” and “\\end{…} has no matching \\begin{…}.” “^ at the end needs an argument, as in x^{2}.”, “Missing close brace.” and “\\left needs a matching \\right (\\right. closes it without a delimiter).” “Something is not closed: a ^ or _ without its argument, a { without its }, or a \\left without its \\right.” when the renderer only noticed at the end. Both renderers typeset your source inside an align*, as Manim does, which is why their own messages can name it. In a formula of several lines, the message ends with (line N). What to do. Fix the source in the formula editor and apply it with Ctrl+Enter; the card updates when the render finishes. To add a package, use the Packages section of the editor. A package that MathJax cannot load is marked in that list, “MathJax cannot load \"…\": the preview ignores it. Exact mode (companion) compiles it with LaTeX.” The browser’s preview then ignores the package, and only the companion’s LaTeX shows the real result. See Writing formulas."],["glyph-order","You see. After you start the companion, or change the glyph source in the View menu, or edit a formula’s source, a card of the Creator shows “⚠ Check glyph colours, pairs and blocks”, and a colour or a pair seems to be on another glyph. Why. MathJax and LaTeX draw the same formula as the same shapes in a different order: MathJax gives \\frac{a}{b} as the a, the b, then the bar, and \\sqrt{c} as the c, then the root. A project names glyphs by number, by their canonical index, so when a different render arrives the editor carries every reference over by matching glyphs. When it is not sure, or something no longer has a glyph to stand on, it says so with that warning; its tooltip lists what was dropped (colours, hidden glyphs, pairs, glyphs of blocks). What to do. Point at the warning to read what changed, then look at the pairs in the relation editor and the colours in the formula’s editor, and Ctrl+Z an edit that went wrong: undoing a source edit gives back its glyph list. If you will export Python with exact glyph numbers, decide on the renderer first and build your pairs with the companion running; without it the exported scene finds the glyphs of a MathJax formula by position, with a helper at its top, which works but is not exact (The generated Python)."],["missing-glyphs","You see. In the video Manim renders from the exported Python, a formula has one dot of three where it has \\cdots, or pieces of a formula stay where it was when its glyphs move to the next one. The scene printed “Warning: Manim made N glyphs of a formula the editor split into M; unmatched glyphs are not animated.” when it ran. Why. The formula was drawn by MathJax, with no companion running, and MathJax split it into another number of glyphs than LaTeX does: \\cdots and the other dots, tall bars, \\neq and a few more. The scene finds Manim’s glyphs by position, and the ones it cannot reach are left out of every animation of glyphs. What to do. Start the companion, let the formulas render with LaTeX (the badge says Renderer: LaTeX), and export again. Without the companion has the whole list and what the video shows."],["companion-offline","You see. Renderer: MathJax in the menu bar. Its tooltip says “No companion at http://127.0.0.1:7799. Start it for exact LaTeX rendering: python3 companion/mfe_companion.py serve”. Why, and what to do. The editor looks for the companion at that address every five seconds while it is missing, and again whenever the window gets the focus. Check, in this order: Is it running? The command is the one in the tooltip. Run python3 companion/mfe_companion.py health too: it says what the machine gives the companion and how to get the rest. Did you open the app from disk? A page opened as a file is refused unless the companion starts with --allow-file-origin; the tooltip then shows the command with it. Or let the companion serve the built app, serve --app app/dist, and open http://127.0.0.1:7799/app/. Is the page on localhost or 127.0.0.1? The companion accepts no other origin. On Windows, the command is python. Where no Python is installed, Windows answers that name with the alias of the Microsoft Store, which runs nothing: use py or the full path of an interpreter. Then click the window once to make the editor look again. The companion has the options."],["latex-missing","You see. The tooltip of the badge says “LaTeX is not installed where the companion runs: formulas use MathJax.” With View ▸ Glyph source: LaTeX (companion) a formula shows the error “The companion cannot run LaTeX: latex or dvisvgm is not installed where it runs.” What to do. The companion needs latex and dvisvgm on its PATH: TeX Live, or MiKTeX on Windows. health prints the install command for your system."],["font-text","You see. Boxes where a font text should be, and a badge needs the companion on its card. The tooltip of Renderer counts them: “1 font text as placeholder boxes (needs the companion)”. Two font texts that nothing has shaped: boxes, and the badge needs the companion. The formula below them draws as usual. Why. A font text is shaped by Pango through the companion, and only on Linux; the companion must also know a Manim interpreter for the project’s target (--ce-python, --gl-python). It starts its text worker the first time a text needs it, which takes a few seconds. A text whose shape is saved in the project file draws from that shape with no companion at all, which is how an example text opens. What to do. On Linux, start the companion with the interpreter of your Manim, for example --ce-python path/to/venv/bin/python. On Windows and macOS font text stays as placeholder boxes, and the Python you export still works: a font text is written as Text(…) with its font, from its saved shape, or from its boxes when nothing has shaped it. See LaTeX texts and font texts."],["file-access","You see. When the editor starts, a notice that stays until you close it: “This browser works on copies of your files.” The notice of a browser with no file access, as the app words it. Why. Only Chrome and Edge let a page write a file or a folder on your disk. Firefox and Safari do not, so the editor works on copies there: the menus look the same, and nothing else tells that Save does not write the file you opened. What to do. For full file access use Chrome or Edge. Elsewhere, Save downloads a file each time, and your browser may number it (scene (1).mfe.json): keep the last one and open it again with File ▸ Open… and its Other file… button. Sync folder reads the folder once, and nothing is written into it. Your templates stay in the browser; Export all in the template library takes them out. See Projects and files."],["video","You see. In Export ▸ Video… an encoder is dimmed, with the reason under it: “This browser cannot encode video (WebCodecs is missing). Use Chrome or Edge, or the companion.” The Browser encoder needs WebCodecs. A format can also be dimmed with “(not supported by this browser at this size)”, and then “This browser cannot encode any format at this size. Try a smaller size, or use the companion.” “The companion is not running. Start it: …” and “ffmpeg is not installed on the companion’s machine (or not on PATH).” for Companion · ffmpeg. “Rendering with Manim is off. Restart the companion with --allow-manim-render (it runs the Python code the app sends).” and “No Manim interpreter is configured. Start the companion with --ce-python and/or --gl-python.” for Render with Manim. The export stops and names a size or a disk. With Companion · ffmpeg, a video over the companion’s limit (4 GiB unless it was started with another) fails with “The video is larger than 4 GiB, the most this companion writes for one video, so it was stopped…”, and nothing is saved: a video is never delivered cut short. The companion also stops an export, of ffmpeg or of Manim, when less than 1 GiB is left free on the disk of its temporary folder: “The disk of the companion’s temporary folder (…) is nearly full…”. How large a video can be tells how to raise the limit and how to write the videos on another disk. The sound of an MP4 does not play. Chrome on Linux has no AAC encoder, so an MP4 encoded in the browser carries Opus sound, and the dialog says so before you export. VLC and many players play it; some players and video editors do not. With a companion whose ffmpeg encodes AAC, a button under that warning switches to Companion · ffmpeg with MP4 in one click, which writes AAC. Specks behind moving objects. Chrome’s own H.264 encoder can leave a few pixels of a moving object behind it for up to two seconds, until the next keyframe. For an MP4 without them choose Companion · ffmpeg, which encodes H.264 with x264; without a companion, WebM · VP9 leaves far fewer. Exporting video has the rest."],["hidden-track","You see. A track you hid with the eye appears in the exported Python or video. Why. Hiding a track only takes it out of the Preview. The export dialog warns: “Track \"Notes\" is hidden in the preview but is exported like the others.” To keep something out of the scene, delete its clips. Or give the project a Fixed duration in File ▸ Project settings… and move them to start after its end: with the default duration, Until the last clip ends, the scene simply grows to take them in. See The timeline."],["locked","Why. There are three locks, and each leaves a mark: The track is locked. The padlock on its header is on. A clip on a locked track cannot be edited: its menu has what would change it greyed out; when you try to relate it, the Animations panel says “Track “Title” is locked: unlock it on the timeline to create, edit or delete relations of its clips.” The clip is locked in the Preview. A small mark, a pointer struck out, shows on the clip. The Preview draws it faded and a click goes through it to what is under it, but the timeline and the panels still edit it. Press the mark, or choose Unlock in the preview in its menu. The clip is locked after its first transformation. A padlock shows next to its name. In the Preview the clip’s end state does not change by accident: you edit a transformation through the end icon of its block. Unlocking, with a warning, brings back editing at the playhead. See Placing things in the Preview."],["dimmed","You see. An entry in grey that does nothing when you choose it. Why. Nothing can use it yet. Undo and Redo wait for something to take back or bring back. Copy properties needs exactly one clip selected, and Paste properties a copy and a clip to paste it on. The entries of Align need clips selected in the Preview (all but By symbol…), and those of Distribute three or more. Apply palette… needs an active palette, and Delete formula a formula selected in the Creator. In the right-click menus, rest the pointer on a dimmed entry: its tooltip says why, for example that a formula with only one clip has nothing to unlink from. What to do. Select what the entry acts on, then open the menu again. Every menu says what each entry needs."],["recover","The editor keeps a safety copy, an autosave, of the project of each browser tab as it changes. It is not your file: File ▸ Save still writes that. You reloaded the tab, or it crashed. The tab brings its work back when it opens again, and the menu bar says “Recovered, not saved”: the work is in the autosave and in no file yet, so save it. You closed a tab by mistake. A tab you open next takes over the work of a tab that is gone (a crashed one after a minute). The work of other closed tabs stays listed under File ▸ Recover autosave for a week, up to five of them. The browser restarted after closing unexpectedly. Each tab gets its work back within a minute. A tab you have not used yet gets it by itself, with the notice “Restored from the autosave”; a tab you have started to use gets the notice “Work from this tab's last session is available”, with a Recover button. “Could not restore the autosaved project”. The notice has a Download it button that keeps a copy of the autosave as a file. The browser keeps a copy too, set apart so that new work never writes over it: one for each autosave that could not be read, the five most recent. “No autosave in this browser”. The browser does not let the app keep one: its storage is blocked or full. Save to a file to keep your work."],["one-tab","The editor is made for one browser tab at a time. A second tab starts a project of its own, so if you open one by mistake and see an empty project, your work is still in the first. Each tab keeps its own autosave, and two tabs never overwrite each other’s. What the browser shares between tabs is its storage of palettes, templates and favourites, so a change in one tab shows in the other, and a palette removed in one is gone in the other. A file saved from two tabs holds what the last save wrote: close the tab you do not work in."],["open-fails","You see. A notice, “Could not open scene.mfe.json”, with one of these messages: “The file is not valid JSON.” It is not JSON at all, or it was cut short. “This is not a Manim Formula Editor project.” It is a JSON file of something else. “The project was saved by a newer version of the app (format N; this app reads up to M).” Open it with a newer build of the editor. “The project file is malformed.” followed by the first paths that failed. Something in the file is out of range or of the wrong type. A package (.mfe) whose project does not open, one saved by a newer editor say, gives that project’s reason under its own name, “Could not open lecture.mfe”. A package is called damaged only when the package itself is. A file that opens can still say “Opened with N issues”: a project with warnings or errors, such as a glyph that no longer exists. The notice lists the first eight. It is red, and stays until you close it, when one of them is an error: the preview and the export may then not represent the project faithfully. With warnings alone it is amber. A file of an older format opens, and the editor says “Upgraded from format N”; Project files shows both notices."],["slow","A new formula takes a moment to appear with the companion: real LaTeX compiles it (up to 20 seconds before the editor gives up). The result is cached, in memory and on disk, so the same formula comes back at once next time. MathJax is in the browser and draws at once. The first font text takes a few seconds. The companion starts its text worker the first time a font text needs it, and the text looks like boxes until the worker is ready. A video takes long. It is one image for every frame of the scene. A smaller size or a lower frame rate in the dialog makes it faster; the dialog says about how large the file will be before you start."],["questions",""],["online","No. The built editor is a page that runs in your browser from files and needs no server. It makes no request to the network but one: to the optional companion on your own computer, at 127.0.0.1. Chroma Lab, the colour studio, comes inside it and works offline too."],["which-browser","Chrome or Edge. They can write your files and folders in place, and they encode video in the page. Firefox and Safari work, on copies of your files (above), and may not encode every video format."],["need-manim","Only to run the Python the editor exports, or to have the companion render with it. Writing, animating, previewing and exporting Python need nothing but the browser. The Python you export runs with ManimCE or ManimGL; see ManimCE and ManimGL."],["scipy","Because a MathJax formula’s glyphs are found by position when the scene runs, and a small helper at the top of the file does that with SciPy, which both Manim installs have. Formulas drawn by the companion’s LaTeX use exact glyph numbers and need no helper. The generated Python explains it."],["narrow","Yes. Below about 760 px wide the four panels stack, each as tall as most of the window, and they scroll down under the menu bar, which stays at the top, never sideways. Under forced colours (Windows high contrast) every state keeps a ring, a border or an underline. A tour of the interface shows both."],["report","Write to the project on GitHub. Say what you did, what you saw, and what Help ▸ About says: it gives the version of the editor, of the project format, of MathJax and of the companion."]],"short":"Troubleshooting"},{"id":"reference/files","url":"reference/files.html","title":"Project files and their format","section":"Reference","level":"reference","description":"The files the editor writes and reads: .mfe.json projects and .mfe packages, what a project stores, versions and opening older files, and where the format is specified.","headings":[["projects","Project files"],["stores","What a project stores"],["leaves-out","What a project leaves out"],["packages","Packages: .mfe"],["older","Versions and opening older files"],["when","Which version a feature needs"],["format","The format itself"]],"chunks":[[null,"What the editor writes when you save, what it reads when you open, what a project holds and what it leaves out, and what happens to a file made by an older or a newer editor."],["projects","A project is one text file in JSON, UTF-8, named after the project with the ending .mfe.json. File ▸ Save As… suggests the name for you: the project’s name in lower case, with hyphens between its words, so Completing the square becomes completing-the-square.mfe.json. File ▸ Open… opens the Projects dialog, which lists the projects of your synced folder; its Other file… button picks a file from anywhere on your computer, and that is how you open a project you downloaded, such as every example of this documentation (Projects and files teaches the dialog). Because it is plain text, a project can be read in any text editor, kept in a version-control system and compared. You do not need to edit it by hand, and the editor does not need you to. Here is a small project to download, open with the editor and read: This is its top level, with the inside of each section cut short: { \"format\": \"manim-formula-editor\", \"version\": 36, \"meta\": { \"name\": \"First transform\", \"target\": \"ce\", \"width\": 1920, \"height\": 1080, \"fps\": 60, \"background\": \"#000000\", \"preamble\": \"\", \"duration\": null }, \"formulas\": [ { \"id\": \"f_…\", \"kind\": \"math\", \"name\": \"step1\", \"source\": \"x + 3 = 5\", … }, … ], \"tracks\": [ { \"id\": \"t_…\", \"kind\": \"visual\", \"name\": \"Track 2\", \"hidden\": false, \"locked\": false, … }, … ], \"clips\": [ { \"id\": \"c_…\", \"formulaId\": \"f_…\", \"trackId\": \"t_…\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 3.2, … }, … ], \"audioAssets\": [], \"audioClips\": [], \"relations\": [ { \"id\": \"r_…\", \"fromClipId\": \"c_…\", \"toClipId\": \"c_…\", \"pairs\": [ … ], … }, … ], \"colorMatches\": [] } The top of every project says what it is: format is always manim-formula-editor, and version is the number of the format it was written in (below)."],["stores","Everything that makes the scene, and nothing that is only how you happen to be looking at it. The sections of a project file Section It holds meta The settings of the scene: name, Manim target, size, frame rate, background, LaTeX preamble and duration. They are what File ▸ Project settings… shows. formulas Every object of the Creator: its kind (formula, LaTeX text, font text, brace, shape or line), its name, its LaTeX source and packages, its size and colour, the colour of each glyph, the glyphs hidden at the start, its intro, and a snapshot of its glyphs. tracks The rows of the timeline, top to bottom, each with its name, whether it is visual or audio, and whether it is hidden, muted or locked. clips Each appearance of an object on the timeline: which object, which track, when it starts and ends, its position, scale, rotation, flips, opacity and colour, its intro and outro, whether it starts empty and the glyphs it leaves out of its intro, its animation blocks and, for a brace, a shape or a line, what it is attached to. relations The transformations between clips: the two clips, the window of the transformation, its easing, its default style and path arc, and the pairs of glyphs, with the custom paths you shaped. A style, the relation’s or a pair’s own, is transform, copy, fade or fade_copy. audioAssets, audioClips The recordings the project owns, and their instances on audio tracks, with their trims and volume curves. colorMatches The project’s lists of match colours. formulaGroups The groups of formulas of the Creator’s list. It is left out until you make a group. textGeometry Only in a .mfe.json: the saved shapes of the font texts, so they draw as they were saved with no companion. A snapshot of its glyphs. A formula keeps the list of its glyphs as the renderer drew them. Every reference to a glyph in the project, a colour, a pair, a block, is a number in that list, so a project does not depend on which renderer is running when you open it. Canonical index and Glyph order say more."],["leaves-out","Preferences. They are the browser’s, not the file’s: Preferences lists them. Palettes. Choosing a palette, or applying one, copies plain colour values into the fields of the project. A project keeps no palette and no role names, so it looks the same on a machine that has no such palette. How you were looking. The selection, the playhead, the zoom and pan of the Preview, the zoom and the track heights of the timeline, and the undo history are not saved. Open a project and they start afresh. An export. A Python file or a video is made from a project; it is not the project. Keep the project."],["packages","A project that has audio recordings cannot be a text file: the recordings are bytes. It is saved as a package, ending in .mfe, which holds the project and every recording it owns, so that a project and its sound travel in one file. File ▸ Save As… suggests the .mfe ending for such a project. If you try to save it into a .mfe.json file, the editor refuses: “A project with recordings, or with more text geometry than a .mfe.json carries, needs a .mfe package destination. Use Save As and keep the .mfe suffix.” A package is not a ZIP file and you cannot unzip it. It is one file made for the editor: a header, the project, then the recordings. Open it like any project: File ▸ Open…, then Other file… for a file that is not in your synced folder. A project with font texts keeps their shapes inside its .mfe.json, up to 64 MiB of them. Beyond that it is saved as a package too. A recording that the browser no longer holds when you save can be left out if you say so: the package then says which, the project opens with that recording marked, and you import it again. A project that has no recordings can be saved as a .mfe.json again with Save As…."],["older","The format has a version, a whole number that grows each time a feature needs a field an older editor could misread. The editor always saves the current version, and it opens every version up to the current one. An older file is upgraded in memory as it opens, and the editor says so. The file on disk is not touched until you save, and then it is saved in the current format. A newer file is refused, with a message that names its version and the newest one this editor reads. Use a newer build of the editor. A file that is not a project is refused too, and nothing about the open project changes. A package (above) whose project the editor does not read, a newer one say, is refused with the reason a .mfe.json of that project gets: “Could not open lecture.mfe”, “The project was saved by a newer version of the app (format 99; this app reads up to 36).” A package is called damaged only when the package itself is. Opening a small project of format 1, kept for this picture. A file of a later format. A JSON file that is something else. A file that opens can still say “Opened with N issues”: its content breaks a rule the editor checks, such as a glyph that does not exist or two clips on one track that overlap. It opens anyway — the notice is red when one of the issues is an error, amber when they are warnings alone —, and the issues show where they apply: the Animations panel, the timeline, the Python export dialog. Troubleshooting lists the messages. A number past what the editor’s fields take is one of those errors too, since a file can hold any number: a dashed line whose dashes are shorter than 0.01, the path arc of a relation or of a Morph block past a full turn (360° either way), a scene, a clip or a sound that ends after 24 hours. Bring the value back within that range, in its field or on the timeline, and the error goes."],["when","An editor refuses every file of a later version than its own, whatever the file holds, and this editor always saves the current version, 36: a project saved here does not open in an older build. A feature is part of the format from some version on, so a file older than that version cannot hold it. The versions, in order: Format versions and the features a file can use from them Format version Features a file can use 1 to 8 Formulas, tracks, clips, relations with pairs of glyphs, hidden glyphs, intros and outros, Appear, Disappear, Transform and Morph blocks, how several sources reach one target, match colours, custom paths. 9 to 14 Objects of several kinds: LaTeX texts, braces with labels, shapes, and Emphasis blocks. 15 to 19 Copies pairs that remember their principal source, audio tracks and recordings, font texts, their saved shapes inside the file, and groups of blocks. 20 to 25 Clips locked in the Preview, braces and shapes over several clips, groups of formulas, the fill of a shape and its transform to another target, where a text’s new clips start, and the cross line. 26 to 30 Lines and arrows, the anchors of their ends, curves and tips, GrowArrow, and the Transform of a line’s ends. 31 Clips that start empty, and several relations between the same two clips. 32 A path of its own for one route of a pair (one source glyph on its way to one target glyph), and pairs of one relation that bring the same targets. 33 Glyphs a clip leaves out of its own intro, besides its formula’s hidden glyphs: a split leaves out of its first half the glyphs that only come on screen after the cut. The style Fade copy, for a relation, a Morph block or a pair. 34 The camera of the scene: where it frames the scene at the start, target cameras with a name, and camera moves in time. 35 The Slides mode: pauses and comments at instants of the scene (Slides and the HTML page). 36 A pause that skips its wait: going on from it, the exported page and the rehearsal jump to where the next animation starts (A pause that skips its wait)."],["format","The format is written down, field by field, in FORMAT.md in the editor’s repository: every section of a project, the rules that the editor checks when it opens one, the layout of a package, and a table of what each version changed and how an older file is upgraded. The editor’s own code is the final word: the types in app/src/model/types.ts and the checks that mirror them in schema.ts. The repository’s examples/ folder holds projects to read next to it, every one of the current format. A project is data you own. If you ever need to find out how something was stored, open the file in a text editor, look for the name of the formula you are after, and read around it."]],"short":"Files and format"},{"id":"tutorials/index","url":"tutorials/index.html","title":"Tutorials","section":"Tutorials","level":"tutorial","description":"Complete animations from start to finish, from a linear equation to an annotated identity.","headings":[["gallery","The tutorials"],["what-you-get","What each tutorial gives you"],["levels","Levels"],["before","Before you start"]],"chunks":[[null,"Complete animations from start to finish, from a linear equation to an annotated identity. Each one is a worked example: you see what it makes, download the project, and build it step by step."],["gallery","They go from gentle to demanding. The basic ones start from your first animation; the later ones use features that the other sections of these pages teach. 01 Solving a linear equation Solve a linear equation step by step, each step a transformation. Basic 02 The Pythagorean theorem, in colour Animate the Pythagorean theorem, each of its terms in a colour of its own. Basic 03 Deriving the quadratic formula Complete the square and derive the quadratic formula, one step after another. Intermediate 04 A derivative from first principles Derive the derivative of x² from its limit definition, row by row, with Morph blocks. Advanced 05 An identity with braces and arrows Explain a² − b² = (a − b)(a + b) with a labelled brace under each side and an arrow. Advanced"],["what-you-get","A tutorial is more than a recipe. Each one ends with something you keep. The finished animation, playing in the page, with its timeline. The project, a .mfe.json file you download and open with File ▸ Open… and its Other file… button, to change or to check your work against. The Python the editor exports for it, for ManimCE and ManimGL. The video that ManimCE renders from that Python. The steps to build it yourself, each shown in the editor. You do not need Manim to follow a tutorial: the editor plays the animation itself. You need Manim only to run the exported Python (Exporting says how)."],["levels","The level of a tutorial says what it asks of you. Level What it assumes Basic Only Your first animation: formulas, clips, an intro, a relation, an export. Intermediate The Basics and Transformations sections: relations in detail, styles, timing. Advanced Features beyond those sections, such as Morph blocks, braces and arrows."],["before","The editor open in your browser. If it is not running yet, see Getting the app running. An empty project for each tutorial: File ▸ New. The tour of the interface, if the names of the panels are new to you: the Creator, the Preview, the Animations panel and the Timeline. New to the editor? Start with Your first animation, and go on to Solving a linear equation."]],"short":"All tutorials"},{"id":"tutorials/linear-equation","url":"tutorials/linear-equation.html","title":"Solving a linear equation","section":"Tutorials","level":"tutorial","description":"Solve a linear equation step by step, each step a transformation.","headings":[["what-you-make","What you will make"],["before","Before you start"],["steps","Step by step"],["formulas","1. Write the five formulas"],["colour","2. Colour the glyphs that travel"],["clips","3. Put the clips on the timeline"],["time","4. Give each formula time"],["relations","5. Relate the formulas"],["play","6. Play it"],["export","7. Export it"],["result","The result"],["try","Try changing it"]],"chunks":[[null,"Make the board of a maths lesson: 2x + 3 = 11 is solved in four moves, and each move is a transformation. The 3 that crosses the equals sign is gold, so the eye follows it. You write five formulas, place their clips, relate them in pairs and export the scene."],["what-you-make","Sixteen seconds of algebra. The first formula writes itself in. Each formula stays long enough to read, and then turns into the next: the 3 crosses the equals sign and the plus becomes a minus, 11 − 3 collapses into 8, the 2 drops under the 8, and the fraction becomes 4. Along the way you meet the moves that every animation of this kind repeats: writing formulas and naming them; colouring a glyph, so a viewer can follow it; placing clips on two tracks, each overlapping the next; relating two clips by label, and linking glyphs by hand where labels cannot; choosing how a group of glyphs becomes one (Fade transform); exporting the scene for Manim. It takes about half an hour. It is the work of Your first animation, five times over, so do that one first if you have not."],["before","Open the editor with an empty project (File ▸ New). Name it: click Untitled in the menu bar, type Solving a linear equation and press Enter. The scene of the export will take that name. Then tick two preferences in the Edit menu: Always add intro (Write) (1) and Always add outro (FadeOut) (2). From then on every new clip starts with a Write and ends with a FadeOut, so you do not have to give them one by one. The two preferences, ticked: Always add intro (Write) (1) and Always add outro (FadeOut) (2). These are the five formulas. Each has a name, which becomes a Python variable in the export: a name takes letters, digits and underscores, and does not start with a digit. Name LaTeX What happens to it equation 2x + 3 = 11 The equation to solve. subtract 2x = 11 - 3 The 3 crosses the equals sign, and the plus becomes a minus. simplify 2x = 8 11 − 3 is 8. divide x = \\frac{8}{2} Both sides are divided by 2: the 2 drops under the 8. answer x = 4 8 over 2 is 4. If you want to skip a part, this project holds the work of the first four steps: the formulas, the colours and the clips, with nothing related yet. Open it with File ▸ Open…: press Other file… in the dialog and pick the downloaded file (Projects and files explains the dialog). Then go on from step 5."],["steps",""],["formulas","For each formula in the table: Press + New. Click the Raw LaTeX field and type the LaTeX of the table. It keeps exactly what you type, a fraction included. Type the name in the Name field and press Enter. Type 2 in the first box of Size, and press Enter: the formulas are drawn twice as large as the default. Press Apply, then Close. When the five are written, the Creator lists them. Its list scrolls; this picture was taken in a taller window to show them all. The five formulas in the Creator."],["colour","The 3 changes sides in the first move, and the 2 changes place in the third. Colour them, and the eye can follow them. The glyph view of the formula editor does it. Press the pencil on the card of equation to open its editor. In the Glyphs column, click the 3 (1). It is outlined: it is selected. Click the gold swatch (2). The 3 takes the colour. Press Close. The glyph picked (1) and the swatch chosen (2). Colour three more glyphs the same way. A glyph colour belongs to the formula, so every clip of the formula shows it. Formula Glyph Colour equation the 3 gold subtract the 3, the last glyph gold simplify the 2, the first glyph blue divide the 2, under the bar blue Colouring glyphs and objects has the other ways to colour: the hex field, colours in order, and copying a sequence of colours."],["clips","Drag each card from the Creator onto a track. Alternate the two tracks, and start each clip three seconds after the one before. The timeline snaps clips to its ticks, half a second apart, so a drop near a time lands on it. Formula Track Drop it at equation Track 2 0 s subtract Track 1 3 s simplify Track 2 6 s divide Track 1 9 s answer Track 2 12 s The timeline is wider than its panel. Press the key that shows the whole timeline, in its toolbar, to see every clip. Each clip starts with a Write and ends with a FadeOut, from the two preferences you ticked."],["time","Every clip is three seconds long, so clips that start three seconds apart only touch, and a relation needs them to overlap. Lengthen each clip by one second: select it, open the Timing tab of the Animations panel, type the new End (1) and press Enter. Or drag the right edge of the clip. Formula End equation 4 subtract 7 simplify 10 divide 13 answer 16 The Start and End of a clip (1), and the Animation of its intro (2). Each clip now overlaps the next by a second, and a formula stays on screen, still, for two seconds between its arrival and its departure. Last, let the answer stay: select the clip of answer, scroll the panel down to its Outro section, and choose None (disappear) as the Animation. The five clips. Click the picture to see it larger."],["relations","Relate each formula to the next: four relations. For each pair of neighbours, do what you did in your first animation. Hold Shift, click the port of the first clip, click the second clip, press Suggest links by label and Accept all, and press Save relation. The first pair needs nothing else. equation to subtract. The glyphs that are the same symbol are linked: 2, x, =, 3 and the two 1s. The + has no partner and leaves, and the − arrives. The first relation: what the editor says (1), the pairs (2) and Save relation (3). subtract to simplify. Suggesting links by label links the 2, the x and the =. The right side is left: nothing in 11 − 3 is the same symbol as 8. Link it by hand. Click the first 1 of the top formula, Shift-click the second 1, the − and the 3, then click the 8 of the bottom one. The four glyphs are one pair. In the list of pairs, set the style of that pair to Fade transform, so that they fade into the 8. Press Save relation. Four glyphs picked (1): the first with a click, the others with Shift. A click on the 8 (2) makes the pair. simplify to divide. By label again: the 2 goes under the 8, and the x, the = and the 8 go to their places. The fraction bar has no partner, so it arrives with the intro of divide. divide to answer. The x and the = are linked by label. Link the fraction by hand: click the 8, Shift-click the 2 and the bar, then click the 4, and set the style of the pair to Fade transform. Press Save relation. The last relation: the pair of three glyphs and one (1), and Save relation (2). Switch from Link to Preview in the relation editor before you save: the transformation loops, so you see a pair that looks wrong before it is in the project."],["play","Move the playhead to the start with Home and press Space. The first formula writes itself in; each next formula takes shape as the glyphs of the one before travel into it, and the glyphs with no partner are written in. If a move is too quick, select its relation by clicking its band on the timeline and make its Duration longer in the Animations panel; the clips must then overlap by that much (Transforming one formula into another says how the window works)."],["export","Choose Export ▸ Python — ManimCE…. The scene is named after your project: SolvingALinearEquation. Press Download SolvingALinearEquation.py. The dialog is the one of your first animation, and Exporting explains its options. Run it from the folder of the file, with the command at the top of the file: manim -r 1920,1080 --fps 60 SolvingALinearEquation.py SolvingALinearEquation That renders at 1920×1080 and 60 frames a second. For a quick look, run manim -ql -p SolvingALinearEquation.py SolvingALinearEquation instead: -ql renders at 854×480 and 15 frames a second, and -p plays the video when it is done. Leave out -r and --fps, which would override -ql."],["result","This is what ManimCE renders from the exported scene with -ql, the quick low-quality render (854×480 at 15 frames a second): And this is the Python the editor exports, for both libraries. It is plain Manim: one MathTex per formula, then a self.play(…) per step of the timeline. Open the project in the editor, change it, and export again:"],["try","Solve another equation: write five new formulas, for 3x - 4 = 11 say, and follow the same steps. Make a transformation slower: select its band and lengthen its Duration, or change its Easing. Line up the equals signs of the five formulas with Align by symbol, so that the sign stays still while the rest moves. Make the next tutorial: The Pythagorean theorem, in colour."]],"short":"A linear equation","difficulty":"basic"},{"id":"tutorials/pythagoras","url":"tutorials/pythagoras.html","title":"The Pythagorean theorem, in colour","section":"Tutorials","level":"tutorial","description":"Animate the Pythagorean theorem, each of its terms in a colour of its own.","headings":[["what-you-make","What you will make"],["before","Before you start"],["steps","Step by step"],["step-theorem","1. Write the theorem"],["step-colour","2. Colour its three terms"],["step-more","3. Write and colour the other two"],["step-timeline","4. Put the formulas on the timeline"],["step-intro","5. Let the first formula write itself"],["step-link","6. Link the glyphs of the first two formulas"],["step-link-two","7. Link the second pair"],["step-play","8. Play it"],["step-export","9. Export it"],["result","The result"],["further","Ideas to go further"]],"chunks":[[null,"Animate a² + b² = c² so that each term keeps its colour while it turns into a number and then into a square. On the way you write three formulas, colour their glyphs, put them on the timeline, link them and export the scene."],["what-you-make","Three lines of one derivation: the theorem, the 3-4-5 triangle that makes it concrete, and the sum of two squares that equals a third. The term a² is blue, b² gold and c² green, and each keeps its colour as it becomes 3² and then 9. You will end with a project file, the Python that Manim runs, and a video like the one at the end of this page. Everything is in your browser. Manim itself is only needed for the very last step."],["before","Open the editor and start an empty project with File ▸ New. Getting the app running tells how to open it. Give the project its name: click Untitled, the project's name in the menu bar, type The Pythagorean theorem, in colour and press Enter. The Manim scene you export in step 9 is named after it. If you have not used the editor, Your first animation is a shorter version of this tutorial, without colour. You can download the finished project and open it at any point, to compare with yours: choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… and pick the file. Projects explains that dialog."],["steps",""],["step-theorem","In the Creator press + New. The editor of a new formula opens. Click the Raw LaTeX field and type a^2 + b^2 = c^2. Press Apply (or Ctrl+Enter). The glyph view on the right draws the formula and counts eight glyphs. Type theorem in the Name field and press Enter. The name becomes a variable in the Python and the label of the clip on the timeline. Type 2 in the first box of Size, the scale, and press Enter. The box at its right then reads 96, Manim's font_size: twice its default of 48, which reads better in a video. The editor after step 1: the name (1), the size (2), the LaTeX (3) and Apply (4)."],["step-colour","Each glyph has a number, its place in the formula: a is 0 and its exponent 1, the + is 2, b and its exponent are 3 and 4, and so on up to the exponent of c, 7. You colour glyphs by selecting them in the glyph view. Drag a box over the a and its exponent. The box selects every glyph it touches. Click the blue swatch under the drawing. Do the same for b and its exponent with the gold swatch, and for c and its exponent with the green one. A box over a and its exponent, then the blue swatch; the same for b with the gold one. Press Close. Colouring glyphs and objects shows the other ways to select glyphs and to give them colours."],["step-more","Press + New twice more, and give each formula its LaTeX, its name, a size of 2 and its colours the same way: Name LaTeX Colours numbers 3^2 + 4^2 = 5^2 3 and its exponent blue, 4 and its exponent gold, 5 and its exponent green squares 9 + 16 = 25 9 blue, 16 gold, 25 green The Creator after step 3. Colouring each formula by hand takes a minute. Once the formulas are on the timeline you can copy the colours of one onto the next with Colours in order, or colour all three at once with a list of Match colors."],["step-timeline","Drag each card of the Creator onto a track of the timeline. A track holds clips that do not overlap, so use both: theorem at 0 s on the upper track, numbers at 2 s on the lower track, squares at 4 s on the upper track again. A dropped clip is three seconds long, and its start snaps to the ticks of the ruler. The clips overlap on purpose: a transformation starts when the second clip does, while the first is still on screen. The timeline at the end of step 7 shows how it should look."],["step-intro","Right-click the clip of theorem and choose Intro ▸ Write. The formula now draws itself during the first second, as Manim's Write does. The Intro submenu of a clip."],["step-link","A relation says which glyphs of one clip become which glyphs of the next. Select the clip of theorem. In the Animations panel, under Relations: Choose numbers in Relate with, and press Create relation…. The relation editor opens: the source above, the target below. Press Suggest links by label. The glyphs that have the same label, the exponents, the + and the =, are proposed as dashed links. Press Accept all. Click the a of the source, then the 3 of the target: a click on a glyph of one side followed by a click on the other links them at once. Do the same for b and 4, and for c and 5. Press Save relation. After Suggest links by label (1): five dashed links, and Accept all (2). All eight glyphs linked, ready for Save relation."],["step-link-two","Select the clip of numbers, relate it with squares and open the relation editor again. This time several glyphs become one or two, so you select them first: Shift+click glyphs of a side to select more than one, then click a glyph of the other side, or press Link when both sides hold several. In numbers In squares How 3 and its exponent 9 Shift+click both, then click the 9 + + click, click 4 and its exponent 1 and 6 Shift+click both, Shift+click the 1 and the 6, press Link = = click, click 5 and its exponent 2 and 5 as for the 4 Press Save relation. The relation editor explains every control. The second relation, as the finished project has it. The timeline after step 7: a band joins the clips of each relation."],["step-play","Press the Play button under the Preview, or Space. Each term keeps its colour as it turns into the next one. The Preview at 2.5 seconds, half way through the first transformation, from theorem to numbers."],["step-export","Choose Export ▸ Python — ManimCE… and press Download ThePythagoreanTheoremInColour.py (1). The button names the file after the scene, and the scene is named after the project, as you set up in Before you start. The first lines of the file say how to render it at the project's size, 1920×1080 at 60 frames a second: manim -r 1920,1080 --fps 60 ThePythagoreanTheoremInColour.py ThePythagoreanTheoremInColour That takes a while. For a quick draft, take -r 1920,1080 --fps 60 out and put -ql in its place, a low-quality 854×480 at 15 frames a second, and add -p to play the video when it is done: manim -pql ThePythagoreanTheoremInColour.py ThePythagoreanTheoremInColour Adding -ql to the first command would change nothing: Manim applies -r and --fps after the quality flag. The Python export dialog, with Download ThePythagoreanTheoremInColour.py (1). The warnings in the dialog say that, without the companion, the scene finds the glyphs of each formula by their position when it runs. The Python page explains why, and the generated file works as it is. The generated Python goes through it, and Exporting covers ManimGL, video and audio."],["result","This is the Python the editor exports for the project you made, for each Manim: And this is the scene as ManimCE renders it from the ManimCE file:"],["further","Make the third line stay longer, and add a block that makes 25 indicate itself. Swap the colours for the roles of a palette and apply it. Change a pair of the second relation to the copy style, so that the source stays, and watch what a relation does."]],"short":"Pythagoras in colour","difficulty":"basic"},{"id":"tutorials/quadratic-formula","url":"tutorials/quadratic-formula.html","title":"Deriving the quadratic formula","section":"Tutorials","level":"tutorial","description":"Complete the square and derive the quadratic formula, one step after another.","headings":[["what-you-make","What you will make"],["before","Before you start"],["plan","The plan"],["steps","Step by step"],["step-formulas","1. Write the seven formulas"],["step-timeline","2. Put them on the timeline"],["step-1","3. Divide by a"],["step-2","4. Move the constant term across the equals sign"],["step-3","5. Add the same term to both sides"],["step-4","6. Factor the left side, simplify the right"],["step-5","7. Take the square root"],["step-6","8. Isolate x"],["step-colour","9. Colour the terms"],["step-last","10. Look at it as a whole"],["step-export","11. Export"],["result","The result"],["variations","Try changing it"]],"chunks":[[null,"Seven formulas, six relations: the classic derivation of the quadratic formula by completing the square, glyph by glyph. You write the formulas, put them on the timeline, relate each one to the next, colour the terms, and export the Python and the video."],["what-you-make","A twenty-second animation in which each formula turns into the next: the a splits to divide everything, a term hops over the equals sign, new terms are written in, a block of glyphs is simplified, and two denominators become one. This is the same scene as ManimCE renders it from the Python the editor exports: Along the way you practise what the pages on relations explain: one glyph that goes to two places, and a block of six glyphs linked in one go; a term that crosses the equals sign along an arc, with its sign flipping by a fade; glyphs that no pair brings, which arrive with the intro of the next formula, and glyphs that no pair takes, which leave with the outro; two glyphs that merge into one; colours that follow the terms, set once for all the formulas with Match colors."],["before","You need the editor open in a browser, with a new project (File ▸ New). Name it: click Untitled in the menu bar, type Deriving the quadratic formula and press Enter. The scene of the export will take that name. To follow the tutorial you do not need Manim; you need ManimCE only if you want to render the video yourself at the end. The tutorial assumes you can write a formula and place a clip on the timeline; Writing formulas and Working with the timeline explain both, and How relations work and The relation editor are the pages this one puts to work. If you prefer to read first, download the finished project above and open it with File ▸ Open…, then Other file… in the dialog that opens (Projects explains it): you can compare your own steps with it at any time. The glyph numbers in this tutorial are the ones of the editor’s own renderer (MathJax). With the companion running, the editor draws exact LaTeX glyphs and may number them differently; the pairs you make by clicking are the same, and only the numbers quoted here would change. Hold the pointer over a glyph in the relation editor to read its number."],["plan","Each formula is written once, and each relation turns one formula into the next while the clips overlap on the timeline. # Name LaTeX Track Start End 1 standard_form ax^2+bx+c=0 Track 2 0 4.1 2 divide_by_a x^2+\\frac{b}{a}x+\\frac{c}{a}=0 Track 1 2.7 6.9 3 move_constant x^2+\\frac{b}{a}x=-\\frac{c}{a} Track 2 5.3 9.3 4 add_to_both_sides x^2+\\frac{b}{a}x+\\frac{b^2}{4a^2}=-\\frac{c}{a}+\\frac{b^2}{4a^2} Track 1 8.1 12.5 5 complete_the_square \\left(x+\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)^2=\\frac{b^2-4ac}{4a^2} Track 2 10.5 15.3 6 square_root x+\\frac{b}{2a}=\\pm\\frac{\\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a} Track 1 13.7 18.1 7 solution x=\\frac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a} Track 2 16.5 20.6 The times follow one rule: a clip starts when the relation that brings it begins, and ends when the relation that takes it away ends. Consecutive clips are on different tracks, so they can overlap, and a relation can only link clips that overlap."],["steps",""],["step-formulas","Press + New in the Creator. The formula editor opens on a new formula. Give it its name (1), type its LaTeX in the Raw LaTeX field (3), and press Apply (4). Do it for each row of the table. The name is what the clip shows on the timeline, and the name of the formula in the exported Python. The first formula in the editor. The size (2) multiplies Manim’s 48: 1.5 gives a font size of 72, which fits the longest formula in the frame. When all seven are in the Creator, make them the same size in one go: point at the ∑ key of the toolbar and choose Formulas in its menu to select every formula, right-click one, choose Edit together… and type 1.5 in Scale (1). The same row has Match colors (2), which step 9 uses. The tools for all the formulas at once: Scale (1) and Match colors (2). The Creator with the seven formulas."],["step-timeline","Drag each formula from the Creator onto the timeline: the odd ones onto Track 2, the even ones onto Track 1. A clip made by a drop lasts three seconds. Select each clip and set its Start and End in the Timing tab of the Animations panel, as in the table. Give the first clip an intro too, in the same tab: Write, 1.5 seconds. The other intros and outros come from the relation editor, below. The finished timeline. Each clip overlaps the next, and an arrow joins them: that is the relation. To skip these two steps, download the project as it is at this point, with the seven formulas on the timeline and no relation yet, and open it the same way:"],["step-1","The first relation takes ax²+bx+c=0 to x²+(b/a)x+(c/a)=0. Hold Shift, click the port of standard_form and then divide_by_a: the relation editor opens on a new relation. Link the a first. Click it in the source (glyph 0), add the two a of the denominators with Shift (glyphs 4 and 9), and press Link: one glyph goes to two places, and the a splits. Then press Suggest links by label and Accept all: the other nine pairs are glyphs that keep their label. Linking the a first matters: left to itself, Suggest would have sent it to the first denominator only. The two fraction bars belong to no pair, so they arrive with the intro of divide_by_a. In the Animation tab choose Write for the intro, tick Run it up to the end of the relation window (starts with the target clip), and set the Duration of the window to 1.4. Press Save relation. The first relation as it should look: ten pairs, and the two bars unlinked."],["step-2","Select divide_by_a and use the Relations tab: choose move_constant in Relate with and press Create relation…. Press Suggest links by label and Accept all: eleven of the twelve pairs come out right. The twelfth is the sign: link the second plus (glyph 7) to the minus (glyph 8), and set that pair’s style to Fade transform in the pair list, so that the plus fades into the minus instead of morphing. In the Animation tab give the window a Path arc of -60, so that the fraction hops over the equals sign, and a Duration of 1.6. The 0 of the source belongs to no pair: choose FadeOut for the outro and tick Run it during the relation window (ends with the source clip). Save."],["step-3","The new formula adds b²/4a² on each side. Make the relation as before. This time the c over a on the right must stay put, and Suggest would send its a and its bar to the new term on the left, which has the same labels and comes first. So link those two yourself before you suggest: the a under the c (glyph 10) to the a under the c of the target (glyph 17), and its bar (glyph 11) to the bar of the target (glyph 18). Then Suggest links by label and Accept all. The two new terms, fourteen glyphs, belong to no pair: Write for the intro, the checkbox ticked, and a Duration of 1.2."],["step-4","This is the biggest relation: 13 pairs. The term b²/4a² on the right of the source becomes the b² of the new numerator and the 4a² of the new denominator, six glyphs on each side, in the same order. Link them as one pair. In the target, press the key before the eye in the toolbar to show the glyph lists, and Shift+click in the list of the target the six glyphs: b and its exponent (10 and 11), the 4, the a and the exponent of the denominator (16, 17 and 18) and the fraction bar (19): the lists give each glyph the number quoted here. In the source, where the six glyphs stand apart, a box does it: drag one over the six glyphs on the right. Press Link. A pair of six glyphs and six glyphs links the first to the first, the second to the second, and so on, as Manim does when two groups have the same size; it works here because both groups are in the same order. The six glyphs of the target are picked from the lists already; a box over the six glyphs of the source, and Link. Link the exponent of x² (glyph 1) to the exponent of the square (glyph 8). Then Suggest and Accept all. Eight glyphs of the source belong to no pair and leave: choose FadeOut for the outro and tick the checkbox. The two parentheses arrive: Write for the intro, the checkbox ticked. Give the window 2 seconds. The fourth relation: thirteen pairs. The six glyphs of the block are a single pair (colours in the drawing are only to tell the pairs apart)."],["step-5","The square root turns (x+b/2a)² into x+b/2a, and the right side moves under a root. Three glyphs would be paired wrongly by label, so link them first: the exponent of the b² in the numerator (glyph 11) to the exponent of the b² under the root (glyph 9); the 4 of 4ac (glyph 13) to the 4 under the root (glyph 11); and the 4 of the denominator (glyph 16) to the 2 of the new denominator (glyph 16), with the style Fade transform, because a 4 turns into a 2 and morphing would look odd. Then Suggest and Accept all. Four glyphs leave (the two parentheses and two exponents): FadeOut for the outro, ticked. Three arrive (the ± and the root with its line): Write for the intro, ticked. The window lasts 1.6 seconds."],["step-6","The last relation has no glyph that arrives or leaves. Two 2a become one, glyph by glyph: click the 2 of b/2a (glyph 3), add the 2 of the denominator under the root (glyph 16) with Shift, and click the 2 of the target (glyph 13): selected sources and one click on the target link them at once, many-to-one. Do the same with the two a (glyphs 4 and 17, to 14) and the two fraction bars (glyphs 5 and 18, to 15). Two glyphs selected with Shift, one click on the target: the first of the three merges. Link the plus (glyph 1) to the minus of the numerator (glyph 2) with a Fade transform, as in step 4, then Suggest and Accept all. Give the window 1.6 seconds and save. The last relation: sixteen pairs; three of them join two glyphs into one."],["step-colour","Colouring the a, b and c of seven formulas by hand would be slow. A list of Match colors does it in one go. Choose Edit ▸ Match colors ▸ Create match colors…, the dialog opens on a new list: name it Terms, and press + Add pattern three times: the patterns a, b and c, with the colours #F4C867 (gold), #58C4DD (blue) and #FC6255 (red). A colour is typed as hex, or picked from the chip beside it. The Terms list. Select the seven formulas with Formulas in the menu of the ∑ key, right-click one and choose Apply match colors ▸ Terms. Every a, b and c takes its colour, and it is one undo step. The colours travel with the glyphs: each glyph changes its colour along its way from the source to the target."],["step-last","Select the last clip and press All related… in its Relations tab: the whole derivation appears as one chain, with every relation as an arrow. Click an arrow to select its relation, and play the scene from the start in the Preview to see the six steps in a row. The six relations at a glance: Relation Pairs Window Path arc Leave (outro) Arrive (intro) 1 → 2 10 2.7–4.1 s 0° none 2 bars 2 → 3 12 5.3–6.9 s −60° the 0 none 3 → 4 12 8.1–9.3 s 0° none 14 glyphs 4 → 5 13 10.5–12.5 s 0° 8 glyphs 2 parentheses 5 → 6 16 13.7–15.3 s 0° 4 glyphs 3 glyphs 6 → 7 16 16.5–18.1 s 0° none none"],["step-export","Choose Export ▸ Python — ManimCE… for the scene as a Python file, or Export ▸ Video… to render a video in the browser. The first lines of the file tell you how to render it: manim -r 1920,1080 --fps 60 DerivingTheQuadraticFormula.py DerivingTheQuadraticFormula"],["result","This is the Python the editor exports for the finished project, for ManimCE and for ManimGL. Each relation is one self.play(…) with an animation for every pair, and the glyphs that belong to no pair leave and arrive in the same call. The page The generated Python explains how to run it and what its parts do."],["variations","Give relation 4 a Path arc of 30° and watch the right side swing. Give the pair of the exponent the style Copy (source stays): the exponent of x² stays, and a copy goes to the square. Lengthen the window of relation 3 and choose Unwrite and Write for the unpaired glyphs. Change the colours in the Terms list and apply it again. When you want to go further, Transformation recipes has more moves of algebra, and Custom paths shows how to draw the path of a glyph by hand."]],"short":"The quadratic formula","difficulty":"intermediate"},{"id":"tutorials/derivative","url":"tutorials/derivative.html","title":"A derivative from first principles","section":"Tutorials","level":"tutorial","description":"Derive the derivative of x² from the limit definition: a five-row derivation that writes itself line by line, built with Morph blocks.","headings":[["what-you-make","What you will make"],["before","Before you start"],["steps","Step by step"],["write","1. Write the derivation"],["timeline","2. Put them on the timeline"],["first-block","3. The first Morph block: row 1 into row 2"],["write-two","4. The glyph no pair brings"],["other-rows","5. The other rows"],["colour","6. Colour the h"],["finish","7. The finish"],["play","8. Play it and export"],["result","The result"],["next","Where to go next"]],"chunks":[[null,"Derive the derivative of x² from the limit definition as a derivation that writes itself, line by line. One formula holds all five rows, Morph blocks grow each row out of the one above, and the result lights up at the end."],["what-you-make","A title, f(x) = x², and a derivation of five rows. The first row is the definition of the derivative, applied to x². The next three appear one after another, each copied out of the row above: the square is expanded, the terms that cancel are left behind, and the common h is divided out. The last row is the answer, 2x, and it swells in a flash of colour. Every h is gold, so the eye can follow the variable that goes to zero. You will use a Morph block four times, an Appear block twice, two more blocks for the finish, and the pair editor to link glyphs by groups. It is a long tutorial: the steps are the same for each row, so the first block is the one to take slowly."],["before","You can write a formula and put it on the timeline: Writing formulas and Working with the timeline. You have read Morph blocks, which explains the pair editor. This tutorial uses it without stopping to explain. No companion is needed. The glyph numbers of this project are those of the browser's own rendering (MathJax); you link by clicking glyphs, so you never type them. The finished project is below. To compare as you go, download it, choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… and pick the file (Projects explains the dialog)."],["steps",""],["write","Press + New in the Creator and give the formula the name derivation. In the Raw LaTeX field type the five rows, then press Apply. Manim sets every formula in an aligned environment, so the & before each = lines the equals signs up and \\\\ ends a row: f'(x) &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{(x+h)^2 - x^2}{h} \\\\ &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{x^2 + 2xh + h^2 - x^2}{h} \\\\ &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{2xh + h^2}{h} \\\\ &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} (2x + h) \\\\ &= 2x The formula editor after Apply: the source on the left and the five rows rendered beside it. Choose FadeIn in the Appears with menu: new clips of this formula start with that intro. Then add a second formula, title, with the source f(x) = x^2, and in its Size row type 72 in the second box, the one that shows Manim's font_size. Nothing needs hiding. A glyph that a Morph block brings in skips the intro and arrives through the block, so the clip will show only the first row when it starts."],["timeline","Right-click each formula and choose Add to the timeline, derivation first. A new clip goes on the top track, Track 2, at the playhead, and a track holds one clip at a time: the second clip lands on the same track, right after the first. So open the Timing tab of the title clip and choose Track 1 in its Track list first: Start and End stop at the neighbour on the same track. Then set the times and the place of both clips: Clip Track Start End Initial state Intro · Outro title Track 1 0 21.4 X −4.6 · Y 3.3 Write 1 s · FadeOut 1 s derivation Track 2 1 21.4 X 0 · Y −0.2 · Scale 1.2 FadeIn 1 s · FadeOut 1 s A dropped clip is three seconds long, so the End field (or the right edge of the clip) is the first thing to change: the blocks below need room until about 18 seconds. The finished project calls its two tracks Derivation and Title; the names of a new project's tracks do not matter."],["first-block","Put the playhead at 3.6 s, right-click the derivation clip and choose Morph within the formula. With the block selected, press Edit pairs… in the Animations panel. The editor draws the whole derivation twice, side by side: what can be a source at the left, what can arrive at the right. You link a glyph of row 1 at the left to a glyph of row 2 at the right. (The playhead does not have to be exactly on 3.6 s: the Start field in the block's panel sets the time.) Suggest pairs by label is not the tool here. On this block it proposes 33 pairs at once, one for nearly every glyph that has a twin, and it picks the nearest twin in reading order. This derivation repeats its symbols (x, h, 2, brackets), so that twin is not always the glyph the algebra means. Link by groups instead. Drag a box over a run of glyphs at the left, another over the matching run at the right, and press Link: one pair for the whole run. For a glyph that goes to two places, click it, then Shift-click both targets and press Link. A box dragged over = lim h→0 in the left copy selects those seven glyphs. Drag a box over the same glyphs of row 2 in the right copy, then press Link: one pair for the whole run. Seven links make the block. The first is the box you just saw. Sources (row 1) Targets (row 2) How = and lim h→0, seven glyphs the same seven a box on each side the x of (x + h) the x of x² and the x of 2xh click, then Shift-click both the exponent of (x + h)² the exponents of x² and h² click, then Shift-click both the + of (x + h) both + of x² + 2xh + h² click, then Shift-click both the h of (x + h) the h of 2xh and the h of h² click, then Shift-click both − x², three glyphs the same three a box on each side the h under the bar, and the bar the same two a box on each side The f′(x) and the brackets of (x + h) are not linked: they stay in row 1 and do not appear in row 2. Press Save pairs. If Save pairs stays grey, the problems listed in the side column of the editor say why. The usual cause is a glyph that ended up on both sides of the block: a source that you also linked as a target. The pair editor of the first block, saved, as the finished project opens it: the two copies one over the other this time, since the drawing takes the layout that shows the glyphs that can take part larger. Each group is one link; the badge at its junction gives its number and the initial of its style (I: when the pair takes the block's)."],["write-two","Row 2 has one glyph that comes from nothing: the 2 of 2xh, the coefficient the expansion creates. Put the playhead at 4.9 s, right-click the clip and choose Appear ▸ Write. In the panel of the new block pick that glyph in the glyph view (it is the 2 in front of the xh of the second row). Set its duration to 0.6 s. A glyph that no pair and no Appear block names appears with the clip's intro, with row 1. If a row shows up too early while you play it, look for a glyph you forgot."],["other-rows","The same two moves, three more times. Add each block where the table says; link with boxes and Shift-clicks as before. In the editor of a later block the glyphs of the rows already on screen are dimmed as targets and the later rows are free: that is how you know you are on the right row. The second block: three links from row 2 to row 3. The terms that cancel stay where they are, unlinked. Start Block Links (sources → targets) 7.2 s Morph, row 2 into row 3 = lim h→0 → the same (box on each side) · the numerator 2xh + h² (six glyphs) → the numerator of row 3 · the h under the bar and the bar → the same 10.2 s Morph, row 3 into row 4 = lim h→0 → the same · 2x of the numerator → the 2x of row 4 · + h of the numerator → the + h of row 4 11.6 s Appear ▸ FadeIn, 0.6 s the two brackets of row 4 14 s Morph, row 4 into row 5 the = → the = · 2x in the brackets → the 2x of row 5 Look at what stays unlinked. In row 2 the terms x² and − x² cancel, so they are not carried into row 3. In row 3 the h of 2xh and the h under the bar cancel, so row 4 starts from 2x + h. The derivation shows the algebra by what it leaves behind."],["colour","In the formula editor, in the Glyphs view on the right, pick the thirteen h of the derivation (Ctrl-click each, or drag a box over several) and give them one colour; the project uses gold (#F4C867). A colour belongs to a glyph, and Morph copies keep it, so every row inherits it. Match colors does the same job in one step for every h at once: see Match colors."],["finish","The answer should arrive with some noise. At 16 s add a Transform ▸ Part of the formula block, pick the 2x of the last row, set its Scale to 1.3, tick Set colour and choose a teal. At 17.2 s add an Emphasis ▸ Indicate block on the same two glyphs. Indicate swells them and turns them yellow for a moment, then lets them go back. The clip with its eight blocks: four Morph blocks, a Write and a FadeIn for the glyphs no pair brings, a Transform and an Indicate for the answer."],["play","Press Space and watch the derivation write itself. Fix timings by dragging blocks, as in the timeline page. When you are happy, Export ▸ Python — ManimCE… or Export ▸ Python — ManimGL… writes the scene (see Exporting)."],["result","This is the scene as ManimCE renders it from the Python the editor exports, at 480p: And the exported Python, for both libraries. Each Morph block is one self.play(…) with an animation for each pair; a pair of groups is one ReplacementTransform of copies of the glyphs. The first block starts like this: self.play( ReplacementTransform(VGroup(*derivation_g[5:12]).copy(), VGroup(*derivation_g[23:30])), ReplacementTransform(VGroup(derivation_g[13]).copy(), VGroup(derivation_g[30], derivation_g[34])), … run_time=1.2, ) The helper at the top of the file matches the editor's glyphs to Manim's by position."],["next","Give a pair a path of its own: select a Morph pair, press Edit path…, and see Custom paths. Change the style of a link: Transform takes the glyph out of its row, and Fade transform fades it. The block's settings compares them. Move a single term across an equals sign, or cancel a factor, with the recipes."]],"short":"A derivative","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"tutorials/annotated-identity","url":"tutorials/annotated-identity.html","title":"An identity with braces and arrows","section":"Tutorials","level":"tutorial","description":"Explain the identity a² − b² = (a − b)(a + b) with a labelled brace under each side and an arrow that arcs over the equals sign, then play it and export it.","headings":[["what-you-make","What you will make"],["before","Before you start"],["steps","Step by step"],["step-brace","1. A brace under the left side"],["step-colour","2. Colour it"],["step-label","3. Give it a label"],["step-intro","4. Make it grow"],["step-right","5. The same on the right"],["step-arrow","6. An arrow over the equals sign"],["step-arc","7. Bend it"],["step-grow","8. Draw it in"],["step-play","9. Play it"],["step-export","10. Export it"],["result","The result"],["make-it-yours","Make it your own"]],"chunks":[[null,"An identity is easier to follow when the picture says what its parts are. You will take a² − b² = (a − b)(a + b) and put a labelled brace under each side and an arrow that arcs from one side to the other, then play it and export it."],["what-you-make","An eleven-second scene. The identity writes itself in; a blue brace grows under the left side and its label fades in; a green brace and its label follow under the right side; last, an arrow is drawn over the equals sign from the left side to the right one. This is the same scene as ManimCE renders it from the Python the editor exports: Along the way you meet the Alt pick of glyphs, the brace of the glyph bar, a label typed in the editor, the compass of an arrow, an arc and GrowArrow."],["before","You need the basics of the Preview and of the timeline, and to have added a formula to a project once (Your first animation). Download the starting project and open it: choose File ▸ Open…, press Other file… in the Projects dialog and pick the downloaded file (Opening a project). It holds the identity on the timeline, written in over 1.8 seconds, and nothing else. The identity has sixteen glyphs. The left side is the first five (a, 2, −, b, 2), then comes the equals sign, and the right side is the last ten, from the first parenthesis to the last. To start from an empty project instead, make the formula as in Your first animation: its LaTeX is a^2 - b^2 = (a - b)(a + b), its size 96, on the timeline from 0 s to 11 s, with the intro Write of 1.8 seconds, and its position a little above the middle of the frame. A clip dropped on the timeline is three seconds long: drag its right edge, or type 11 in the End field of the Animations panel. Your tracks may be numbered differently from the ones in the pictures, and your clips may sit on other tracks. Nothing below depends on it."],["steps",""],["step-brace","Click the ruler of the timeline to put the playhead at about 2.6 s (the time is read beside the transport buttons): the brace will start where the playhead is. In the Preview, Alt+click the first glyph of the left side, then Alt+Shift+click its last: the five glyphs are picked. The bar at the corner of the Preview says so. Open Brace ▾ and choose Below. Five glyphs picked; Brace ▾ offers the four sides. The brace has its own clip on a track above the identity's, from 2.6 s to the end. Braces has all the ways to add one."],["step-colour","Right-click the brace. Colour ▸ offers the Manim colours as swatches: choose the blue one (its tooltip says BLUE). The menu of the brace, with Colour ▸ open."],["step-label","Right-click the brace again and choose Add label ▸ Formula. An empty formula hangs at the brace's tip and its editor opens. Click the Raw LaTeX box, type \\text{difference of squares}, press Apply and close the editor. The label is typed as raw LaTeX in the editor, then applied."],["step-intro","Right-click the brace once more. Intro ▸ gives it the animation its clip starts with: choose GrowFromCenter. The label fades in with the brace. The menu of the brace, with Intro ▸ open. With an intro, the brace is not drawn yet at the first instant of its clip, and a label that fades in is not either: at 2.6 s the Preview shows neither. Move the playhead a second later to see them, and right-click the brace's clip on the timeline when you need its menu again."],["step-right","Move the playhead to 4.4 s. Alt+click the first parenthesis of the right side and Alt+Shift+click the last. Choose Brace ▾ ▸ Below, then in its menu Colour ▸ the green swatch, a label \\text{a product} and Intro ▸ GrowFromCenter."],["step-arrow","Move the playhead to 6.4 s. Pick the whole left side again, as in step 1. Right-click the equals sign, which is not picked: the menu of the clip opens. Choose Add line ▸ Arrow. The arrow starts on the glyphs you picked, and a compass asks where on them: choose the top edge, the arrow pointing up. Now say where it ends. Hold Alt and drag a rectangle over the ten glyphs of the right side: the end goes on all of them. The compass asks again: choose the top edge. With the left side picked, right-click the equals sign: Add line ▸ Arrow. The Alt drag has picked the right side: the compass asks where on it the arrow ends. The compass and the other ways to move an end are in Lines and arrows."],["step-arc","Right-click the arrow and choose Curve ▸ Arc. In the Line section of the Animations panel (the Timing tab) set Arc to −55. A negative number bulges an arrow that points right upwards. The arrow arcs over the equals sign; its Arc field (1) says −55."],["step-grow","In the Intro section choose the animation GrowArrow and make it 1.4 seconds long. An arrow that is bent is drawn along its curve, with its tip on the end of what is drawn. The animation (1) and its length (2)."],["step-play","Press Space, or go to the start with Home and play. The braces grow at 2.6 and 4.4 seconds and the arrow is drawn at 6.4. Everything stays to the end at 11 seconds. If a label starts at the wrong time, drag its clip on the timeline, or type its Start in the panel."],["step-export","Export ▸ Python — ManimCE… gives the scene as a .py file, and Export ▸ Video… renders a video (Exporting). The player above and the video were made from the finished project that you can download in the card."],["result","The Python of the finished scene, for ManimCE and ManimGL: A few lines tell the story. The two braces are built on the glyphs they hang on and their labels are put at their tips; the arrow is built between the top of the left side and the top of the right side, bent by path_arc, and drawn in with the editor's own trace animation because it is bent."],["make-it-yours","Give the arrow a Transform that moves its end onto the two factors one after the other (Moving the ends with a Transform). Put a box round the right side and carry it to the left one with Transform to another formula or glyphs…. Colour the glyphs the labels talk about (Colouring glyphs and objects)."]],"short":"Annotated identity","difficulty":"advanced"}]};
